INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1522,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12481797','Interaction between toll-like receptor 4 polymorphism and abdominal obesity on ovarian cancer risk in Chinese women.','Zhu J, Yang B, Zhai X, Ni F, Tao D, Tan C.','2025','multifactor rs4986790 rs11536889 gmdr rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs11536889gc rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 s12905 deadliest predispositions tlrs 9q32 q33 tlrs jiangyin nantong nantong rs4986791 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs7873784 maf centrifuge resuspend digest vigorously shake centrifuge naac precooled dntps taq uncut endonucleases thr399ile rs4986791 ggttgctgttctcaaagtgattttgggagaa acctgaagactggagagtgagttaaatgct hinfi asp299gly rs4986790 gattagcatacttagactactacctccatg gatcaacttctgaaaaagcattcccac ncoi rs7873784 acgttggatggtatgcaagatgaat tagct acgttggatggaacacttaacatga gaggt xhoi rs11536889 7263g acaagtgatgtttgatggac gccattctacctggtataag eari snpstats bioinfo iconcologia snpstats multifactor hwe rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986791 rs7873784 hwe c1196t thr399ile rs4986791 asp299gly rs4986790 7263g rs11536889 rs7873784 gmdr gmdr rs4986790 rs11536889 tba rs4986790 tba gmdr rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986791 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986791 rs7873784 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs4986791 rs4986790 rs4986791 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986791 rs11536889 rs7873784 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986791 rs7873784 tlr3 tlr5 tlr9 9q32 q33 rs4986791 rs4986790 asp299gly gmdr rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 rs11536889 rs4986790 cooperated gmdr multifactor hwe wuxi q202310 jiangyin nantong l044 ervik laversanne colombet mery pineros znaor gco momenimovahed ghoncheh pakzad hasanpour salehiniya cukurova wojtyła bertuccio giermaziak santucci odone ciebiera negri wojtyła vecchia verkasalo iliadou kaprio koskenvuo pukkala skytthe hemminki prz menopauzalny momenimovahed tiznobaik taheri salehiniya trabert desantis samimi runowicz gaudet burghaus fasching haberle rubner buchner ekici renner kutikhin e3139 rakhesh vijay shrikant shanjana sw480 oncoimmunology asp299gly thr399ile kania hareza wilczynski wilczynski jarych malinowski paradowska asp299gly snider r79 urbute frederiksen kjaer metabolicsyndrome webdata metabolic_syndrome_definition elston anthoney foldi dev156018 shafeghat kazemian aminorroaya aryan rezaei semeraro ammollo friese esmon esmon histones promotethrombin khademalhosseini arababadi raithatha khandelwal choxi kapadia tlr9 kutikhin yuzhalin volkov zhivotovskiy brusina lupi cucielo gaiotte cesario seiva frf chuffa heffron grieco schmelz delort kwiatkowski chalabi satih bignon gallon calle fakhrabadi shokoohi thun tyrer wiensch salvatore tworoger whittemore culver sieh berchuck goode chenevix trench rossing ziogas fortner gayther bandera winham kjaer modugno schildkraut wentzensen trabert høgdall huntsman pharoah pdp','Abstract Objectives the aim of this study was to evaluate the impact of TLR4 gene single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) and additional TLR4 gene SNP- SNP and SNP- abdominal obesity (AO) interaction on ovarian cancer (OC) risk. Methods Generalized multifactor dimensionality reduction method were utilized to identify the most informative interactions between four SNPs in the TLR4 gene and abdominal obesity. Logistic regression was employed to investigate the association between 4 SNPs within TLR4 gene and OC risk, and additional SNP- SNP and gene- AO interaction on OC risk, ORs (95%CI) were calculated. Results The analysis of logistic regression indicated a markedly elevated risk of OC in individuals carrying either the rs4986790-G or rs11536889-C alleles in the TLR4 gene compared to those with the standard genetic variations, adjusted ORs (95%CI) were 1.61 (1.28–1.96) and 1.48 (1.09–1.91). GMDR analysis indicated a significant two-locus model ( p = 0.018) involving rs4986790 and rs11536889, and a significant two-locus model ( p = 0.001) involving rs4986790 and AO. Participants with rs4986790- AG/GG and rs11536889GC/ CC genotype has the highest OC risk, compared to participants with rs4986790-AA and rs11536889-GG genotype, OR (95%CI) = 2.58 (1.46–3.71), and abdominal obese participants with rs4986790- AG/GG genotype have the highest OC risk, compared to non- abdominal obese participants with rs4986790-AA genotype, OR (95%CI) = 3.17 (1.78–4.58). Conclusions The findings suggested that TLR4 gene rs4986790 and rs11536889 polymorphisms were associated with increased OC risk. Significant interaction also existed between rs4986790 and AO, which means that the WC levels may influence the impact of rs4986790 on OC risk. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12905-025-04001-w. Keywords: Abdominal obesity, Ovarian cancer, Single nucleotide polymorphisms, Toll-like receptors, Interaction Introduction Ovarian cancer (OC) is the third most common type of gynecological cancer but is the deadliest [ 1 , 2 ]. Recently, there has been a concerning rise in its occurrence in both China [ 3 ] and Europe [ 4 ]. Epidemiology studies [ 5 – 7 ] on OC have highlighted multiple risk factors, including advancing age, having no children, excess body weight, an early onset of menstruation, late onset of menopause, lifestyle choices, and genetic predispositions. Previously published studies [ 8 , 9 ] also reported a significant relationship between genetic factors and susceptibility to OC. Microglia express toll-like receptors (TLRs), which can detect harmful pathogens and trigger inflammatory responses. Specifically, the TLR4 gene, which has a structure consisting of four exons, is located on chromosome 9q32 - q33 [ 10 ]. Previously, several single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) within the TLR4 gene have been reported [ 11 , 12 ]. Mounting evidence suggested that TLRs play a crucial role in regulating tumor dynamics and progression [ 13 , 14 ]. A limited number of studies [ 15 , 16 ] have also suggested that SNPs in the TLR4 gene were associated with the risk of OC. Additionally, susceptibility to OC can be influenced by both genetic factors, environmental factors, and gene- environment interactions. Previously, a review [ 17 ] showed that obesity can trigger ovarian inflammation and lead to a decline in oocyte quality. Epidemiology studies [ 18 , 19 ] have confirmed the association between obesity and OC. Currently, as far as we understand, there is a lack of research on the impact of the interaction between TLR4 polymorphisms and obesity on the risk of OC. Given this situation, the purpose of this study is to evaluate the role of TLR4 gene polymorphism, SNP-SNP interaction, and the gene-obesity interaction in the susceptibility to OC in Chinese women. Materials and methods Subjects From January 2019 to September 2022, all patients with OC were recruited from the Affiliated Jiangyin Hospital of Nantong University and Nantong Tumor Hospital. All patients were diagnosed and classified according to the guidelines of the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) [ 20 ]. Patients with other systemic diseases, including other primary tumors, were excluded. Controls were selected from normal individuals undergoing physical examination in our hospital. They were age -matched (± 3 years) to OC patients. Those participants with an individual family history of cancer were excluded from the control group. Participants with other systemic diseases, including any type of tumor, were excluded from the control group. A total of 930 participants were enrolled in this study. Among them, there were 310 °C patients and 620 age -matched (with a ratio of 1:2) normal controls. All the enrolled participants were of Chinese Han ethnicity. In this study, PASS 15.0.5 software [ 21 ] was used for power and sample size calculation. With a significance level 𝛼 = 0.05, P 2 = 0.21, OR = 1.8, and N 2 /N 1 = 2, it was calculated that the number of subjects needed to achieve 95% power to detect a difference between the two groups. The results showed that the minimum sample size was 912 (N 1 =304, N 2 =608). In our study, 310 °C patients and 620 normal controls were enrolled. Thus, the sample size of this study was considered sufficient. Trained staff administered questionnaires to all participants to collect general demographic information, lifestyle information, and clinical gynecological data. They obtained this information through a survey questionnaire (supplementary file 1) and clinical data. Abdominal obesity was defined as a waist circumference (WC) ≥ 90 cm for men and ≥ 80 cm for women [ 22 ]. We also collected blood samples from participants who had fasted for at least 8 h. All of them had read and signed a written informed consent form. Genomic DNA extraction and genotyping Four SNPs (rs4986791, rs4986790, rs11536889 and rs7873784) of TLR4 gene were selected from NCBI database ( https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/snp/ ) based on the following criteria: first, minor allele frequency (MAF) ≥ 5% in global populations; second, SNPs previously Reported in OC studies; third, previous studies concluded inconsistent results, the relationship between SNPs with OC risk was not verified. Collect 3 mL of peripheral venous blood from all participants and store it in EDTA anticoagulant tubes (purple lid). Centrifuge within 30 min (2000 × g, 10 min, 4 ° C) to separate plasma (upper layer) and blood cells (lower layer). The blood cell layer is then divided into 1.5 mL sterile EP tubes and stored at −80 °C. Resuspend the blood cell pellet in lysis buffer, add proteinase K (final concentration 100 µ g/mL), digest overnight at 56 °C, then add an equal volume of phenol chloroform, vigorously shake and centrifuge (12000 × g, 10 min), and transfer the supernatant (aqueous phase). Add 0.1 times the volume of 3 M NaAc and 2 times the volume of precooled ethanol to precipitate DNA (−20 °C for 1 h). PCR-based RFLP was utilized to determine the genotypes of four selected SNPs. The 25 µ L PCR reaction system includes: 50–100 ng DNA sample, forward and reverse primers 0.5 µ M, 200 µ M dNTPs, 1 U Taq enzyme, 1 x buffer, 1.5 mM MgCl 2 , pre denaturation: 95 ° C 5-minute cycle (35 rounds): 95 °C 30 s, 55–60 °C (adjusted according to primer Tm value) 30 s, 72 ° C 30 s, final extension: 72 °C 5 min. Enzyme digestion reaction system (20 µ L): PCR product 10 µ L + enzyme 1 U + buffer 1 x, incubated at 37 ° C for 4 h. Finally, 2% agarose gel electrophoresis (100 V, 30 min) was used to observe the band pattern: wild type: uncut (200 bp). Mutant type: cleaved into 100 bp + 100 bp. Heterozygous type: 200 bp + 100 bp + 100 bp. The nucleotide sequence of primers used for genotyping were shown in Table 1 . To validate the genotyping outcomes, we randomly selected 5% of the initial samples for re-analysis, and the results guaranteed that there were no discrepancies in the replicated genotypic data. Table 1. Description and primers used for genotyping for 4 SNPs with in TLR4 gene ID Chromosome Functional Consequence Primers (5’→3’) Restriction endonucleases Thr399Ile 1196 C > T (rs4986791) 9:117713324 Coding sequence variant missense variant Forward: 5′-GGTTGCTGTTCTCAAAGTGATTTTGGGAGAA − 3′ Reverse: 5′-ACCTGAAGACTGGAGAGTGAGTTAAATGCT − 3′ HinfI 896 A > G Asp299Gly (rs4986790) 9:117713024 Coding sequence variant missense variant Forward: 5′-GATTAGCATACTTAGACTACTACCTCCATG − 3′ Reverse: 5′- GATCAACTTCTGAAAAAGCATTCCCAC − 3′ NcoI rs7873784 (C > G) 9:117716658 3′ UTR Forward: 5′- ACGTTGGATGGTATGCAAGATGAAT TAGCT − 3′ Reverse: 5′- ACGTTGGATGGAACACTTAACATGA GAGGT − 3′ XhoI rs11536889 + 7263G/C 9:117715853 3′ UTR Forward: 5′- ACAAGTGATGTTTGATGGAC − 3′ Reverse: 5′- GCCATTCTACCTGGTATAAG − 3′ EarI Open in a new tab Statistical analysis In the current study, the SPSS 22.0 software package (SPSS Inc, Chicago) was used for all statistical analyses. For normally distributed continuous variables, the means and standard deviations (SDs) were calculated. For categorical variables, percentages were calculated. To compare the means and SDs between two groups, Student’s t test was used. To compare the percentages between two groups, χ 2 test was used. We used the SNPstats online platform ( http://bioinfo.iconcologia.net/SNPstats ) to test for Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. We utilized the generalized multifactor dimensionality reduction [ 23 ] method to identify the most informative interactions between four SNPs in the TLR4 gene and abdominal obesity. Logistic regression was employed to examine the association between 4 SNPs within TLR4 gene and risk of OC. Additionally, the gene - AO interaction on OC risk was explored, and odds ratios (ORs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated. All reported p - values were calculated using a two-sided test. Statistically, results with a value below 0.05 were considered significant. Results This case-control study enrolled 930 participants, consisting of 310 °C patients and 620 normal controls. The mean age of all participants was 61.9 ± 11.8 years. Among them, the mean age of OC patients was 62.7 ± 11.2 years, while that of normal controls was 61.4 ± 10.8 years. Table 2 presents the general and clinical attributes of OC patients and healthy controls. There was no significant difference in the means of age, smoker rates, and the number of pregnancies between OC patients and normal controls. However, the mean WC values were higher in OC patients than in normal controls. There were also significant differences in the distributions of tubal ligation and menopause status between OC patients and normal controls. Table 2. General and clinical characteristics of study participants in OC patients and normal controls Variables OC patients ( n = 310) Normal controls ( n = 620) p -values Age (years) 62.7 ± 11.2 61.4 ± 10.8 0.088 Smoking, N (%) 8 (2.6) 12 (1.9) 0.522 WC (cm) 84.3 ± 13.6 80.2 ± 12.6 < 0.001 Tubal ligation, N (%) 10 (3.2) 44 (7.1) 0.017 Menopause status, N (%) < 0.001 Pre 98 (31.6) 276 (44.5) Post 212 (68.4) 344 (55.5) Number of pregnancies, N (%) 0.515 1 or 2 267 (86.1) 524 (84.5) More than 3 43 (13.9) 96 (15.5) Differentiation, N (%) Middle/high 90 (29.0) Low 220 (71.0) Open in a new tab Means ± standard deviation for age and WC WC Waist circumference In this study, the genotype frequencies of four SNPs conformed to HWE, with all p -values > 0.05. The frequencies of the rs4986790 - G allele and the rs11536889 - C allele within the TLR4 gene were higher in OC patients than in normal controls. Specifically, for the rs4986790 - G allele, the frequency is 28.6% in patients and 19.6% in controls; for the rs11536889 - C allele, the frequency is 25.3% in patients and 19.0% in controls. Logistic regression analysis showed that carriers with the rs4986790 - G allele and those with the rs11536889 - C allele within the TLR4 gene had a higher susceptibility to OC compared to those with wild genotypes. The adjusted ORs (95%CI) were 1.61 (1.28–1.96) and 1.48 (1.09–1.91) respectively, after adjusting for age, smoking, WC, tubal ligation, and menopause status. We also found that rs4986791 and rs7873784 were not statistically significantly associated with the risk of OC. (Table 3 ) Table 3. Logistic regression on association between TLR4 gene genotypes and OC risk SNPs Genotypes and alleles Frequencies, N (%) OR (95%CI) * p - values for HWE test OC patients ( n = 310) Normal Control ( n = 620) C1196T, Thr399Ile (rs4986791) Co-dominant CC 176 (56.8) 398 (64.2) 1.00 0.274 CT 114 (36.8) 192 (31.0) 1.31 (0.90–1.75) TT 40 (6.4) 30 (4.8) 1.42 (0.81–2.03) Dominant CC 176 (56.8) 398 (64.2) 1.00 CT + TT 134 (43.2) 222 (35.8) 1.36 (0.88–1.87) Allele, T (%) 154 (24.8) 252 (20.3) 896 A > G Asp299Gly (rs4986790) Co-dominant AA 161 (51.9) 404 (65.2) 1.00 0.155 AG 121 (39.0) 189 (30.5) 1.54 (1.21–1.89) GG 28 (9.0) 27 (4.3) 1.87 (1.16–2.61) Dominant AA 161 (51.9) 404 (65.2) 1.00 AG + GG 149 (48.1) 216 (34.8) 1.61 (1.28–1.96) Allele, G (%) 177 (28.6) 243 (19.6) + 7263G/C, rs11536889 Co-dominant GG 172 (55.5) 405 (65.3) 1.00 0.649 GC 119 (38.4) 195 (31.5) 1.43 (1.06–1.83) CC 19 (6.1) 20 (3.2) 1.62 (1.13–2.17) Dominant GG 172 (55.5) 405 (65.3) 1.00 GC + CC 138 (44.5) 215 (34.7) 1.48 (1.09–1.91) Allele, C (%) 157 (25.3) 235 (19.0) rs7873784 Co-dominant CC 191 (61.6) 411 (66.3) 1.00 0.443 CG 105 (33.9) 192 (31.0) 1.25 (0.89–1.63) GG 14 (4.5) 17 (2.7) 1.58 (0.80–2.37) Dominant CC 191 (61.6) 411 (66.3) 1.00 CG + GG 119 (38.4) 209 (33.7) 1.29 (0.87–2.05) Allele, G (%) 133 (21.5) 226 (18.2) Open in a new tab *Adjusted for age, smoking, WC, tubal ligation and menopause status In this study, we used the GMDR model to screen the best SNP- SNP and SNP- AO interactions for 4 SNPs within the TLR4 gene and AO. The mean value of WC in OC patients was significantly higher than that in normal controls. Table 4 presented the results of GMDR analysis. It indicated a significant two-locus model ( P = 0.018) involving rs4986790 and rs11536889. The cross-validation consistency (CVC) of the two- locus models was 10/10, and the testing balanced accuracy (TBA) was 0.608. We also found a significant two-locus model ( P = 0.001) that involves rs4986790 and AO. The CVC was 10/10, and the TBA was 0.622. Table 4. Best gene–gene and gene- AO interaction combinations in GMDR model Locus no. Best combination Sign test p values Testing balanced accuracy Cross-validation consistency SNP- SNP interaction 1 2 rs4986790, rs11536889 0.018 0.608 10/10 3 rs4986790, rs11536889, rs4986791 0.426 0.540 7/10 4 rs4986790, rs11536889, rs4986791, rs7873784 0.532 0.496 6/10 Gene- AO interaction 2 2 rs4986790, AO 0.001 0.622 10/10 3 rs4986790, rs4986791, AO 0.256 0.549 8/10 4 rs4986790, rs4986791, rs11536889, AO 0.359 0.512 7/10 5 rs4986790, rs4986791, rs11536889, rs7873784, AO 0.624 0.496 6/10 Open in a new tab 1 Adjusted for age, smoking, WC, tubal ligation and menopause status 2 Adjusted for age, smoking, tubal ligation and menopause status We also employed logistic regression analysis to examine the SNP- SNP interaction between rs4986790 and rs11536889, as well as the SNP- AO interaction effect between rs4986790 and AO. We found that the risk of OC was highest in participants with the rs4986790- AG/GG and rs11536889-GC/CC genotypes, compared to those with rs4986790-AA and rs11536889-GG genotypes, OR (95%CI) = 2.58 (1.46–3.71). We also found that among participants with rs4986790 - AG/GG genotype, the risk of OC was highest in those with AO. When compared to non- abdominal obese participants with rs4986790 - AA genotype, the OR (95%CI) = 3.17 (1.78–4.58) (Table 5 ). Table 5. Analysis for SNP-SNP and gene - AO interaction by using logistic regression Variables OR (95% CI) * p -values SNP- SNP interaction 1 rs4986790-AA and rs11536889-GG 1.00 - rs4986790-AA and rs11536889-GC/CC 1.25 (0.94–1.61) 0.306 rs4986790-AG/GG and rs11536889-GG 1.59 (1.08–2.12) 0.028 rs4986790- AG/GG and rs11536889-GC/CC 2.58 (1.46–3.71) 0.001 Gene- AO interaction 2 rs4986790-AA and normal WC 1.00 - rs4986790-AA and AO 1.36 (1.07–1.69) 0.021 rs4986790-AG/GG and normal WC 1.47 (1.11–1.87) 0.012 rs4986790- AG/GG and AO 3.17 (1.78–4.58) < 0.001 Open in a new tab 1 Adjusted for age, smoking, WC, tubal ligation and menopause status 2 Adjusted for age, smoking, tubal ligation and menopause status. AO: abdominal obesity Discussion In the current study, we found that carriers with the G allele of rs4986790 and the C allele of rs11536889 within the TLR4 gene had an increased risk of OC compared to participants with wild genotypes. Nevertheless, we also found that rs4986791 and rs7873784 were not statistically associated with the risk of OC. The human TLR family, which is part of the broader IL-1R superfamily, has multiple subtypes, including TLR2 , TLR3 , TLR4 , TLR5 , and TLR9. The specific gene for TLR4 is located at the 9q32-q33 locus on chromosome 9 and consists of four exons [ 24 , 25 ]. TLR4 is the main receptor in the TLR family associated with platelet production and function as well as inflammation [ 26 ]. Previous studies have indicated a statistical association between TLR4 gene SNPs and the risk of malignant tumors in multiple sites, including breast cancer [ 27 ] and cervical cancer [ 28 ]. Luo et al. [ 29 ] proposed that the expression of TLR4 might be implicated in the development and progression of tumor cell tissues within ovarian tissue. Wang et al. [ 15 ] performed a case- control study in Chinese populations. They suggested that the frequencies of rs4986791 and rs4986790 alleles were lower in OC patients compared to controls. Another case– control study [ 30 ] conducted in Russian showed that TLR4 - 896 A/G was not related to OC susceptibility. However, a recent case- control study in the Polish population indicated that the presence of the TLR4 gene Asp299Gly polymorphism may increase the risk of developing OC [ 16 ]. The precise biological mechanism by which TLR4 gene SNPs influence the risk of OC remains unclear. A previous study [ 31 ] demonstrated that the components involved in the TLR4 signaling pathway and their downstream effectors are overexpressed. This overexpression is mediated through the NF- κB signaling pathway, which in turn mediates OC differentiation. TLR4 is present in both normal and cancerous ovarian cells, suggesting how OC utilizes inflammation to its benefit [ 32 ]. Shea et al. [ 33 ] showed that OC cells spread by directly altering cellular and genetic patterns in crucial peritoneal tissues, thereby creating environments favorable for both pre-metastasis and metastasis. Furthermore, they promote and sustain the modifications induced by carcinoma. The cellular and molecular characteristics of the pro-tumor environment, which bridges obesity and cancer, offer viable targets for designing interventions to prevent or manage metastatic OC. The onset of OC is contributed to by many factors, including genetic and environmental factors, as well as their synergistic effects [ 7 ]. In this study, we also employed the GMDR model and logistic regression to examine the SNP- SNP and gene- environment interactions among four SNPs within TLR4 and AO. We found that the risk of OC could be influenced by both the rs4986790- rs11536889 interaction and the rs4986790 - AO interaction. Central fat accumulation was associated with an increased likelihood of developing OC [ 34 ]. Rodriguez et al. [ 35 ] discovered that obese individuals have a 36% higher risk of OC compared to non-obese individuals. Although previous studies [ 36 , 37 ] have reported on the impact of the interaction between other gene and environmental factors on OC risk, no study has focused on the impact of TLR4 gene SNP- SNP and gene- environment interaction on susceptibility to OC. So, this study for the first time reported the influence of TLR4 gene SNP- SNP and gene- environment interaction on the risk of OC, particularly in the Chinese Han population. The detailed biological mechanism underlying the synergistic effect between genes and environmental factors remained unclear. There might be a significant association between TLR4 gene SNPs and AO. Moreover, when both risk factors coexist, the risk of developing OC was significantly higher compared to carrying single risk factor. This study had several limitations. First, the sample size of OC patients was limited, which might result in unstable results. In the future, more OC patients should be included to validate the findings of this study. Secondly, all the participants enrolled in this study were of the Chinese Han population. Therefore, the results obtained from this study may not necessarily be representative of populations of other races. In conclusion, this case-control study indicated a statistically significant association between the rs4986790- G allele or with the rs11536889- C allele within TLR4 gene and increased OC risk. Both SNP- SNP interaction between rs4986790 and rs11536889 or gene- environment interaction between rs4986790 and AO were significantly correlated with increased susceptibility to OC. In the future, more epidemiological studies should be performed to verify the impact of TLR4 genes on OC risk. Biological studies were also needed to verify the results obtained in our study and to explain the biological mechanism involved relationship between TLR4 gene and OC risk. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (20.3KB, docx) Acknowledgements We appreciate the cooperation of the partners and staff who cooperated in this study. Abbreviations AO Abdominal obesity BMI Body mass index SNPs Single nucleotide polymorphisms GMDR Generalized multifactor dimensionality reduction OC Ovarian cancer FIGO International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics SD Standard deviation HWE Hardy- Weinberg equilibrium WC Waist circumference Authors’ contributions The manuscript writing, editing, and review were conducted by Zhu J and Tan C, Yang B and Zhai X participated in the genomic DNA extraction and genotyping. Ni Feng and Tao D participated information collection and questionnaire investigation. Funding This study was supported by the Youth Project of Wuxi Municipal Health Commission (Q202310). Data availability The data and tables generated and analyzed during this study are available from the corresponding authors upon reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Each participant understood the study process and signed a written informed consent before the study. The protocol was approved by committee of the Affiliated Jiangyin Hospital of Nantong University (2024-L044) in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. 1. Ferlay J, Ervik M, Lam F, Laversanne M, Colombet M, Mery L, Piñeros M, Znaor A, Soerjomataram I, Bray F. Global Cancer Observatory: Cancer Today. Lyon, France: International Agency for Research on Cancer, 2024. Available from: https://gco.iarc.who.int/today , accessed [28 February 2024]. 2. Momenimovahed Z, Ghoncheh M, Pakzad R, Hasanpour H, Salehiniya H. Incidence and mortality of uterine cancer and relationship with human development index in the world. Cukurova Med J. 2017;42(2):233–40. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Han B, Zheng R, Zeng H, Wang S, Sun K, Chen R, Li L, Wei W, He J. Cancer incidence and mortality in China, 2022. J Natl Cancer Cent. 2024;4(1):47–53. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Wojtyła C, Bertuccio P, Giermaziak W, Santucci C, Odone A, Ciebiera M, Negri E, Wojtyła A, La Vecchia C. European trends in ovarian cancer mortality, 1990–2020 and predictions to 2025. Eur J Cancer. 2023;194:113350. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Lichtenstein P, Holm NV, Verkasalo PK, Iliadou A, Kaprio J, Koskenvuo M, Pukkala E, Skytthe A, Hemminki K. Environmental and heritable factors in the causation of cancer-analyses of cohorts of twins from Sweden, Denmark, and Finland. N Engl J Med. 2000;343:78–85. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Ali AT, Al-Ani O, Al-Ani F. Epidemiology and risk factors for ovarian cancer. Prz Menopauzalny. 2023;22(2):93–104. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Momenimovahed Z, Tiznobaik A, Taheri S, Salehiniya H. Ovarian cancer in the world: epidemiology and risk factors. Int J Womens Health. 2019;11:287–99. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Torre LA, Trabert B, DeSantis CE, Miller KD, Samimi G, Runowicz CD, Gaudet MM, Jemal A, Siegel RL. Ovarian cancer statistics, 2018. CA Cancer J Clin. 2018;68(4):284–96. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] 