Gallon DJ. Central adiposity as a major risk factor of ovarian cancer. Anticancer Res. 2009;29(12):5229–34. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Rodriguez C, Calle EE, Fakhrabadi-Shokoohi D, Jacobs EJ, Thun MJ. Body mass index, height, and the risk of ovarian cancer mortality in a prospective cohort of postmenopausal women. Cancer Epidemiol Biomarkers Prev. 2002;11(9):822–8. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Zhang H, Zhou L. Single nucleotide polymorphism of PIK3CA and its interaction with the environment are risk factors for Chinese Han ovarian cancer. Pathol Res Pract. 2019;215(9):152520. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Kim S, Wang M, Tyrer JP, Jensen A, Wiensch A, Liu G, Lee AW, Ness RB, Salvatore M, Tworoger SS, Whittemore AS, Anton-Culver H, Sieh W, Olson SH, Berchuck A, Goode EL, Goodman MT, Doherty JA, Chenevix-Trench G, Rossing MA, Webb PM, Giles GG, Terry KL, Ziogas A, Fortner RT, Menon U, Gayther SA, Wu AH, Song H, Brooks-Wilson A, Bandera EV, Cook LS, Cramer DW, Milne RL, Winham SJ, Kjaer SK, Modugno F, Thompson PJ, Chang-Claude J, Harris HR, Schildkraut JM, Le ND, Wentzensen N, Trabert B, Høgdall E, Huntsman D, Pike MC, Pharoah PDP, Pearce CL, Mukherjee B. A comprehensive gene-environment interaction analysis in Ovarian Cancer using genome-wide significant common variants. Int J Cancer. 2019;144(9):2192–205. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material 1. (20.3KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data and tables generated and analyzed during this study are available from the corresponding authors upon reasonable request.',751,'2025-10-13 06:12:59','2025-10-13 06:12:59');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1523,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12481838','Risk factors and adverse pregnancy outcomes associated with large for gestational age infants in pregnant women with type 2 diabetes mellitus.','Yan X, Zhang L, Zheng W, Yuan X, Han W, Song W, Wang X, Liang S, Jia J, Li G.','2025','multiparity multiparity multiparity multiparity figdraw aerobics abruption multiparous pregancy prepregnancy prepregnancy multiparity abruption multiparity multiparity prepregnancy multiparity model1 model2 model3 multiparity multiparity multiparous multiparity multiparity multiparous multiparous multiparity lirui weiling xiaoxin shengnan lirui guanghui xianxian guanghui lirui guanghui lirui xianxian weiling xiaoxin shengnan guanghui lingjunrencai lirui lirui alexopoulos mackin kerssens colhoun leese fadl e000221 e1002926 pyle nadeau goland conrey bullard clausen mathiesen ekbom hellmuth mandrup poulsen damm howgate valabhji knighton barned khambalia algert collie weissmann simchen zilberberg kalter weisz achiron dulitzky scifres hod hadar renzo cabero roura divakar s173 padiatr vats sachdeva walia abell ranasinha misso rode bogaerts devlieger teede hadfield lain raynes greenow pasupathy hopper hattersley e3245 laughon kiely brite salihu macrosomic fadl pasupathy rosenn miodovnik','Abstract Objective To investigate the risk factors for delivering large for gestational age (LGA) infants in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) and their pregnancy outcomes. Methods This was a retrospective cohort study of T2DM patients who registered for regular prenatal care and delivered at the Beijing Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital from January 1, 2012, to December 31, 2020. Patients were divided into the LGA and non-LGA groups to explore the risk factors and pregnancy outcomes associated with delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients. Results (1)A total of 691 T2DM patients were included, of which 257 delivered LGA infants, accounting for 37.19%. (2) Pre-pregnancy body mass index (OR: 1.067, 95%CI: 1.026–1.109), multiparity (OR: 2.455, 95%CI: 1.704–3.538), history of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) (OR: 2.211, 95%CI: 1.283–3.810), late pregnancy glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1c) (OR: 2.168, 95%CI: 1.567–3.001), gestational weight gain (OR: 1.073, 95%CI: 1.034–1.114), and female newborns (OR: 1.571, 95%CI: 1.109–2.225) were independent risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients. (3) After adjusting for pre-pregnancy BMI, multiparity, history of PCOS, gestational weight gain, and late pregnancy HbA1c, delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients increased the risk of postpartum hemorrhage (OR: 1.872, 95%CI: 1.171–2.992), shoulder dystocia (OR: 6.046, 95%CI: 1.767–20.681), admission to the neonatal intensive care unit (OR: 1.542, 95%CI: 1.061–2.242), and neonatal hypoglycemia (OR: 1.991, 95%CI: 1.250–3.170). Conclusion Strengthening the identification of risk factors, improving pre-pregnancy weight management, ensuring adequate weight gain during pregnancy, and maintaining good glycemic control are important measures for T2DM patients to avoid delivering LGA infants and adverse pregnancy outcomes. Graphical Abstract The risk factors and adverse pregnancy outcomes for delivering LGA in patients with T2DM. ♦ Pre-pregnancy BMI, multiparity, history of PCOS, late pregnancy HbA1c, gestational weight gain, and female newborns were independent risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients. ♦ After adjusting for pre-pregnancy BMI, multiparity, history of PCOS, gestational weight gain, and late pregnancy HbA1c (Model 2), delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients increased the risk of postpartum hemorrhage, shoulder dystocia, admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, and neonatal hypoglycemia. LGA, large for gestational age; T2DM, type 2 diabetes mellitus; BMI, body mass index; PCOS, polycystic ovary syndrome; HbA1c, glycosylated hemoglobin; PROM, premature rupture of membranes; NICU, neonatal intensive care unit; aOR, adjusted odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. The picture was drawn by Figdraw. Keywords: Type 2 diabetes mellitus, Large for gestational age, Risk factors, Pregnancy outcomes Background With the increasing age of childbearing and the rising prevalence of obesity, the global prevalence of T2DM among pregnant women is rapidly growing [ 1 ]. Studies have reported that the number of women with T2DM during pregnancy in Scotland and Sweden increased by 90% and 111%, respectively, between 1998 and 2013 and 1998 and 2012 [ 2 , 3 ]. A population-based cohort study in China from 2010 to 2016, which included 6.4 million women aged 20–49, estimated the incidence of diabetes to be 1.18% [ 4 ]. Pregnancy combined with T2DM increases the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes, such as LGA, gestational hypertension, cesarean section, macrosomia, neonatal congenital malformations, embryonic arrest, and neonatal death [ 1 , 5 – 8 ]. LGA is defined as a birth weight above the 90th percentile for the same sex and gestational age, which is recently associated with adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes such as cesarean section, postpartum hemorrhage, shoulder dystocia, and neonatal hypoglycemia, and can increase the risk of obesity, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease in the long term [ 9 – 12 ]. Current research mainly focuses on the risk of LGA and adverse pregnancy outcomes in women with gestational diabetes, with fewer studies on the risk factors and pregnancy outcomes of LGA in T2DM patients. Therefore, this study aims to explore the risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients and its impact on maternal and pregnancy outcomes through a retrospective cohort study, to provide references for the perinatal management of T2DM patients. Methods Study subjects This study included women with T2DM who received regular prenatal check-ups and delivered at Beijing Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital, Capital Medical University, from January 1, 2012, to December 31, 2020. The inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) maternal age between 18 and 45 years; (2) a diagnosis of T2DM based on at least one of the following: fasting blood glucose ≥ 7.0 mmol/L, 2-h blood glucose ≥ 11.1 mmol/L after 75 g oral glucose tolerance test, HbA1c ≥ 6.5%, or the presence of typical symptoms of hyperglycemia or a hyperglycemic crisis with random blood glucose ≥ 11.1 mmol/L [ 13 , 14 ]; (3) singleton pregnancy; and (4) gestational age at delivery ≥ 28 weeks. Cases with incomplete clinical data of gestational weight gain were excluded. Informed consent was obtained from all participants involved in the study. Women diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus attended a hospital-based"one-day diabetes clinic,"which involved a full day of theoretical education and practical training. Patients received instruction on following a standardized low glycemic index diet, participated in supervised aerobics classes, and were taught how to self-monitor their blood glucose levels. Additionally, all patients were followed up with diabetes specialists every two weeks until delivery. The Ethics Committee of Beijing Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital, Capital Medical University approved this study (2018-ky-009–01) and the study was carried out in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki as revised in 2008. Data collection Through the hospital''s electronic medical record system, demographic and clinical data of the pregnant women were collected, including age, pre-pregnancy weight, height, parity, history of PCOS, history of chronic hypertension, history of adverse pregnancy outcomes (including embryonic arrest, intrauterine fetal death, and stillbirth), history of spontaneous abortion, family history of T2DM in first-degree relatives, whether insulin treatment was used, total daily insulin dose before delivery, early pregnancy HbA1c, late pregnancy HbA1c, gestational weight gain, gestational age at delivery, mode of delivery, gestational hypertension, premature rupture of membranes, placental abruption, preterm birth, postpartum hemorrhage, shoulder dystocia, newborn sex, newborn birth weight, admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, neonatal malformations, neonatal hypoglycemia, neonatal jaundice, and neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. Study grouping According to the"Growth standard curves of birth weight, length and head circumference of Chinese newborns of different gestation" [ 15 ], patients with a newborn birth weight above the 90th percentile for the same sex and gestational age were classified into the LGA group (study group), while the remaining patients were classified into the non-LGA group (control group). The risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients were analyzed, and their impact on pregnancy outcomes was further explored. Statistical analysis Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS 26.0 software. Measurement data conforming to a normal distribution were expressed as mean ± standard deviation ( ± s), and t-tests were used for comparisons between groups; count data were expressed as n (%), and chi-square tests were used for comparisons between groups. Binary logistic regression was used to analyze the risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients and their impact on pregnancy outcomes. A P -value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results From 2012 to 2020, a total of 702 pregnant women with T2DM who delivered after 28 weeks of gestation at Beijing Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital, Capital Medical University were enrolled. Eleven patients were excluded for not meeting the inclusion criteria (3 cases with age < 18 or > 45 and 8 cases with incomplete clinical data of gestational weight gain). A total of 691 patients were finally included in this study for analysis. The 691 patients were divided into the LGA group and the non-LGA group, with 257 women in the LGA group, accounting for 37.19%. The study flowchart is shown in Fig. 1 . Fig. 1. Open in a new tab The flowchart of the study Comparison of baseline characteristics and pregnancy outcomes between the two groups The baseline information of the pregnant women is shown in Table 1 . Compared with the non-LGA group, the LGA group had higher pre-pregnancy BMI, greater gestational weight gain, a higher proportion of multiparous women, and a higher proportion of women with a history of PCOS. Additionally, the LGA group had higher early pregnancy and late pregnancy HbA1c levels. The differences between the two groups were statistically significant ( p < 0.05). The pregancy outcomes of the two groups are shown in Table 2 . The risks of cesarean section, postpartum hemorrhage, shoulder dystocia, admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, and neonatal hypoglycemia were higher in the LGA group than in the non-LGA group, with statistically significant differences between the groups ( p < 0.05). The risk of premature rupture of membranes was higher in the non-LGA group than in the LGA group, with a statistically significant difference ( p = 0.004). There was a statistically significant difference in the sex of the newborns between the two groups, with a higher risk of LGA in female newborns ( p = 0.042). Table 1. Baseline characteristics between the two groups in patients with T2DM Characteristics The LGA group ( n = 257) The non-LGA group ( n = 434) t/χ 2 P -value Maternal age (years), ±s 33.91 ± 4.05 34.01 ± 3.97 0.308 0.758 Advanced age (> 35 years) 96(37.35) 163(37.56) 0.003 0.957 Height (cm), ±s 163.95 ± 5.19 162.71 ± 5.08 −3.066 0.002 Prepregnancy weight (kg), ±s 76.21 ± 13.41 72.35 ± 13.8 −3.593 < 0.001 Prepregnancy BMI (kg/m2), ±s 28.29 ± 4.41 27.3 ± 4.96 −2.637 0.009 Multiparity 111(43.19) 111(25.58) 22.968 < 0.001 History of PCOS 37(14.40) 35(8.06) 6.934 0.008 History of chronic hypertension 31(12.06) 75(17.28) 3.385 0.066 History of adverse pregnancy outcomes 80(31.13) 132(30.41) 0.039 0.844 History of spontaneous abortion 38(14.79) 60(13.82) 0.123 0.726 Family history of T2DM in first-degree relatives 12(4.67) 24(5.53) 0.242 0.623 Insulin treatment 227(88.33) 375(86.41) 0.531 0.466 Total daily insulin dose before delivery (IU), ±s 63.19 ± 47.28 56.84 ± 42.65 −1.818 0.069 Early pregnancy HbA1c 7.10 ± 1.46 6.76 ± 1.37 −3.053 0.002 Late pregnancy HbA1c 6.14 ± 0.68 5.79 ± 0.60 −6.676 < 0.001 Gestational weight gain (kg) 11.03 ± 5.41 9.31 ± 4.99 −4.23 < 0.001 Gestational age at delivery(weeks), ±s 37.86 ± 1.69 37.83 ± 2.03 −0.153 0.878 Open in a new tab Values are expressed as number (percentage), unless indicated otherwise LGA large for gestational age, BMI body mass index, PCOS polycystic ovary syndrome, T2DM type 2 diabetes mellitus, HbA1c glycosylated hemoglobin Table 2. Pregnancy outcomes between the two groups in patients with T2DM Maternal and neonatal outcomes The LGA group ( n = 257) The non-LGA group ( n = 434) t/χ 2 P -value Cesarean section 177(68.87) 236(54.38) 14.102 < 0.001 Assisted vaginal delivery 15(5.84) 27(6.22) 0.042 0.838 Gestational hypertension 26(10.12) 44(10.14) 0 0.993 Preeclampsia 30(11.67) 49(11.29) 0.023 0.879 Chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia 13(5.06) 30(6.91) 0.951 0.330 Premature rupture of membranes 48(18.68) 124(28.57) 8.452 0.004 Placental abruption 4(1.56) 9(2.07) 0.234 0.629 Preterm birth 25(9.73) 60(13.82) 2.512 0.113 Postpartum hemorrhage 49(19.07) 53(12.21) 6.027 0.014 Shoulder dystocia 12(4.67) 4(0.92) 10.023 0.002 Newborn sex/male 121(47.08) 239(55.07) 4.127 0.042 Newborn sex/female 136(52.92) 195(44.93) Newborn birth weight (g), ±s 3959.65 ± 551.68 3095.75 ± 526.39 −20.48 < 0.001 NICU admission 91(35.41) 106(24.42) 9.556 0.002 Neonatal malformations 10(3.89) 31(7.14) 3.058 0.080 Neonatal hypoglycemia 57(22.18) 50(11.52) 14.011 < 0.001 Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome 25(9.73) 37(8.53) 0.286 0.593 Neonatal jaundice 27(10.51) 59(13.59) 1.413 0.235 Open in a new tab Values are expressed as number (percentage), unless indicated otherwise LGA large for gestational age, NICU neonatal intensive care unit Multivariable logistic regression analysis of risk factors for delivering LGA infants in patients with T2DM Using pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI), multiparity, history of PCOS, early pregnancy glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1c), late pregnancy HbA1c, gestational weight gain, and newborn sex as independent variables, and LGA as the dependent variable, a multivariable logistic regression analysis was conducted. The results showed that pre-pregnancy BMI (OR: 1.067, 95%CI: 1.026–1.109), multiparity (OR: 2.455, 95%CI: 1.704–3.538), history of PCOS (OR: 2.211, 95%CI: 1.283–3.81), late pregnancy HbA1c (OR: 2.168, 95%CI: 1.567–3.001), gestational weight gain (OR: 1.073, 95%CI: 1.034–1.114), and female newborns (OR: 1.571, 95%CI: 1.109–2.225) were independent risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients, as shown in Table 3 . Table 3. Multivariable logistic regression analysis of risk factors for delivering LGA in patients with T2DM β Wald aOR (95%CI) P Prepregnancy BMI 0.065 10.635 1.067(1.026–1.109) 0.001 Multiparity 0.898 23.22 2.455(1.704–3.538) < 0.001 History of PCOS 0.794 8.171 2.211(1.283–3.81) 0.004 Early pregnancy HbA1c 0.007 0.011 1.007(0.88–1.153) 0.916 Late pregnancy HbA1c 0.774 21.788 2.168(1.567–3.001) < 0.001 Gestational weight gain 0.071 13.917 1.073(1.034–1.114) < 0.001 Newborn sex/female 0.452 6.473 1.571(1.109–2.225) 0.011 Open in a new tab BMI body mass index , PCOS polycystic ovary syndrome, HbA1c glycosylated hemoglobin, aOR adjusted odds ratio, CI confidence interval Multivariable logistic regression analysis of the relationship between delivering LGA infants and pregnancy outcomes in patients with T2DM Three models were used to explore the relationship between LGA and cesarean section, premature rupture of membranes, postpartum hemorrhage, shoulder dystocia, admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, and neonatal hypoglycemia, as shown in Table 4 . Table 4. Multivariable logistic regression analysis of the relationship between delivering LGA and pregnancy outcomes in patients with T2DM Unadjusted crude OR (95%CI) Model1 aOR (95%CI) Model2 aOR (95%CI) Model3 aOR (95%CI) Cesarean section 1.856(1.342–2.568) 1.677(1.199–2.345) 1.272(0.883–1.833) 1.274(0.883–1.838) PROM 0.574(0.394–0.837) 0.620(0.422–0.912) 0.671(0.444–1.013) 0.680(0.449–1.028) Postpartum hemorrhage 1.693(1.109–2.587) 1.686(1.091–2.608) 1.872(1.171–2.992) 1.785(1.113–2.863) Shoulder dystocia 5.265(1.680–16.503) 6.940(2.122–22.696) 6.046(1.767–20.681) 5.898(1.716–20.271) NICU admission 1.696(1.211–2.375) 1.727(1.221–2.441) 1.542(1.061–2.242) 1.555(1.067–2.266) Neonatal hypoglycemia 2.189(1.443–3.319) 2.047(1.335–3.139) 1.991(1.250–3.170) 2.034(1.272–3.250) Open in a new tab Model 1 adjusted for pre-existing risk factors, including pre-pregnancy BMI, history of PCOS, and multiparity. Model 2 adjusted for all risk factors in Model 1, as well as for gestational weight gain and late pregnancy HbA1c. Model 3 adjusted for all risk factors in Model 2, as well as for newborn sex PROM premature rupture of membranes, NICU neonatal intensive care unit, aOR adjusted odds ratio, CI confidence interval Without adjusting for any risk factors, LGA increased the risk of cesarean section (OR: 1.856, 95%CI: 1.342–2.568), postpartum hemorrhage (OR: 1.693, 95%CI: 1.109–2.587), shoulder dystocia (OR: 5.265, 95%CI: 1.680–16.503), admission to the neonatal intensive care unit (OR: 1.696, 95%CI: 1.211–2.375), and neonatal hypoglycemia (OR: 2.189, 95%CI: 1.443–3.319), while reducing the risk of premature rupture of membranes (OR: 0.574, 95%CI: 0.394–0.837). Model 1 adjusted for pre-existing risk factors, including pre-pregnancy BMI, history of PCOS, and multiparity. The results showed that LGA increased the risk of cesarean section (OR: 1.677, 95%CI: 1.199–2.345), postpartum hemorrhage (OR: 1.686, 95%CI: 1.091–2.608), shoulder dystocia (OR: 6.940, 95%CI: 2.122–22.696), admission to the neonatal intensive care unit (OR: 1.727, 95%CI: 1.221–2.441), and neonatal hypoglycemia (OR: 2.047, 95%CI: 1.335–3.139), while reducing the risk of premature rupture of membranes (OR: 0.620, 95%CI: 0.422–0.912). Model 2 adjusted for all risk factors in Model 1, as well as for gestational weight gain and late pregnancy HbA1c. The results showed that LGA increased the risk of postpartum hemorrhage (OR: 1.872, 95%CI: 1.171–2.992), shoulder dystocia (OR: 6.046, 95%CI: 1.767–20.681), admission to the neonatal intensive care unit (OR: 1.542, 95%CI: 1.061–2.242), and neonatal hypoglycemia (OR: 1.991, 95%CI: 1.250–3.170); however, there was no statistically significant difference in the increased risk of cesarean section (OR: 1.272, 95%CI: 0.883–1.833) and premature rupture of membranes (OR: 0.671, 95%CI: 0.444–1.013). Model 3 adjusted for all risk factors in Model 2, as well as for newborn sex. The results showed that LGA increased the risk of postpartum hemorrhage (OR: 1.785, 95%CI: 1.113–2.863), shoulder dystocia (OR: 5.898, 95%CI: 1.716–20.271), admission to the neonatal intensive care unit (OR: 1.555, 95%CI: 1.067–2.266), and neonatal hypoglycemia (OR: 2.034, 95%CI: 1.272–3.250); however, there was no statistically significant difference in the increased risk of cesarean section (OR: 1.274, 95%CI: 0.883–1.838) and premature rupture of membranes (OR: 0.680, 95%CI: 0.449–1.028). Discussion A nationwide cohort study in the UK, which included pregnancy data from 172 obstetric clinics over 5 years, found that the proportion of LGA infants was 52.2% in patients with type 1 diabetes and 26.2% in patients with T2DM. In this study, the proportion of LGA infants in T2DM patients was 37.19% [ 8 ]. The risk of delivering LGA infants is high in diabetic patients, but the exact mechanism of excessive fetal intrauterine weight gain is not yet clear. This excessive growth may be related to maternal and genetic factors. LGA significantly increases the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes in the short term and metabolic diseases in the long term. Therefore, identifying the risk factors for LGA in T2DM patients and managing them during the perinatal period is crucial. Pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI) is closely related to neonatal weight, and the risk of delivering LGA infants is higher in obese pregnant women [ 16 ]. A meta-analysis of 86 studies involving more than 20 million pregnant women examined the relationship between pre-pregnancy BMI and pregnancy outcomes. The results showed that pre-pregnancy obesity was associated with an increased risk of LGA (OR: 2.36, 95%CI: 2.17–2.56) [ 17 ]. Excessive gestational Weight gain is another risk factor for delivering LGA infants. A systematic review involving more than 1.3 million pregnant women found that excessive gestational weight gain increased the risk of LGA (OR: 1.85, 95%CI: 1.76–1.95) [ 18 ]. The results of this study are consistent with the above studies. In T2DM patients, pre-pregnancy BMI and excessive gestational weight gain are associated with an increased risk of LGA. Therefore, strengthening weight management before pregnancy and guiding adequate weight gain during pregnancy in T2DM patients may reduce the occurrence of LGA. With the full implementation of the two-child policy and the improvement of living standards, on one hand, the proportion of multiparous women has increased, and on the other hand, the incidence of PCOS has been rising. Previous studies have confirmed that multiparity is one of the risk factors for LGA. A study by Wei Zheng et al., which included singleton pregnancies delivered at Beijing Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital in 2011, 2014, and 2017 (with 11,132, 13,167, and 4,973 cases respectively), found that multiparity was a risk factor in all three years, with attributable risk percentages of 3.5%, 6.3%, and 15.9%, respectively [ 19 ]. Additionally, neonatal weight increases in PCOS patients. A meta-analysis of 59 studies, including 13,378 PCOS patients and 49,395 non-PCOS patients, found that the proportion of macrosomia in PCOS patients was 9.84%, significantly higher than in the control group [ 20 ]. This study similarly found that multiparous women and PCOS patients are high-risk groups for delivering LGA infants among T2DM patients. Therefore, attention should be paid to the weight management of multiparous women and PCOS patients. This study found that both early and late pregnancy HbA1c levels were higher in the LGA group than in the non-LGA group (both P < 0.05). When pre-pregnancy BMI, multiparity, history of PCOS, early pregnancy HbA1c, late pregnancy HbA1c, gestational weight gain, and newborn sex (female) were included as independent variables and LGA as the dependent variable in a multivariable logistic regression, it was found that late pregnancy HbA1c was an independent risk factor for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients, but early pregnancy HbA1c was not statistically significant for the risk of LGA. It should be noted that although early pregnancy blood glucose was not statistically significant for the risk of LGA after multivariable logistic regression, this may be due to collinearity between early and late pregnancy HbA1c. Therefore, the importance of early pregnancy blood glucose management in controlling the occurrence of LGA should not be overlooked. Studies have suggested that in addition to blood glucose control in mid to late pregnancy, blood glucose management during the perinatal period and early pregnancy is also crucial for preventing LGA [ 21 ]. Overall, good blood glucose control before pregnancy and during pregnancy is important for reducing the risk of LGA in T2DM patients. Fetal sex is another important factor influencing fetal weight. This study found that the proportion of LGA infants was higher in female fetuses among T2DM patients. A population-based study in New South Wales from 1994 to 2005, which included 952,671 full-term singleton newborns, found that female newborns had a higher risk of LGA than male newborns (OR: 1.03, 95%CI: 1.02–1.04) [ 22 ]. Another retrospective study involving 3,705 newborns of type 1 diabetic patients also found that the birth weight of female newborns was higher than that of male newborns ( p < 0.01) [ 23 ]. Previous studies on umbilical cord blood insulin levels in newborns of non-diabetic mothers indicated that although the weight of full-term and preterm female newborns was lower than that of male newborns, the insulin levels in the umbilical cord blood of female newborns were significantly higher than those of male newborns, and the body fat percentage was higher in female newborns. It is speculated that female newborns may have insulin resistance [ 24 ]. It is unclear whether female newborns of T2DM patients have more severe insulin resistance and whether there are sex differences in insulin levels in umbilical cord blood, but this may be why the incidence of LGA is higher in female newborns of T2DM patients. Additionally, some studies suggest that the risk of LGA is higher in male newborns. Further research is needed to confirm the impact of fetal sex on birth weight in different populations [ 25 ]. Moreover, some studies suggest that race and genetic factors may also influence the occurrence of LGA [ 26 , 27 ]. This study found that delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients increased the risk of maternal cesarean section and postpartum hemorrhage, as well as the incidence of neonatal shoulder dystocia, neonatal hypoglycemia, and admission to the neonatal intensive care unit. These findings are consistent with previous studies. Research suggests that newborns delivered by diabetic mothers exhibit disproportionate growth with increased birth weight. Compared to LGA infants delivered by non-diabetic mothers, LGA infants delivered by diabetic mothers have higher chest-to-head and shoulder-to-head ratios. This disproportionate weight gain increases the risk of shoulder dystocia and birth injuries [ 28 , 29 ]. Strengths and limitations This study included data from 2012 to 2020, comprehensively collecting patients''demographic information, prenatal examination records, and pregnancy outcomes. Detailed analyses were conducted to identify various potential risk factors and their impact on pregnancy outcomes. However, as a retrospective cohort study, it may be subject to selection bias and information bias, and it cannot establish causal relationships. Despite spanning eight years and including 691 patients, the sample size is still relatively limited. Additionally, the study was conducted at a single center, which may limit the generalizability of the findings to other populations or regions. Furthermore, lifestyle factors such as diet, physical activity, and adherence to interventions were not systematically recorded and thus could not be included in the analysis, which may affect the comprehensiveness of the risk assessment. Overall, this study provides a detailed analysis of the risk factors for delivering LGA infants and the pregnancy outcomes in women with T2DM. However, future prospective, multi-center studies with larger sample sizes and more comprehensive data, including lifestyle variables, are warranted to validate and extend these findings. Conclusion In summary, the risk of delivering LGA infants is higher in T2DM patients compared to the general population. Therefore, it is important to identify the risk factors for delivering LGA infants in T2DM patients and implement scientific medical and nutritional interventions to reduce the incidence of adverse pregnancy outcomes. Strengthening weight management before pregnancy, guiding adequate weight gain during pregnancy, and maintaining good glycemic control are important measures for T2DM patients to avoid delivering LGA infants and adverse pregnancy outcomes. Acknowledgements We sincerely thank all the patients and their families for their cooperation and participation in this research. Authors’ contributions Conceptualization, Xin Ya