ero eradicates trousil phenformin victorino barroso assuncao akm pgc s13277 houtkooper vaz cardiolipin s00018 s12943 tsaur rutz makarevic s00345 soong chemotactic buserelin nakada saatcioglu lkb1 jci82152 s13058 samperio calmette monocytic thp arcmed anshita ravichandiran intimp toi saji chemoattractant nature10138 bonapace coissieux wyckoff nature13862 loberg gr1 s216146 falabella cardiolipin arellano nardello rataj szunerits jitc jitc jitc jitc jitc jitc jitc jitc jitc s005 jitc','Abstract Objective Endometrial cancer (EC) is a female malignancy closely linked to metabolic dysregulation. Most patients with EC exhibit poor responses to immunotherapy, underscoring the need to identify novel therapeutic targets at the intersection of metabolism and immune regulation. Methods In vitro: integrated proteomics, CUT&Tag (cleavage under targets and tag mentation) sequencing, dual-luciferase reporter assays, lipidomic profiling, and macrophage-tumor co-culture systems collectively demonstrated estrogen-related receptor (ERR) α’s dual metabolic-immunomodulatory role in KLE and HEC-1A human cell lines. Patient-derived organoids were used to validate the therapeutic efficacy of ERRα targeting. In vivo, the KLE cell xenograft model was used to evaluate tumorigenicity and therapeutic efficacy in mice. In humans, a retrospective cohort of 166 patients with EC was analyzed by immunohistochemistry (IHC) to quantify ERRα expression and macrophage infiltration, establishing clinical correlations and therapeutic implications. Spatial analysis of M2 macrophages in EC progression was performed using multiplex IHC. Results In EC cells, ERRα transcriptionally upregulates protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1 through direct promoter binding (-624 to −609 bp). This interaction promotes cardiolipin biosynthesis, thereby stabilizing mitochondrial inner membrane ultrastructure, enhancing oxidative phosphorylation activity, and elevating reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels. Subsequently, ROS activates the NF-κB signaling axis, inducing CCL2 secretion to recruit M2 macrophages into the tumor microenvironment. Importantly, combined inhibition of ERRα (using XCT790) and CCL2 (using carlumab) significantly enhanced antitumor efficacy in EC. Additionally, ERRα expression in EC tissues may serve as a clinical indicator for disease evaluation. Conclusions This study uncovers a pivotal role of the ERRα metabolic axis in reshaping the EC immune microenvironment, providing the mechanistic evidence linking mitochondrial lipid metabolism to macrophage-driven immunosuppression. Our findings establish a theoretical foundation for developing combination therapies targeting metabolic-immune crosstalk, offering a strategy to overcome immunotherapy resistance in EC. Keywords: Macrophage, Mitochondria, Cytokine WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC. WHAT THIS STUDY ADDS We presently demonstrate that protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1 activation in EC is directly regulated by ERRα, which binds to its promoter (−624 to –609 bp) to drive transcription. This induces cardiolipin-dependent mitochondrial reprogramming, stabilizes mitochondrial inner membrane ultrastructure, enhances oxidative phosphorylation, and increases reactive oxygen species. The resulting metabolic transformation activates NF-κB/CCL2 signaling, recruits tumor-promoting M2 macrophages, and establishes an immunosuppressive microenvironment. Critically, our findings identify ERRα and CCL2 as synergistic therapeutic targets in EC, as validated by the enhanced efficacy of combined XCT790/carlumab inhibition. HOW THIS STUDY MIGHT AFFECT RESEARCH, PRACTICE OR POLICY This study uncovers the role of ERRα in linking mitochondrial lipid metabolism to macrophage-driven immunosuppression. It paves the way for new targeted antitumor metabolism and immunotherapy, which will be beneficial for the patients with EC. Introduction The overall incidence of endometrial cancer (EC) has increased by 132% over the past three decades, with a trend towards younger age at diagnosis. 1 Patients with early-stage EC (Federation International of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage I and stage II) exhibit a 5-year survival rate of 74%–91%, while those with stage III disease show a 5-year survival rate of 57%–66%. Notably, the 5-year survival rate is only 20%–26% among patients with stage IV disease. 2 Previous studies have demonstrated that estrogen-related receptor α (ERRα/ESRRA) is highly expressed in EC 3 and modulates lipid reprogramming to promote EC invasion and metastasis. 4 Moreover, ERRα transcriptionally regulates mitochondrial metabolic pathways, including oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS), the tricarboxylic acid cycle and β-oxidation of fatty acids. 5 Cardiolipins (CLs) are important lipid components of the inner mitochondrial membrane that play critical roles in metabolic processes, including mitochondrial electron transport chain and OXPHOS. 6 Protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1 (PTPMT1) is a tyrosine phosphatase in mitochondria that is important for regulating mitochondrial morphology and metabolism by controlling CL biosynthesis. 7 The aberrant expression of PTPMT1 has been observed in a variety of cancer types, including breast cancer 8 , lung cancer 9 , hepatocellular carcinoma 10 , and pancreatic cancer. 11 High PTPMT1 expression levels were correlated with advanced tumor stage, aggressive tumor phenotypes, and poor prognosis in patients with cancer. 8 10 In addition, a previous study showed ERRα upregulation enhances CL synthesis in breast cancer cells. 12 Another study reported that mitochondrial autophagy induced by CL toxicity can significantly inhibit the occurrence of pancreatic cancer, lung cancer and colon cancer. 13 However, the relationship between ERRα and PTPMT1 remains unclear, and whether ERRα regulates CL via PTPMT1 and thus affects mitochondrial function in EC is unknown. Recently, ERRα was found to be directly associated with tumor metabolism and immunity. 14 Inhibiting ERRα can kill tumor cells by suppressing energy metabolism and activating two immune mechanisms: (1) cytokine induction, which causes proinflammatory macrophage polarization, and (2) antigen-presentation stimulation, which promotes the recruitment of CD8+T cells into tumors. 14 EC is a “cold tumor”, with little T-cell infiltration. Ren et al reported that the proportion of cytotoxic CD8 T cells was significantly lower in endometrioid adenocarcinoma samples than in normal samples via single-cell transcriptome analysis. 15 However, it has been reported that the number of macrophages rather than CD8+T cells increases in all pathologic EC types, including endometrioid adenocarcinoma, uterine serous carcinomas and uterine clear cell carcinomas. 16 In addition, treatment with immune checkpoint inhibitors targeting the programmed cell death protein-1 (PD-1) receptor plays a limited role in patients with advanced and/or EC with microsatellite instability-high. Combining immunotherapy with chemotherapy has been shown to yield better clinical efficacy. 17 Macrophages and lymphocytes account for a large proportion of immune cells in EC, with M2 macrophages being predominant. 15 18 M2 macrophages promote tumor angiogenesis, epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT), immune evasion and metastasis by secreting cytokines and acting synergistically with other immune cells. 19 , 21 Research has highlighted a range of immune phenomena resulting from the modulation of ERRα in macrophages, such as polarization and cytokine secretion. 22 However, studies on the regulation of M2 macrophages by ERRα in EC are still limited. This study aimed to investigate how ERRα in EC cells (ECCs) promotes M2 macrophage infiltration by regulating mitochondrial OXPHOS. Elucidating the mechanisms underlying ERRα-mediated metabolic and immune regulation in EC may provide novel therapeutic strategies to improve the prognosis of patients with EC. Materials and methods Cell lines and cell culture Human KLE (American Type Culture Collection, Maryland, USA), HEC-1A (National Collection of Authenticated Cell Cultures, Shanghai, China) and THP-1 (National Collection of Authenticated Cell Cultures, Shanghai, China) cells were cultured in DMEM/F12 or McCoy’s 5A medium, both of which contained 1% penicillin-streptomycin (#15140122; Gibco) and 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS) (#10091148; Gibco, New Zealand), at 37°C in a moist atmosphere (5% CO 2 , 95% air). All the cell lines were verified via short tandem repeat DNA profiling. The regulation of ERRα and PTPMT1 was achieved by constructing lentiviral vectors and infecting cells. The lentiviral vector LV-sh-ESRRA (named siERRα) was synthesized by GeneChem (Shanghai, China). The lentiviral vector used to overexpress ERRα was named ovERRα. The lentiviral vector LV-sh-PTPMT1 (named siPTPMT1) was synthesized by GeneChem (Shanghai, China). The lentiviral vector used to overexpress PTPMT1 was ovPTPMT1. The negative control vector was purchased from GeneChem (Shanghai, China). Xenograft model Female BALB/c nude mice (aged 4–5 weeks) were purchased from Zhejiang Weitong Lihua Experimental Animal Technology (Charles River). The ovERRα, siERRα, ovPTPMT1, siPTPMT1, and normal control (NC) KLE cells were generated via lentiviral transduction and subsequently injected into nude mice to establish a KLE cell line-derived xenograft (CDX) model for analyzing subcutaneous tumor formation. For the treatment group, the KLE cell line was transplanted into the forelimb armpit of female BALB/c mice. The mice were injected intraperitoneally with physiological saline, carlumab (20 mg/kg), XCT790 (4 mg/kg) or carlumab (20 mg/kg) + XCT790 (4 mg/kg). The tumor size was measured every 3 days, and the tumor volume was calculated using the following formula: tumor volume=(length×width 2 )/2. At the end of the study, the mice were anesthetized and euthanized. Tumor tissues were carefully removed for subsequent analyses. The study procedure was approved by the Experimental Animal Ethics Committee of Fujian Medical University (No. FJMU IACUC 2021–0451). Participants and specimens This retrospective study included 218 patients who underwent an initial hysterectomy for EC with immunohistochemical pathology at Fujian Provincial Maternity and Children’s Hospital from 2013 to 2018. The exclusion criteria were as follows: patients who were receiving chemotherapy, radiotherapy or immunotherapy before surgery (n=31); patients with other malignant tumors (n=11); and patients without sufficient cancerous tissue to obtain a pathological wax-embedded mass (n=10). After applying these criteria, 166 patients were ultimately included in this study. Pathological and clinical data were obtained from the hospital. Additionally, we enrolled six female donors who provided peripheral venous blood for establishing in vitro peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC) and EC tumor cell co-culture systems to perform transwell migration assays. This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of Fujian Maternal and Child Health Hospital (No. 2023KY117). Bioinformatics data analysis ESRRA, CD11b, CD68, CD80, CD86, CD115, and CD163 expression levels and clinical information for the EC cohort were obtained from the UCSC Xena database and The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) database ( https://portal.gdc.cancer.gov/ ). 23 ESRRA and CD163 expression levels and clinical data for the EC cohort were obtained from the UCSC Xena database and TCGA database. The data from TCGA used in this analysis were derived from the comprehensive cancer genomics project of the TCGA program, which has been widely used in cancer research for molecularly characterizing over 20,000 primary cancer and matched normal samples spanning 33 cancer types since its initiation in 2006 by the National Cancer Institute and the National Human Genome Research Institute. For more detailed information regarding the TCGA data, please refer to the official website of the TCGA program ( https://www.cancer.gov/ccg/research/genome-sequencing/tcga ). Patients in the TCGA EC cohort were divided into low-expression and high-expression groups based on CD115, CD163, or ESRRA expression levels, with subsequent survival analysis performed for these groups. We selected the top 150 genes related to ESRRA or PTPMT1 in the ARCHS4 database for the Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathway analysis. We selected pathways that were associated with both genes and calculated a comprehensive score. The pathway with the highest score was selected for further study. ELISA assay CCL2, fractalkine, and eotaxin levels in ECC culture supernatant from different groups were measured using ELISA kits (RK00052, RK10110, RK10020, ABclonal, Wuhan, China) following the manufacturer’s instructions. Three independent experiments were conducted, each with three technical replicates. Use the same method to detect the levels of CCL2 in the serum of mice in different groups (RK00381, ABclonal, Wuhan, China). Each group contained five mice, with three technical replicates conducted per mouse. Macrophage chemotaxis assay THP-1 cells were seeded in the upper chamber of a 6-well transwell culture chamber with an 8 µm pore size. Culture medium containing 100 ng/mL phorbol ester (Phorbol-12-myristate-13-acetate, PMA) was added and incubated with the cells for 24 hours to induce the production of M0 macrophages. In parallel, peripheral blood (20 mL) was collected from six healthy adult female donors into anticoagulant tubes, respectively. PBMCs were isolated according to the SepMate protocol, counted, and seeded in 6-well plates at a density of 4×10⁶ cells/well. Following overnight incubation, the supernatant was discarded and replaced with Roswell Park Memorial Institute (RPMI)-1640 medium containing 10% FBS and 20 ng/mL Macrophage Colony Stimulating Factor (M-CSF) to induce monocyte-to-macrophage differentiation. Medium was refreshed every 3 days. After 6 days of culture, adherent cells were confirmed as M0 macrophages. Two source-derived M0 macrophages were subsequently cultured in medium supplemented with lipopolysaccharide (100 ng/mL) and interferon gamma (20 ng/mL) for 48 hours to induce M1 polarization. M0-type macrophages were cultured in medium supplemented with interleukin-4 (20 ng/mL) and interleukin-13 (20 ng/mL) for 48 hours to induce M2 polarization. The upper chamber of a new 6-well plate was filled with macrophages (#3428, Corning, New York, USA). ECCs were seeded in the lower chamber and cultured in a 37°C cell culture incubator for 24 hours. During drug treatment, the ECCs were treated with 5 mM N-acetyl-L-cysteine or 5 µg/mL carlumab for 24 hours. The transwell chamber was removed, and the cells in the upper chamber were gently removed with a cotton swab. Then, the cells that had migrated were fixed with 4% paraformaldehyde for 20–30 min. The cells were washed three times with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS), stained with 0.1% crystal violet for 5–10 min, then rinsed three times with PBS. After complete drying in air, five fields of view were selected for observation, and the cells were counted under a microscope (Olympus, Tokyo, Japan). Three independent experiments were conducted. Cytokine array Supernatants from ECCs cultured under different experimental conditions (KLE -NC , KLE -ovERRα , KLE -ovPTPMT1 , and HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 ) were filtered through 0.2 µm filters (Sartorious Stedium Biotech). The filtered media were then incubated with reagents from a Human Cytokine Antibody Array Kit (AAH-CYT-G5, RayBiotech). The intensity of each spot was measured using ImageJ and analyzed with the Ray Biotech analysis tool (AAH-ANG-1000, RayBiotech). Heatmaps were generated via the ‘‘Heatmap.2’’ function of the R package. Each experimental group included four technical replicates. Intracellular reactive oxygen species detection Intracellular reactive oxygen species (ROS) formation was assessed using a DCFDA/H2DCFDA Cellular ROS Assay Kit (ab113851; Abcam, USA) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. The fluorescence intensity in cells (KLE -NC , KLE -ovERRα , KLE -siERRα , KLE -ovPTPMT1 , KLE -siPTPMT1 , and HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -siERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 , HEC-1A -siPTPMT1 ) was analyzed using two measurement methods: flow cytometry (BD LSRFortessa, USA) and laser scanning confocal microscopy (Leica TCS SP8, Germany). For flow cytometry analysis, three independent experiments were performed, each with three technical replicates. For confocal microscopy analysis, five randomly selected fields of view per group were examined across three independent experiments. Mitochondrial cardiolipin assay 10-N-nonyl acridine orange (45 µM) was added to the ECCs, which were incubated for 15 min at 37°C. Cells were washed two times with 10 mM Tris/HCl (pH=7). The red fluorescence of 10-N-nonyl acridine orange bound to the ECCs was measured at 640 nm. High-magnification images (1,000×) were captured using a laser scanning confocal microscope, and the fluorescence intensity was analyzed by ImageJ. For each experimental group, five fields of view were randomly selected and analyzed across three independent experiments. Lipidomic Liquid chromatography (LC)-mass spectrometry analyses were performed using a high-performance LC system (Series 1260; Agilent Technologies, USA) coupled with a triple quadrupole mass spectrometer (Agilent 6460; Agilent Technologies, USA). ECC lines (KLE -NC , KLE -ovERRα , KLE -ovPTPMT1 , and HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 ) were seeded at a density of 1×10⁵ cells/mL and cultured until 80% confluence. Cells were washed three times with ice-cold PBS to remove serum lipids, detached using 0.25% trypsin-EDTA, and pelleted by centrifugation at 300×g for 5 min at 4°C. Cell pellets were re-suspended in 1 mL ice-cold PBS, and viable cells were counted using trypan blue exclusion on a hemocytometer. Cell suspensions were adjusted to 10⁷ cells/mL, and 1.5 mL of each sample was homogenized in 1.5 mL of chloroform/methanol (2:1, v/v) containing 0.01% butylated hydroxytoluene to prevent lipid oxidation. Samples were vortexed for 1 min, sonicated on ice for 30 s using a probe sonicator (30% amplitude), and centrifuged at 3,000 rpm for 10 min. The supernatant was transferred to a clean glass tube, and the organic phase was washed with 800 µL of 0.1 M KCl. The lower organic layer was collected, gently dried under a stream of nitrogen, and stored at −80°C until analysis. Prior to analysis, dried samples were reconstituted in 200 µL of an isopropanol/methanol solution (1:1, v/v). Quality control samples were prepared by pooling equal volumes of all experimental samples. For targeted metabolomic analyses, multiple reaction monitoring transitions representing the metabolites were simultaneously monitored using positive/negative polarity switching. Data were analyzed according to the standard operating procedures provided by Shanghai Applied Protein Technology. There are six groups (KLE -NC , KLE -ovERRα , KLE -ovPTPMT1 , and HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 ) of cells to be tested, each group containing six biological replicates. Tandem mass tag labeling proteomics The total proteins were extracted from KLE cells treated with or without 10 µM XCT790 for 24 hours and evaluated via sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis and staining. Briefly, the cells were first lysed. Sequencing-grade modified trypsin (Promega, Madison, Wisconsin, USA) was used to digest the proteins, and the resulting peptide mixture was labeled with a TMT kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). After desalination, high-pH HPLC was used for separation. The protein fractions were subsequently analyzed via online electrospray tandem mass spectrometry. Differentially expressed proteins were selected according to the fold changes and p values. Western blotting Standard techniques were used for protein quantification, separation, transfer, and blotting of lysates from KLE and HEC-1A cells. Primary antibodies against the following proteins were used: ERRα (1:500, ab76228, Abcam, London, UK), PTPMT1 (1:500; 11493–1-AP, Proteintech, Wuhan, China), CCL2 (1:500, ab214819, Abcam, London, UK), NF-κB1 p50 (1:1000, 12540S, CST, USA), NF-κB1 p65 (1:1000, 8242S, CST, USA) and GAPDH (1:2000; YM3029, AB_2692314, Immunoway, USA). GAPDH served as a loading control. The gray value analysis was performed using ImageJ, and GraphPad Prism was used for statistical analysis and graphing. Three independent experiments were conducted. Reverse transcription-quantitative polymerase chain reaction (RT-qPCR) ECCs (KLE and HEC-1A) were collected after gene expression regulation or drug treatment (specific grouping is described in the Results section). Cells were lysed directly in a culture dish for RNA extraction. After thorough vortexing, the lysates were centrifuged and the supernatant was transferred to a new tube. Then, 0.25 mL of chloroform was added to each 1 mL of RNA extraction mixture. Following centrifugation, the upper aqueous phase was transferred to a fresh tube. The RNA was precipitated with isopropyl alcohol. The RNA was washed with 75% ethanol. First-strand complementary DNA was synthesized using PCR Master Mix (#A2791, Promega, Madison, USA). PCR amplification was performed using Eastep QPCR Master Mix (#LS2062; Promega, Madison, USA). Relative gene expression was calculated using the 2 −ΔΔCt method, with GAPDH serving as the reference gene. Three independent experiments were conducted, each with three technical replicates. Cell Mito Stress Test The oxygen consumption rate was determined using a Seahorse XF Cell Mito Stress Test Kit (#103015–100; Agilent Technologies, USA). Two cell lines (KLE and HEC-1A) after gene expression regulation (KLE -NC , KLE -ovERRα , KLE -siERRα , KLE -ovPTPMT1 , KLE -siPTPMT1 , KLE -ovERRα+ siPTPMT1 , and HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -siERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 , HEC-1A -siPTPMT1 , HEC-1A -ovERRα+ siPTPMT1 ) were seeded at 4×10 ∧ 4 cells/well in a 24-well plate. Seahorse XF basic medium (#103575–100; Agilent Technologies, USA) supplemented with 1 mM pyruvate, 2 mM glutamine and 10 mM glucose was used as the detection solution. Working solutions of oligomycin, FCCP and Rot/AA were prepared and subsequently added to the wells of the plate. The cell growth medium was replaced with warm detection solution, and the cells were cultured in a CO 2 -free incubator at 37°C for 1 hour. Seahorse XF24 (Agilent Technologies, USA) was subsequently run on the computer, and the data were analyzed. Three independent experiments were conducted, each with three technical replicates. Transmission electron microscopy Cell pellets (HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -siERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 , HEC-1A -siPTPMT1 ) were collected, resuspended in 0.1 M phosphate buffer (PB, pH 7.4), and fixed with transmission electron microscopy (TEM) fixative (2.5% glutaraldehyde and 2% paraformaldehyde in 0.1 M PB) for 2–4 hours at 4°C. After centrifugation at 3,000×g for 10 min, the supernatant was discarded, and the pellet was washed three times with 0.1 M PB (10 min each wash). A 1% low-melting-point agarose solution was prepared in 0.1 M PB at 65°C, cooled to 45°C, and the cell precipitate was rapidly suspended in 100 µL of the warm agarose solution. The mixture was immediately transferred to a 4°C cold plate to solidify. The agarose-embedded pellet was cut into 1 mm³ cubes and post-fixed with 1% osmium tetroxide in 0.1 M PB at room temperature for 2 hours in the dark. Samples were then dehydrated through a graded ethanol series: 30%, 50%, 70%, 90%, and 100% ethanol (15 min each), followed by two 15 min incubations in 100% acetone. Resin penetration was performed by sequential incubation in 1:1 acetone: EMbed 812 resin (2 hours), 1:3 acetone: resin (overnight), and pure EMbed 812 resin (3 changes of 4 hours each). Samples were embedded in fresh resin and polymerized at 37°C for 24 hours, followed by 65°C for 48 hours. Ultrathin sections (60–80 nm) were cut using a diamond knife on an ultramicrotome, collected on 200-mesh copper grids, and stained with 2% uranyl acetate (15 min) and lead citrate (5 min). Sections were observed under a transmission electron microscope (JEOL JEM-1400 Plus) operating at 80 kV, and images were captured using a Gatan Orius SC1000 camera and analyzed with Digital Micrograph software. For each experimental group, five fields of view were randomly selected and analyzed across three independent experiments. Luciferase reporter assays Using the UCSC database, we found that ESRRA can bind to the promoter region of PTPMT1 and transcribe it. The JASPAR database ( https://jaspar.genereg.net/analysis ) was used to identify potential binding sites in the PTPMT1 promoter region. The PTPMT1 promoter sequence relative to the transcription start site was amplified by PCR and inserted into the pGL3-basic plasmid. Four PTPMT1 promoter fragments with gradually decreasing lengths were ligated into the pGL3-basic plasmid: PTPMT1-15 (−1438 to +24), PTPMT1-16 (−1123 to +24), PTPMT1-18 (−724 to +24) and PTPMT1-17 (−549 to +24). The potential binding sequence 3′-GTTCACCCTGG-5′ was mutated and named PTPMT1-1-MUT. The potential binding sequence 3′-CCTGCCCTAGGACAGG-5′ was mutated and named PTPMT1-2-MUT. HEK-293T cells were co-transfected in 24-well plates. After 48 hours, firefly and Renilla luciferase activities were measured using a Dual-Lite Luciferase Assay System (E1910; Promega, Beijing, China) and a microplate reader (Gen1), and the firefly/Renilla luciferase activity ratio was calculated. Cleavage under targets and tag mentation, library preparation, sequencing and data analysis CUT&Tag (cleavage under targets and tag mentation) (N259-YH01; Novoprotein, Jiangsu, China) assays were performed using the standard technique. Briefly, the cells were bound to concanavalin A-coated magnetic beads, and the cell membranes were permeabilized with digitonin. The enzyme pA-Tn5 transposase precisely binds to the DNA sequence near the target protein under antibody guidance and results in factor-targeted tag mentation. The DNA sequence was tagmented, with adapters added simultaneously at both ends, which were enriched by PCR to form sequencing-ready libraries. After PCR, the libraries were purified with AMPure beads, and the library quality was assessed on an Agilent Bioanalyzer 2100 system. After sequencing and QC, the reads were mapped to the reference genome. ChIPseeker was used to retrieve the nearest genes around the peak and annotate the genomic region of the peak. ChIPseeker was used to identify genes related to the peak. Immunohistochemical staining For the population-based studies, endometrial cancer tissues from 166 patients were analyzed. For in vivo experiments, tumor tissues were collected across experimental cohorts (including gene regulation and drug treatment groups; n=5 mice/group), as detailed in the Results section. Mice were euthanized when statistically significant differences in tumor volume were observed, and tumor tissues were collected for analysis. Immunohistochemical staining was performed according to standard procedures with the following primary antibodies: rabbit polyclonal anti-ERRα (1:100; #ab137489; Abcam, London, UK), anti-PTPMT1 (1:100; 11493–1-AP; Proteintech, Wuhan, China), anti-CCL2 (1:50; ab283927; Abcam, London, UK), anti-CD115 (1:100; 25949–1-AP; Proteintech, Wuhan, China) and anti-CD163 (1:50; ab182422; Abcam, London, UK). Three low-magnification images (100×) were randomly selected under the microscope. The scores for strong staining, moderate staining, weak staining, and negative staining were 3, 2, 1 and 0, respectively, according to the degree of positive staining. The proportion of stained cells was divided into 76%–100%, 51%–75%, 26%–50%, and 0–25%, and the corresponding scores were 4, 3, 2 and 1 points, respectively. The product of the intensity and percentage of positively stained cells was used to calculate the immunohistochemical score for each sample. The samples were divided into four groups based on their immunoreactive scores: 0, negative (−); 1–4, weakly positive (+); 5–8, positive (++); and 9–12, strongly positive (+++). For immune cells, five high-magnification images (400×) were selected and counted under a microscope. Multiplex immunohistochemistry Multiplex immunohistochemistry (mIHC) was performed on 4 μm thick formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded tumor tissue sections using a tyramide signal amplification (TSA)-based 6-color fluorescence system (abs50015-100T, Absin Bioscience, Shanghai). Following deparaffinization in xylene and rehydration through graded ethanol series, heat-induced epitope retrieval was conducted in citrate buffer (pH 6.0) at 90°C for 15 min using microwave irradiation (20% power). The staining procedure involved sequential cycles of primary antibody incubation (30 min at room temperature), Horseradish Peroxidase (HRP)-conjugated secondary antibody treatment (abs50015-02, Absin Bioscience; 10 min), and tyramide-fluorophore conjugate development (10 min), with microwave-based antibody stripping between each cycle. Antibody/fluorophore combinations included rabbit polyclonal anti-ERRα (#ab137489; Abcam, London, UK) with TSA 780, anti-PTPMT1 (11493–1-AP; Proteintech, Wuhan, China) with TSA 480, anti-CCL2 (ab283927; Abcam, London, UK) with TSA 570, anti-CD115 (25949–1-AP; Proteintech, Wuhan, China) with TSA 620 and anti-CD163 (ab182422; Abcam, London, UK) with TSA 520. Finally, nuclei were counterstained with DAPI (abs47047616, Absin Bioscience) and slides were mounted with anti-fade medium. Whole slide images were acquired using a Pannoramic MIDI II digital slide scanner (3DHISTECH) and analyzed with Indica HALO platform for quantitative assessment of fluorescence intensity and cellular localization. Organoids Fresh tumor tissues from three patients with EC were obtained surgically, rinsed thoroughly with Dulbecco''s Phosphate-Buffered Saline (DPBS) containing antibiotics, minced extensively using sterile surgical scissors, and transferred to a 15 mL conical tube containing tissue digestion solution (Dulbecco''s Modified Eagle Medium (DMEM) basal medium supplemented with collagenase, dispase, and 10 µM Y-27632). The mixture was digested with gentle agitation at 37°C until no obvious tissue pellets remained. Digestion was terminated by adding DMEM supplemented with 5% FBS. The solution was filtered through a 100 µm cell strainer to remove debris, centrifuged at 200×g for 5 min at 4°C, and the supernatant was discarded. Cell pellets were gently resuspended in organoid culture medium, mixed with Matrigel at a 1:3 (v/v) ratio, and seeded in pre-warmed 24-well plates. The plates were incubated at 37°C with 5% CO₂ for 15–30 min to allow Matrigel polymerization. After polymerization, 400 µL of organoid culture medium was added to each well for continuous culture. The medium was changed every 3–4 days, and organoid growth was regularly observed and recorded. Three independent experiments were conducted per patient, each experiment with three technical replicates. Statistics and reproducibility SPSS V.23.0 software (IBM SPSS, Armonk, New York, USA), ImageJ, and FlowJo were used for statistical analyses, and GraphPad Prism 8 and R were used to create graphs. The statistical tests used for the results presented in each figure are indicated in the figure legend. All samples represent biological replicates, and the number is mentioned in the corresponding figure legend. The experiments were repeated independently with similar results to demonstrate reproducibility. No statistical method was used to predetermine the sample size. No data were excluded from the analyses. The experiments were not randomized. The investigators were not blinded to the group allocation during the experiments or outcome assessments. Results ERRα promotes M2 macrophage infiltration and is associated with poor prognosis in patients with EC Our previous studies revealed that ERRα is strongly associated with EC progression, 4 and ERR α has been reported to be associated with macrophages. 14 Online supplemental figure 1 shows the entire research flowchart. According to previous studies, 24 25 we used CD11b and CD68 as M0 macrophage markers, CD80 and CD86 as M1 macrophage markers, and CD115 and CD163 as M2 macrophage markers. Analysis of TCGA data revealed no statistically significant differences in M0 or M1 macrophage numbers between advanced (III–IV) and early-stage (I–II) EC ( figure 1A–D ). Compared with early-stage EC tissues, advanced-stage EC tissues exhibited significantly increased numbers of M2 macrophages ( figure 1E–F ). Unexpectedly, ERRα (encoding ESRRA) expression was significantly elevated in advanced-stage versus early-stage EC tissues ( figure 1G ). Notably, patients with EC exhibiting increased M2 macrophage infiltration ( figure 1H–I ) or higher ERRα expression ( figure 1J ) demonstrated significantly poorer clinical outcomes. In addition, ERRα expression showed significant positive correlations with M2 macrophage markers CD115 and CD163, but no significant correlations with M1 macrophage markers (CD80, CD86) or M0 macrophage marker CD68 ( figure 1K–P ). To further investigate the interactions between ERRα and macrophages, we constructed a co-culture model of ECCs and macrophages ( online supplemental figure 1B ). The polarization states of macrophages, including M0, M1, and M2, were identified by reverse transcription-quantitative polymerase chain reaction (RT-qPCR) analysis of phenotype-specific marker genes ( online supplemental figure 2A-B ). While chemotaxis remained unchanged for M0 and M1 macrophages across conditions ( figure 1Q–T ), M2 macrophage chemotaxis was significantly increased when co-cultured with ERRα-overexpressing (ovERRα) ECCs compared with controls ( figure 1U–V ). Similarly, ERRα-knockdown (siERRα) in ECCs significantly reduced M2 macrophage chemotaxis ( figure 1U–V ) but had no effect on M0 or M1 macrophages ( figure 1Q–T ). Importantly, M2 macrophage co-culture did not alter ERRα expression at neither the protein nor the messenger RNA (mRNA) levels of in ECCs ( figure 1W–Y ). These findings suggest that increased ERRα expression in tumor cells is the initiating factor that promotes M2 macrophage chemotaxis, rather than the M2 macrophage infiltration inducing ERRα upregulation in tumors. Figure 1. ERRα promotes M2 macrophage infiltration and correlates with poor prognosis in EC. ( A–B ) CD11b and CD68 were used as M0 macrophage markers to evaluate M0 macrophage levels in patients with early-stage or advanced-stage EC. Data were obtained from patients with uterine corpus endometrial carcinoma (UCEC) in TCGA database. P values were calculated using Student’s t-test. The TCGA database contains 539 patients with EC, including 339 in stage I, 51 in stage II, 120 in stage III, and 29 in stage IV. ( C–D ) CD80 and CD86 were used as M1 macrophage markers to evaluate M1 macrophage levels in patients with early-stage or advanced-stage EC. Data were obtained from patients with UCEC in the TCGA database. P values were calculated using Student’s t-test. The TCGA database contains 539 patients with EC, including 339 in stage I, 51 in stage II, 120 in stage III, and 29 in stage IV. ( E–F ) CD115 and CD163 were used as M2 macrophage markers to evaluate M2 macrophage levels in patients with early-stage or advanced-stage EC. Data were obtained from patients with UCEC in TCGA database. P values were calculated using Student’s t-test. The TCGA database contains 539 patients with EC, including 339 in stage I, 51 in stage II, 120 in stage III, and 29 in stage IV. ( G ) Evaluation of the expression of ERRα in patients with early-stage or advanced-stage EC. Data were obtained from UCEC patients in the TCGA database. P values were calculated using Student’s t-test. The TCGA database contains 539 patients with EC, including 339 in stage I, 51 in stage II, 120 in stage III, and 29 in stage IV. ( H–J ) Kaplan-Meier curves depicting the overall survival of patients with UCEC (TCGA cohort) in the model group according to the CD115, CD163 and ERRα signatures. P values were calculated using the log-rank test. The TCGA database contains 539 patients with EC, including 339 in stage I, 51 in stage II, 120 in stage III, and 29 in stage IV. ( K–P ) Correlations between ERRα expression and macrophage marker expression. The data are presented as scatter plots with a linear regression line. The shaded area represents the 95% CI. Pearson’s correlation coefficients (R values) were calculated to assess the linear relationship between the variables. P values are exact and two-sided. The TCGA database contains 539 patients with EC, including 339 in stage I, 51 in stage II, 120 in stage III, and 29 in stage IV. ( Q–R ) Representative images revealing the effects of regulating ERRα expression in EC cells on M0 macrophage chemotaxis in a co-culture model (scale bar 200 µm). The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. Image analysis was performed on five random fields of view per sample. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( S–T ) Effects of regulating ERRα expression in EC cells on M1 macrophage chemotaxis in a co-culture model (scale bar 200 µm). The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. Image analysis was performed on five random fields of view per sample. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( U–V ) Effects of regulating ERRα expression in EC cells on M2 macrophage chemotaxis in a co-culture model (scale bar 200 µm). The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. Image analysis was performed on five random fields of view per sample. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( W ) RT-qPCR was used to detect the relative messenger RNA expression level of the ESRRA gene in EC cells after co-culture with M2 macrophages. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, each performed with three technical replicates. P values were calculated using Student’s t-test. ( X–Y ) Western blot was performed to detect the ERRα protein expression level in EC cells after co-culture with M2 macrophages. The samples were derived from the same experiment as those in panel W, processed in parallel. GAPDH served as a loading control. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated using Student’s t-test. ns, not significant; *p<0.05, **p<0.01, and ***p<0.001. ANOVA, analysis of variance; EC, endometrial cancer; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; TCGA, The Cancer Genome Atlas; RT-qPCR, reverse transcription-quantitative polymerase chain reaction. Open in a new tab PTPMT1 is the target gene of ERRα and is required for promoting M2 chemotaxis Quantitative proteomic analysis was conducted in KLE ECCs to explore how ERRα regulates M2 macrophage infiltration. The following criteria were applied: a p value<0.1 with fold change (FC)>1.3 for upregulated proteins and FC<0.7 for downregulated proteins. A total of 14 differentially expressed proteins were identified, including 5 upregulated (DDRGK1, TXNDC12, IFRD1, RSRC2, and APLP2), and 9 downregulated proteins (MRPS2, UCK2, PTPMT1, NUCB1, KIF20A, PPL, HMGCS1, ACSS2, and S100A6). A previous study has shown that ERRα is involved in multiple mitochondrial metabolic pathways. 5 Among the differentially expressed proteins, PTPMT1, which regulates mitochondrial morphology and metabolism by regulating CL biosynthesis 7 , is the most relevant to mitochondrial function. Therefore, we selected PTPMT1 for further investigation ( figure 2A ). Compared with the NC group, PTPMT1 mRNA and protein levels were increased in the ovERRα group but decreased in the siERRα group. However, ERRα levels showed no significant differences in either PTPMT1-overexpressing (ovPTPMT1) or PTPMT1-silenced (siPTPMT1) groups compared with NC group ( figure 2B–F ). Dual-luciferase reporter gene assay identified the ERRα binding site in the PTPMT1 promoter region between −724 bp and −549 bp ( figure 2G–H ). Within this region, we detected two potential binding sites and generated corresponding point mutants for validation. Further dual-luciferase reporter gene assays revealed a functional ERRα binding site at positions −624 bp to −609 bp, with the putative consensus sequence CCTGCCCTAGGACAGG ( figure 2I–J ). These findings were further validated by Chromatin Immunoprecipitation followed by sequencing (ChIP-seq) analysis, which demonstrated direct transcriptional binding of ERRα to the PTPMT1 promoter ( figure 2K–L ). To determine whether ERRα promotes M2 macrophage chemotaxis through PTPMT1, we investigated PTPMT1’s chemotactic effects using an M2 macrophage-ECC co-culture system. M2 macrophage chemotaxis was significantly enhanced with PTPMT1 overexpression (ovPTPMT1) and reduced on PTPMT1 knockdown (siPTPMT1) compared with the NC group. Notably, the increase in the M2 macrophage chemotactic induced by ERRα overexpression was weakened by the downregulation of PTPMT1 expression ( figure 2M–N ). Figure 2. ERRα regulates PTPMT1 to drive M2 macrophage chemotaxis. ( A ) ERRα-related proteins were identified through hierarchical clustering analysis of proteomic data. Volcano plots of the 14 proteins significantly correlated with ERRα. The x-axis is the log2 (fold change), and the y-axis is the −log10 (p value). PTPMT1 is the molecule most closely related to mitochondrial metabolism. ( B–C ) RT-qPCR analysis of ERRα or PTPMT1 protein levels in ECCs from the NC, ovERRα, and siERRα groups. GAPDH served as a loading control. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( D–F ) Western blot analysis of ERRα or PTPMT1 protein levels in ECCs from the NC, ovPTPMT1, and siPTPMT1 groups. GAPDH served as a loading control. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( G–H ) Dual-luciferase reporter gene assay identified the ERRα binding site in the PTPMT1 promoter region between −724 bp and −549 bp. Four PTPMT1 promoter fragments ligated to the pGL3-basic plasmid, named PTPMT1-15 (−1438 to +24), PTPMT1-16 (−1123 to +24), PTPMT1-18 (−724 to +24) and PTPMT1-17 (−549 to +24), were co-transfected with the ERRα transcription factor. The luciferase activity was measured and normalized to that of the Renilla luciferase control 48 hours after transfection. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( I–J ) PTPMT1-mut-1, PTPMT1-mut-2, wild-type PTPMT1 and their empty vectors were co-transfected with the ERRα transcription factor. The luciferase activity was measured and normalized to that of the Renilla luciferase control 48 hours after transfection. The binding site for ERRα in the PTPMT1 promoter is likely CCTGCCCTAGGACAGG, which is located in the promoter (−624 bp to −609 bp). The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, and p values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( K ) CUT&Tag (cleavage under targets and tag mentation) and sequencing analyses of the distribution map of ERRα peaks in functional gene regions. ( L ) PTPMT1 promoter occupancy in HEC-1A cells was evaluated. ( M–N ) Effects of regulating PTPMT1 expression in endometrial cancer cells on M2 macrophage chemotaxis in a co-culture model (scale bar 200 µm). The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown; for each experiment, five fields of view were randomly selected for microscopic counting. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ns, not significant; *p<0.05, **p<0.01, and ***p<0.001. ANOVA, analysis of variance; ECC, endometrial cancer cell; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; NC, normal control; PTPMT1, protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1; RT-qPCR, reverse transcription-quantitative polymerase chain reaction. Open in a new tab The ERRα-PTPMT1 axis activates mitochondrial OXPHOS and recruits M2 macrophages in an ROS-dependent manner To explore the pathways involved in ERRα/PTPMT1-mediated M2 macrophage chemotaxis, we identified the top 150 ESRRA-associated or PTPMT1-associated genes through Spearman correlations analysis of combined RNA sequencing and ChIP-seq datasets. Subsequent KEGG pathway analysis using the signature search (ARCHS4) database revealed OXPHOS as the top comprehensive score pathway (excluding disease pathways) for both genes ( figure 3A–B ). Based on these findings, we select OXPHOS for further investigation. ECCs overexpression or knockdown for ERRα or PTPMT1 was established and subjected to mitochondrial stress assays using a Seahorse XF24 analyzer. The results showed that ECCs overexpressing either ERRα or PTPMT1 exhibited significantly increased basal respiration, ATP production, maximal respiration, and the spare respiratory capacity ( figure 3C–F ). Conversely, ERRα or PTPMT1 knockdown in ECCs resulted in significantly decreased levels of these respiratory parameters ( figure 3C–F ). Notably, the ovERRα+siPTPMT1 group showed no significant changes in any of the measured respiratory parameters, indicating that PTPMT1 is necessary for ERRα-mediated activation of mitochondrial OXPHOS. Mitochondrial OXPHOS serves as a major source of ROS production. Consistent with this, ROS assays—including flow cytometry and laser confocal microscopy analyses in both KLE and HEC-1A cell lines—demonstrated significantly increased ROS levels in ERRα-overexpressing or PTPMT1-overexpressing ECCs groups, while showing significantly decreased levels in ERRα-knockdown or PTPMT1-knockdown ECCs groups ( figure 3G‒P ). We next investigated whether M2 macrophage chemotaxis is ROS-dependent. In the EEC-M2 macrophage co-culture system, M2 macrophage migration was significantly reduced following ROS scavenging with N-acetylcysteine (NAC) in both NC (NC+NAC) and ERRα or PTPMT1 ECCs overexpressing (ovERRα+NAC, ovPTPMT1+NAC) groups compared with their untreated NC groups ( figure 3Q‒R ). These results demonstrate that the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis promotes M2 macrophage recruitment through an ROS-dependent mechanism. Figure 3. The ERRα-PTPMT1 axis promotes M2 macrophage recruitment via ROS-dependent mitochondrial OXPHOS activation. ( A–B ) Bioinformatic analysis of pathways associated with both ERRα and PTPMT1. Among the top 15 pathways, oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS) had the highest comprehensive score (excluding disease pathways) for both genes. ( C–F ) The associations of the oxygen consumption rate (OCR), basal capacity, maximal respiration capacity, spare respiratory capacity, and ATP production regulated by the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis are shown by the Seahorse XF Cell Mito Stress Test Kit. KLE -NC and HEC-1A -NC cells were used as the control groups. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, each performed with three technical replicates. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( G–L ) The ROS levels in the different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups were analyzed using flow cytometry. KLE -NC and HEC-1A -NC cells were used as control groups. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, each performed with three technical replicates. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( M–P ) ROS levels in the different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups were analyzed using laser scanning confocal microscopy. KLE -NC and HEC-1A -NC cells were used as the control group. The data are shown as the means±SDs. For each experimental group, five fields of view were randomly selected and analyzed across three independent experiments. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( Q–R ) Effects of ROS produced in endometrial cancer cells on M2 macrophage chemotaxis in a co-culture model (scale bar 200 µm). The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown; for each experiment, five fields of view were randomly selected for microscopic counting. P values were calculated by Student’s t-test. *p<0.05 and ***p<0.001. ANOVA, analysis of variance; ECC, endometrial cancer cell; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; CL, cardiolipin; NAC, N-acetylcysteine; NC, normal control; PG, phosphatidylglycerol; PTPMT1, protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1; ROS, reactive oxygen species. Open in a new tab ERRα-mediated increase in CL levels is partially dependent on PTPMT1 Next, to investigate the regulatory effect of ERRα on CL levels — key phospholipids essential for mitochondrial structure and function 26 —we conducted lipidomic profiling in ECs across six experimental groups: KLE -NC , KLE -ovERRα , KLE -ovPTPMT1 , and HEC-1A -NC , HEC-1A -ovERRα , HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 . A total of 48 lipids were detected ( figure 4A ). The lipidomic analysis revealed that CL levels were significantly increased in both ECCs (KLE and HEC-1A) overexpressing ERRα (ovERRα) or PTPMT1 (ovPTPMT1) ( figure 4B ). Specifically, the top two CL species elevated in HEC-1A -ovERRα cells were CL (14:0/18:0/14:0/18:2) and CL (16:0/16:0/16:0/16:0), while HEC-1A -ovPTPMT1 cells showed increases in CL (14:0/18:0/14:0/18:2) and CL (16:0/16:0/16:0/16:0) ( figure 4C–D ). Similarly, KLE -ovERRα cells exhibited higher levels of CL (14:0/18:0/14:0/18:2) and CL (14:0/18:2/16:0/18:1), whereas KLE -ovPTPMT1 cells had elevated CL (14:0/18:0/16:0/18:1) and CL (16:0/16:0/16:0/16:0) ( figure 4E–F ). Finally, two CL species including CL (14:0/16:0/18:0/18:2) and CL (14:0/16:0/16:0/18:1) were significantly elevated across all ERRα-overexpressing or PTPMT1-overexpressing groups compared with the untreated NC groups ( figure 4G ). Additionally, since PTPMT1 dephosphorylates phosphatidyl glycerol phosphate (PGP) to phosphatidylglycerol (PG), 27 we also found that PG levels were significantly increased in both ECCs overexpressing ERRα (ovERRα) or PTPMT1 (ovPTPMT1) compared with the untreated NC groups ( figure 4H ). Furthermore, we observed a positive correlation between PG and CL levels across all experimental groups in both examined cell lines ( figure 4I ). Figure 4. The ERRα-PTPMT1 axis affects the CL levels and mitochondrial structure. ( A ) 36 classes of lipids in KLE and HEC-1A cells were detected using lipidomics. ( B ) The cardiolipin content in different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups was analyzed using lipidomics. KLE -NC and HEC-1A -NC cells were used as control groups. Each group contains six biological replicates. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( C–F ) The significant differences in cardiolipin subclass contents in the different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups were analyzed using lipidomics (fold change >1.2, p<0.05 and VIP>1). KLE -NC and HEC-1A -NC cells were used as control groups. ( G ) A Venn diagram shows that two CL species—CL (14:0/16:0/18:0/18:2) and CL (14:0/16:0/16:0/18:1)—were significantly elevated in all ERRα-overexpressing or PTPMT1-overexpressing groups compared with untreated NC groups. ( H ) The phosphatidylglycerol content in the different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups was analyzed using lipidomics. KLE -NC and HEC-1A -NC cells were used as control groups. Each group contains six technical replicates. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( I ) Schematic diagram of the relationship between the contents of CLs and PG in the test samples. Each group contains six technical replicates. ( J–L ) Mitochondrial cardiolipin was stained with 10-N-nonyl acridine orange. Images (scale bar 20 µm) were captured using a laser scanning confocal microscope, and the fluorescence intensity was analyzed by ImageJ. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, five fields of view were randomly selected for microscopic counting. P values were calculated using one-way ANOVA. ( M ) Observation of the mitochondrial structure in different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups using transmission electron microscopy (scale bar 2 µm). For each experimental group, five fields of view were randomly selected and analyzed across three independent experiments. *p<0.05. ANOVA, analysis of variance; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; CL, cardiolipin; NC, normal control; PG, phosphatidylglycerol; PTPMT1, protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1. Open in a new tab CL-specific 10-N-nonyl acridine orange staining and laser confocal microscopy revealed that ECCs overexpressing either ERRα (ovERRα) or PTPMT1 (ovPTPMT1) exhibited significantly enhanced CL fluorescence intensity, while those with ERRα or PTPMT1 knockdown (siERRα and siPTPMT1) showed markedly reduced CL fluorescence intensity. Additionally, we observed that while CL fluorescence intensity in the ovERRα+siPTPMT1 group remained significantly higher than in the NC group, it was significantly reduced compared with the ovERRα-only group ( figure 4J– L). These data indicate that ERRα-mediated regulation of CL biosynthesis occurs, at least in part, through PTPMT1-dependent mechanisms. In addition to affecting mitochondrial function, changes in CL levels also affect mitochondrial morphology. 26 TEM analysis of the mitochondrial morphology in HEC-1A cells revealed that, compared with the NC group, ECCs overexpressing ERRα (ovERRα) or PTPMT1 (ovPTPMT1) maintained intact inner membranes and clearly visible cristae of mitochondria. In contrast, ECCs with downregulated ERRα (siERRα) or PTPMT1 (siPTPMT1) exhibited deteriorated mitochondrial membranes, characterized by vacuoles and disrupted cristae ( figure 4M ). This phenomenon may be caused by a reduction in CL synthesis in the mitochondrial inner membrane due to the downregulation of ERRα or PTPMT1. Insufficient CL levels lead to a decrease in mitochondrial cristae integrity and their eventual breakage, resulting in changes in mitochondrial morphology and function. 26 ROS production mediated by the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis promotes CCL2 secretion via the NF-κB pathway To further investigate the mechanism by which ERRα/PTPMT1 signaling regulates macrophage chemotaxis, we performed cytokine microarray assays on ovERRα, ovPTPMT1 and control (NC) ECCs in both KLE and HEC-1A ECC lines. We found that three kinds of cytokines (fractalkine, eotaxin, and CCL2) were significantly increased in both the ovERRα and ovPTPMT1 groups compared with the NC group ( figure 5A–B ). The protein levels of fractalkine, eotaxin, and CCL2 levels were subsequently assessed using ELISA kits in all experimental groups. The results revealed that CCL2 levels were increased in both the ovERRα and ovPTPMT1 groups compared with the NC group ( figure 5C–D ). In contrast, no significant changes were observed in the levels of fractalkine or eotaxin in either ovERRα or ovPTPMT1 groups compared with the NC group (the data were not shown). Notably, after scavenging ROS with NAC, CCL2 secretion was significantly reduced in the NC+NAC, ovERRα+NAC, and ovPTPMT1+NAC groups compared with their respective untreated groups ( figure 5E–F ). Since ERRα and CCL2 lack transcriptional binding sites ( online supplemental figure 2C ), these results suggest that ERRα may regulate CCL2 secretion through ROS-mediated mechanisms. Furthermore, in the ECC-M2 macrophage co-culture system, M2 macrophage chemotaxis was significantly reduced following CCL2 blockade with carlumab in NC+carlumab, ovERRα+carlumab, and ovPTPMT1+carlumab groups compared with their respective untreated controls ( figure 5G–H ). These results confirm the essential role of CCL2 in mediating M2 macrophage chemotaxis in EC. Figure 5. Reactive oxygen species promote CCL2 secretion via the NF-κB pathway. ( A ) Human cytokine antibody array kit detected the levels of 80 cytokines in the different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups. Each experimental group included four technical replicates. ( B ) Three cytokines—fractalkine, eotaxin, and CCL2—were identified as being significantly elevated in both the ovERRα and ovPTPMT1 groups compared with the NC group (fold change >1.2, p<0.05 and VIP>1). ( C–D ) Detection of CCL2 levels in different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups using the Human CCL2 FAST ELISA Kit. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, each performed with three technical replicates. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( E–F ) The effects of ROS produced in endometrial cancer cells on CCL2 levels were determined using the Human CCL2 FAST ELISA Kit. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, each performed with three technical replicates. P values were calculated by Student’s t-test. ( G–H ) Effects of CCL2 produced in endometrial cancer cells on M2 macrophage chemotaxis in a co-culture model. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, five fields of view were randomly selected for microscopic counting (200 µm scale). P values were calculated by Student’s t-test. ( I–L ) Western blot analysis of NFκB-P65 and NFκB-P50 levels in ECCs from the NC, ovERRα, siERRα, ovPTPMT1, and siPTPMT1 groups. GAPDH served as a loading control. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( M–N ) Western blot analysis of NFκB-P65 and NFκB-P50 levels in ECCs treated with or without NAC. GAPDH served as a loading control. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated by Student’s t-test. ( O–T ) Western blot analysis showed significantly reduced CCL2 expression in ECCs following NF-κB inhibition. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA *p<0.05, **p<0.01, and ***p<0.001. ANOVA, analysis of variance; ECC, endometrial cancer cell; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; NAC, N-acetylcysteine; NC, normal control; PTPMT1, protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1. Open in a new tab Additionally, previous studies have shown that ROS regulate cellular CCL2 secretion through the NF-κB pathway. 28 Western blot analysis revealed significantly increased NF-κB expression in both the ovERRα and ovPTPMT1 groups, while NF-κB expression was markedly reduced in the siERRα and siPTPMT1 groups compared with NC groups ( figure 5I–L ). Interestingly, in NC groups, ROS scavenging with NAC significantly decreased NF-κB expression levels compared with untreated NC groups ( figure 5M‒N ). After 24-hour treatment with 10 µM BAY11-7082 (NF-κB inhibitor), it was shown significantly reduced CCL2 expression in ECCs following NF-κB inhibition ( figure 5O–T ). These findings suggest that ROS production mediated by the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis may promote CCL2 secretion through the NF-κB pathway. Combination of carlumab and XCT790 inhibits tumor growth and progression We previously constructed a KLE CDX model in tumor-bearing mice. 29 Building on this model, we generated CDX -ovERRα , CDX -siERRα , CDX -ovPTPMT1 , and CDX -siPTPMT1 model via lentiviral transduction and subcutaneously injected them into nude mice to evaluate tumorigenicity. After observing a statistically significant difference in tumor volume, euthanize the mice and harvest tumor tissues and serum for further analysis. In the CDX mouse model, tumor tissues and serum were collected following euthanasia on day 27 in the gene-regulation experiment, whereas for the drug-treatment experiment, tumor tissues and serum were obtained after euthanasia on day 29. Day 0 was defined as the day when different cells were implanted into nude mice. The results showed that tumors in the CDX -ovERRα and CDX -ovPTPMT1 groups exhibited significantly accelerated growth and larger volume compared with NC groups. Conversely, tumors in the CDX -siERRα and CDX -siPTPMT1 groups displayed markedly slower growth and reduced sizes ( figure 6A–B ). Immunohistochemical analysis of paraffin-embedded tumor sections revealed significantly upregulated ERRα, PTPMT1, CCL2 and enhanced M2 macrophage infiltration (as indicated by CD163 and CD115, figure 6C–G ) in tumor specimens from the CDX -ovERRα and CDX -ovPTPMT1 groups relative to NC groups (representative immunohistochemical images are shown in online supplemental figure 3A ). We then subcutaneously injected KLE -NC cells into nude mice to induce subcutaneous tumor formation and evaluate drug efficacy. The results showed that individual administration of XCT790 (ERRα-specific blocker) or carlumab (a CCL2-specific blocker) significantly reduced tumor volume ( figure 6H–I ), downregulated CCL2 expression, and decreased M2 macrophage infiltration (as indicated by CD115 and CD163) compared with the NC group ( figure 6J–N , online supplemental figure 3B ). Additionally, serum CCL2 levels were significantly reduced in mice treated with either carlumab or XCT790 compared with the NC group ( figure 6O ). Generally, combined treatment with carlumab and XCT790 exhibited synergistic effects, showing the smallest tumor size, the lowest CCL2 expression and M2 macrophage infiltration (CD115 and CD163), and the most pronounced reduction in serum CCL2 levels among all groups. Figure 6. Combined treatment with carlumab and XCT790 suppresses tumor growth and progression. ( A–B ) Representative images of tumors captured at the end of the study and the tumor volume in BALB/c nude mice in the different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups (day 0 was defined as the day when different cells were implanted into nude mice). CDX -NC served as the control group. n=5 mice per group. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( C–G ) The panels show the protein levels of ERRα, PTPMT1, CCL2, CD163, and CD115 in tumors from different ERRα/PTPMT1 expression groups, as measured by immunofluorescence staining, n=5 mice per group. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( H–I ) Representative images of tumors captured at the end of the study and the tumor volume in BALB/c nude mice in the four experimental groups (day 0 was defined as the day when different cells were implanted into nude mice). CDX -NC served as the control group, n=5 mice per group. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( J–N ) The panels show the protein levels of ERRα, PTPMT1, CCL2, CD115, and CD163 in tumors from the four experimental groups, as measured by immunofluorescence staining. n=5 mice per group. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( O ) The panel shows serum CCL2 levels in mice from the four experimental groups, as measured by ELISA. The data are shown as the means±SDs. CDX -NC served as the control group. n=5 mice per group. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( P ) Cell morphology in the organoids from the different experimental groups was recorded via microscopy. Scale bar: 100 µm. ( Q ) The panel shows the evaluation of cell viability following different treatments using the Cell Titer-Glo assay. Cells treated with DMSO were used as the control group. The data are shown as the means±SDs. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, each performed with three technical replicates. P values were calculated by one-way ANOVA. ( R–S ) The panels show the IC50 of carlumab and the effect of XCT790 on this parameter. ns, not significant; *p<0.05, **p<0.01, and ***p<0.001. ANOVA, analysis of variance; CDX, cell line-derived xenograft; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; NC, normal control; PTPMT1, protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1; DMSO, Dimethyl sulfoxide; IC50, half maximal inhibitory concentration. Open in a new tab To better mimic in vivo conditions, a three-dimensional organ-like model (EC-derived organoids) was established to evaluate drug efficacy. EC organoids were treated with carlumab (80 µg/mL), XCT790 (20 µM), or a combination of both (80 µg/mL carlumab+20 µMXCT790). Morphological analysis revealed that treatment with carlumab or XCT790 alone significantly reduced organoid size (shrinkage) compared with the untreated control group. In contrast, the carlumab+XCT790 combination not only induced shrinkage but also led to further morphological changes, including apoptosis and hollow vesicle formation ( figure 6P ). Cell viability analysis revealed significantly reduced survival rates in treatment groups compared with untreated controls: 77.65% in the 80 µg/mL carlumab group, 75.19% in the 20 µM XCT790 group. Notably, the combination treatment (80 µg/mL carlumab+20 µM XCT790) resulted in the lowest cell survival rate (57.99%) compared with either monotherapy ( figure 6Q ). Furthermore, the IC₅₀ of carlumab was significantly reduced from 87.05 µg/mL (monotherapy) to 65.99 µg/mL following combination treatment with 20 µM XCT790 ( figure 6R,S ). These findings confirm that ERRα inhibition enhances the sensitivity of EC organoids to carlumab. Clinical association of ERRα-PTPMT1-M2 macrophage signaling with the progression of EC in patients A total of 166 patients with EC were included in the analysis. Detailed clinical information of the EC cohort is presented in online supplemental tables 1,2 . Patients with EC were divided into two groups: patients with early-stage (FIGO stages I–II) or advanced-stage (FIGO stages III–IV) tumors. The results showed that carbohydrate antigen 125 (CA125, a tumor marker) and Ki67 (a tumor proliferation-related antigen) expression levels were significantly higher in patients with advanced-stage tumors ( online supplemental tables 1 and 2 ) compared with patients with early-stage tumors. These findings indicate that CA125 and Ki67 are effective biomarkers for evaluating EC progression. The expression of ERRα, PTPMT1, and CCL2, as well as the abundance of M2 macrophages in the center area of cancer tissues, were analyzed via IHC ( figure 7A ). The results revealed that the expression of ERRα, PTPMT1, and CCL2, along with the abundance of M2 macrophages (as indicated by CD115 and CD163), was significantly higher in advanced-stage tumors than in early-stage tumors ( figure 7B–F ). We then grouped patients with EC according to ERRα expression levels. Patients with high ERRα expression exhibited a higher percentage of advanced tumor stage and elevated Ki67 expression ( online supplemental table 1 ). Additionally, patients with high ERRα expression had increased PTPMT1 and CCL2 expression and more M2 macrophage infiltration (CD115 and CD163) ( figure 7G–K ). Notably, on assessing the invasive front of the tumor, we found that patients with high ERRα expression exhibited higher CCL2 expression and more M2 macrophage infiltration than those with low ERRα expression ( figure 7L–O ). To further elucidate the correlation and spatial distribution between the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis and M2 macrophages in patients, we selected five patients with early-stage EC and matched them with eight patients with advanced-stage EC with similar age and body mass index who were from our populations, then performed mIHC to detect relevant markers. Morphological observation revealed that M2 macrophage infiltration was more pronounced in advanced-stage tumor tissues compared with early-stage tumor tissues ( figure 7P , online supplemental figure 3C ). The results showed that the fluorescence intensity of ERRα, PTPMT1, and CCL2, as well as the abundance of M2 macrophages (CD115+ and CD163+ double-positive cells), was significantly higher in patients with advanced-stage EC than in early-stage patients ( figure 7Q ). Correlation analysis revealed that ERRα abundance was positively correlated with PTPMT1 (R 2 =0.81, p<0.0001), CCL2 (R 2 =0.44, p=0.0139), and M2 macrophages (R 2 =0.70, p=0.0003). Similarly, PTPMT1 was positively correlated with CCL2 (R 2 =0.50, p=0.0068) and M2 macrophages (R 2 =0.56, p=0.0032) ( online supplemental figure 3D-H ). Meanwhile, IHC results indicated that vimentin abundance was significantly higher in late-stage tumors than in early-stage tumors ( online supplemental figure 3I-J ). We then co-cultured M2 macrophages (differentiated from human PBMCs) with ECCs and evaluated the role of the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis in promoting M2 macrophage chemotaxis. M2 macrophage chemotaxis was significantly enhanced when co-cultured with ECCs overexpressing ERRα (ovERRα) or PTPMT1 (ovPTPMT1) compared with controls. Conversely, downregulating ERRα (siERRα) or PTPMT1 (siPTPMT1) in ECCs attenuated M2 chemotaxis ( figure 7R–T ). These results indicate that ECCs with high ERRα or PTPMT1 expression may promote CCL2 secretion to recruit M2 macrophages, thereby facilitating tumor progression. Figure 7. ERRα-PTPMT1 expression and M2 macrophages in clinical patients with EC. ( A ) Representative images of ERRα, PTPMT1, CCL2, CD115, and CD163 expression in tumors from patients with early-stage (125 patients) and advanced-stage (41 patients) EC, detected by immunohistochemistry (scale bar 200 µm). ( B–F ) Panels showing the levels of ERRα, PTPMT1, CCL2, CD115, and CD163 in the center area of tumor tissue from patients with early-stage or advanced-stage EC. The data are shown as percentages, and p values were calculated by Pearson’s χ 2 test. ( G–K ) Panels showing the levels of PTPMT1, CCL2, CD115, and CD163 in the invasive tumor front from patients with EC with different ERRα expression levels (ERRα (±): 122 patients; ERRα (++/+++): 44 patients). The data are shown as percentages, and p values were calculated by Pearson’s χ 2 test. ( L ) Representative images of CCL2, CD115, and CD163 expression in the invasive front of tumor tissues from patients with EC with different ERRα expression levels, as determined by immunohistochemistry (scale bar 200 µm). ( M–O ) Panels showing the levels of CCL2, CD115, and CD163 in invasive tumor front from patients with EC with different ERRα expression levels. The data are shown as the means±SDs, and p values were calculated by Student’s t-test. ( P ) Representative multiplex immunohistochemistry images show M2 macrophages (CD115+CD163+), ERRα, PTPMT1, and CCL2 expression in tumors from patients with early-stage (five patients) and advanced-stage (eight patients) EC (scale bar 100 µm). ( Q ) The levels of M2 macrophages (CD115+CD163+), ERRα, PTPMT1, and CCL2 in tumors from patients with early-stage (5 patients) and advanced-stage (8 patients) EC. The data are shown as the means±SDs, and p values were calculated by Student’s t-test. ( R–T ) The role of the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis in promoting chemotaxis of M2 macrophages (differentiated from six female donors’ peripheral blood mononuclear cells) in a co-culture system. Representative data from three independent experiments are shown, five fields of view were randomly selected for microscopic counting. The data are shown as the means±SDs, and p values were calculated by one-way analysis of variance. EC, endometrial cancer; ECC, endometrial cancer cell; ERRα, estrogen-related receptor α; NC, normal control; PTPMT1, protein tyrosine phosphatase mitochondrial 1. Open in a new tab Discussion Tumors alter their energy metabolism to meet increasing bioenergetic demands and maintain proliferation. 30 Altered energy metabolism in tumor cells also contributes to cancer cell immune escape by affecting the microenvironment. 31 Treatments targeting energy metabolism can kill tumors directly or through immune-mediated mechanisms. 32 33 ERRα is a molecule that is directly associated with tumor metabolism and immunity. 14 ERRα-PTPMT1 signaling activates mitochondrial OXPHOS through increased CL synthesis and ROS production. This process contributes to the increased secretion of CCL2 by ECCs, which leads to the recruitment of M2 macrophages and the promotion of tumor progression. The targeted blockade of ERRα and CCL2 reduces M2 macrophage recruitment and inhibits tumor progression. ERRα is involved in mitochondrial OXPHOS as a transcription factor. 34 Mao et al reported that ERRα is associated with EC progression and that the lipid reprogramming regulated by ERRα is related mainly to mitochondrial function. 4 CLs are found mainly in the inner mitochondrial membrane and are important for electron respiratory chain transfer during OXPHOS. 35 PTPMT1 dephosphorylates PGP to PG to regulate CL synthesis. 27 Here, we found that ERRα and PTPMT1 are positively correlated with CL synthesis and mitochondrial OXPHOS. Furthermore, ERRα could transcriptionally upregulate PTPMT1. Numerous studies have shown that CCL2 expression is increased in different cancers, such as prostate, colorectal and breast cancers, and promotes malignant transformation and progression by mediating the entry of monocytes into the tumor microenvironment (TME) to become macrophages. 36 , 38 CCL2 expression is significantly higher in ECCs than in normal cells. 39 Pena et al reported higher CCL2 expression in high-grade EC than in low-grade EC and observed that CCL2 recruits macrophages to promote EC progression. 40 In this study, after discovering differences in macrophage recruitment by ECCs, we performed cytokine microarray assays and ELISAs. The results revealed that CCL2 secretion was significantly increased in ovERRα and ovPTPMT1 ECCs. Reduced chemotaxis of M2 macrophages was observed after the treatment of ECCs with the CCL2-specific blocker carlumab. Differences in the recruitment of macrophages by ECCs may be due to CCL2 secretion, and this process may be caused by the ERRα-PTPMT1 axis affecting mitochondrial OXPHOS and ROS production. This hypothesis is supported by the findings of previous studies. The accumulation of ROS in tumor cells induces oxidative stress in cells, leading to alterations in interleukin-6 (IL-6) and CCL2 expression. IL-6 and CCL2 levels decrease after NAC-mediated ROS clearance. 41 Previous studies have shown that ROS can upregulate CCL2 expression through the NF-κB pathway. 28 42 Increased CCL2 levels are an important initiating event for tumor progression. CCL2 secreted by tumor cells induces monocyte chemotaxis to the TME to become tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs; predominantly M2 macrophages). In turn, TAMs secrete more CCL2 to recruit more macrophages, thereby creating an immunosuppressive microenvironment that promotes tumor progression. 43 44 Importantly, the expression of ERRα, PTPMT1, and CCL2 and the presence of M2 macrophages (CD115 and CD163) were significantly greater in patients with advanced-stage EC than in patients with early-stage EC. The expression of CCL2 and the presence of M2 macrophages at the invasive tumor front were significantly greater in patients with high ERRα expression than in those with low ERRα expression. The expression of CCL2 and M2 macrophages at the edges of tumors tended to accumulate from the periphery to the tumor area. Namely, ERRα promoted the secretion of CCL2 and attracted macrophages to accumulate in the tumor area, thereby promoting EC progression. Altered metabolism in tumor cells is followed by changes in cytokine secretion. Cytokines act on immune cells in the TME. In summary, our research builds a bridge between tumor metabolism and immunity. CCL2 plays a role in tumor development by recruiting TAMs and can be used as a therapeutic target. Preclinical animal studies using CCL2-neutralizing antibodies or C-C motif chemokine receptor 2 (CCR2) inhibitors targeting the CCL2-CCR2 axis have shown that these agents effectively inhibit tumor growth and metastasis in patients with malignant tumors such as breast cancer, 45 46 prostate cancer, 47 hepatocellular carcinoma, 48 and colorectal cancer. 49 In our study of tumor-bearing nude mice, the tumor size decreased in the group treated with carlumab or XCT790 alone, and the serum CCL2 concentration decreased. The group treated with carlumab combined with XCT790 had the smallest tumor size and lowest serum CCL2 concentration. The results from EC organoids also revealed that the combined administration of XCT790 and carlumab had a stronger inhibitory effect than either drug alone. These findings suggest that targeting ERRα may delay tumor progression by modulating cellular mitochondrial OXPHOS and reducing CCL2 levels. Previous studies have shown that inhibiting ERRα improves chemotherapy sensitivity in patients with EC. 29 The treatment of EC with ERRα combined with endocrine therapy has better outcomes than treatment with endocrine therapy alone. 50 Recent studies have shown that in PD-1-resistant melanoma, the inhibition of ERRα can assist in tumor therapy by inhibiting tumor energy metabolism and activating two immune pathways, that is, inducing cytokine production to cause macrophage polarization, and stimulating antigen presentation and recruiting CD8+T cells into tumors. 14 These findings are consistent with our results, suggesting that in future therapeutic strategies, ERRα may be a significant target for modulating tumor cell metabolism and reshaping the tumor immune microenvironment. Moreover, the lipidomic analysis revealed that CL and PG levels were significantly increased in ovERRα ECCs and ovPTPMT1 ECCs. Coincidentally, the increased CL levels in ovERRα or ovPTPMT1 ECCs mainly consisted of saturated fatty acids. CLs are important lipids that constitute the inner membrane of mitochondria, 51 which contain more saturated fatty acids and have relatively poorer membrane fluidity. 52 This finding may explain why the number of mitochondrial cristae increased and OXPHOS increased in ECCs when ERRα or PTPMT1 was overexpressed. These findings provide a new direction for understanding how the regulation of CLs and mitochondrial function by ERRα-PTPMT1 affects tumor progression through metabolism, potentially even providing new targets for EC therapy. One limitation of this study is that data from clinical patients were obtained from Fujian Maternal and Child Health Hospital. The sample size was limited, which may have led to bias in the results. For example, CA125, Ki67, estrogen receptor (ER) and progesterone receptor (PR) are known as routine indices for EC; however, their efficacy in predicting outcomes is limited in the clinic. The regulatory mechanism of the molecular pathway has not been elucidated. In this study, only a tumor-centric CCL2 analysis was performed, and macrophages were not analyzed for CCR2 or other relevant receptors, nor was macrophage activity assessed. Clinical trials of carlumab, a specific antibody for CCL2, have shown non-significant therapeutic effects and a rebound increase in CCL2 levels after the drug is stopped, and much more work is needed before XCT790 can be used in combination with carlumab for clinical treatment. In summary, we employed high-throughput assays, including proteomics, lipidomics and cytokine arrays, to show that ERRα functions as a bridge between EC metabolism and immunity in a PTPMT1-dependent manner. We elucidated the mechanism by which ERRα-PTPMT1 signaling promotes CL synthesis to improve the structure of the inner mitochondrial membrane, leading to OXPHOS activation and ROS production through the NF-κB pathway and a subsequent increase in CCL2 secretion. These results pave the way for the development of new treatments targeting tumor metabolism and immunotherapy, which will be beneficial for the treatment of patients with EC. Supplementary material online supplemental figure 1 jitc-13-9-s001.tif (57.8MB, tif) DOI: 10.1136/jitc-2025-012521 online supplemental figure 2 jitc-13-9-s002.tif (57.8MB, tif) DOI: 10.1136/jitc-2025-012521 onli