INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1528,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12481533','LncRNA LBX2-AS1 exacerbates LPS-induced stress on periodontal ligament cells by activating the TLR2 signaling pathway through miR-654-3p.','Liu F, Qian C, Wang W, Zhang R, Xu L, Liu L.','2025','periodontally crevicular gcf runx2 ocn gcf counteracted represses odontitis orderly multidirectional restorations occlusal maf bzip mafg lysyl loxl1 linc00687 ladybird homeobox linc01566 linc00687 linc01566 innovatively bengbu bengbu periodontally 3mm coloration calculus radiographically orthodontic appliances restorations lingual sulcus cemento enamel cej cej cej gingiva turesky flecks antisepsis supragingival spatula calculus tartar blowing deepest crevicular gcf gcf aliquoted fuyu reintroduced passaging sangon lipofectamine solarbio dishes glycerophosphate runt runx2 osteocalcin ocn trypsinized fitc isothiocyanate propidium annexinv fitc beyotime facscalibur cytometer becton cellquest cck8 takara sorbent resuspending lysed trizol gcf spectrophotometer vazyme nanjing vazyme nanjing δδct p65 p65 ocn runx2 lyse centrifuge tiangen boiling pvdf skimmed tbst tbst ecl p0063 ladder beyotime lncrnasnpv3 gong_lab hzau lncrnasnp3 starbase starbase sysu pgl3 lipofectamine lucy solarbio ribobio sulcus gcf loos periodontally gcf gcf periodontally crevicular loos loos ocn runx2 elisas ocn runx2 gcf gcf periodontally elisas ocn runx2 downregulate gcf gcf periodontally κbp65 elisas ocn runx2 overexpress p65 p65 s1a s1b s1c gcf gcf 30b 125b gcf miat gcf gcf gcf ocn osteoblast runx2 ocn runx2 osteoblast knocking hcp5 ho1 elk1 linc01126 sponging 518a chondrocyte tnfrsf9 mapk6 aggravation tp73 akt3 elucidates counteracting p65 translocates pamps damps sponging gcf sponges osteoclastogenesis gcf iann_a_2554931_sm1528 wenrui rongxiu bengbu approvalnumber trindade jcpe iniesta chamorro subgingival gingivitis jcpe shapira periodontology efp wonca jcpe bobetsis graziani gursoy molon rossa thurlings ijms20184541 agaricus blazei murrill e2300112 mnfr dcst1 plap jre trpa1 perk eif2α atf oxid longev circ_0138959 traf6 osteoblastic immunocyte intimp odi wangzhou mafg odi loxl1 pdlscs downregulates jre jre fstl3 rarα mylip e24812 e24812 nfic boosts znf703 s12935 tonetti greenwell kornman s149 s161 jcpe insoles barbell squats bioengineering9070301 turesky glickman chloromethyl notch2 parihar gcf njms njms_53_23 crevicular s12903 30b 125b s12903 miat dkk1 archoralbio circ_0062491 socs6 phf8 jper circ_0066881 rora s12094 hcp5 ho1 elk1 e34203 e34203 518a prolif e12957 alkbh5 tnfrsf9 zha circ_0000285 sponging mapk6 tp73 akt3 s11658 rdcm beghini crb granulomas radicular joen biocel thuy tien corm lipoteichoic vcam traf6 kazmierski tlr3 cells9081910 barreiros fwg mmp2 mmp9 tsg neuroinflamm s12974 huc rsad2 intimp s41420 traf6 tak1 ikkβ cyto iann_a_2554931_sm1528','Abstract Background Periodontitis poses challenges in both early detection and late-stage treatment. The purpose of this study was to understand the role and mechanism of the lncRNA LBX2-AS1 in periodontitis. Methods Baseline clinical data were collected from 107 patients in the periodontitis group and 107 participants in the periodontally healthy control group (HC). Expression of LBX2-AS1 in gingival crevicular fluid (GCF) was assessed using qRT-PCR. To verify the binding relationships between miR-654-3p, LBX2-AS1 and TLR2, dual luciferase reporter gene and pull-down assays were performed. Periodontal ligament cells (PDLCs) were stimulated with LPS to mimic periodontitis conditions. ELISA was used to measure TNF-α, IL-6, and IL-1β levels. ALP activity and RUNX2/OCN protein expression were detected to assess osteogenic differentiation. CCK-8 and flow cytometry were performed to analyze PDLCs proliferation and apoptosis. Results LBX2-AS1 and TLR2 were upregulated, while miR-654-3p was downregulated in periodontitis patients GCF. LBX2-AS1 could efficiently identify the onset of periodontitis. Downregulation of LBX2-AS1 inhibited inflammatory factor release, suppressed PDLCs proliferation, and differentiation, promoted apoptosis, and reversed LPS-induced damage to PDLCs. LBX2-AS1 negatively regulated miR-654-3p, and its downregulation counteracted the effects of LBX2-AS1 knockdown. As a target of miR-654-3p, TLR2 exacerbated LPS-induced inflammation and injury in PDLCs. miR-654-3p overexpression represses Myd88/NF-κB protein expression. Conclusions Upregulation of LBX2-AS1 has the potential to be a marker for the development of periodontitis. miR-654-3p mediates the impact of LBX2-AS1 on LPS-stimulated inflammatory responses and cellular damage in PDLCs via the TLR2/Myd88/NF-κB signaling pathway. Keywords: Differentiation, periodontitis, PDLCs, inflammation, LBX2-AS1, miR-654-3p, TLR2 Introduction Periodontitis is a chronic and progressive condition affecting the periodontal supporting tissues, primarily driven by bacterial, immune, and genetic influences. Early signs of the disease are often subtle and easily overlooked, but as it advances, it can lead to tooth mobility, weakened chewing ability, recurrent periodontal abscesses, and other symptoms, severely impacting human health [ 1 , 2 ]. Pre-odontitis, the early subclinical phase before overt tissue damage, is often asymptomatic—lacking obvious signs like bleeding on probing—making it easily missed in routine checks. Yet, emerging evidence shows this stage already impacts systemic health. These early inflammatory states have been correlated with elevated systemic inflammatory markers and are potentially associated with an increased risk of several medical conditions, including cardiovascular disease, diabetes complications, and preterm birth [ 3–5 ]. This highlights the importance of timely diagnosis and preventive strategies at the earliest phases of periodontal disease. Periodontal ligament cells (PDLCs), serving as pivotal cellular constituents of periodontal tissue, are essential for preserving its normal structure and function [ 6 ]. In a normal physiological state, PDLCs maintain a dynamic balance in cell number through orderly proliferation and apoptosis processes, thereby ensuring periodontal tissue stability. They possess multidirectional differentiation potential, capable of differentiating into osteoblasts, fibroblasts, among others, and are involved in the formation and regeneration of alveolar bone, as well as the synthesis and repair of periodontal fibers [ 7 ]. However, when external stimuli such as long-term accumulation of oral biofilm and their metabolites, local irritation from faulty restorations, and occlusal trauma affect the periodontal tissue, the normal physiological function of PDLCs is significantly disrupted, leading to periodontitis [ 8 , 9 ]. Investigating its regulatory mechanism is crucial for understanding the pathogenesis, identifying early diagnostic markers, and developing effective treatments. This is crucial for improving patients’ oral health and overall quality of life, as well as for reducing the associated healthcare burden. In recent years, long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs) have emerged as key regulators in various biological processes and diseases. lncRNAs, which are non-coding RNAs consisting of more than 200 nucleotides, do not directly encode proteins but play a pivotal role in regulating gene expression [ 10 ]. This regulation is achieved through intricate mechanisms such as DNA methylation, histone modifications, transcription factor binding, and interactions with other RNAs. Recent advancements in high-throughput sequencing technology have facilitated the extensive identification of lncRNAs, uncovering their significant roles in numerous diseases [ 11 ]. In periodontitis research, lncRNAs have garnered attention due to their involvement in regulating various cellular processes. For instance, lncRNA MAF BZIP Transcription Factor G Antisense RNA 1 (MAFG-AS1) may play a role in periodontitis development by modulating the proliferation of PDLCs [ 12 ]. Additionally, lncRNA Lysyl oxidase-like 1-antisense 1 (LOXL1-AS1) has been found to inhibit the release of inflammatory factors and alleviate periodontitis symptoms in patients [ 13 ]. Further exploration of the specific mechanisms of action and regulatory networks of lncRNAs in periodontitis could offer novel insights and approaches for early prediction and precision medicine in this condition. Su et al. identified three novel and crucial lncRNAs associated with periodontitis—LINC00687, lncRNA Ladybird homeobox 2 antisense RNA 1 (LBX2-AS1), and LINC01566—using weighted gene co-expression network analysis [ 14 ]. Among these, LINC00687 and LINC01566 have received limited attention in scientific literature. In contrast, LBX2-AS1 has been shown to play a pivotal regulatory role in various cancers, underscoring its significance in disease modulation and possessing substantial research merit. For instance, in thyroid cancer, LBX2-AS1 participates in the regulation of cancer cell metastasis and proliferation [ 15 ]; in acute myeloid leukemia, it can inhibit cancer cell proliferation and promote apoptosis by targeting miR-455-5p [ 16 ]; in gastric cancer cells, LBX2-AS1 functions as an oncogenic factor and speeds up the malignant process [ 17 ]. Although the multiple functions of LBX2-AS1 in cancer cell activities have been gradually uncovered, its role in periodontitis remains unclear. The objective of this study is to innovatively delve into the function of LBX2-AS1 in periodontitis, treating it as a pivotal focus of research. Our aim is to uncover its potential significance, and the regulatory mechanisms involved in periodontitis, with the hope of discovering novel diagnostic and therapeutic targets for this condition. Methods Research object The participants in this study were selected from patients who sought medical attention at The First Affiliated Hospital of Bengbu Medical University between 2020 and 2022. Prior to the study’s commencement, ethical approval was granted by The First Affiliated Hospital of Bengbu Medical University (approval number:2019112) Ethics Committee, and all participants gave their written informed consent. The study protocols adhered strictly to the principles outlined in the Helsinki Declaration. Inclusion criteria Periodontally healthy control group (HC): Individuals with healthy periodontal tissues ( n = 107) Clinically assessed periodontal tissues exhibiting a probing depth of ≤3mm, accompanied by the absence of gingival inflammation, characterized by pink gingival coloration, firm consistency, and absence of bleeding upon probing. Maintained oral hygiene indicated by a plaque index of ≤1 and negligible calculus accumulation. No recent (within the past 3 months) utilization of antibiotics, anti-inflammatory agents, or other medications capable of influencing periodontal tissue status. Periodontitis patient group (Periodontitis): Patients diagnosed with periodontitis ( n = 107) Enrollment was limited to patients with periodontitis of at least Stage II severity, as defined by the 2018 classification system [ 18 ]. Diagnosis was confirmed by meeting the following minimum criteria: Radiographically verified crown-related alveolar bone loss ≥2 mm at ≥1 site, via periapical imaging; Clinically observed attachment level loss ≥2 mm; Periodontal pocket depth ≥4 mm; Periodontal tissues exhibited inflammatory signs, including redness, edema, and bleeding upon probing. Willing and able to comply with all study procedures and complete the required examinations and sample collections. No history of periodontal systemic interventions, such as periodontal flap surgery or guided tissue regeneration, within the last 3 months. Note on staging and grading: The cross-sectional design of this study and the lack of longitudinal data on disease progression precluded a precise determination of the disease stage (I–IV) and grade (A–C) for each individual according to the full 2018 classification criteria. Therefore, patients were included based on the presence of clear, objective signs of established periodontitis (as detailed above) that are indicative of at least Stage II severity Exclusion criteria Presence of severe systemic diseases, encompassing uncontrolled diabetes mellitus (HbA1c > 8%), hematological disorders, autoimmune diseases, and malignancies. Current state of pregnancy or lactation. Mental health conditions or cognitive impairments that impede comprehension of the study or cooperation with study procedures. Recent (within the past three months) utilization of medications that have an impact on periodontal tissues or inflammation, such as immunosuppressants and anticoagulants. Presence of orthodontic appliances or inadequate dental restorations that induce gingival irritation. Incomplete or absent clinical data records. Sample size An initial assessment of the sample size was conducted using G*Power, with the following parameters: a medium effect size assumption (Cohen’s d = 0.5), an alpha error probability set at 0.05, and a target power level (1–β error probability) of 0.80 [ 19 ]. The calculated sample size was determined to be within the range of the planned population size for inclusion in this study. Examination of periodontal clinical indicators All patients with periodontitis underwent a comprehensive periodontal examination, excluding the 3rd molars. The test sites, including the lingual, buccal distal–medial, buccal medial, and buccal proximal–medial aspects of each tooth, were evaluated, with the average value of each site serving as the final result. The periodontal indicators assessed were as follows: Probing pocket depth: distance from gingival margin to periodontal pocket or gingival sulcus bottom. Clinical Attachment level: Measured as the distance from the cemento-enamel junction (CEJ) to the bottom of the periodontal pocket. In cases of gingival recession, Clinical Attachment level equals probing pocket depth plus the distance from the gingival margin to the CEJ. In the absence of recession, Clinical Attachment level equals probing pocket depth minus the distance from the gingival margin to the CEJ. Bleeding index: 0 for healthy non - bleeding gingiva; 1 for inflamed non - bleeding on probing; 2 for pinpoint bleeding on probing; 3 for obvious bleeding; 4 for severe bleeding; 5 for spontaneous bleeding. Plaque index was assessed according to the Turesky modification of the Quigley-Hein Index [ 20 ]: 0 = no plaque; 1 = separate flecks of plaque at the cervical margin; 2 = a thin, continuous band of plaque (up to 1 mm) at the cervical margin 3 = a band of plaque wider than 1 mm but covering less than one-third of the tooth surface; 4 = plaque covering at least one-third but less than two-thirds of the tooth surface; 5 = plaque covering two-thirds or more of the tooth surface. Collection of samples Subjects underwent oral antisepsis, supragingival plaque removal with a sterile spatula, and scaling to eliminate calculus and soft tartar. The tooth surface was then dried by air-blowing to isolate it from moisture. Sterile periapical strips were positioned in the two deepest, non-adjacent periodontal pockets for optimal gingival crevicular fluid (GCF) contact. If contamination was confirmed, the strips were discarded and re-sampled. After 30 s, the strips were placed in phosphate buffer (pH 7) for 30 min at room temperature. The samples were then centrifuged at 4 °C and 12,000× g for 15 min to isolate the GCF, which was then aliquoted and stored at −80 °C. Cell culture and cell transfection Human PDLCs (Fuyu Biotechnology Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China) were sub-cultured that were cultivated within DMEM medium in a mixture containing 1% penicillin–streptomycin and 10% fetal bovine serum. After wall attachment, the cells were digested and reintroduced to the above environment for passaging culture. The cells were maintained at 37 °C, 5% CO 2 . For transfection experiments, the following vectors were utilized: small interfering RNA targeting LBX2-AS1 (si-LBX2-AS1) and its negative control (si-NC), miR-654-3p inhibitor (miR-inhibitor), miR-654-3p mimic (miR-mimic) and its negative control (miR-NC), as well as TLR2 overexpression plasmid (oe-TLR2) and its corresponding negative control (oe-NC). All transfection vectors were obtained from Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) Co., Ltd. According to the experimental design, the transfection vectors were combined, uniformly mixed with Lipofectamine 2000 transfection reagent (Solarbio, Beijing, China), and then transfected into PDLCs. The transfection efficiency was measured after 48 h. Transfection efficiency was validated 48 h post-transfection by quantifying the expression levels of target genes (LBX2-AS1, miR-654-3p, TLR2 mRNA) using qRT-PCR. LPS induction and osteogenic differentiation induction Cells from both the transfection and control groups, which were in the logarithmic growth phase, were chosen. After digestion and cell counting, they were inoculated into 24-well plates. Subsequently, 1.0 μg/mL of lipopolysaccharide (LPS) was added to these plates to treat the cells for 24 h, thereby establishing an in vitro model of periodontitis based on the method described by Deng et al. [ 9 ]. PDLCs, both treated and untreated with LPS, were selected for osteogenic differentiation induction. These cells were routinely cultured until they reached a density of approximately 70% in the culture dishes. Following this, they were transferred to complete DMEM medium containing osteogenic induction medium, which comprised of 10 mmol/L β-glycerophosphate, 100 μmol/L ascorbic acid, and 10 nmol/L dexamethasone. After 14 days, osteogenic differentiation markers including alkaline phosphatase (ALP) activity, and runt-related transcription factor 2 (Runx2) and osteocalcin (OCN) protein expression were detected to assess the osteogenic induction status. Apoptosis assay PDLCs in different treatment groups, including the blank control group (Ctrl; untreated), LPS-only treated group (without transfection), and the LPS-treated groups that had been transfected with various vectors, were trypsinized, washed with PBS, and resuspended in 1× binding buffer. The cells were then double-stained with 5 μL of both FITC-Annexin V and PI using the Membrane Associated Protein V-Fluorescein Isothiocyanate/Propidium Iodide (AnnexinV-FITC/PI) kit (Beyotime Biotech Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China), while ensuring the process occurred in a dark environment for 5 min. Apoptosis analysis was performed using a FACSCalibur flow cytometer (Becton-Dickinson) with CellQuest software. The percentages of cells in different phases were quantified as follows: viable cells (Annexin V – /PI – , Q4), early apoptotic cells (Annexin V + /PI – , Q3), late apoptotic/necrotic cells (Annexin V + /PI + , Q2), and necrotic cells (Annexin V – /PI + , Q1). The total apoptosis rate was defined as the sum of early and late apoptotic populations. Cell proliferation assay Cell proliferation verified by CCK8 assay. PDLCs subjected to various treatments were seeded in 96-well plates. At predetermined time points, media were aspirated from wells, and CCK-8 reagent (Takara, Japan) was added. After a gentle shaking for mixing, plates were incubated in an incubator for 2 h to allow for adequate cell-CCK-8 reaction. A microplate reader measured the OD 450 value for each well, and cell proliferation rates were subsequently calculated. Enzyme-linked immune sorbent assay (ELISA) experiment The levels of inflammation-associated cytokines interleukin-6 (IL-6), tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α), interleukin-1β (IL-1β), mature IL-1β were measured using ELISA kits (Abcam, UK). The process began with resuspending cells in the lysis buffer provided in the kit, followed by centrifugation to isolate the supernatant as the sample for testing. Preparation of both samples and standards adhered strictly to the instructions outlined in the ELISA kit. Finally, the OD 450 value was measured to calculate the concentrations of the inflammatory factors. Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) activity assay ALP is a key early-stage marker of osteogenic differentiation. ALP activity was measured using a commercial ALP assay kit, following the manufacturer’s protocol and established methodologies [ 21 ]. Briefly, after the induction of osteogenic differentiation, the culture medium was aspirated, and the cells were gently washed. Cells were then lysed with pre-cooled lysate on ice for 15–30 min with gentle shaking, followed by centrifugation to collect supernatants. The ALP working solution was prepared as instructed, mixed with the supernatants on a 96-well plate, and incubated for 60 min. The optical density at a wavelength of 680 nm (OD 680 ) was measured using a microplate reader and ALP activity was calculated according to the kit instructions for the specific formula. Quantitative reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (qRT-PCR) The Trizol method was used to extract total RNA from GCF samples and PDLCs, which was then evaluated for quality using an ultra-micro spectrophotometer. The RNA was reverse transcribed into cDNA template using a reverse transcriptase kit (Vazyme Biotechnology Co., LTD., Nanjing, China). cDNA was amplified using an RT-qPCR kit (Vazyme Biotech Co., Ltd., Nanjing, China), which was performed in a thermal cycle of initial denaturation at 95 °C (1 min), denaturation at 95 °C (20 s) and 60 °C (1 min) for annealing by 40 cycles. After completion, LBX2-AS1, Toll-like receptor 2 (TLR2) mRNA (normalized to GAPDH) and miR-654-3p (normalized to U6) relative expression levels were quantified by means of the 2 –ΔΔCt method. Western blotting assay The expression levels of TLR2, Myeloid differentiation primary response 88 (Myd88), phosphorylated NF-κB p65 (p-NF-κB p65) proteins, and osteogenic differentiation markers including OCN, Runx2, NLRP3, and cleaved caspase-1 p20 proteins were examined in PDLCs from various treatment groups. The lysate was mixed with the PDLCs suspension and allowed to lyse for 30 min on ice. Following this, the mixture was centrifuged using an ultra-high-speed centrifuge (14,000 g , 15 min, 4 °C, Thermo, USA). The supernatant was then extracted for protein concentration determination. Preparation of the samples was carried out according to the guidelines provided by the protein extraction kit (Tiangen biotech, Beijing, China), with a subsequent boiling step for 5 min. SDS-PAGE electrophoresis was conducted for 2 h to separate the proteins, which were then transferred onto a PVDF membrane. The membrane was blocked with 5% skimmed milk for 1 h at room temperature and incubated with the corresponding primary antibody overnight at 4 °C. The membrane was rinsed with TBST and then incubated with the secondary antibody for 1 h. Subsequently, it was washed again with TBST, followed by the addition of ECL reaction solution. The image was then developed using a developer. Lastly, the resulting images underwent quantitative analysis in Image Lab software. Molecular marker: #P0063, Protein Ladder; Beyotime, Shanghai, China. Dual-luciferase reporter assay The interaction sites of miR-654-3p with both LBX2-AS1 and TLR2 were identified via the LncRNASNPv3 ( https://gong_lab.hzau.edu.cn/lncrnasnp3#!/ ) and StarBase ( http://starbase.sysu.edu.cn/ ) databases. Sequences with and without binding sites were cloned into the vector pGL3 (Pro-mega, Madison, WI, USA) to generate wild-type (WT-LBX2-AS1, WT-TLR2) and mutant (MT-LBX2-AS1, MT-TLR2) luciferase plasmids. They were simultaneously transfected with miR-NC or miR-654-3p mimic or miR-654-3p inhibitor plasmids into PDLCs via LiPofectamine 2000. After 24 h, the activity of the luciferase was determined according to the Dual-Lucy Assay Kit (Solarbio, Beijing, China). Pull-down assay A pull-down assay was conducted using biotin-labeled miR-654-3p probe (Bio-miR-654-3p) and a negative control probe (Bio-NC), both supplied by Ribobio. The assay was performed following the protocol outlined in the Pierce ™ Magnetic RNA-Protein Pull-Down Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA). RNA molecules bound to Bio-miR-654-3p were subsequently eluted and isolated for further qPCR analysis. Statistical analysis Experimental data were obtained from three or more independent experimental replicates. Statistical analysis was performed using GraphPad Prism 9.5 and SPSS 23.0 software. Measurement results were presented as mean ± standard deviation and count data were presented as numbers and analyzed using the chi-squared test. For comparing data between two sample groups, a t test was used, while for multiple group sample data, ANOVA was used. The diagnostic ability of LBX2 - AS1 for periodontitis was evaluated by plotting the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. Pearson’s correlation coefficient was used to assess correlation. P values less than 0.05 were regarded as a statistically significant difference. Results Expression of LBX2-AS1 in gingival sulcus fluid of patients with periodontitis and its correlation with clinical features Clinical data was gathered for both HC and periodontitis patients. Analysis revealed no statistically significant differences between the two groups in terms of age, gender, BMI, dietary favor, smoking history, or drinking history ( p > 0.05, Table 1 ). However, periodontitis patients exhibited significantly elevated levels of inflammatory markers such as IL-6, IL-1β, and TNF-α compared to HC ( p < 0.001). Similarly, periodontitis patients showed greater probing pocket depth, attachment loss, plaque index, and bleeding index values ( p < 0.0001).qRT-PCR analysis of GCF indicated an upregulation of LBX2-AS1 expression in periodontitis patients ( Figure 1a ). The ROC curve analysis demonstrated that LBX2-AS1 had a good diagnostic performance for periodontitis, with an AUC of 0.887 (95% CI: 0.842–0.931), sensitivity of 81.31%, and specificity of 85.05% ( Figure 1b ). Using the average expression level of LBX2-AS1 as a benchmark, periodontitis patients were categorized into high and low expression groups. Analysis revealed a statistically significant difference between these two groups in inflammatory factor indicators (IL-6, IL-1β, TNF- α) and periodontal clinical indices (probing pocket depth, attachment loss, plaque index, and bleeding index values ( p < 0.05, Table 2 ). Additionally, LBX2-AS1 expression in periodontitis patients positively correlated with IL-6, IL-1β, TNF-α, probing pocket depth, attachment loss, plaque index, and bleeding index ( r > 0.5, p < 0.0001, Table 3 ). Table 1. Clinical data of the study subjects. Clinical information HC Periodontitis p ( n = 107) ( n = 107) Age (years) 40.49 ± 12.01 40.2 ± 12.27 0.812 Gender (female/male) 56/51 57/50 0.791 BMI (kg/m 2 ) 24.61 ± 1.91 24.51 ± 2.01 0.705 Dietary favor (light/heavy) 51/53 48/59 0.412 Smoking history (yes/no) 51/56 59/48 0.274 Drinking history (yes/no) 51/56 57/50 0.412 IL-6 (pg/ml) 5.03 ± 1.21 8.59 ± 1.36 <0.001 IL-1β (pg/ml) 3.53 ± 0.84 4.45 ± 0.94 <0.001 TNF-α (pg/ml) 6.22 ± 1.18 11.07 ± 1.81 <0.001 Probing pocket depth (mm) 1.71 ± 0.71 5.18 ± 1.38 <0.001 Clinical attachment loos (mm) 0.53 ± 0.29 3.95 ± 1.17 <0.001 Plaque index 0.6 ± 0.35 2.49 ± 0.29 <0.001 Bleeding index 0.26 ± 0.14 2.04 ± 0.59 <0.001 Open in a new tab HC: Periodontally health control; BMI: Body mass index; IL-6: Interleukin-6; IL-1β: Interleukin-1β; TNF-α: Tumor necrosis factor. Figure 1. Open in a new tab The expression of LBX2-AS1 was increased in the periodontitis patient GCF. qRT-PCR detected LBX2-AS1 expression in GCF from HC and periodontitis patients (a). ROC curve analysis evaluated its diagnostic value in periodontitis (b). *** p < 0.001 vs. HC. HC: Periodontally healthy controls. Table 2. Correlation analysis between gingival crevicular fluid LBX2-AS1 levels and clinical baseline characteristics of patients with periodontitis. Variant Cases ( n = 107) LBX2-AS1 expression p value Low ( n = 52) High (n = 