% and 5.39% ± 1.0%, respectively (vs. LPS group). Conversely, reduced LBX2-AS1 expression mitigated the LPS-induced inflammatory response, promoted cell proliferation, differentiation trends, and inhibited apoptosis, thereby partially reversing LPS-induced cellular damage. Figure 2. Open in a new tab LBX2-AS1 Knockdown reduced LPS-induced inflammatory response and cellular damage in PDLCs. qRT-PCR examined LBX2-AS1 expression after LPS treatment and si-LBX2-AS1 transfection (a). ELISAs detected IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α levels (b). CCK-8 assessed cell proliferation (c). ALP activity was measured using an ALP kit (d). Western-blotting detected OCN and Runx2 expression (e). Flow cytometry analyzed cell apoptosis (f). *** p < 0.001 vs. ctrl, ## p < 0.001 vs. LPS. Ctrl: Untreated PDLCs (control group); LPS: PDLCs treated with lipopolysaccharide (LPS); LPS+si-LBX2-AS1: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with LBX2-AS1 knockdown plasmid (si-LBX2-AS1); LPS+si-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with negative control plasmid for si-LBX2-AS1 (si-NC). Interaction between LBX2-AS1 and miR-654-3p Bioinformatics analysis identified binding sites between LBX2-AS1 and miR-654-3p. Luciferase reporter assays confirmed that miR-654-3p negatively regulated WT-LBX2-AS1 luciferase activity but had no effect on the mutant ( Figure 3a ). RNA pull-down experiments demonstrated specific enrichment of LBX2-AS1 on a miR-654-3p probe compared to a negative control ( Figure 3b ). miR-654-3p expression was downregulated in the GCF of periodontitis patients ( Figure 3c ) and inversely correlated with LBX2-AS1 expression ( r = −0.656, p < 0.001, Figure 3d ). Figure 3e showed that LPS treatment downregulated miR-654-3p expression in PDLCs, and knockdown of LBX2-AS1 upregulated miR-654-3p. Figure 3. Open in a new tab LBX2-AS1 Targeted miR-654-3p. Luciferase reporter (a) and pull-down assays (b) verified their binding. qRT-PCR detected miR-654-3p expression in periodontitis GCF (c). Pearson correlation analyzed the relationship between LBX2-AS1 and miR-654-3p (d). qRT-PCR examined miR-654-3p expression in PDLCs after LPS treatment and si-LBX2-AS1 transfection (e). *** p < 0.001 vs. HC or Bio-NC or ctrl, ## p < 0.001 vs. LPS. WT-LBX2-AS1: Plasmid containing wild-type LBX2-AS1 sequence (for luciferase reporter assay); MT-LBX2-AS1: Plasmid containing mutant LBX2-AS1 sequence (with disrupted miR-654-3p binding site, for luciferase reporter assay); Bio-miR-654-3p: Biotin-labeled miR-654-3p (for pull-down assay); Bio-NC: Biotin-labeled negative control miRNA (for pull-down assay); HC: Periodontally healthy controls; ctrl: Untreated PDLCs (control group); LPS: PDLCs treated with LPS; LPS+si-LBX2-AS1: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with si-LBX2-AS1; LPS+si-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with si-NC. miR-654-3p mediates the role of LBX2-AS1 on LPS-stimulated PDLCs injury On this basis, the miR-654-3p inhibitor decreased the expression of miR-654-3p significantly ( Figure 4a ), which reverses the role of LBX2-AS1 downregulation on LPS-stimulated PDLCs. The downregulation of miR-654-3p could greatly stimulate the release of inflammatory factors ( Figure 4b ), suppress the proliferation, ( Figure 4c ), and differentiation ( Figure 4d–e ) of PDLCs, and accelerate the apoptosis of PDLCs ( Figure 4f ). Specifically manifested as: inhibition of miR-654-3p increased early apoptosis to 9.16% ± 1.5% and late apoptosis to 8.05% ± 0.73% compared to the LPS+si-LBX2-AS1 group. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Downregulating miR-654-3p worsened LPS-induced inflammation and cellular damage in PDLCs. qRT-PCR was used to detect the effect of miR-654-3p inhibitor on miR-654-3p expression in PDLCs (a). ELISAs measured IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α levels (b). CCK-8 assessed cell proliferation (c). ALP activity was measured using an ALP kit (d). Western-blotting detected OCN and Runx2 expression (e). Flow cytometry analyzed cell apoptosis (f). *** p < 0.001 vs. LPS+si-NC, ## p < 0.001 vs. LPS+si-LBX2-AS1. LPS+si-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with si-NC; LPS+si-LBX2-AS1: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with si-LBX2-AS1; LPS+si-miR-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS, transfected with si-LBX2-AS1, and co-transfected with miRNA negative control (miR-NC); LPS+si-miR-inhibitor: PDLCs treated with LPS, transfected with si-LBX2-AS1, and co-transfected with miR-654-3p inhibitor (to downregulate miR-654-3p). Association between miR-654-3p and TLR2 Binding sites between miR-654-3p and TLR2 were discovered via bioinformatics databases. To verify the relationship between them, a luciferase reporter gene experiment was performed. It was noticed that miR-654-3p could negatively regulate the WT-TLR2 luciferase activity, but there was no alteration in the activity of the MT-TLR2 group ( Figure 5a ). The pull-down experiment revealed that TLR2 was highly enriched on the Bio-miR-654-3p probe compared to Bio-NC ( Figure 5b ). TLR2 expression was up-regulated in the GCF of periodontitis patients ( Figure 5c ) and was significantly positively correlated with LBX2-AS1 ( r = 0.676, p < 0.001, Figure 5d ) and negatively correlated with miR-654-3p ( r = −0.658, p < 0.001, Figure 5e ). In addition, Figure 5f showed that the TLR2 mRNA expression was upregulated in LPS-stimulated PDLCs, and this upregulation was attenuated by knockdown of LBX2-AS1. Figure 5. Open in a new tab TLR2, a miR-654-3p Target, was upregulated in periodontitis. Luciferase reporter gene experiments (a) and pull-down assays (b) were conducted to verify the binding relationship between TLR2 and miR-654-3p. qRT-PCR detected TLR2 mRNA expression in GCF from HC and periodontitis patients (c). Pearson correlation analyzed relationships between TLR2 and LBX2-AS1 (d), and TLR2 and miR-654-3p (e). qRT-PCR examined TLR2 mRNA expression in PDLCs after LPS treatment and si-LBX2-AS1 transfection (f). *** p < 0.001 vs. HC or Bio-NC or ctrl, ## p < 0.001 vs. LPS. WT-TLR2: Plasmid containing wild-type TLR2 3′UTR sequence (for luciferase reporter assay); MT-TLR2: Plasmid containing mutant TLR2 3′UTR sequence (with disrupted miR-654-3p binding site, for luciferase reporter assay); Bio-miR-654-3p: Biotin-labeled miR-654-3p (for pull-down assay); Bio-NC: Biotin-labeled negative control miRNA (for pull-down assay); HC: Periodontally healthy controls; ctrl: Untreated PDLCs (control group); LPS: PDLCs treated with LPS; LPS+si-LBX2-AS1: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with si-LBX2-AS1; LPS+si-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with si-NC. miR-654-3p/TLR2 axis regulated LPS-stimulated PDLCs injury TLR2 upregulation intensified the inflammatory response in PDLCs ( Figure 6a ), inhibited cell proliferation ( Figure 6b ), and differentiation ( Figure 6c,d ), and increased apoptosis ( Figure 6e ; Specifically, TLR2 overexpression elevated the early apoptotic rate from 4.82% ± 0.6% (LPS+miR-mimic group) to 8.16% ± 1.7% and the late apoptotic rate from 5.36% ± 0.5% to 9.27% ± 1.6%), partially reversing the protective effects of miR-654-3p against LPS-induced damage. At the protein level, miR-654-3p overexpression could clearly inhibit the expression of TLR2 protein, and transfection with oe-TLR2 vector successfully induced the expression of TLR2 protein ( Figure 7a ). Further analysis revealed that miR-654-3p overexpression significantly inhibited LPS-induced Myd88 ( Figure 7b ) and p-NF-κBp65 ( Figure 7c ) protein expression, which was reversed by TLR2 overexpression. Figure 6. Open in a new tab Upregulating TLR2 protein aggravated LPS-induced inflammation and cellular damage in PDLCs. ELISAs measured IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α levels (a). CCK-8 assessed cell proliferation (b). ALP activity was measured (c). Western-blotting detected OCN and Runx2 expression (d). Flow cytometry analyzed cell apoptosis (e). *** p < 0.001 vs. ctrl, ## p < 0.001 vs. LPS, ^^vs. LPS+miR-mimic. Ctrl: Untreated PDLCs (control group); LPS: PDLCs treated with LPS; LPS+miR-mimic: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with miR-654-3p mimic (to overexpress miR-654-3p); LPS+miR + oe-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS, transfected with miR-654-3p mimic, and co-transfected with negative control plasmid for TLR2 overexpression (oe-NC); LPS+miR + oe-TLR2: PDLCs treated with LPS, transfected with miR-654-3p mimic, and co-transfected with TLR2 overexpression plasmid (oe-TLR2). Figure 7. Open in a new tab Overexpressing miR-654-3p inhibited LPS-stimulated Myd88 and p-NF-κB p65 protein expression. Western-blotting detected TLR2 protein expression after miR-654-3p overexpression and oe-TLR2 vector transfection (a). Western-blotting detected Myd88 (b) and p-NF-κB p65 (c) proteins. *** p < 0.001 vs. ctrl, ## p < 0.001 vs. LPS, ^^ vs. LPS+miR-mimic. Ctrl: Untreated PDLCs (control group); LPS: PDLCs treated with LPS; LPS+miR-mimic: PDLCs treated with LPS and transfected with miR-654-3p mimic; LPS+miR + oe-NC: PDLCs treated with LPS, transfected with miR-654-3p mimic, and co-transfected with oe-NC; LPS+miR + oe-TLR2: PDLCs treated with LPS, transfected with miR-654-3p mimic, and co-transfected with oe-TLR2. The LBX2-AS1/miR-654-3p axis regulates NLRP3 inflammasome activation Given the established crosstalk between TLR2 signaling and NLRP3 inflammasome activation in the context of LPS stimulation, we further investigated whether the LBX2-AS1/miR-654-3p axis modulates this pathway. Western blot analysis revealed that LPS treatment significantly upregulated the protein expression of the core inflammasome sensor NLRP3 ( Figure S1a ) and the active form of cleaved caspase-1 p20 ( Figure S1b ), indicating robust inflammasome assembly and activation. Consistent with this, the level of mature IL-1β, the key effector cytokine processed by active caspase-1, was also markedly elevated ( Figure S1c ). Notably, knockdown of LBX2-AS1 substantially attenuated these LPS-induced effects, leading to a significant reduction in NLRP3, cleaved caspase-1 p20, and mature IL-1β levels. Furthermore, the inhibitory impact of si-LBX2-AS1 on inflammasome activation was effectively reversed by co-transfection with the miR-654-3p inhibitor. These results demonstrate that the LBX2-AS1/miR-654-3p axis plays a pivotal role in regulating NLRP3 inflammasome activation in LPS-stimulated PDLCs. Discussion GCF, derived from periodontal tissue, is easy to collect and more accurate for reflecting periodontal diseases than serum samples. It has a stable composition, high specificity and sensitivity, and is widely used in collecting periodontitis diagnostic markers, unlike complex and easily interfered salivary samples [ 22 ]. For instance, the miR-200 family in GCF has been established as a valuable diagnostic tool for periodontitis [ 23 ]. Similarly, abnormal expression levels of miR-30b-3p and miR-125b-1-3p in the GCF of periodontitis patients are closely associated with inflammation and disease exacerbation [ 24 ]. Ren et al. discovered that the upregulation of LncRNA MIAT in the GCF of periodontitis patients correlates with disease progression and can serve as a potential early detection biomarker for periodontitis [ 25 ]. Consistent with these findings, our study also observed a significant upregulation of LBX2-AS1 in the GCF of periodontitis patients and The ROC curve further demonstrates that LBX2-AS1 exhibits high sensitivity and specificity in periodontitis. This indicated that LBX2-AS1 in GCF has the potential to be a biomarker for early detection of periodontitis. Previous studies have extensively demonstrated the critical role of LPS in the pathogenesis of periodontitis. It is capable of triggering an inflammatory response and influencing the activity of PDLCs, accelerating periodontal tissue destruction [ 26 ]. In this study, LPS treatment was stimulated inflammation in PDLCs, as indicated by elevated levels of IL-6, IL-1β, and TNF-α. This treatment also inhibited PDLCs proliferation, disrupting periodontal tissue homeostasis and repair. Furthermore, ALP is crucial for early mineralization, OCN reflects osteoblast function, and Runx2 regulates differentiation and maturation, all vital for bone tissue development [ 27 ]. In this study, LPS reduced ALP activity and the expression of OCN and Runx2 proteins, hindering osteogenic differentiation. Impaired osteoblast differentiation can lead to damage to periodontal support structures, which in turn can lead to periodontitis. Additionally, LPS enhanced PDLCs apoptosis, disturbing the normal physiology of periodontal tissues [ 28 ]. This echoes the results of previous cellular experiments and re-emphasises the severely disruptive effect of LPS as a major pathogenic factor on cellular homeostasis in periodontal tissues. This provides a reliable in vitro cellular model basis for subsequent investigation of the molecular regulatory mechanisms. The potential role of LBX2-AS1 in periodontitis was revealed in a related report by Su et al. [ 14 ] While its specific role in inflammatory diseases remains understudied, LBX2-AS1 is known to regulate various cellular processes in oncology [ 29 ]. On this basis, this study revealed for the first time that LBX2-AS1 expression increases in response to LPS stimulation in periodontitis, and knocking down LBX2-AS1 reverses LPS-induced cell damage. Other lncRNA studies in periodontitis also offer valuable insights. For instance, lncRNA HCP5 was found to enhance osteogenic differentiation of PDLCs by modulating the miR-24-3p/HO1/P38/ELK1 pathway, thereby mitigating periodontitis pathology [ 30 ]. Similarly, LINC01126 was shown to mediate the inflammatory response of PDLCs by sponging miR-518a-5p, regulating proliferation and apoptosis, and contributing to periodontitis development [ 31 ]. These findings indicated that LBX2-AS1 may act as a regulator of the PDLCs response to LPS through an unknown downstream signaling pathway. In our study, miR-654-3p, a downstream miRNA of LBX2-AS1, was of particular interest. Hao et al. identified miR-654-3p through microarray analysis and found its close association with the osteogenic differentiation of human periodontal ligament stem cells [ 32 ], suggesting its potential role in periodontitis development. Here, miR-654-3p was further shown to be downregulated in periodontitis patients and negatively correlated with LBX2-AS1. miR-654-3p has been shown to play an important role in the regulation of inflammatory responses in previous studies. For instance, in chondrocyte inflammatory responses, miR-654-3p modulates the inflammatory reaction by targeting TNFRSF9 as an NF-κB pathway activator [ 33 ]. In diabetic nephropathy, the downregulation of miR-654-3p inhibited MAPK6 expression, leading to the promotion of inflammatory factor release and the aggravation of the inflammatory response [ 34 ]. In this study, it was found that the inhibition of miR-654-3p worsened the LPS-induced inflammatory responses and damage on PDLCs and reversed the protective effect of LBX2-AS1. Likewise, in atherosclerosis, miR-654-3p reversed the impacts of LncRNA TP73-AS1 on oxidized LDL-induced inflammatory responses and apoptosis in endothelial cells by targeting AKT3 [ 35 ]. This not only elucidates the crucial role of miR-654-3p in the development of periodontitis but also suggests that LBX2-AS1 may further exacerbate cell damage by regulating miR-654-3p in mediating LPS-induced PDLCs inflammatory response. Further research has revealed that TLR2, which is directly targeted by miR-654-3p, worsened the LPS-triggered inflammation and cell damage when overexpressed, counteracting the impact of miR-654-3p. The significance of TLR2 in innate immunity and inflammation is widely acknowledged [ 36 , 37 ]. Previous periodontitis research has shown that TLR2 signaling activation is linked to disease progression [ 38 , 39 ]. TLR2 often plays a crucial part in signaling via the Myd88-dependent pathway [ 40 ]. MyD88 functions not only as a key downstream signaling ligand for the TLR2 receptor complex but also as an important adapter protein for the NF-κB pathway. As a nuclear mediator, the NF-κB pathway plays a central role in immune and inflammatory responses. Upon activation, the p65 subunit of NF-κB translocates to the nucleus, triggering the production of inflammatory cytokines [ 41 ]. For example, Gao et al. showed that inhibiting the TLR2/MyD88/NF-κB pathway with targeted molecules reduced inflammation and relieved neuropathic pain [ 42 ]. Based on this, this study discovered that miR-654-3p overexpression also decreased Myd88 and NF-κB protein levels in PDLCs. Thus, it was speculated that miR-654-3p blocks MyD88/NF-κB signaling by targeting TLR2, suppressing LPS-induced inflammation in PDLCs and reducing cell damage. TLR2 is a well-recognized upstream regulator of the NLRP3 inflammasome. Upon activation by PAMPs (e.g. LPS) or DAMPs, TLR2 triggers signaling cascades (e.g. NF-κB) to upregulate the transcription of NLRP3 and pro-IL-1β, providing the ‘priming signal’ for inflammasome activation[ 43 , 44 ]. Our results extend this relationship by demonstrating that the LBX2-AS1/miR-654-3p axis modulates the NLRP3 inflammasome through TLR2. Specifically, knockdown of LBX2-AS1 reduced NLRP3 and cleaved caspase-1 p20 protein levels and inhibited mature IL-1β secretion in LPS-stimulated PDLCs, while these effects were reversed by miR-654-3p inhibition. These findings suggest that LBX2-AS1, by sponging miR-654-3p and upregulating TLR2, enhances NLRP3 inflammasome activation, thereby amplifying the inflammatory response in periodontal tissues. This adds a novel layer to our understanding of how LBX2-AS1 contributes to periodontitis pathogenesis. However, this study has several limitations. Firstly, the interactions between this axis and other related signaling pathways remain unexplored, which restricts our understanding of the disease mechanism. Secondly, our measurement of TLR2 was confined to GCF and in vitro PDLCs; validation of its protein expression and localization within periodontal tissues from patients at different stages, for instance via immunohistochemistry, would strengthen our conclusions. Thirdly, the impact on immune cell regulation—particularly the Treg/Th17 balance and neutrophil-mediated responses—was not thoroughly examined. As neutrophils are the first line of defense in periodontitis and their dysregulation contributes significantly to tissue damage, future studies should investigate whether LBX2-AS1 modulates neutrophil recruitment or function (e.g. via IL-8) through the miR-654-3p/TLR2 axis. Furthermore, the precise molecular mechanism by which LBX2-AS1 sponges miR-654-3p, such as its binding affinity and subcellular localization, along with its direct effects on alveolar bone resorption via osteoclastogenesis, remain to be clarified. Finally, as this study relied mainly on cell experiments, further validation in animal models is essential to confirm the pathophysiological relevance and reliability of the results. In conclusion, our study suggests that LBX2-AS1 present in GCF may emerge as a novel diagnostic biomarker for periodontitis. It also appears to mediate LPS-induced inflammatory responses and cellular damage in PDLCs by regulating the miR-654-3p/TLR2/MyD88/NF-κB signaling pathway ( Figure 8 ). Figure 8. Open in a new tab Schematic of LBX2-AS1 mediating LPS-induced injury to PDLCs via regulation of Myd88/NF-κB pathway by miR-654-3p. Supplementary Material Figure S1.tif IANN_A_2554931_SM1528.tif (395.9KB, tif) Acknowledgments Fang Liu: Conceptualization; Data curation; Formal Analysis; Supervision; Validation; Writing–Original Draft; Writing–Review and Editing. Cheng Qian: Conceptualization; Data curation; Investigation; Methodology; Resources; Software; Writing–Review and Editing. Wenrui Wang: Conceptualization; Methodology; Supervision; Validation; Writing–Review and editing. Rongxiu Zhang: Conceptualization; Project administration; Resources; Writing–Review and Editing. Li Xu: Conceptualization; 