 Benitz WE, Achten NB. Finding a role for the neonatal early-onset sepsis risk calculator. EClinicalMedicine. 2020;19:100255. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 70. Ts R, Mp S, G B Syndromic Multiplex Polymerase Chain Reaction (mPCR) testing and antimicrobial stewardship: current practice and future directions. Current Infectious Dis Rep. 2021;23. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33679252/ . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 71. Maurer FP, Christner M, Hentschke M, Rohde H. Advances in rapid identification and susceptibility testing of bacteria in the clinical microbiology laboratory: implications for patient care and antimicrobial stewardship programs. Infect Dis Rep. 2017;9:6839. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement All the included studies are accessible via Pubmed.',41,'2025-10-13 06:15:18','2025-10-13 06:15:18');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1534,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11574222','Gestational Diabetes and Analysis of Maternal and Fetal Outcomes Among Pregnant Women: A Cross-Sectional Study.','Biju PB, Chaudhary RK, Radhakrishnan K, Shetty S, Mateti UV, Raju BN, Sonkusare S.','2023','dipsi polyhydramnios polyhydramnios dipsi teenage pih polyhydramnios 24th 28th dipsi 75g ngsmips ctri kuppuswamy 75g 24th 28th polyhydramnios releveled dipsi polyhydramnios polyhydramnios polyhydramnios bodyweight polyhydramnios microsomia dipsi seshiah assam morlando polyhydramnios polyhydramnios polyhydramnios koivunen actrapid staynova polyhydramnios ngsm ngsmips mangalore kshema mangalore uvm rkc bnr bnr rkc pbb rkc pbb uvm uvm ngsmips praisy biju raushan krishnapriya shraddha uday venkat mateti barma naga shipra sonkusare jci24531 karasneh migdady alzoubi amsu seshiah balaji balaji s35 lajili htira temessek mahanta deuri mahanta assam cegh riaz nawaz masood dipsi cegh kachhawa ijem ijem_582_17 monod kotzaeridi s00592 zargar bashir kashmiri subcontinent kuppuswamy intjcommunitydent v10i1 priyanka contracept ijrcog20183803 monir zeba jsf v16i1 odar wandabwa kiondo mulago rajput haryana koivunen viljakainen mannisto e0229496 muche olayemi gete sagheer hamdi minya ejim_11_18 morlando savoia ojog jadav feto contracept jdi staynova gueorguiev petkova gueorguieva','Abstract Purpose of the Study To determine the occurrence of gestational diabetes mellitus and analyze the maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women. Methods A prospective cross-sectional study was conducted for a duration of 7 months in the Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics at tertiary care hospital where 518 pregnant women participants of age above 18 years who had come for labor and undergone DIPSI (diabetes in pregnancy study of India) test were included in the study after the ethical clearance and obtaining written informed consent, whereas pregnant women with pre-existing diabetes mellitus and on other medication that alter glucose metabolism were excluded from the study. Results The occurrence of GDM was found to be 15.44%. The majority of GDM patients (53.75%) were able to manage their condition through dietary modifications alone. Metformin was required for 36.25% of the subjects, while 10% needed insulin. The rates of cesarean delivery (72.1%) and postpartum hemorrhage (17.5%) were also higher in the GDM group compared to the non-GDM group ( p < 0.001). Maternal complications, including gestational hypertension (11.3%), polyhydramnios (35%), and vaginal candidiasis (18.8%), were significantly more prevalent among GDM subjects ( p < 0.001). Conclusions GDM is a frequently observed complication among pregnant women which can lead to adverse maternal and fetal outcomes as well as several complications such as cesarean delivery, postpartum hemorrhage, pre-eclampsia, polyhydramnios, UTI and vaginal candidiasis as compared to those without GDM. Keywords: Adverse outcomes, Complications, DIPSI test, Gestational diabetes, Pregnant women Introduction The occurrence of Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM), characterized by diabetes or glucose intolerance during pregnancy, has been increasing globally [ 1 ]. Numerous studies have established a correlation between GDM and various adverse outcomes, particularly an elevated risk of pregnancy complications [ 2 , 3 ]. The prevalence of GDM varies among nations, with the USA reporting a range of 17.3 to 25.5%, Germany reporting a lower prevalence of 13.2%, and Asian countries reporting a combined frequency of 11.5% [ 4 ]. Indians womens are eleven times more likely to experience pregnancy-related diabetes mellitus, which can be attributed to factors such as the rising epidemic of childhood and teenage obesity, sedentary lifestyles, dietary changes, ongoing immigration from high-risk populations, and alterations in dietary habits [ 5 ]. Pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH), a higher likelihood of cesarean delivery, postpartum hemorrhage, preeclampsia, polyhydramnios, candidiasis, macrosomia, stillbirth, and shoulder dystocia are among the complications associated with GDM. Thus, it is recommended to regularly screen for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) between the 24th and 28th weeks of pregnancy for women with specific risk factors such as history of previous GDM, a pre-gestational BMI of 30 kg/m 2 or higher, previous occurrence of macrosomia, a first-degree relative with type 2 diabetes, maternal age of 40 years or older, previous prenatal loss or fetal death, and a history of polycystic ovary syndrome. Blood glucose screening is the most effective approach for reducing maternal and fetal problems in pregnant women [ 4 ]. The Diabetes in Pregnancy Study Group of India (DIPSI) recommends conducting a 75g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) in a non-fasting state. In this test, a single 2-h value exceeding 140 mg/dl is considered diagnostic for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) [ 6 ]. The treatment of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) has a positive impact on woman''s overall quality of life, reduces significant prenatal morbidity, and can potentially improve both fetal and maternal outcomes. Managing GDM typically involves early consultation with a dietitian and a diabetes educator, along with self-monitoring of capillary blood glucose levels before and after meals [ 7 , 8 ]. The prevalence of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) in India varies across regions and based on socioeconomic status, affecting a significant percentage of pregnant women who were not previously diagnosed with diabetes [ 9 ]. Studies indicate that the occurrence of GDM ranges from 3.8% to 21% in India [ 5 ]. Given the higher occurrence and its impact on both maternal and fetal outcomes, it is crucial to evaluate the management and effects of GDM. Therefore, this study aimed to assess the occurrence of GDM, as well as its maternal and fetal outcomes, in order to mitigate its effects. Material and Methods A cross-sectional observational study was conducted at the inpatient’s labor room of the gynecology department at tertiary care hospital for the duration of seven months. Ethical approval was obtained from the Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC) before commencing the study (Ref. No: NGSMIPS/IEC/05/2021) and also registered in the clinical trial registry of India (CTRI/2022/11/047267). This study included a total 518 pregnant women which was calculated using 0.0572 as expected proportion (p) at 2% expected precision and at 95% confidence level. This sample size was calculated using the formula; n = Z ∝ 2 2 p 1 - p d 2 All pregnant women above 18 years of age who had come for labor and who were willing to participate in the study were included in the study, whereas pregnant women with pre-existing diabetes mellitus, those on medication which alters glucose metabolism and those patients who were not willing to participate were excluded from the study. The data sources for the study included participant medical records, treatment charts, and laboratory data. The materials used in the study comprised of self-designed data collection form, an informed consent form, and a participant information sheet. Study Procedure Patient demographic details such as age, socioeconomic status, BMI, family history of diabetes was collected from participant medical records and face to face interview with patients. Modified Kuppuswamy socioeconomic scale 2023 was used to classify patients into different socioeconomic classes [ 10 ]. Laboratory investigations were performed for estimation of blood sugar levels. Diagnosis of GDM was made by GCT (Glucose Challenge Test) using 75g glucose which was performed between the 24th to 28th weeks of pregnancy. Patient was labeled as GDM if the value was ≥ 140 mg/dl (Fig. 1 ). Maternal outcomes like type of delivery, preeclampsia, polyhydramnios, vaginal candidiasis, postpartum hemorrhage were assessed. Fetal outcomes like macrosomia and late fetal death were determined. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Blood glucose profile of GDM patients releveled by DIPSI test Statistical Analysis The data were summarized using descriptive statistics, i.e., mean ± SD, frequency and percentage. Data were analyzed using unpaired t-test to compare between the two groups. Statistical analysis for categorical variable categories was compared using Chi-square test. Association of treatment modalities with complications, maternal and fetal outcomes were assessed using Pearson-chi square and ANOVA. p value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Analysis was carried out using SPSS version 20. Results Estimation of GDM Occurrence A total of 518 pregnant women were enrolled in the study, out of which 80 patients were diagnosed with GDM (Fig. 1 ) and 438 were non-GDM. The occurrence of GDM (GCT ≥ 140) was found to be 15.4% in our study. Assessment of Sociodemographic Characteristics of Subjects, Family History of Diabetes and Clinical History The mean age was found to be 28 ± 5.034 years in the non-GDM and 29.23 ± 5.45 years in the GDM group. Both groups were comparable in terms of age. Incidence of GDM increased as the age of subjects increased. In the 19–25 years age group, the incidence was 11% meanwhile in the age groups above 35, it was found to be 20%. The mean pre-pregnancy BMI in non-GDM group was 24.8 ± 2.8 and in GDM, it was 23.37 ± 2.7. Majority of the subjects had normal BMI (74.6% in non-GDM vs 58.75% in GDM group). The incidence of GDM was found to increase when the BMI increased. It was found to be 12% in women with normal weight, 22% in overweight women and 28.5% in obese women. Majority of GDM patients (72.5%) and non-GDM (66%) were found belonging to the upper lower socioeconomic class. The presence of family history of diabetes was found to be significantly higher in the GDM group. Around 44% of GDM subjects had a family history of diabetes mellitus, whereas only 13% of non-GDM subjects had a family history of diabetes mellitus. Overall, majority of subjects were in their second pregnancy (51.2% in GDM vs 47.5% in non-GDM) followed by third pregnancy (23% each in GDM and non-GDM groups). The highest incidence of GDM was observed among second parity followed by third, first, fourth and fifth. The details are summarized in Table 1 . Table 1. Baseline sociodemographic characteristics, family history and clinical history of GDM and Non-GDM Parameters Non GDM GDM p value Number of cases Percentage (%) Number of cases Percentage (%) Age in years 0.628 18–25 153 34.93 24 30 26–30 153 34.93 27 33.75 31–35 98 22.38 17 21.25 36–40 31 7.08 11 13.75 41–45 3 0.68 1 1.25 BMI category (kg/m 2 ) 0.459 Underweight BMI < 18.5 5 1.41 0 0 Normal (BMI:18.5–24.9) 327 74.6 47 58.75 Overweight (BMI: 25–29.9) 91 20.7 27 33.75 Obese (BMI > 30) 15 3.3 6 7.5 Socioeconomic class 0.703 Upper Upper middle 21 4.8% 4 5% Lower middle 107 24.4% 15 18.8% Upper lower 289 66% 58 72.5% Lower 21 4.8% 3 3.8% Family history of diabetes 57 13% 36 45% < 0.001* Family history of hypertension 56 12.8% 19 23.8% 0.01* Parity 0.463 First 92 21% 9 11.3% Second 208 47.5% 41 51.2% Third 101 23.1% 19 23.8% Fourth 28 6.4% 8 10% Fifth 6 1.4% 3 3.8% Sixth 3 7% 0 0 Open in a new tab *Statistical significance i.e., p < 0.05 BMI Body mass index Comparison of Maternal and Fetal Outcomes and Complication Among GDM and Non-GDM Overall, 72.1% of GDM subjects ( n = 58) and 32.6% of non-GDM subjects ( n = 143) underwent cesarean delivery. This difference was found to be statistically significant. Incidence of postpartum hemorrhage was also found to be significantly higher in the GDM subjects when compared with non-GDM subjects (17.5% vs 1.1%). On analyzing the antenatal complications, 35% of GDM subjects were found to have polyhydramnios, 18.8% had vaginal candidiasis and 11.3% subjects had gestational hypertension and UTI (Urinary Tract Infection). All these outcomes were increased when compared with non-GDM subjects and was found to be statistically significant. The details are summarized in Table 2 . Table 2. Comparison of antenatal complications and maternal and fetal outcomes of GDM and Non-GDM Parameters GDM Non-GDM p value Complications Gestational hypertension 11.3% 1.4% < 0.001* Polyhydramnios 35% 1.1% < 0.001* UTI 11.3% 1.4% < 0.001* Vaginal candidiasis 18.8% 0.9% < 0.001* Maternal outcomes Cesarean delivery 72.1% 32.6% < 0.001* Vaginal delivery 27.5% 67.4% < 0.001* Postpartum hemorrhage 17.5% 1.1% < 0.001* Fetal outcomes Baby weight 3.049 ± 0.582 3.010 ± 0.371 0.431 Macrosomia 5% 0 – Fetal hypoglycemia 0 0 – Fetal death 0 0 – Open in a new tab *Statistical significance i.e., p < 0.05 UTI Urinary tract infection Treatment Modalities used for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus On considering the mode of treatment of gestational diabetes, majority of the subjects (53.75%) responded to dietary modification or medical nutrition therapy. Around 36.25% required metformin and 10% were prescribed insulin. Further, we observed that there was significant association between gestational hypertension (Table 3 ) and weight of baby with different modalities of treatments (Table 4 ). The * represents the statistical significance i.e., p < 0.05 in Table 3 and Table 4 . Table 3. Association of complications, maternal and fetal outcomes with treatment modalities Parameters Treatments p value Diet ( n = 43) Metformin ( n = 29) Insulin ( n = 8) Complications Gestational hypertension No 97.7% 75.9% 87.5% 0.016* Yes 2.3% 24.1% 12.5% Polyhydramnios No 69.8% 58.6% 62.5% 0.616 Yes 30.2% 41.4% 37.5% UTI No 90.7% 86.2% 87.5% 0.834 Yes 9.3% 13.8% 12.5% Vaginal candidiasis No 65.1% 82.8% 75.0% 0.147 Yes 34.9% 17.2% 25.0% Maternal outcomes Cesarean delivery No 34.9% 17.2% 25.0% 0.255 Yes 65.1% 82.8% 75.0% Vaginal delivery No 88.4% 75.9% 62.5% 0.255 Yes 11.6% 24.1% 37.5% Postpartum hemorrhage No 83.7% 75.9% 100.0% 0.269 Yes 16.3% 24.1% 0.0% Fetal outcomes Macrosomia No 97.68% 93.10% 87.5% 0.404 Yes 2.32% 6.90% 12.5% Open in a new tab Table 4. Association of bodyweight with treatment modalities Parameter N Mean ± SD 95% CI F p value LB-UB Baby weight Diet 43 2.91 ± 0.66 2.70–3.11 3.448 0.037* Metformin 29 3.26 ± 0.38 3.12–3.41 Insulin 8 2.98 ± 0.52 2.54–3.42 Open in a new tab Discussion The current study highlights prevalence pattern of gestational diabetes mellitus (15.44%) among pregnant women and provides its management approach with the help of dietary modification, metformin and insulin. GDM has been linked to several complications, maternal and fetal outcomes, such as gestational hypertension, polyhydramnios, uti, vaginal candidiasis, cesarean delivery, vaginal delivery, postpartum hemorrhage, baby weight, microsomia, fetal hypoglycemia, fetal death etc. We found significant difference between occurrence of complications and maternal outcomes among GDM as compared to non-GDM group ( p < 0.001). Further, we found significant association of treatment modalities with gestational hypertension and baby weight ( p < 0.05). In the present study, the occurrence of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) was determined to be 15.44% based on DIPSI criteria, which was found to be higher when compared with a North-Indian study conducted by Kumari et al. [ 7 ] where the prevalence was reported as 5.7%. Conversely, in the study conducted by Seshiah et al., the prevalence of GDM was found to be slightly higher, at 17.8% in an urban population. The observed differences in these findings could potentially be attributed to variations in geographical locations, dietary habits, or the specific characteristics of the populations under investigation. In the current study, the highest proportion of GDM subjects (33.75%) fell into the age group of 26–30 years, which corresponds with the findings of an Indian study in which 35% of GDM subjects fell in the age group of 26–30 years [ 11 ]. Another cross-sectional study conducted in Bangladesh disclosed similar results in which majority of the GDM subjects fell in the same age group [ 12 ]. The mean age of GDM subjects was 29.23 ± 5.45 years, while the mean age of the non-GDM subjects was 28 ± 5.034 years. These results indicate that there was no statistically significant difference in age distribution between the GDM group and the non-GDM group. The BMI of majority of GDM subjects (74.65%) was in optimal category in this study. The mean BMI of GDM subjects was 23.37 ± 2.747, while that of non-GDM subjects was also 23.37 ± 2.747. No statistical association was found between BMI and GDM subjects or the non-GDM group. These findings correlate with a retrospective study which similarly reported the lack of significance in BMI between GDM and non-GDM groups [ 7 ]. In contrast to these findings, in a study conducted in Uganda, majority of GDM subjects (36.6%) belonged to overweight category and 30% belonged to obese category [ 13 ]. In this study, a higher prevalence of GDM (72.5%) was observed among individuals from the lower socioeconomic class, which aligns with the findings of an Indian study which also reported majority of GDM subjects from a similar socioeconomic background [ 14 ]. However, in contrast to these findings, a European study reported a majority of GDM subjects from the middle and upper socioeconomic class [ 15 ]. A significant statistical association was observed between the family history of diabetes mellitus and the occurrence of GDM in comparison to non-GDM subjects. The majority of GDM subjects (44%) had a family history of diabetes, which is consistent with the findings of Indian study demonstrating a significant association between a family history of diabetes and the occurrence of gestational diabetes [ 5 ]. In contrast to this an African study disclosed that a higher proportion of GDM subjects (78.5%) did not have a family history of diabetes mellitus [ 16 ]. During this study, it was observed that the highest incidence of GDM occurred during second pregnancies, accounting for 51.2% of the cases. Additionally, 23.8% of the GDM subjects experienced the condition during their third pregnancies, while 11.3% developed GDM during their first pregnancies. An Egyptian study showed similar results in which higher proportion of GDM (61.5%) occurred during second or third pregnancies and 32.5% of GDM subjects experienced the condition during their first pregnancies [ 17 ]. In the present study, cesarean delivery was significantly higher (72.1%) among GDM subjects when compared with non-GDM subjects (27.5%). A study conducted in Assam revealed parallel results as 60.7% of the GDM population underwent cesarean delivery when compared with non-GDM subjects [ 5 ]. Comparable results were found from another study conducted by Morlando et al. [ 18 ], which reported higher occurrence of cesarean delivery (34.7%) among GDM subjects. In addition, one study also showed that GDM subjects underwent cesarean delivery (60.3%) more than vaginal delivery [ 19 ]. On the contrary, a study conducted in North-India observed that there was no significant difference in the mode of delivery in GDM as compared to controls [ 7 ]. In the present study, in comparison to non-GDM groups, subjects with GDM were found to have a higher incidence of postpartum hemorrhage, with a rate of 17.5%. This observation was in line with the study carried out in Delhi which showed higher proportion of postpartum hemorrhage (1.2%) in GDM subjects when compared with control groups [ 7 ]. Another study also showed a statistical significance in the rate of postpartum hemorrhage between the case and control group [ 20 ]. Significant associations were found between maternal complications such as UTI (11.3%), vaginal candidiasis (18.8%), gestational hypertension (11.3%), and polyhydramnios (35%) in GDM subjects compared to the non-GDM group in this study. These findings correspond with a Chinese study where the occurrence of UTI, gestational hypertension, polyhydramnios, and vaginal candidiasis was high among GDM subjects [ 21 ]. Another Indian study also demonstrated notable complications among GDM subjects in comparison to controls, including gestational hypertension, polyhydramnios and vaginal candidiasis [ 22 ]. Among the overall enrolled subjects, the mean baby weight of GDM subjects was found to be 3.049 ± 0.58 and that of non-GDM subjects was 3.01 ± 0.37. This showed that there is no statistical difference between the baby weight of GDM subjects and non-GDM groups. Fetal macrosomia was seen among GDM subjects (4) which was associated with GDM. These findings were in support with a Scottish study where fetal macrosomia was positively associated with GDM [ 23 ]. On the contrary, the study conducted by Koivunen et al. [ 15 ], observed that baby weight was significantly higher among GDM groups as compared to non-GDM groups ( p = < 00.001). A retrospective study conducted in Delhi reported that 79.41% of the gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) subjects in their study adhered to a dietary approach as a form of management. Additionally, 12.35% of the subjects required insulin therapy, while 8.23% received oral hypoglycemic agents [ 7 ]. Similarly, in our study, we observed that 53.75% of the GDM subjects followed a dietary approach for managing their condition. In terms of medical interventions, 10% of the subjects received human actrapid, a type of insulin, while 36.25% were prescribed metformin, an oral hypoglycemic agent. Comparable results were observed in the study conducted by Staynova et al. [ 24 ], in which majority of GDM subjects (85.7%) followed diet and 14.3% followed insulin. These findings highlight the different approaches to manage GDM in the two studies. While both studies recognized the importance of dietary modifications, there were variations in the utilization of insulin and oral hypoglycemic agents. The choice of treatment may depend on factors such as the severity of GDM, individual patient characteristics, and the preferences of healthcare providers. Conclusion GDM is one of the frequently observed complications among pregnant women which can be managed via dietary modification and/ or with the use of pharmacological agents, such as metformin and insulin. GDM can cause adverse maternal outcomes and complications such as cesarean delivery, postpartum hemorrhage, pre-eclampsia, polyhydramnios, UTI and vaginal candidiasis when compared to those without GDM. These findings underscore the severity of GDM and its substantial influence on pregnancy outcomes. Consequently, it is strongly recommended to enhance maternal care and implement intervention strategies to minimize the occurrence of these potentially life-threatening obstetric complications and improve overall maternal outcomes. Acknowledgements We would like to thank Principal, NGSM Institute of Pharmaceutical Sciences (NGSMIPS), Mangalore, India and Department of Gynaecology and obstetrics, Justice K S Hegde Charitable Hospital (KSHEMA), Mangalore, India. Author Contributions Conceptualization: [UVM, KR], Methodology: [RKC, BNR], Formal analysis and investigation: [SS, BNR]; Writing—original draft preparation: [RKC, PBB]; Writing–review, revision and editing: [RKC, PBB, SS]; Supervision: [UVM, KR]. All authors critically revised the manuscript and approved the final version. UVM holds the final responsibility of the article. Funding No funds, grants, or other support was received. Declarations Conflict of interest The authors declare that they have no conflict of interest. Ethical Approval The study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC) on human subjects (Ref. No: NGSMIPS/IEC/05/2021) and the study was performed in accordance with the ethical standards as laid down in the 1964 Declaration of Helsinki. Informed Consent Informed consent was obtained from all individual participants included in the study. Footnotes Praisy B. Biju: Post Graduate Student, Department of Pharmacy Practice; Raushan Kumar Chaudhary: PhD Research Scholar, Department of Pharmacy Practice; Krishnapriya Radhakrishnan: Associate Professor, Consultant, Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology; Shraddha Shetty: Assistant Professor, Department of Community Medicine; Uday Venkat Mateti: Assistant Professor, Department of Pharmacy Practice; Barma Naga Raju: Assistant Professor, Department of Pharmacy Practice; Shipra Sonkusare: Professor, Consultant, Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology. Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Buchanan TA, Xiang AH. Gestational diabetes mellitus. J Clin Invest. 2005;115(3):485–91. 10.1172/JCI24531. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Karasneh RA, Migdady FH, Alzoubi KH, et al. Trends in maternal characteristics, and maternal and neonatal outcomes of women with gestational diabetes: a study from Jordan. Ann Med Surg (Lond). 2021;11(67):102469. 10.1016/j.amsu.2021.102469. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Seshiah V, Balaji V, Balaji MS, et al. Pregnancy and diabetes scenario around the world: India. Int J Gynaecol Obstet. 2009;104(Suppl 1):S35–8. 10.1016/j.ijgo.2008.11.035. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Lajili O, Htira Y, Temessek A, et al. Incidence of maternal and fetal outcomes in women with gestational diabetes. Tunis Med. 2022;100(3):241–6. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Mahanta TG, Deuri A, Mahanta BN, et al. Maternal and foetal outcome of gestational diabetes mellitus in a rural block of Assam, India. Clin Epidemiol Glob Health. 2014;2(1):9–15. 10.1016/j.cegh.2013.10.001. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Riaz M, Nawaz A, Masood SN, et al. Frequency of gestational diabetes mellitus using DIPSI criteria, a study from Pakistan. Clin Epidemiol Glob Health. 2019;7(2):218–21. 10.1016/j.cegh.2018.06.003. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kumari R, Dalal V, Kachhawa G, et al. Maternal and perinatal outcome in gestational diabetes mellitus in a tertiary care hospital in Delhi. Indian J Endocrinol Metab. 2018;22(1):116–20. 10.4103/ijem.IJEM_582_17. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Monod C, Kotzaeridi G, Linder T, et al. Prevalence of gestational diabetes mellitus in women with a family history of type 2 diabetes in first- and second-degree relatives. Acta Diabetol. 2023;60(3):345–51. 10.1007/s00592-022-02011-w. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Zargar AH, Sheikh MI, Bashir MI, et al. Prevalence of gestational diabetes mellitus in Kashmiri women from the Indian subcontinent. Diabetes Res Clin Pract. 2004;66(2):139–45. 10.1016/j.diabres.2004.02.023. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Kumar G, Dash P, Patnaik J, et al. Socioeconomic status scale-modified Kuppuswamy scale for the year 2022. Int J Community Dent. 2022;10(1):1–6. 10.56501/intjcommunitydent.v10i1.26. [ Google Scholar ] 11. Priyanka P. Maternal and fetal outcome in participants of gestational diabetes mellitus. Int J Reprod, Contracept, Obstet Gynecol. 2018;7(9):3831–6. 10.18203/2320-1770.ijrcog20183803. [ Google Scholar ] 12. Monir N, Zeba Z, Rahman A. Comparison of knowledge of women with gestational diabetes mellitus and healthy pregnant women attending at hospital in Bangladesh. J Sci Found. 2018;16(1):20–6. 10.3329/jsf.v16i1.38175. [ Google Scholar ] 13. Odar E, Wandabwa J, Kiondo P. Maternal and fetal outcome of gestational diabetes mellitus in Mulago hospital, Uganda. Afr Health Sci. 2004;4(1):9–14. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Rajput R, Yadav Y, Nanda S, et al. Prevalence of gestational diabetes mellitus & associated risk factors at a tertiary car e hospital in Haryana. Indian J Med Res. 2013;137(4):728. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Koivunen S, Viljakainen M, Männistö T, et al. Pregnancy outcomes according to the definition of gestational diabetes. PLoS ONE. 2020;15(3):e0229496. 10.1371/journal.pone.0229496. [ DOI ] [ PMC free a