INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1537,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11334951','3D vector MR elastography applications in small organs.','Atamaniuk V, Hańczyk Ł, Chen J, Pozaruk A, Obrzut M, Gutkowski K, Domka W, Cholewa M, Ehman RL, Obrzut B.','2024','modulus viscoelastic modulus wavefield propagating displacements propagating elastograms magnet tubing drum vibrations propagating meg displacements microns propagating snapshots wavefield displacements wavefield modulus imaginary modulus modulus modulus modulus modulus imaginary modulus viscous tangent modulus modulus damping tangent modulus arctangent viscoelastic modulus modulus contoured curl threading linux modulus modulus modulus elastograms _the modulus viscoelastic modulus viscous viscoelastic stiffer viscoelastic fibroid fibroid fibroid fibroid viscoelastic 3dmre modulus modulus modulus modulus modulus modulus contoured sited modulus contouring ehman sternothyroid sternohyoid wrapping cricoid isthmus bahn modulus modulus modulus modulus bahn bahn kugel ehman perineum transurethral endorectal prostates transurethral vibrations endorectal actuator vibrations actuator perineum indwelling modulus fibroid fibroid stiffer jondal modulus modulus popf popf mfp mfp vibrations moduli repeatable modulus sahebjavaher viscoelastic sahebjavaher nawa r01eb001981 ramiao rynkevic barroso mechanobiol s10237 krupski gundersen moskaluk gerling 15_suppl e15109 e15109 15_suppl e15109 manduca ehman chalasani younossi lavine s103 s117 flamm ytter gastroenterological gastroenterological gultekin wetz tomoelastography cancers13205185 tomoelastography asbach aldoj fluidity elsholtz fhj marticorena multifrequency tomoelastography tomoelastography tomoelastography multifrequency tzschatzsch dittmann tomoelastography multifrequency propagating dittmann tomoelastography multifrequency pressurized nijiati s40644 habermann munscher bier sinkus guertler anisotropic ehman iembs felmlee greenleaf riederer ehman sack actuator guglielmo ehman rossman sahakian ehman chatelin charpentier corbin meylheuc vappou kolipaka ac145a barnhill ariyurek fehlner sack multifrequency hmdi yoshimitsu shinagawa mitsufuji mrms buntkowsky bernarding tolxdorff sack oscillators s0730 725x numano reeder manduca bayly ehman asbach streitberger jondal pancreaticoenteric bahn bahn kugel ehman arani plewes endorectal arani plewes transurethral dittmann tomoelastography multifrequency pressurized sigrist liau kaffas chammas thno wartelle bladou sack johrens wurfel viscoelastic powerlaw c3sm50552a c3sm50552a hesley gorny woodrum a13 a13 taran obrzut obrzut zmuda ginekol a2020 liyanage hogarth makdissi sinkus ismrm intl ehman takehara kawase sinkus lorenzen nolte ernsting stork rofo sahebjavaher baghani honarvar sinkus salcudean sahebjavaher honarvar nbm','Abstract Background: Magnetic resonance elastography (MRE) is a rapidly developing medical imaging technique that allows for quantitative assessment of the biomechanical properties of the tissue. MRE is now regarded as the most accurate noninvasive test for detecting and staging liver fibrosis. A two-dimensional (2D MRE) acquisition version is currently deployed at more than 2000 locations worldwide. 2D MRE allows for the evaluation of the magnitude of the complex shear modulus, also referred to as stiffness. The development of 3D vector MRE has enabled researchers to assess the biomechanical properties of small organs where wave propagation cannot be adequately analyzed with the 2D MRE imaging approach used in the liver. In 3D vector MRE, the shear waves are imaged and processed throughout a 3D volume and processed with an algorithm that accounts for wave propagation in any direction. Additionally, the motion is also imaged in x, y, and z directions at each voxel, allowing for more advanced processing to be applied. Purpose: This review describes the technical principles of 3D vector MRE, surveys its clinical applications in small organs, and discusses potential clinical significance of 3D vector MRE. Conclusion: 3D vector MRE is a promising tool for characterizing the biomechanical properties of small organs such as the uterus, pancreas, thyroid, prostate, and salivary glands. However, its potential has not yet been fully explored. Keywords: Magnetic resonance elastography, MRE, tissue stiffness, viscosity, viscoelastic properties of the tissue Graphical Abstract Introduction Medical imaging technologies have revolutionized medical practice by allowing the internal anatomy of the body to be visualized non-invasively and by allowing changes in tissue properties caused by disease to be detected. However, until recently, it was not possible to assess the mechanical properties of the tissue with available imaging technologies. Many diseases cause changes in tissue stiffness and for centuries physicians have used the physical examination technique of palpation to detect tumors and other abnormalities in regions of the body accessible by touch. The mechanical characteristics of the tissue vary significantly between normal and pathological states 1 , 2 . However, palpation is a subjective technique and is suitable only for superficial tissues. Conventional magnetic resonance imaging and ultrasonography imaging methods are not capable of directly visualizing the mechanical properties of tissue quantitatively. However, the advent of shear wave elastography, implemented in both modalities, has now made the routine quantitative evaluation of the mechanical properties of tissue possible. Specifically, magnetic resonance elastography (MRE) is a rapidly advancing technology that is already considered to be the most accurate method for the diagnosis of liver fibrosis, a common disease 3 - 6 . The current regulatory-approved commercial versions of MRE for evaluating the liver use special hardware to generate shear waves in the liver that can be adequately imaged and analyzed with a two-dimensional (2D MRE) slice-by-slice approach to generate valid results in the form of stiffness maps representing the complex shear modulus of the liver. In 2D MRE all waves are assumed to propagate approximately parallel with the slice plane, which is correct in large parenchymal organs like the liver. However, several studies that used advanced 2D MRE technology have also shown its feasibility in numerous smaller organs, for instance, the pancreas, parotid glands, or prostate, where fairly good detail resolution was achieved. 7 - 14 Nevertheless, imaging and analyzing applied shear waves in three-dimensional space using a robust technique known as 3D vector MRE may be beneficial for the accurate evaluation of organs other than liver 15 , 16 . In contrast to 2D MRE, 3D vector MRE overcomes the shear wave propagation-related assumptions by imaging the wavefield throughout a volume, acquiring motion in x, y, and z, and then employing an inversion algorithm that uses a 3D kernel to account for wave propagation in all directions in 3D space. This avoids the errors that occur when a 2D inversion is used on data that do not meet the underlying assumption that waves are propagating only in direction parallel to the plane of section. Imaging tissue motion in three directions at each voxel also enables the application of more sophisticated image processing algorithms. Nevertheless, despite its superior performance, to date, 3D vector MRE is predominantly used for research rather than clinical purposes. This is primarily attributed to the limited availability of the technique and the necessity for large-scale multicenter validation studies. Many of the previously reported studies were conducted with relatively small, non-representative study groups. This review describes the physical principles of 3D vector MRE and surveys potential clinical applications that have been explored in organs such as the uterus, salivary glands, pancreas, thyroid, and prostate glands. Physical principles of MRE An MRE examination consists of three steps: Generating shear waves in tissue; Imaging the tissue displacements caused by the propagating shear waves; Processing the wave images using an inversion algorithm to create cross-sectional maps of tissue stiffness - also known as elastograms. Shear waves can be generated in the body using either internal or external sources of excitation 17 - 20 . Commercially available, regulatory-cleared versions of MRE generate shear waves in the body with a system that consists of an active device located outside the magnet room which generates air pressure waves that are conducted by flexible tubing to a passive driver that is placed against the body wall 21 . The passive driver is an MRI-safe drum-like device that applies vibrations that create propagating shear waves in the range of 20-150 Hz in the body. Passive drivers of different sizes and shapes can be customized for specific applications 22 . To visualize the shear wave propagation in tissue, a phase-contrast MRI sequence with special MRE motion-encoding gradients applied in the chosen direction (2D MRE) or all directions consecutively (3D vector MRE) is used. MRE sequences may be based on gradient-recalled echo (GRE) or spin-echo echo planar imaging (SE-EPI) 2D MRI acquisitions with motion encoding gradients (MEG). The motion-encoding gradients are synchronized with the mechanical excitation and the resulting images can display minute displacements (as small as a few microns) caused by propagating shear waves 23 . The timing relationship between the motion-encoding gradients and the applied shear waves is varied to acquire several snapshots of shear wave propagation throughout the wave cycle. MRE post-processing is designed to calculate tissue stiffness, based on information in the wave images. Various inversion algorithms have been described for MRE, including direct wave equation inversion, local spatial frequency estimation, phase-gradient techniques, and others 24 - 29 . Current, commercially available implementations use a standardized “2D MRE” method that has been optimized for assessing liver stiffness to diagnose hepatic fibrosis. The driver system is designed to provide a spatial pattern of shear waves in the liver that can be analyzed adequately in individual axial sections. The motion encoding gradients are sensitized in the z direction to encode the displacement perpendicular to the axial plane 30 . This 2D MRE approach works well for detecting and staging liver fibrosis and the entire acquisition time for a standard liver exam can be as short as a single breath-hold of 12-20 seconds. In most other applications of MRE, such as evaluating other abdominal organs, the spatial pattern of shear waves is much more complex than in the liver. It is therefore necessary to image the wavefield in three dimensions (3D) 31 . In addition, it is usually also needed to image displacements in x, y, and z directions. The requirement to image the wavefield in 3D space, with three directions of motion encoding and at several times in the wave cycle causes the acquisition time to be longer than for 2D MRE. However, by obtaining 3D motion data, reliable estimation of additional biomechanical parameters becomes possible, which is beneficial both for small organs and the liver. In 3D vector elastography, not only the magnitude of shear modulus (∣G*∣) may be measured but also its real and imaginary components can be calculated on a more robust basis than with 2D MRE. These components are referred to as the storage modulus (G’) and loss modulus (G”) respectively ( Figure 1 ). The storage modulus is real part of the complex shear modulus and is a measure of the elastic property of the tissue. The loss modulus is the imaginary component of the complex shear modulus and measures the viscous properties of the tissue 32 . Surrogates like the loss factor or loss tangent (a ratio between loss modulus and storage modulus), damping ratio (ξ, calculated as half of the loss tangent), or the phase angle of the shear modulus (φ, arctangent of the loss factor) can also be derived. These MRE parameters are related to tissue viscosity rather than elasticity and are good markers of tissue inflammation 32 . The 3D vector MRE uses the same hardware equipment (MRI coils, active and passive drivers) as 2D MRE; the difference between these two methods lies in the way the wave propagation is imaged and the wave data is processed. The slices acquired in 3D vector MRE are typically thinner than in 2D MRE, so that smaller inclusions may be detected. Nevertheless, both 2D MRE and 3D vector MRE of small organs usually require the use of a custom passive driver and an organ-specific excitation frequency. Depending on the size of small organs, the scan time can take 1 to 5 minutes. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Magnitude image (A) and the viscoelastic components – storage modulus (B) and loss modulus (C) obtained in 60-Hz 3D vector MRE of the parotid glands. Parotid glands are contoured with a green line. The inversion processing for 3D vector MRE is also more complex than for 2D MRE. The 3D vector MRE processing takes into account shear wave propagation in 3-dimensional space, rather than just in 2D. In addition, the 3D MRE data set allows for the mathematical curl operation to be performed on the data, thus removing the stationary or longitudinal wave field. Additional directional filter directions are also employed to separate possible wave interference. Therefore, there is a need for higher computation resources and a longer processing time. Processing times for 2D MRE are typically around 4 seconds per image location. With multi-threading, 3D MRE processing times under 2 minutes are typical using 16 cores for 32 slices on a standard Linux system. However, the richer data obtained with 3D vector MRE acquisitions allows MRE to be extended to the complex anatomy of small organs and structures and for accurate calculation of the individual components of the complex shear modulus (storage modulus and loss modulus). Applications of MRE in small organs Due to the differences in the anatomy, size, and shape of the small organs compared to the liver, the imaging and inversion parameters used in 3D vector MRE of such organs like processing kernel width, vibration frequency, and amplitude are modified according to achieve the optimal spatial resolution at the required penetration depth. The spatial resolution of MRE may be depend on vibration frequency as well as voxel size of the elastograms 33 . The higher excitation frequency results in shear waves of a shorter wavelength and hence enhances the inversion accuracy, leading to higher resolution. However, shorter waves also attenuate faster, which limits the penetration depth. The typical mechanical excitation frequencies used in the MR elastography of small organs are as follows: 30 to 60 Hz for the uterus 34 , 35 , 25 to 80 Hz for the salivary glands 10 , 36 , 30 to 60 Hz for the pancreas 7 , 8 , 37 , 38 , 50 to 100 Hz for the thyroid 36 , 39 , and 60 to 300 Hz for the prostate 9 , 40 - 43 . Besides, custom passive drivers fixed in proximity to the examined organ are often used when performing the 3D vector MRE of these organs since they are tailored to the specific shape of the examined body part, providing superior performance._The use of custom drivers addresses the wave attenuation issue since the distance traveled by shear waves to the area of interest is minimized as is attenuation. 1). Uterus Conventional imaging modalities including computed tomography, magnetic resonance imaging, and especially ultrasonography are used extensively to evaluate the uterus in clinical practice. There is also an emerging interest in applying elastography techniques to characterize uterine pathology. Ultrasound-based shear wave elastography has been explored for this application and performed quite well 44 . 2D and 3D vector MRE offer potential advantages as more robust methods that can evaluate a larger volume of tissue, without the need for an acoustic window and with less depth limitation 45 , 46 . Most exploratory studies of uterine 3D vector MRE have used a driver that is placed over the anterior pelvic wall to generate shear waves in pelvic organs ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab 60-Hz 3D vector MRE of the uterus in a 46-year-old and 60-kg patient with multiple uterine fibroids (shown in the 4 ROIs). Magnitude images in axial planes (A), and 0-8 kPa magnitude of complex shear modulus map (B). An early study in a group of sixteen healthy volunteers showed the feasibility of uterine MRE and provided evidence that the stiffness of the uterus and cervix varies during the menstrual cycle 34 . This is also one of a few studies where investigators focused on evaluating the full set of viscoelastic properties of the uterine tissue represented as a magnitude and the phase angle of the complex shear modulus G*, which describe the elastic and viscous behavior of the tissue, respectively. While the magnitude of G* provides information similar to 2D MRE (how stiff the tissue is), the phase angle of G* corresponded to the micro-architectural variations as this parameter was shown to be sensitive to the topology of the underlying viscoelastic network 47 . Several studies have applied 2D MRE or 3D vector MRE to assess uterine leiomyomas 35 , 48 - 50 . Leiomyomas, also known as fibroids, are the most common benign uterine tumors and represent pathological changes in the myometrial smooth muscle. Although leiomyomas are often asymptomatic, some may cause pelvic pain and other problems and require surgical treatment. Fibroids are usually stiffer on palpation than surrounding tissues, and this suggests that assessing their viscoelastic properties may provide clinically helpful information 50 . An early study found significant heterogeneity in the stiffness of uterine leiomyomas 49 . Another study confirmed this finding and showed correlations between lesion stiffness and the histologic composition of the fibroid 50 . Early studies used 2D MRE, however, a more recent study comparing 2D MRE and 3D vector MRE has shown similar findings 35 . Still, the assumption of in-plane wave propagation made in 2D MRE caused the overestimation of fibroid stiffness and higher standard deviation of stiffness within the regions of interest. For this reason, authors suggest using primarily 3D MRE as it allows for detailed characterization of the fibroid and a more accurate representation of the overall physiological parameters of the uterine fibroid. Preliminary studies have explored the potential of 3D vector MRE to provide clinically useful information in uterine cancer, especially in the preoperative assessment of tumor aggressiveness 15 . MRE-based measurements of the stiffness of endometrial carcinoma have been shown to correlate with the myometrial invasion depth, grade of tumor differentiation, and International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage, which may be helpful for treatment planning and risk stratification 15 . 2). Salivary glands Ultrasonography is the primary imaging modality in most cases for evaluating the salivary glands. Additional evaluation with CT and MRI may also be used 51 . Given that many tumors involving salivary glands are first detected by palpation, there has been interest in exploring the potential of quantitative elastography techniques in this condition. Several studies investigated the feasibility of using MRE to assess the viscoelastic characteristics of the salivary glands and tumors 10 , 16 , 52 . Due to the small size and location, applying 3DMRE to image the salivary glands is technically challenging ( Figure 3 ), though beneficial compared to 2D MRE, as the viscosity-related parameters estimated in 3D MRE are of particular interest in the diagnostics of salivary glands tumors, as they may have a relationship to invasiveness 10 Investigators have developed a specialized mechanical driver for such applications and have applied it for salivary gland 3D vector MRE, successfully assessing the storage modulus (G’) and loss modulus (G”) of normal parotid tissue 36 . MRE-based assessment of salivary gland tumors has demonstrated increased stiffness, elasticity, and viscosity values (represented as the magnitude of shear modulus, storage modulus, and loss modulus, respectively) in the lesions compared with normal glandular tissue 52 . Figure 3. Open in a new tab 60-Hz 3D vector MRE of salivary glands (upper row) in a healthy volunteer obtained with a custom-made passive driver placed over both sides of the subject’s face and the 2D MRE of the benign parotid tumor (lower row) acquired with the same driver. Magnitude images (A and C), and 0-8 kPa magnitude of complex shear modulus maps (B and D). Parotid glands are outlined with red contours in (A) and (B), and the tumor mass is contoured with a red line in (C) and (D). 3). Pancreas Investigators have explored the potential role of MRE in pancreatic imaging, motivated by the limitations of existing modalities in characterizing benign and malignant diseases that affect this organ. Application of MRE to the pancreas is technically challenging because of spatial resolution requirements and the potential difficulty of adequately illuminating this deeply sited organ with shear waves. Considering the location of the pancreas in the body, it was found that the low vibration frequencies ensure adequate spatial resolution with sufficient penetration depth. Investigators have used specialized acoustic drivers placed directly over the pancreas to accomplish adequate shear wave amplitude in the pancreas at frequencies in the range of 30-60 Hz 8 , 38 , 53 . Pilot studies in healthy volunteers have used MRE to characterize the normal stiffness values of the head, body, and tail of the pancreas in healthy volunteers 53 , 54 . Pancreatic MRE has been shown to be promising for differentiating between benign and malignant pancreatic masses and for predicting the likelihood of postoperative fistula after pancreatic resection 11 , 37 , 38 . Additionally, 3D vector MRE of the pancreas was found to be highly reproducible in terms of stiffness evaluation 55 . An example of a pancreas 3D vector MRE is shown in Figure 4 . Figure 4. Open in a new tab 40-Hz 3D vector MRE of the normal pancreas (upper row) and the pancreas affected by the pancreatic adenocarcinoma (lower row). Magnitude MRE image (A), T2w image (C) and magnitude of complex shear modulus maps (B and D) with a yellow solid line delineating the normal pancreas in (A) and (B), and a yellow dashed line contouring the tumor mass in (C) and (D). (C) and (D) were adapted from “Differentiation of benign and malignant solid pancreatic masses using magnetic resonance elastography with spin-echo echo planar imaging and three-dimensional inversion reconstruction: a prospective study.”, Shi Y, Gao F, Li Y, Tao S, Yu B, Liu Z, Liu Y, Glaser KJ, Ehman RL, Guo Q. Eur Radiol. 2018 Mar;28(3):936-945. DOI: 10.1007/s00330-017-5062-y ., Copyright (2019), with permission from Springer Nature. 4). Thyroid gland Applying MRE to evaluate the thyroid gland has similar challenges to those encountered in salivary gland imaging. The thyroid gland lies posterior to the sternothyroid and sternohyoid muscles, wrapping around the cricoid cartilage and tracheal rings, and is divided into two lobes that are connected by the isthmus. While the superficial location of the thyroid gland makes close placement of a specialized shear wave driver feasible, few published studies have assessed this application. In an early 2D MRE study, Bahn et al. investigated the feasibility of 2D MRE of the thyroid gland and achieved adequate shear wave illumination of this organ at 80-100 Hz, using a specialized driver. The investigators also compared thyroid stiffness in healthy volunteers and patients with Hashimoto''s thyroiditis patients 39 . Affected thyroid glands proved to have higher stiffness values than normal glands ( Figure 5 ). Another early study assessed the complex shear modulus of thyroid tissue in healthy volunteers and a single patient with thyroid cancer using 3D vector MRE 36 . In this study, a lower vibration frequency of 50 Hz was successfully implemented to minimize shear wave attenuation caused by the proximity of bone tissues. The authors reported a decrease in both storage modulus and loss modulus of thyroid cancer compared to normal thyroid 36 . However, this study was rather focused on describing the new driver design and the study group comprised only one healthy volunteer and one patient with thyroid cancer, so defining the clinical significance of this finding is not possible. Figure 5. Open in a new tab T1w anatomic images of the normal thyroid (A) and thyroid affected by Hashimoto''s thyroiditis (C) along with the corresponding magnitude of complex shear modulus maps (B and D). Adapted from “Development and application of magnetic resonance elastography of the normal and pathological thyroid gland in vivo”, Bahn MM, Brennan MD, Bahn RS, Dean DS, Kugel JL, Ehman RL, J Magn Reson Imaging 2009; 30(5):1151-4. DOI: 10.1002/jmri.21963 .., Copyright (2009), with permission from John Wiley and Sons. 5). Prostate gland Investigators have explored the technical feasibility of prostate MRE and the potential to use this unique technology to help characterize prostatic disease and assess the effects of treatment. An active area of investigation has been to find the best approach for generating suitable shear waves in the prostate gland. A variety of approaches have been tested, including external drivers on the anterior pelvic wall and perineum, as well as transurethral and endorectal devices 14 , 40 , 41 , 56 - 58 . An early study tested a driver device that was placed over the pubic bone, which reportedly provided adequate shear wave illumination to allow stiffness measurements of the central and peripheral zones in a series of healthy volunteers 56 . Another early study obtained promising results in prostate-simulating phantoms and canine prostates using a transurethral driver delivering vibrations at 200 Hz 41 . The same research team later evaluated the technical feasibility and patient tolerability of performing MRE elastography with the use of an endorectal actuator 40 . Among 12 volunteers with prostate cancer, no intolerance to the vibrations generated by the actuator was observed. Another approach, using an external driver applied to the perineum, was successfully tested in healthy subjects and patients with prostate cancer 57 . The investigators reported that malignant lesions did not always have higher stiffness than benign prostate tissue 59 . A more recent study used an array of small external passive drivers to generate shear waves in the prostate for MRE 14 . This study reported shear wave speed values in the peripheral zone, the central gland, and the entire prostate in healthy volunteers and reported no significant difference between them. Another recent study employed a driver positioned over the anterior pelvic wall to generate suitable shear waves in the prostate at 60 and 90 Hz 60 . The investigators evaluated a group of 49 patients with prostate cancer. The authors reported that MRE-based measurements of the stiffness of the prostate tumor were highly predictive of the presence of lymph node metastasis in this series of patients. A later publication from the same research group evaluated the performance of a driver system consisting of a vibration device which was coupled externally to a standard indwelling urinary catheter 42 . The system was tested in a series of 35 patients and demonstrated that this approach provides reliable shear wave illumination of the prostate at frequencies as high as 150 Hz, enhancing the MR spatial resolution of the technique. An example of prostate MRE in a patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia is shown in Figure 6 . Figure 6. Open in a new tab Magnitude T2w image (A) and a 60 Hz 0-8 kPa magnitude of complex shear modulus map (B) obtained in a 3D vector MRE of the prostate gland in a 71-year-old patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia. An external driver was attached to a silicone urethral catheter that had been placed prior to the exam for clinical reasons. Conclusions This review summarizes the current knowledge on the applications of the 3D vector MRE in small organs ( Table 1 and Table 2 ) and highlights the promise of this technique in the examination of the uterus, pancreas, thyroid, prostate, and salivary glands. While 3D vector MRE proved its versatility and effectiveness, its potential has not yet been fully demonstrated and numerous points still need to be developed to improve the performance of 3D vector MRE in these organs. Table 1. A table summarizing recent papers on the applications of 3D MRE in the selected small organs. Organ Purpose Results & Conclusions References Uterus To investigate the feasibility of uterine 3D MRE and the variation of uterine stiffness during the menstrual cycle. High-resolution maps of the uterus and cervix were obtained showing the changes in the biomechanical properties of the uterus during the menstrual cycle and the elasticity and viscosity values of the uterine corpus and cervix. The uterine corpus had similar viscosity but higher elasticity than the cervix. Jiang et al. 34 To examine the correlation between MRI-derived imaging characteristics of the uterine fibroid and its stiffness measured with 3D vector MRE. A statistically significant relationship between fibroid stiffness and T2 intensity was found - stiffer fibroids were darker in T2. Jondal et al. 35 To evaluate the utility of the 3D vector MRE in predicting the aggressiveness of endometrial carcinoma. The 3D vector MRE-derived uterine tumor stiffness showed a positive correlation with the myometrial invasion rate and may be a helpful parameter in the preoperative assessment of endometrial carcinoma. Zhang et al. 15 Parotid glands To develop a driver enabling 3D MRE of the head and neck and evaluate its performance in MRE of the parotid gland, thyroid, and lymph nodes. The novel driver enabled 3D MRE of the parotid glands, and the storage modulus and loss modulus values of the normal parotid gland obtained using this method were 1.12 kPa and 0.48 kPa, respectively. Yeung et al. 36 To assess the added value of 3D MRE for diagnosis of salivary gland masses. The MRE of salivary masses was found to be feasible and potentially helpful in their evaluation. While no significant difference in the values of the biomechanical properties of the tumors and normal tissue was observed in the pooled data from all patients, tumors exhibited higher values compared to healthy tissue in the same patient. Bhatia et al. 52 Pancreas To investigate the relationship between MRE parameters and occurrence of the postoperative pancreatic fistula (POPF). Low pancreatic stiffness was shown to be an independent predictor of high-grade POPF, highlighting the importance of the preoperative assessment of the pancreatic stiffness using 3D vector MRE. Shi et al. 38 To assess the diagnostic performance of 3D vector MRE in differentiating benign (mass-forming pancreatitis - MFP) and malignant (pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma - PDAC) pancreatic masses. MRE enables non-invasive differentiation between benign and malignant pancreatic masses. The stiffness of MFP was significantly higher than the stiffness of healthy pancreatic tissue and lower than that of PDAC. Shi et al. 37 To test the feasibility of non-invasive evaluation of the healthy pancreas using 3D vector MRE. 3D vector MRE was found to be a reliable and reproducible method for the evaluation of pancreatic stiffness with data being more consistent when acquired at 40 Hz vibrations, and the mean pancreatic stiffness was 1.15 ± 0.17 kPa at 40 Hz. Shi et al. 53 To investigate the reproducibility and repeatability of 3D vector MRE for the evaluation of pancreatic stiffness of healthy pancreas and pancreas affected by pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma and chronic pancreatitis. 3D vector MRE of the pancreas was found to be highly reproducible in all patients. The calculated intraclass correlation coefficient was 0.975 across the two time points. An et al. 55 Thyroid To describe a driver for the 3D MRE examination of the head and neck and evaluate its performance in 3D MRE of the parotid gland, thyroid, and lymph nodes. The implementation of the novel driver was successful, enabling the 3D MRE of the thyroid gland. The assessed values of the storage and loss moduli were 0.58 kPa and 0.42 kPa for healthy thyroid and 0.17 kPa and 0.28 kPa for thyroid cancer, respectively. Yeung et al. 36 Prostate To test the repeatability of the trans perineal 3D MRE in phantoms and volunteer studies and its diagnostic performance in prostate cancer patients. Prostate 3D MRE was found to be highly repeatable with an intraclass correlation coefficient ranging from 73% to 99%. The stiffness of cancer tissue was not always higher than that of healthy tissue. Still, the MRE-derived elastic modulus allowed for the diagnosis of prostate cancer with a mean AUC of 0.7. Sahebjavaher et al. 59 To evaluate the correlation between prostate cancer stiffness assessed by MRE and the extent of lymph node metastasis (LNM). MRE-derives prostate cancer stiffness enables non-invasive preoperative prediction of LNM, and when combined with the maximum diameter of prostate cancer, the 100% sensitivity and 91.9% specificity in predicting LNM was achieved. Hu et al. 60 To investigate the feasibility of high-frequency prostate MRE and assess its added diagnostic value in patients with lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS). 3D vector MRE of the prostate is feasible at frequencies as high as 150 Hz, and the higher frequencies provide images of better quality and improve the diagnostic performance (AUC was 0.944 at 150 HZ compared to 0.575 at 60 Hz). Deng et al. 42 Open in a new tab Table 2. Summary of reported values for selected tissue viscoelastic properties of small organs assessed using 3D vector MRE. Healthy Tissue References Abnormal tissue References Uterus Uterine corpus ∣G*∣ = 2.58 ± 0.52 kPa φ = 0.54 ± 0.08 Cervix ∣G*∣ = 2.00 ± 0.34 kPa φ = 0.57 ± 0.12 Jiang et al. 34 Type I endometrial carcinoma tumor ∣G*∣ = 2.88 ± 0.85 kPa Type II endometrial carcinoma tumor ∣G*∣ = 3.67 ± 0.59 kPa Zhang et al. 15 Parotid Glands Normal parotid G'' = 1.12 ± 0.13 kPa Normal parotid G" = 0.48 ± 0.14 kPa Yeung et al. 36 Salivary tumor G'' = 0.73 ± 0.43 kPa Salivary tumor G" = 0.31 ± 0.19 kPa Salivary tumor ∣G*∣ = 0.83 ± 0.47 kPa Bhatia et al. 52 Pancreas Normal pancreas ∣G*∣ = 1.21 ± 0.12 kPa Shi et al. 37 F0 Pancreatic fibrosis ∣G*∣ = 1.24 ± 0.13 kPa F1 Pancreatic fibrosis ∣G*∣ = 1.42 ± 0.12 kPa F2 Pancreatic fibrosis ∣G*∣= 1.65 ± 0.08 kPa F3 Pancreatic fibrosis ∣G*∣ = 1.94 ± 0.28 kPa Shi et al. 38 Normal pancreas ∣G*∣ = 1.15 ± 0.17 kPa Shi et al. 53 Normal pancreas ∣G*∣ = 1.11 ± 0.08 kPa An et al. 55 Benign pancreatic masses ∣G*∣ (median [IQR]) = 1.96 [1.66 - 2.33] kPa Malignant pancreatic masses ∣G*∣ (median [IQR]) = 3.27 [2.43 - 4.38] kPa Shi et al. 37 Thyroid Normal thyroid G'' = 0.58 ± 0.26 kPa Normal thyroid G" = 0.42 ± 0.20 kPa Yeung et al. 36 Thyroid cancer G'' = 0.17 ± 0.14 kPa Thyroid cancer G" = 0.28 ± 0.07 kPa Yeung et al. 36 Prostate Healthy parotid (peripheral zone) G'' = 2.26 ± 0.45 kPa Healthy parotid (peripheral zone) G” = 2.38 ± 0.54 Pa.s Li et al. 58 Prostate cancer ∣G*∣ = 3.73 ± 1.24 kPa Sahebjavaher et al. 59 Benign prostatic hyperplasia ∣G*∣ = 3.38 ± 0.28 kPa Deng et al. 42 Open in a new tab Research Highlights. 3D Vector MRE has high clinical potential for the assessment of small organs 3D Vector MRE of the uterus, pancreas, thyroid, prostate, and parotid is feasible 3D Vector MRE provides information on the biomechanical properties of small organs 3D Vector MRE helps to differentiate between healthy and pathological tissue More research is needed to optimize the performance of 3D Vector MRE in small organs Funding This study was supported by grants №25/NAWA-PROM-UR/2019 from the Polish National Agency for Academic Exchange, and USA National Institutes of Health R01EB001981. Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. Ethical Approval This study was approved by the District Medical Chambers’ Ethics Committee. Resolution No 60/2022/B from 16.05.2022. Each participant further signed a written informed consent in order to be enrolled in the study. Competing interests The Mayo Clinic, R.L.E., and J.C. have intellectual property rights and a financial interest in magnetic resonance elastography technology. The remaining authors declare no conflict of interest. References 1. Ramiao NG, Martins PS, Rynkevic R, Fernandes AA, Barroso M, Santos DC. Biomechanical properties of breast tissue, a state-of-the-art review. Biomech Model Mechanobiol 2016;15(5):1307–23. DOI: 10.1007/s10237-016-0763-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Krupski T L, Gundersen C, Carson WC, Moskaluk C, Harper J, Gerling GJ. Assessing mechanical properties of benign and malignant prostate tissue. Journal of Clinical Oncology 2010;28(15_suppl):e15109–e15109. DOI: 10.1200/jco.2010.28.15_suppl.e15109. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Glaser KJ, Manduca A, Ehman RL. Review of MR elastography applications and recent developments. J Magn Reson Imaging 2012;36(4):757–74. DOI: 10.1002/jmri.23597. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Chalasani N