mata mazzuca uteroplacental raffetto maccoll ulbrich zitta hiendleder sinowatz bauersachs metallopeptidases mmp14 mmp2 metallopeptidase timp2 kizaki koshi ushizawa hosoe emmprin degrading estrous anovulatory sada reslan narula e55 emmprin eleuterio palei tanus cavalli sandrim mmp2 trophoblasts decidua gelatinases timps bubalus bubalis estrous debrah mazzuca mata uteroplacental h491 h507 uteroplacental h33 h47 tnfalpha uteroplacental kassab abram reckelhoff r1962 shirasawa pavlish maywood mazzuca jadeja rachakonda bagi khurana vasoactivity myography e50997 osol brekke mcelroy yaggy gokina h2260 laher breemen izzard bund heagerty eckman brayden hyposmolarity potocnik meininger mechanotransduction wigg tare parkington luff tare arcuate mazzuca wigg tare tonta parkington edhf edhf h232 stennett falone koledova h745 mata reslan opsasnick h1679 cockrell cline llinas sedeek sesperez chhor ghorbanpour clc spheroid paradis mata mulvany arcade bohr r530 hockenberry sorokin p66shc h1096 h102 carnevale facchinello iodice bizzotto perrotta stefani emilin transactivation jarajapu h1917 ungvari koller thromboxane reho toot giardina cgmp r390 orshal cgmp abdalvand bourque quon davidge osol h78 gariepy ohuchi yanagisawa inscho schiffrin touyz tirapelli casolari yogi montezano tostes legros prostanoid etb mazzuca mcritchie matz overloop gairard rinewalt cockrell lamarca cockrell cockrell arany kanashiro kanashiro cockrell rebounds r295 revelles redondo avendano varona palao orriols lysyl elastin p38mapk antioxid avendano revelles aguado simoes amor lacolley protogerou safar reho brozoski rhobtb1 depolymerization e158043 huyard yzydorczyk cloutier bertagnolli sartelet e92287 wagenseil mecham elastin yasmin mceniery dakham pulsalkar maki petaja patron zouki whittal davidge filep possomato rizzi kushima delella adrenomedullin raffetto rgd raffetto hyperpolarization varicose nugent lamarca cockrell lamarca ramin kellems lamarca wallukat llinas herse dechend uteroplacental endoglin colson depoix leducq bedin beukelaer 2alpha pt2385 sedeek lamarca sholook vaka deer agonistic r256 r62','Abstract Preeclampsia is a pregnancy-related disorder manifested as hypertensive pregnancy (HTN-Preg) and often fetal growth restriction (FGR), but the mechanisms involved are unclear. We have reported enhanced reactivity of systemic vessels in HTN-Preg rats, but the critical changes in the uterine circulation are less clear. We tested whether HTN-Preg involves localized aberrations in uterine arterial tone, stiffness and remodeling by matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). Blood pressure (BP) and litter size were recorded in normal pregnant (Preg) rats and Preg rats with reduced uteroplacental perfusion pressure (RUPP). Isolated uterine arteries were placed in a pressure myograph for measuring intrinsic and extrinsic tone and arterial stiffness. Arteries were bathed in normal Krebs solution (2.5 mM Ca 2+ ), Ca 2+ -free (2 mM EGTA) Krebs, treated with sodium nitroprusside (SNP), or endothelium denuded, then pressurized at 10 mmHg steps from 10 to 110 mmHg, and the % change in diameter was analyzed to measure total (active+passive), active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic, passive, and endothelium-dependent tone, respectively. BP was higher and the litter size and pup weight were reduced in RUPP vs Preg rats. In normal Krebs, increasing intraluminal pressure caused smaller increments in diameter in arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats, suggesting greater total vascular tone. Arterial incubation in Ca 2+ -free Krebs, treatment with SNP or endothelium-removal abolished the differences in vascular tone, and subtraction of each of these components from total vascular tone revealed significant active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic, passive, and endothelium-dependent tone, respectively, in RUPP vs Preg rats. The total and passive strain-stress curves were shifted leftward in arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats, indicating increased uterine arterial stiffness. Arterial sections showed decreased lumen/total and increased wall/total area, and immunohistochemistry revealed greater MMP-1 and MMP-7 staining particularly in the media, suggesting uterine arterial remodeling by MMPs in RUPP vs Preg rats. The increased uterine arterial active myogenic, passive, and endothelium-dependent tone, arterial stiffness and remodeling by MMPs would further reduce uterine blood flow and exacerbate uteroplacental ischemia, FGR and HTN-Preg. Keywords: hypertension, intrinsic tone, preeclampsia, strain-stress relationship, vascular smooth muscle Graphical abstract 1. Introduction Normal pregnancy is characterized by marked hemodynamic, vascular and uteroplacental changes. The heart rate, blood volume and cardiac output are elevated while the systemic vessels are dilated and vascular resistance is reduced, thus providing sufficient blood supply to various organs with a slight decrease in blood pressure (BP) [ 1 ]. Also, in normal pregnancy, the uterus progressively increases in size to provide space for the developing fetus, while the placenta continues to develop and the cytotrophoblasts increasingly invade the spiral arterioles deep into the decidua in order to provide sufficient blood, oxygen and nutrients to the growing fetus [ 2 , 3 ]. These physiological changes in the maternal hemodynamics, uterus and placenta require marked adaptations in the structure/function of the maternal systemic vessels and, even more critically, in the uterine circulation [ 4 , 5 ]. Preeclampsia (PE) is a complication of pregnancy that affects approximately 5–8% of pregnant (Preg) women in the United States and about 8 million Preg women globally [ 6 ]. PE is manifested as a new-onset hypertension-in-pregnancy (HTN-Preg) at ≥20 weeks of gestation and may or may not be accompanied with proteinuria [ 7 , 8 ]. If PE is not managed adequately, it would lead to eclampsia, manifested as severe HTN, cerebral edema, seizures, and even maternal death [ 9 – 13 ]. PE could also be associated with fetal growth restriction (FGR), premature labor and small-for-gestational-age newborn weight [ 6 , 14 ]. PE may also cause in-utero programming and genetic alterations in the fetus, leading to long-term cardiovascular-renal disorders, HTN, metabolic syndrome and diabetes in the offspring when they grow to adulthood [ 15 ]. While PE causes grave maternal and fetal morbidity/mortality, its underlying pathophysiological mechanisms are not fully understood. PE and HTN-Preg remit after delivering the fetus and the placenta, suggesting that the placenta and localized alterations in the uterine circulation are major culprits in the disorder. It has been suggested that PE is triggered by initial placental ischemia which promotes the release of multiple vasoactive factors into the systemic circulation causing generalized vasoconstriction and HTN-Preg. Mechanistic studies are difficult to conduct in Preg women, and therefore we and others have used experimental models of HTN-Preg [ 9 , 15 – 17 ]. In late Preg rats, reduction of uteroplacental perfusion pressure (RUPP) induces uteroplacental ischemia and causes many of the manifestations of PE particularly HTN-Preg and FGR [ 16 – 18 ]. Also, our research group has shown increases in vascular contraction in response to external vasoconstrictors such as phenylephrine (Phe) and endothelin-1 (ET-1) in several systemic vascular beds including the thoracic aorta, the carotid, renal and mesenteric arteries as well as mesenteric microvessels of the RUPP rat model of placental ischemia and HTN-Preg [ 16 , 19 , 20 ]. It is important to note that vascular tone is regulated not only by external stimuli but also by intrinsic factors including endothelium-derived factors, active myogenic tone, passive tone of structural proteins, and arterial stiffness. Intravascular release of endothelium-derived contracting factors (EDCFs) including ET-1 and thromboxane A2 causes increases in basal vascular tone. Active myogenic tone originating from vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) is a physiological mechanism through which small arteries react to alterations in intraluminal pressure and control regional and tissue blood flow [ 21 ]. Myogenic tone is dependent on Ca 2+ entry through mechanosensitive cation channels [ 22 – 24 ]. On the other hand, passive intrinsic response is independent of Ca 2+ entry and is controlled by the cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins as well as remodeling by proteolytic enzymes. Additionally, total arterial stiffness is the aggregate of the active contractile response and the passive stiffness derived from the structural and mechanical properties of the vessel wall. Constructing the strain-stress curves has been used to measure stiffness in small arteries [ 25 , 26 ], and arterial stiffness was found to be increased in systemic HTN [ 27 – 30 ]. Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) are zinc-dependent proteases that proteolyze ECM proteins during tissue remodeling [ 31 , 32 ]. MMPs include collagenases, gelatinases, stromelysins, matrilysins, and membrane-type MMPs [ 33 , 34 ]. MMP-2 (gelatinase A) and MMP-9 (gelatinase B) are involved in endometrium remodeling during the menstrual/estrous cycle and pregnancy [ 35 – 37 ]. Our research group has previously shown upregulation of MMP-2 and MMP-9 in the aorta and uterus of Preg rats, suggesting a role in vascular and uterine remodeling during pregnancy [ 38 , 39 ]. Some studies reported elevated levels of circulating MMP-2 and MMP-9 [ 40 ], but other studies showed that the levels of MMP-9 are decreased in PE vs normal Preg women [ 31 ]. Other MMPs may also play a role in pregnancy-related vascular and uteroplacental remodeling. MMP-1 (collagenase-1) has been detected in placental cytotrophoblasts where it may promote trophoblast invasion of spiral arteries [ 41 ]. Also, MMP-7 (matrilysin) has been detected in the uterus where it may promote endometrium remodeling during the menstrual/estrous cycle and pregnancy [ 42 ]. We and others have shown increases in myogenic tone [ 26 , 43 ], arterial stiffness [ 26 ] and reactivity to exogenous vasoconstrictors [ 20 ] in systemic mesenteric microvessels of RUPP compared with Preg rats. We have also shown that MMP-2 and MMP-9 are decreased while MMP-1 and MMP-7 are increased in the aorta, uterus and placenta of RUPP vs Preg rats [ 32 , 44 – 46 ]. However, the localized changes in the critical uterine circulation, and the alterations in the uterine arterial structure, function and remodeling along with the tissue distribution of MMPs in HTN-Preg have not been examined. In the present study, we utilized uterine arteries from control Preg rats and the RUPP rat model of HTN-Preg to test if: 1) HTN-Preg is associated with increases in total (active+passive), endothelium-dependent, VSMC-mediated myogenic, and ECM-dependent passive intrinsic tone as well as arterial stiffness of uterine arteries, 2) HTN-Preg involves increased uterine arterial reactivity to external vasoconstrictors, and decreased relaxation to vasodilators, 3) HTN-Preg is associated with increases in uterine arterial remodeling and MMPs distribution in the vessel wall. 2. Materials and Methods 2.1. Animals Timed-Preg Sprague-Dawley rats (12 weeks of age, gestational day 11, Charles River Laboratories, Wilmington, MA, USA) were maintained in the animal facility on standard rat chow and tap water ad libitum in 12 h light-dark cycle. On gestational day 14, control Preg rats were sham-operated, while Preg rats in the RUPP group underwent surgical procedure under isoflurane inhalational anesthesia to induce reduction in uteroplacental perfusion pressure as previously described [ 16 , 17 , 19 , 47 , 48 ]. The RUPP procedure involves banding of the lower abdominal aorta above the iliac bifurcation and the main uterine branches of the ovarian arteries. Briefly, the lower abdominal aorta near the iliac bifurcation was carefully cleared of surrounding fat and dissected from the inferior vena cava. A blunt plastic rod (outside diameter, OD 0.3 mm) was laid parallel to the aorta, and a 4–0 silk braided ligature was knotted two times around both the aorta and the adjacent plastic rod. Once taut, the rod was pulled out from the knotted ligature, thus leaving a constrictive band (inside diameter, ID 0.3 mm) that reduces blood flow in the aorta and the perfusion pressure in the gravid uterus by ∼40%. To account for compensation of blood flow to the placenta through adaptive increase in ovarian blood flow, a blunt plastic rod (OD 0.1 mm) was utilized to create a constrictive ligature band (ID 0.1 mm) on the main uterine branches of the right and the left ovarian arteries. All rats received postoperative analgesia including buprenorphine 0.05 mg/kg subcutaneous every 12 h plus meloxicam 2 mg/kg subcutaneous every 24 h for 48 h. The success rate of the RUPP procedure was ~85%, and the remaining 15% of RUPP rats in which the banding procedure caused maternal death or total placenta and pup resorption were excluded from the study. Measurements of the hemodynamics and vascular changes in RUPP rats on gestational day 19 have previously shown significant elevation of BP, increased vascular reactivity, changes in proteolytic enzymes and excessive collagen deposition in the aorta, mesenteric microvessels and uterus [ 16 – 18 , 32 , 44 , 48 , 49 ]. The RUPP procedure and similar aortic banding far below the renal arteries and just above the iliac bifurcation in virgin non-Preg female rats did not cause significant changes in BP or vascular function as previously described [ 47 ]. All animal procedures were performed following the guidelines of the American Physiological Society and the National Institutes of Health Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals, and were approved by the Brigham and Women Hospital Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (protocol # 2016N000262). 2.2. Measurement of blood pressure On gestational day 19, rats were placed under isoflurane anesthesia and one end of a PE-50 catheter was inserted in the carotid artery, and the other end was channeled through the subcutaneous tissue and exteriorized at the back of the neck. The rats were allowed to recover from anesthesia for ≥ 1 h, then the catheter was connected to a pressure transducer attached to an amplifier and BP recorder (Living System Instrumentation, Burlington, VT). BP in conscious rats was then recorded every 20 min for 1 h and the mean arterial pressure was measured [ 20 , 26 ]. 2.3. Tissue preparation Rats were euthanized by CO 2 inhalation, and the whole-body weight was recorded. The uterus was then excised, the maternal body weight and uterus weight without the placentae and pups, the litter size (number of pups), number of placentae, the weight of each individual pup and placenta, and the placental efficiency (pup weight/corresponding placenta weight) were measured. The uterus was then placed in oxygenated Krebs solution, and the uterine arteries (~500 μm OD) were carefully isolated and cleaned of adipose tissue under dissection microscope. Uterine arterial segments (~3 mm long) were placed in a 5 ml temperature-controlled perfusion chamber, mounted between two glass cannulas, and secured with 10–0 ophthalmic nylon monofilament (Living Systems Instrumentation) [ 20 , 26 , 50 , 51 ]. One end of the artery was mounted and tied on one of the glass cannulas and the lumen was gently perfused with Krebs solution to remove any remaining blood. The other end of the artery was then mounted on the second glass cannula and tied. The arterial segment in the perfusion chamber was then placed on the stage of an inverted microscope (TE300, Nikon, Melville, NY) equipped with a Nikon camera. The distal end of the vessel was closed using a stopcock, while the proximal end was connected to a pressure transducer. The artery was pressurized and maintained under 60 mmHg constant pressure using a pressure-servo control system (Living Systems Instrumentation). The artery was continuously superfused in 5 ml fresh Krebs solution bubbled with 95% O 2 and 5% CO 2 at 37°C and replenished at a rate of 1 ml/min using a peristaltic minipump (Master-Flex; Cole-Parmer, Vernon Hills, IL). Prior to experimentation, the vessel was primed by eliciting a half maximal contractile response to KCl (51 mM) and Phe (6×10 −7 M), and the endothelium integrity was confirmed by eliciting a relaxation response to acetylcholine (ACh, 10 −5 M) in Phe pre-constricted vessels. Arteries that showed leaks or failed to produce maintained constriction to KCI or Phe or ≥30% dilation to ACh were discarded. The uterine artery was allowed to recover for 30 min and continuously observed using objective lens ×20, a video camera and a monitor. 2.4. Intrinsic tone and strain-stress relationship protocol The active and passive intrinsic responses and arterial stiffness were first determined in the absence of any pre-constrictor such as Phe [ 51 – 54 ]. After stabilizing the vessel at 60 mmHg in Ca 2+ -containing Krebs for 30 min, intraluminal pressure was lowered to 10 mmHg for a 20 min resting period. The artery was then pressurized in 10-mmHg step increments from 10 to 110 mmHg. The vessel OD, ID and wall thickness were recorded after the response to each pressure step reached its maximum in ~3 min. Using the Nikon camera, snap-pictures of the artery were acquired at specific time points [ 20 , 50 ]. The artery OD was measured by tracing the arterial surface using automatic edge-detection system (Crescent Electronics, Sandy, UT), digitized signal at 1 Hz and a desk computer. Upon pressurization, changes in the artery ID as well as the wall thickness can be used to construct the strain-stress relationship and measure arterial stiffness. Therefore, at each pressure increment, wall thickness was also measured from the artery picture on the video monitor using a ruler. The total intrinsic response to intraluminal pressure in normal Ca 2+ -containing Krebs includes endothelium-dependent tone, active VSMC-mediated Ca 2+ dependent myogenic tone, and passive ECM-regulated Ca 2+ -independent intrinsic response [ 24 , 55 , 56 ]. To test for any endothelium-dependent tone, the endothelium was removed by infusing an air bubble (total volume ~0.3 ml) inside the artery, and the pressure-diameter response was measured in the endothelium-denuded artery, then subtracted from the total intrinsic response to determine the endothelium-dependent tone. To test for the Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic response, arteries were perfused for 10 min in Ca 2+ -free Krebs solution supplemented with 2 mM ethylene glycol-bis(β-aminoethyl ether)-N,N,N’,N’-tetraacetic acid (EGTA) to chelate Ca 2+ and abolish active VSM myogenic tone. The incremental pressure protocol was repeated in 0 Ca 2+ (2 mM EGTA) Krebs and subtracted from the total tone to measure the Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone. To measure the passive changes in arterial diameter and the strain-stress relationship, sodium nitroprusside (SNP, 10 −5 M) was added to the 0 Ca 2+ (2 mM EGTA) Krebs, and the pressure-diameter relationship was elicited and subtracted from the total intrinsic response to measure the ECM-dependent passive intrinsic tone, construct strain-stress relationships, and examine the mechanical properties of the vessel wall. 2.5. Calculation of vascular tone, passive intrinsic response and strain-stress relationship The endothelium-dependent, active (Ca 2+ -dependent) and passive (Ca 2+ -independent) changes in diameter at each intraluminal pressure were first measured in μm. The % change of diameter from baseline at 10 mmHg was then calculated as: [(Final diameter – Initial diameter at 10 mmHg) / (Initial diameter at 10 mmHg pressure)] × 100. To assess arterial stiffness, the wall strain and stress were calculated at each pressure increment and the strain-stress relationship was constructed [ 25 , 26 ]. Wall strain = (Final ID diameter − ID at 10 mmHg pressure) / ID at 10 mmHg pressure. Wall stress (kPa) = [Intraluminal pressure (Pa) × ID (μm)] / [2 × Wall thickness (μm)] [ 25 , 26 , 57 , 58 ]. 2.6. Uterine artery reactivity to external stimuli We further confirmed functional viability of the uterine arteries and tested if structural differences and basal functional properties in RUPP compared with Preg rats could affect their responsiveness to external vasoconstrictor and vasodilator stimuli. Using the pressure-servo control system, the uterine artery was maintained at constant 60 mmHg pressure for 30 min [ 48 , 59 ]. After vessel equilibration, a control constriction to 51 mM KCl was elicited, followed by rinsing with Krebs 3×5 min. Graded concentrations of KCl (16, 24, 36, 51, 66, 78, 76, 96 mM) were also used to induce stepwise membrane depolarization and arterial constriction, followed by rinsing with Krebs 3×5 min. The vessel was then stimulated cumulatively with increasing concentrations of Phe (10 −9 to 10 −5 M) or ET-1 (10 −11 to 10 −7 ), and concentration-constriction curves were constructed. To investigate endothelial function, the vessels were preconstricted with Phe (6×10 −7 M), increasing concentrations of ACh (10 −9 to 10 −5 M) were added 2 min for each concentration, the changes in vessel diameter were recorded and the % relaxation of Phe constriction was measured. Phe (6×10 −7 M) produced equivalent preconstriction in uterine arteries of Preg and RUPP rats. Phe, ACh and ET-1 act on different membrane receptors minimizing the possibility of tachyphylaxis. To test for endothelium-dependent release of nitric oxide (NO) and prostacyclin (PGI 2 ), ACh relaxation was measured in endothelium-intact arteries pretreated for 15 min with N ω -nitro-L-arginine methyl ester (L-NAME, 3×10 −4 M) to inhibit nitric oxide synthase (NOS) and indomethacin (INDO, 10 −6 M) to inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX). Previous studies have indicated that PGI 2 minimally contributes to vasodilation in rat microvessels [ 20 , 60 ], therefore, both NOS and COX were inhibited simultaneously. To test for the contribution of an endothelium-derived hyperpolarizing factor (EDHF), experiments were conducted in vessels treated with tetraethylammonium chloride (TEA, 30 mM) to block non-selective K + channels. To further test the role of the endothelium, Phe and ET-1 constriction and ACh relaxation experiments were also performed in endothelium-denuded vessels. To test the ability of VSM to relax in response to vasodilators, the uterine arteries were preconstricted with Phe (6×10 −7 M), then increasing concentrations of the exogenous NO donor sodium nitroprusside (SNP, 10 −9 -10 −5 M) were applied cumulatively and the arterial relaxation was allowed to plateau between successive concentrations and measured. 2.7. Tissue histology To assess if adaptive changes in the vessel wall play a role in HTN-Preg, uterine arteries from Preg and RUPP rats were rapidly embedded in Tissue-Tek 4583 optimal cutting temperature compound (OCT, Fisher Scientific). Using forceps, the cylindrical arterial segment was kept upright and the lumen maintained open in order to fill with sufficient OCT, then the tissue block was stored at −80°C. Transverse uterine artery cryosections (6 μm thick) were cut and placed on glass slides and stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E). Arterial sections from Preg and RUPP rats were stained at the same time under identical staining conditions. Using a Nikon microscope with digital camera mount and Nikon NIS Elements software, images of the arterial sections were acquired at ×400 magnification using the same light intensity, camera gain, and analyzed using ImageJ software (National Institute of Health, NIH). Outlines of the arterial tissue section exterior and interior were used to measure the whole tissue area, lumen area, wall area, lumen/total ratio, and wall/total ratio. Also, the number of pixels in the image of the arterial lumen and wall were counted and translated into lumen and wall areas in mm 2 using a calibration bar. A line was also drawn across the different layers of the arterial wall to determine the thickness of the intima, media and adventitia. The thickness of each arterial layer was calculated by averaging the thickness at 8 different regions going clockwise in the vessel cross-section to minimize artifacts from non-uniformity of the vessel wall thickness [ 61 ]. 2.8. Tissue immunohistochemistry To analyze the distribution of MMPs in the vessel wall, cryosections (6 μm thick) from the uterine arteries of Preg and RUPP rats were thawed and fixed in ice-cold acetone for 30 min. The tissue sections were treated with 1.5% H 2 O 2 solution for 30 min to quench endogenous peroxidase, followed by 10% horse serum for 30 min to block nonspecific binding. Arterial sections were then treated with MMP-1 (1:100) or MMP-7 (1:100) polyclonal antibody (Santa Cruz Biotechnology, Dallas, TX) for 1 h. Other sections were stained with polyclonal CD31 (1:100) antibody (Santa Cruz Biotechnology) for 1 h to help differentiate the tunica intima from the media. Arterial sections were rinsed with phosphate buffered saline (PBS), then treated with biotinylated anti-rabbit secondary antibody, rinsed with PBS, then treated with avidin-labeled peroxidase (VectaStain Elite ABC Kit, Vector Laboratories, Burlingame, CA) for 30 min. Positive labeling was detected by visualizing diaminobenzadine which appeared as brown spots. Negative control tissue sections were run in parallel with no primary antibody, and showed no measurable staining under the microscope. Arterial sections were counterstained with hematoxylin for 40 sec, rinsed with PBS, topped with cytoseal 60, and covered with slide coverslips. Tissue images were obtained on a Nikon microscope using the same light intensity, total magnification ×400 and camera gain, and analyzed using ImageJ software. The number of pixels in the arterial wall image was first defined, then the number of brown spots or pixels that correspond to MMPs were differentiated from the clear background threshold, then counted by ImageJ software, and presented as % of total pixels [ 39 , 61 , 62 ]. The number of pixels in each layer of the vessel (intima, media, adventitia) was also defined and translated into the area in μm 2 using a calibration bar. The number of brown spots or pixels that represent MMP-1 or MMP-7 in each layer was then counted by ImageJ software and presented as number of pixels/μm 2 [ 39 , 61 , 62 ]. All experiments were performed in a blinded fashion, and analysis of the slides/images was conducted by two different investigators in an independent fashion. 2.9. Solutions and drugs Krebs physiological solution was composed of (in mM) 120 NaCl, 5.9 KCl, 25 NaHCO 3 , 1.2 NaH 2 PO 4 , 11.5 dextrose, 2.5 CaCl 2 , 1.2 MgCl 2 , at pH 7.4 when bubbled with 95% O 2 and 5% CO 2 . High KCl solution (16 to 96 mM) was prepared as normal Krebs solution but with equimolar substitution of NaCl with KCl. For 0 Ca 2+ Krebs solution, CaCl 2 was omitted and replaced with 2 mM EGTA. Stock solutions of Phe, ACh, SNP (10 −1 M) and ET-1 (10 −4 M) were prepared in deionized water. PBS was composed of (in mM): 137 NaCl, 2.7 KCl, 8 Na 2 HPO 4 , 2 KH 2 PO 4, at pH 7.4. All other ingredients were of reagent grade or better. 2.10. Statistical analysis Data from different rat groups were presented as means±SEM with the “n” value corresponding to the number of rats in each group (Preg = 7, RUPP = 8). Data were first analyzed with Two-way repeated measures ANOVA, and when a significant interaction was observed, data were further analyzed with Bonferroni’s post hoc correction for multiple comparisons using Prism software (v.6.01, GraphPad Software, San Diego, CA). Student’s unpaired t -test was used to compare two means. Differences were statistically significant when P < 0.05. 3. Results On gestational day 19, BP was significantly greater (p<0.05) in RUPP vs Preg rats (123.5±2.6 vs 100.9±2.9 mmHg) as previously described [ 20 , 26 , 45 , 48 , 49 , 63 , 64 ]. RUPP rats also showed significantly smaller (p<0.05) maternal body weight with uterus (302.5±11.0 vs 395.1±17.2 g), maternal body weight without uterus (283.6±9.6 vs 360.3±20.7 g), uterus weight without placentae or pups (3.8±0.3 vs 4.9±0.2 g), litter size (number of pups 5–12 average 8.3±0.9 vs 9–15 average 12.4±0.8), average pup weight (1.4±0.1 vs 2.1±0.2 g), average placenta weight (377.4±29.4 vs 459.1±23.0 mg), and placental efficiency (pup weight/placenta weight g/g 3.8±0.2 vs 4.6±0.3) compared with Preg rats, respectively, as previously described [ 26 ]. We tested the basal vascular functions in resting uterine arteries. In intact uterine arteries incubated in normal Krebs (2.5 mM Ca 2+ ), stepwise increases in intraluminal pressure from 10 to 110 mmHg were associated with corresponding increases in arterial diameter that were significantly less in RUPP vs Preg rats, suggesting increases in total (active+passive) intrinsic response in RUPP rats ( Fig. 1A ). Interestingly, the differences observed in intact arteries wereeliminated in endothelium-denuded vessels which showed that stepwise increases in intraluminal pressure caused increases in diameter that were not different in Preg vs RUPP rats. Also, stepwise increases in intraluminal pressure were associated with significantly smaller increases in arterial diameter in endothelium-denuded vs intact vessels of Preg rats ( Fig. 1B ), but little changes in diameter in endothelium-denuded vs intact vessels of RUPP rats ( Fig. 1C ). Additionally, subtraction of the changes in diameter in endothelium-denuded vessels from intact vessels showed increases (dilation) in Preg rats, but decreases (constriction) in RUPP rats, suggesting compromised endothelial function in RUPP rats ( Fig. 1D ). On the other hand, incubation of the arteries in 0 Ca 2+ Krebs caused little changes in diameter in Preg rats ( Fig. 1B ), but significant increases in RUPP rats ( Fig, 1C ). Subtraction of the changes in diameter in 0 Ca 2+ Krebs from those in normal 2.5 mM Ca 2+ Krebs, showed little changes in Preg rats, but significant decreases (constriction) in RUPP rats, indicating greater active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone in RUPP rats ( Fig. 1E ). Additionally, arterial treatment with SNP to essentially paralyze VSMCs caused little change in diameter in Preg rats ( Fig. 1B ), but significant increases in diameter in RUPP rats ( Fig, 1C ). Subtraction of these passive changes in diameter in 0 Ca 2+ +SNP from those in normal 2.5 mM Ca 2+ Krebs showed little changes in Preg rats, but significant decreases in diameter (constriction) in RUPP rats, suggesting changes in the vessel mechanical properties and structural architecture that hinder dilation in RUPP rats ( Fig. 1F ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Total, endothelium-dependent, active myogenic and passive changes in diameter in uterine arteries of Preg vs RUPP rats. Intact uterine arteries from Preg and RUPP rats were first incubated in normal Ca 2+ -containing (2.5 mM) Krebs, then sequentially incubated in 0 Ca 2+ (2 mM EGTA), treated with SNP (10 −5 M) and endothelium-denuded, and pressurized from 10–110 mmHg under each condition to record the total (active+passive) (A), Ca 2+ -independent, passive, and endothelium-independent changes in arterial diameter relative to the diameter at 10 mmHg, respectively (B, C). The total intrinsic response was then dissected to calculate its different components: endothelium-dependent response (intact – endothelium-denuded) (D), VSMC Ca 2+ dependent active myogenic tone (normal Krebs 2.5 mM Ca 2+ – 0 Ca 2+ ) (E), ECM-dependent passive tone (normal Krebs – 0 Ca 2+ +SNP) (F). Data represent means±SEM, n=6– 8/group. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. # P<0.05, endothelium-denuded vs intact arteries, † P<0.05, 0 Ca 2+ or SNP vs intact arteries in normal Krebs 2.5 mM Ca 2+ . To further assess the basal mechanical properties of the arterial wall, the total (active+passive) and passive strain-stress relationships were constructed as a measure of uterine arterial stiffness in RUPP vs Preg rats. In normal Ca 2+ -containing Krebs solution, the total strain-stress curve was slightly shifted to the left, suggesting some increase in uterine arterial stiffness in RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 2A ). Also, in vessels incubated 0 Ca 2+ (2 mM EGTA) Krebs solution and treated with SNP, the passive strain-stress relationship was significantly shifted to the left ( Fig. 2B ), further indicating increased arterial stiffness and marked differences in the mechanical properties and structural architecture of the uterine arterial wall in RUPP compared with Preg rats. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Strain-stress relationships (indicative of arterial stiffness) in uterine arteries of Preg and RUPP rats. Total (active+passive) (A) and passive (B) strain-stress relationships were measured in uterine arteries to assess arterial wall stiffness. Total strain-stress relationships were constructed in arteries incubated in Ca 2+ -containing Krebs. Passive strain-stress relationships were constructed in arteries incubated in 0 Ca 2+ (2 mM EGTA) Krebs and treated with SNP (10 −5 M) to assess the structural and mechanical properties of the arterial wall. A left-ward shift in the strain-stress relationship (indicated by an arrow) suggests increased arterial wall stiffness. Data represent means±SEM, n=6–8. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. We also assessed the uterine arterial response to extrinsic vasoconstrictor and vasodilator stimuli. The α-adrenergic receptor agonist Phe caused concentration-dependent constriction that was not different in intact ( Fig. 3A ) or endothelium-denuded uterine arteries ( Fig. 3B ) of RUPP vs Preg rats. In comparison, ET-1 caused concentration-dependent constriction that was enhanced and more sensitive particularly to lower ET-1 concentrations in intact uterine arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 3C , 3D , 3E ), but the differences were not observed in endothelium-denuded vessels of RUPP compared with Preg rats ( Fig. 3F ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Uterine arterial reactivity to external vasoconstrictors in Preg and RUPP rats. Intact (A) and endothelium-denuded uterine arteries (B) from Preg and RUPP rats were incubated in Ca 2+ containing (2.5 mM) Krebs and stimulated with increasing concentrations of Phe and the % arterial constriction was recorded. In other experiments, intact (C, D, E) and endothelium-denuded uterine arteries (F) from Preg and RUPP rats were stimulated with increasing concentrations of ET-1 and the changes in diameter and % arterial constriction were measured. Data represent means±SEM, n=6–8. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. Membrane depolarization by 51 mM KCl which promotes Ca 2+ entry through Ca 2+ channels, caused greater constriction in uterine arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 4A , 4B , 4C , 4D , 4E ). Also, graded increases in Ca 2+ influx by stepwise increases in extracellular KCl concentration (16 to 96 mM), caused graded increases in arterial constriction that were enhanced and more sensitive particularly to lower KCl concentrations in RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 4F ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Effect of high KCl on uterine arteries. Uterine arteries of Preg (A, C) and RUPP rats (B, D) were stimulated with high KCl (51 mM) depolarizing and the changes in diameter and % vasoconstriction were measured (E). In other experiments, uterine arteries of Preg and RUPP rats were stimulated with increasing concentrations of KCl (16 to 96 mM) and the % vasoconstriction was measured (F). Data represent means±SEM, n=6–8. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. In intact uterine arteries preconstricted with Phe, ACh caused concentration-dependent relaxation that was markedly less in RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 5A ). We investigated the potential vascular mediators released during uterine arterial relaxation. ACh relaxation was not affected by the NOS blocker L-NAME and COX inhibitor INDO, indicating a little role of NO and PGI 2 ( Fig. 5B , 5C ). The L-NAME+INDO-resistant portion of ACh-induced relaxation was blocked by the K + channel blocker tetraethylammonium (TEA, 30 mM) or in endothelium-denuded arteries of both Preg and RUPP ( Fig. 5B , 5C ), indicating a role of endothelium-dependent hyperpolarization factor. To assess the responsiveness of VSM to vasodilators, the exogenous NO donor SNP elicited concentration-dependent relaxation that was not different in uterine arteries of RUPP compared with Preg rats ( Fig. 5D ). Fig. 5. Open in a new tab ACh- and SNP-induced relaxation in uterine arteries. Intact uterine arteries of Preg and RUPP rats were preconstricted with Phe (6×10 −7 M) then treated with increasing concentrations of ACh and the % vascular relaxation was measured (A). The vessels were treated with L-NAME (10 −4 M) and indomethacin (INDO, 10 −5 M), TEA (30 MM) or endothelium-denuded and the effects on ACh-induced relaxation was recorded in Preg (B) and RUPP rat arteries (C). In other experiments, Phe preconstricted arteries of Preg and RUPP rats were treated with increasing concentrations of SNP and the % vascular relaxation was measured (D). Data represent means±SEM, n=6–8. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. # P<0.05, +L-NAME+INDO+TEA or - Endo vs +Endo. We tested if the changes in uterine arterial basal function and reactivity to extrinsic stimuli reflect changes in the vessel architecture and remodeling by proteolytic enzymes. Analysis of the vessel wall structure using tissue histology and H&E staining showed distinct intima, media and adventitia in uterine arteries of Preg and RUPP rats ( Fig. 6A , 6B ). Endothelial CD31 immunostaining helped to further differentiate the intima from the media ( Fig. 6C , 6D ). The vessel lumen and wall areas and the lumen/total ratio were reduced in RUPP compared with Preg rats ( Fig. 6E , 6G ). On the other hand, the wall/total ratio and the thickness of the tunica media and adventitia were greater in uterine arterial sections of RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 6F , 6H ). Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Histology of uterine arteries of RUPP and Preg rats. Cryosections of the uterine arteries of Preg (A, C) and RUPP rats (B, D) were prepared for H&E staining (A, B), or immunohistochemical staining using CD31 antibody (1:100) to detect endothelial cells and differentiate the tunica intima from the tunica media (C. D), and images were acquired using Nikon microscope (total magnification 400). Using ImageJ software, outlines of the arterial section exterior and interior were used to define the whole tissue, lumen, and wall areas, and the lumen/total ratio (E), and wall/total ratio (F) were calculated. Also, the number of pixels in the lumen and wall were counted and translated into lumen and wall areas in mm 2 using a calibration bar (G). A line was also drawn across the different layers of the arterial wall to determine the thickness of the intima, media and adventitia in mm using a calibration bar (H). The layer thickness was measured by averaging the thickness at 8 different regions in the vessel cross-section to minimize experimental artifacts. Bar graphs represent means±SEM, n = 4 to 6/group. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. Analysis of proteolytic enzymes by immunohistochemistry showed prominent MMP-1 and MMP-7 brown immunostaining that was greater in uterine arterial sections of RUPP vs Preg rats ( Fig. 7A , 7B , 7C , 7D ). Quantitative image analysis of MMP-1 and MMP-7 brown immunostaining showed distinct distribution in the intima, media and adventitia, with prominent and greater distribution in the arterial media of RUPP compared with Preg rats ( Fig. 7E , 7F ). Fig. 7. Open in a new tab Distribution of MMP-1 and MMP-7 in uterine artery of Preg and RUPP rats. Cryosections (6 μm) of uterine artery of Preg and RUPP rats were prepared for immunohistochemical staining using MMP-1 (A) or MMP-7 antibody (1:100) (B), and images were acquired using Nikon microscope (total magnification 400). Using ImageJ software, the total number of pixels in the tissue section wall was first defined, then the number of brown spots (pixels) was counted and presented as % of total wall area (C and D). The number of pixels in the specific vascular layer (intima, media and adventitia) was also defined and transformed into the area in μm 2 using a calibration bar. The number of brown spots (pixels) representing MMP-1 and MMP-7 in each vascular layer was then counted and presented as the number of pixels/μm 2 (E, F). Bar graphs represent means±SEM, n= 4 to 6/group. * P<0.05, RUPP vs Preg. 4. Discussion The main observations of this study are: 1) Uterine arteries show smaller increases in diameter and greater basal intrinsic tone in response to intraluminal pressure increments in RUPP compared with Preg rats. 2) The increased basal intrinsic tone in RUPP uterine arteries involves endothelium dysfunction, active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone, passive intrinsic response, and arterial stiffness. 3) The increased basal intrinsic tone is buttressed by increased reactivity to ET-1 and decreased endothelium-dependent relaxation to ACh in RUPP uterine arteries. 4) The increased basal intrinsic tone and mechanical properties reflect changes in RUPP uterine arterial structure and remodeling by MMPs. The endothelium dysfunction, and the increased active myogenic tone, passive intrinsic response, arterial stiffness, vascular reactivity, and remodeling by MMPs further reduce uterine blood flow and exacerbate uteroplacental ischemia, HTN-Preg and FGR. PE is clinically characterized by HTN-Preg and often FGR [ 6 , 14 ]. While the mechanisms of PE are not clearly understood, insufficient trophoblast invasion of the uterine spiral arteries and the resulting placental ischemia are widely viewed as initiating events [ 9 , 14 , 65 ]. In support, studies in RUPP and hypoxic Preg rats have reported many of the characteristics of PE, HTN-Preg and FGR [ 9 , 16 – 18 , 66 ]. Specifically, our laboratory and other researchers have shown elevations in systolic, diastolic and mean arterial pressure in RUPP compared with Preg rats [ 45 , 48 , 49 , 63 , 64 ]. In accordance with these previous reports, the present data supported that RUPP induced in late Preg rats was associated with elevated BP, and reduced litter size and the average pup weight, and therefore make the RUPP rat an appropriate experimental model to assess the changes in uterine arterial structure and function in HTN-Preg. BP regulation is an intricate physiological process involving various vascular, neuronal and renal mechanisms, and the vascular resistance is regulated by small arteries and microvessels [ 67 , 68 ]. While studies have examined vascular function in systemic vessels of different animal models of HTN-Preg [ 9 , 16 , 18 , 26 , 43 , 66 ], the present experiments focused on the critical functional and structural changes in the uterine circulation. We first analyzed the uterine arterial intrinsic response in the absence of any extrinsic vasoconstrictors/vasodilators stimulants or inhibitors. When compared to Preg rats, the uterine arteries of RUPP rats elicited less increases in diameter in response to stepwise increments in intraluminal pressure, suggesting increased total (active+passive) intrinsic response in HTN-Preg rats. Further analysis of the intrinsic and extrinsic responses revealed underlying alterations in endothelial cell function, VSMC myogenic tone, ECM passive response, arterial stiffness, vascular reactivity, and remodeling by proteolytic enzymes in RUPP rat uterine arteries. Endothelial cells maintain generalized vasodilation and counterbalance the increased plasma volume and cardiac output during normal pregnancy. The present results support functional endothelial cells and enhanced endothelium-mediated vasodilation in uterine arteries during normal pregnancy because: 1) The intrinsic response to intraluminal pressure in Preg rat arteries was reduced by endothelium removal. 2) Subtraction of the changes in diameter in endothelium-denuded from intact arteries revealed a substantial endothelium-mediated response in Preg rat arteries, where increasing intraluminal pressure still caused vasodilation and increases in arterial diameter. 3) ACh caused endothelium-dependent relaxation of uterine arteries of Preg rats that was abolished by endothelium removal. In contrast, endothelial cell function appears to be compromised in uterine arteries during HTN-Preg because: 1) The increases in diameter in response to intraluminal pressure in RUPP arteries were not altered by endothelium removal, suggesting that endothelial cells are already dysfunctional and basal endothelium-mediated vasodilation is already reduced in RUPP rats. 2) Subtraction of the changes in diameter in endothelium-denuded from intact arteries revealed minimal endothelium-mediated dilation in RUPP arteries, where the increases in intraluminal pressure caused significant vasoconstriction and decreases in diameter compared with the vasodilation observed in Preg rat arteries, 3) ACh-induced relaxation was reduced in RUPP vs Preg rat arteries. 4) The remaining reduced ACh-relaxation in RUPP rat arteries was completely inhibited by endothelium removal. The observed endothelial cell dysfunction in RUPP rat uterine arteries is in agreement with reports of endothelium dysfunction in the aorta, carotid, renal and mesenteric arteries of RUPP rats and other models of HTN-Preg [ 16 , 19 , 69 ]. Endothelial cells synthesize and release various endothelium-derived relaxing factors (EDRFs) such as NO, PGI 2 and EDHF in addition to EDCFs such as ET-1 and thromboxane A2. NO goes into VSMCs and increases cGMP which promotes vascular relaxation by decreasing the levels of VSM intracellular Ca 2+ and reducing the sensitivity of the contractile proteins to Ca 2+ . PGI 2 stimulates prostanoid receptors in VSMCs causing increases in the levels of cAMP which promotes vascular relaxation through pathways similar to those stimulated by cGMP. EDHF causes membrane hyperpolarization and blocks Ca 2+ influx into VSMCs. The present study suggests reduced contribution of EDRFs to uterine arterial dilation in HTN-Preg vs normal pregnancy because: 1) Subtraction of the changes in diameter in endothelium-denuded from intact arteries revealed a substantial endothelium-mediated vasodilation and increases in diameter in response to intraluminal pressure increments in Preg rats, but significant vasoconstriction and decreases in arterial diameter in RUPP rats. This may also suggest the release of EDCFs in uterine arteries of RUPP rats. In effect, a previous study has shown increased myogenicity in mesenteric microvessels of RUPP rats that is sensitive to meclofenamate (COX inhibitor), and proposed that a metabolite of arachidonic acid e.g. a vasoconstrictor prostaglandin could be involved [ 43 ]. However, a role of other EDCFs including ET-1 cannot be ruled out. 2) NOS and COX blockade by L-NAME+INDO did not cause significant inhibition of ACh-induced relaxation in Preg or RUPP rat arteries. 3) Blocking EDHF using TEA abolished ACh relaxation in Preg rat arteries and blocked the remaining ACh-induced relaxation in RUPP rat arteries. 4) SNP-induced relaxation was not different in uterine arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats, indicating that the reduced ACh-induced relaxation in RUPP arteries is not due to decreased sensitivity of VSMCs to NO-cGMP. These observations in uterine arteries are in agreement with reports that ACh-induced relaxation is compromised in systemic vessels of RUPP, sFlt-1 and L-NAME induced model of HTN-Preg [ 16 , 19 , 69 ]. The results in the small uterine arteries also support the contention that the NO-cGMP relaxation pathway plays a predominant role in large conduit arteries such as the aorta and carotid artery, while the hyperpolarization pathway is more dominant in small arteries such as the mesenteric microvessels [ 16 , 19 , 20 , 50 ]. VSMCs in small arteries have active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone that controls blood flow to different tissues. Also, increases in myogenic tone have been shown in small arteries of animal models of HTN [ 70 – 73 ]. VSMC Ca 2+ -dependent active myogenic tone in uterine arteries appears to be reduced during normal pregnancy but enhanced in HTN-Preg because: 1) Removal of external Ca 2+ caused a small effect in Preg rats, but significantly increased arterial diameter with intraluminal pressure in RUPP rats. 2) Subtraction of the changes in diameter in Ca 2+ -free Krebs from those in Ca 2+ -containing Krebs showed minimal vasoconstriction in Preg rats, but significantly greater vasoconstriction in RUPP rats. These observations are in agreement with other reports in mesenteric and uterine microvessels of RUPP vs Preg rats [ 20 , 43 , 74 ], and provide further evidence for enhanced active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone in VSMCs of uterine arteries in the rat model of uteroplacental ischemia and HTN-Preg. VSMCs also contribute to vascular reactivity and the changes in vascular resistance and BP in response to extrinsic stimuli. Phe and ET-1 caused vasoconstriction in uterine arteries of both Preg and RUPP rats, indicating functional α-adrenergic and ET-1 receptors and signaling pathways. Phe-induced vasoconstriction was not significantly different in intact arteries of RUPP compared with Preg rats, indicating that the sensitivity of α-adrenergic receptors is not altered. This is different from our reports showing increased Phe-induced contraction in the aorta, carotid, renal and main mesenteric artery of RUPP, sFlt-1, cytokine and L-NAME induced models of HTN-Preg [ 16 , 19 , 69 , 75 , 76 ], but is in agreement with other reports that Phe-induced vasoconstriction was not significantly different in mesenteric and uterine microvessels of RUPP vs Preg rats [ 20 , 43 , 74 ]. The differences could be due to differential distribution/activity of α-adrenergic receptors in large conduit arteries vs small arteries during normal pregnancy and HTN-Preg. Compared with Phe, ET-1 induced vasoconstriction was increased in intact uterine arteries of RUPP compared with Preg rats. These data are in agreement with our previous report that ET-1 induced vasoconstriction is enhanced in mesenteric microvessels of RUPP rats [ 20 ], but are different from other studies that ET-1 causes equivalent contraction in mesenteric arteries of RUPP and Preg rats [ 48 , 77 ]. The differences in the findings could be due to regional differences in vascular signaling along the arterial tree [ 78 ], the vessel size (first order mesenteric artery [ 77 ] vs third branch mesenteric microvessels [ 20 ]), or the technique used to measure vascular reactivity (wire myography [ 77 ] vs pressurized microvessels [ 20 ]). The differences could also be due to the relative activation of different ET-1 receptors in vascular cells ET-1 stimulates not only ET A R in VSMCs to produce vasoconstriction, but also ET B R in endothelial cells to promote the release of EDRFs [ 79 – 83 ]. In support, while ET-1 is recognized for its prominent vasoconstrictor actions, when it is injected intravenously it causes transient decrease in BP (likely because of initial activation of ET B R in endothelial cells) followed by delayed increase in BP (due to activation of ET A R in VSMCs) [ 84 , 85 ]. Also, we have shown that upregulation of endothelial ET B R is a major factor in the reduced ET-1 induced vasoconstriction and augmented ET B R-mediated vasodilation in Preg vs virgin rats [ 59 ]. Interestingly, studies have reported that treating RUPP rats with an ET A R antagonist causes reduction in BP, and proposed a role for ET A R in HTN-Preg [ 66 , 86 – 88 ]. However, the reduced BP in RUPP rats infused with ET A R antagonist could also be due to possible shift of ET-1 towards ET B R in endothelial cells where it would promote vasodilation. The present study showed that ET-1 enhanced vasoconstriction in intact, but not endothelium-denuded uterine arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats. Importantly, we confirmed that endothelium-dependent relaxation was intact in our pressurized uterine arteries, whereas other studies did not assess endothelial cell function [ 48 , 77 ] and the endothelium could be injured during dissection or insertion of a wire inside the vessel during wire myography experiments. Also, endothelium removal appeared to enhance ET-1 vasoconstriction in Preg but not RUPP rats, indicating that an endothelium-dependent signaling pathway that reduces ET-1 vasoconstriction in Preg rats is defective in RUPP rats. Furthermore, endothelium removal did not increase Phe-induced constriction in uterine arteries of Preg or RUPP rats, indicating specific alterations in ET-1 receptors or signaling mechanisms in endothelial cells. These findings are in agreement with our previous report in mesenteric microvessels of RUPP rats [ 20 ] and support that decreased endothelial ET B R levels/signaling are involved in the enhanced ET-1 mediated vasoconstriction and activation of VSMC ET A R in uterine arteries during HTN-Preg. VSMC contraction is initiated by elevations in cytosolic Ca 2+ and enhanced by Ca 2+ sensitization mechanisms such as protein kinase C and Rho-kinase [ 89 ]. Membrane depolarization by high KCl promotes Ca 2+ influx through Ca 2+ channels [ 19 ]. The finding that KCl-induced constriction was augmented in uterine arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats suggests enhanced Ca 2+ entry mechanisms. Also, the stepwise and more robust increases in arterial constriction in response to graded increases in Ca 2+ influx by increasing extracellular KCl concentration in RUPP compared with Preg rats suggest increases in the myofilament force sensitivity to cytosolic Ca 2+ . Ca 2+ sensitization pathways such as protein kinase C and Rho-kinase have been shown to enhance constriction in small arteries of experimental models of HTN [ 72 , 73 ]. Also, we have reported that the activity of Ca 2+ -dependent protein kinase C isoforms is enhanced in the aorta of L-NAME induced model of HTN-Preg [ 90 , 91 ]. The role of these Ca 2+ -sensitization signaling pathways in the increased uterine arterial constriction needs to be further examined in the uteroplacental ischemia model of HTN-Preg. The cytoskeleton and ECM glue the vascular cells together, maintain the structural integrity of the vessel wall, and provide a Ca 2+ -independent passive intrinsic response in small arteries. Ca 2+ -free Krebs abolishes the VSMC active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone, and the exogenous NO donor SNP increases cGMP in VSMCs where it inhibits Ca 2+ entry through Ca 2+ channels, promotes Ca 2+ extrusion via the plasmalemmal Ca 2+ pump and reduces the myofilament force sensitivity to Ca 2+ , leaving only ECM-dependent passive intrinsic response. The Preg rat arteries appear to have minimal passive intrinsic response because 0 Ca 2+ +SNP did not affect the increases in arterial diameter with the increases in intraluminal pressure, and subtraction of the changes in diameter in 0 Ca 2+ +SNP from those in Ca 2+ -containing Krebs showed minimal vasoconstriction. In comparison, RUPP arteries showed greater passive intrinsic response because 0 Ca 2+ +SNP significantly increased arterial diameter with intraluminal pressure, and subtraction of the changes in diameter in 0 Ca 2+ +SNP from those in Ca 2+ -containing Krebs showed greater vasoconstriction in RUPP compared with Preg rats. To further examine the uterine arterial wall mechanics, we assessed arterial stiffness by constructing the strain-stress relationship. The total stress-strain curve showed a slight leftward shift in RUPP compared with Preg rats, indicating alterations in arterial stiffness. The passive strain-stress curve showed further leftward shift in RUPP compared with Preg rats, further indicating increased uterine arterial stiffness in RUPP rats. Increased arterial stiffness has been detected in hypertensive human subjects, in preclinical models of HTN [ 25 , 27 , 92 – 95 ], and in pressurized mesenteric microvessels of HTN-Preg rats [ 26 ]. The present data provide evidence of increased arterial stiffness in uterine arteries of uteroplacental ischemia model of HTN-Preg. We examined if the observed changes in uterine arterial basal function, reactivity to extrinsic stimuli, and mechanical properties in RUPP rats reflect structural changes in the vascular wall and remodeling by proteolytic enzymes. The uterine arteries appear to undergo extensive remodeling by MMPs during HTN-Preg because: 1) Tissue histology and analysis of the vessel architecture showed that the lumen size, and lumen/total ratio were reduced in RUPP compared with Preg rats. 2) The wall/total ratio and the thickness of the tunica media and adventitia were greater in RUPP compared with Preg rats. 3) Immunohistochemical experiments showed more robust MMP-1 and MMP-7 staining in uterine arteries of RUPP vs Preg rats. 4) MMP-1 and MMP-7 showed distinct distribution in the tunica intima, media and adventitia, with greater distribution in the arterial media of RUPP compared with Preg rats. The present findings are in agreement with our previous Western blot data showing increases in the protein amount of MMP-1 (collagenase) and MMP-7 (matrilysin) in uterine artery of RUPP vs Preg rat [ 45 ]. Vascular remodeling and increased arterial stiffness often involve alterations in the ECM structural proteins collagen and elastin and reduction in the elastin/collagen ratio [ 92 , 96 – 98 ]. We have previously reported increases in collagen content in the aorta and uterine spiral arteries of RUPP compared with Preg rats [ 32 , 44 ]. Our previous Western blot data have also shown greater collagen-I and collagen-IV levels in uterine arteries of RUPP compared with Preg rats [ 45 ]. Together, these observations support the contention that uterine arteries undergo extensive remodeling by MMP-1 and MMP-7 leading to thickening of the vessel wall and arterial stiffness during uteroplacental ischemia and HTN-Preg. While the detection of MMPs in the uterine artery adventitia supports their proteolytic effects on ECM, their localization in the tunica intima and media suggests additional effects on the endothelium and VSM. Interestingly, MMP-2 and MMP-9 promote endothelial cell release of the vasoconstrictor peptide ET-1 while decreasing the release of the vasodilator peptide adrenomedullin [ 99 , 100 ], thus causing endothelial dysfunction and vasoconstrictor/vasodilator imbalance. MMP-2 could also enhance big ET-1 induced vasoconstrictor effects in mesenteric arteries of RUPP rats [ 77 ]. Thus, MMPs could target endothelial cells and proteolyze inactive big ET-1 into active ET-1 and thereby contribute to the increased uterine arterial intrinsic tone and reactivity to external ET-1. MMPs may also affect VSMC contraction mechanisms. We have reported that MMP-2 and MMP-9 promote relaxation of rat aorta precontracted with Phe [ 101 ] and induce dilation in isolated rat inferior vena cava through activation of a hyperpolarization pathway and K + channels [ 102 , 103 ]. We have also proposed that downregulation of vascular MMP-2 and MMP-9 could increase vasoconstriction and HTN-Preg, while reduction in uterine MMP-2 and MMP-9 could exaggerate uterine contraction and cause premature labor [ 32 , 38 ]. Also, in isolated omental vessels of Preg women, MMP-1 induces vasoconstriction and increases the vascular responsiveness to angiotensin II likely through activation of a protease-activated receptor and ET-1 release in endothelial cells [ 104 ]. Thus, the increased MMP-1 and MMP-7 in the uterine arterial media may contribute to the increased intrinsic tone and reactivity to vasoconstrictors, further promoting uteroplacental ischemia, HTN-Preg and FGR. Other considerations include: 1) The use of the terms “active” vs “passive” to designate “Ca 2+ -dependent” vs “Ca 2+ -independent” intrinsic responses should be carefully interpreted as they intend to indicate the presence vs absence of Ca 2+ in the external milieu. As the intrinsic responses were measured in the absence of external vasoconstrictor agonist, receptor-mediated generation of inositol trisphosphate (IP 3 ) and release of Ca 2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum are predicted to be negligible. However, the potential role of any basal IP 3 - and Ca 2+ -induced Ca 2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum needs to be further examined. 2) With the increased arterial stiffness in RUPP rat uterine artery one would expect a decrease in vasodilation in response to sodium-nitroprusside (SNP), which was not observed in the present experiments. This is likely because SNP is an exogenous NO donor that mainly stimulates the NO-cGMP relaxation pathway. Our vascular relaxation data suggest that the NO-cGMP pathway plays only a partial role in the decreased relaxation of uterine vessels of RUPP rats because NOS blockade by L-NAME did not affect ACh relaxation, blocking EDHF with TEA abolished ACh relaxation, and maximal SNP concentrations only caused ~80% relaxation, suggesting additional role of other relaxing factors such as EDHF, Importantly, in addition to the effects of MMPs on collagen content, we have shown that they could also affect the vascular membrane potential [ 103 ] and thereby cause increases in arterial stiffness while modulating EDHF-mediated relaxation, making it important to further examine the effects of MMPs on vascular contraction/relaxation pathways. 3) One may suggest that the observed alterations in the small uterine arteries are merely due to the fixed clips on the main uterine arteries. We propose that the initial uterine artery clipping and physically induced RUPP cause initial uteroplacental ischemia that triggers the release of multiple bioactive factors including cytokines, anti-angiogenic soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 (sFlt-1), reactive oxygen species (ROS), hypoxia-inducible factor, and angiotensin type-1 receptor antibodies [ 17 , 18 , 75 , 87 , 105 – 114 ]. These factors are thought to be released in the maternal circulation causing oxidative stress, vascular dysfunction and HTN-Preg and also locally in the uterine circulation contributing to the observed changes in uterine arterial tone, reactivity to vasoconstrictors and remodeling, leading to additional placental ischemia and a detrimental vicious circle that exacerbate HTN-Preg and FGR. Future research should explore the combined role of these vasoactive factors in the alterations of uterine arterial tone, arterial stiffness and remodeling in experimental HTN-Preg and human PE. In conclusion, uterine arteries of RUPP rats show increased basal intrinsic tone that involves endothelium dysfunction, active Ca 2+ -dependent myogenic tone, passive intrinsic response, and arterial stiffness. The increased basal intrinsic tone and mechanical properties are reinforced by increased reactivity to ET-1 and decreased EDRFs such as EDHF. The increased basal intrinsic tone and mechanical properties may reflect changes in uterine arterial structure and remodeling by MMPs. The endothelium dysfunction, and the increased active myogenic tone, vascular reactivity to vasoconstrictors, passive intrinsic response, arterial stiffness and vascular remodeling by MMP-1 and MMP-7 could further reduce uterine blood flow and exacerbate uteroplacental ischemia, HTN-Preg and FGR. The present findings have significant clinical implications as they suggest that targeting MMP-1 and MMP-7 could reverse the extensive uterine arterial structural remodeling, improve uterine blood flow and thereby reduce uteroplacental ischemia in pregnancies complicated with HTN-Preg and PE. Acknowledgements This research article is a modest contribution to Dr. Sam Enna’s Legacy of Excellence, and a tribute to a great editorial team leader and a dear friend who is truly missed. This research work was partly supported by BRI Fund to Sustain Research Excellence from Brigham Research Institute, and grant award from the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (H