INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1543,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12478560','Homologous recombination deficiency and survival in ovarian high-grade serous carcinoma by self-reported race.','Lawson-Michod KA, Johnson CE, Barnard ME, Davidson NR, Collin LJ, Nix DA, Huff CD, Berchuck A, Salas LA, Greene CS, Marks JR, Peres LC, Doherty JA, Schildkraut JM.','2025','unannotated unannotated schildkrautb schildkrautw aaces ncocs aaces ncocs ncocs aaces oversampled strelka rad51c rad51d brip1 palb2 rrid scr_002260 sigprofilerextractor rnaseqcnv bard1 blm chek1 chek2 fancc fancd2 fance fancf fancg fanci fancl fancm mre11a nbn rad50 rad51 rad51b rad52 rad54l rpa1 rrid scr_006169 unannotated unannotated unannotated multiplicative dbgap phs002262 bsd clause kmichod hgsc_hrd_characterization oversampled chr17 grch38 unannotated chr13 grch38 unannotated unannotated unannotated rad51c rad51d brip1 palb2 multiplicative unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated unannotated aaces ncocs rad51c rad51d brip1 palb2 aaces unannotated nihms2110652 nihms2110652 nihms2110652 28kb nihms2110652 nihms2110652 nihms2110652 ca200854 schildkraut ca188943 schildkraut ca142081 schildkraut ca076016 schildkraut ca237170 aaces latonda burczyk bayakly vicki paddock manisha narang slone yingli wolinsky waggoner heugel deb strater taryn ferber donna borzi jenison nairmeen debbie vivian gruenigen mccarroll neading geisler smiddy vaccarello elayna freese pavelka nahhas cato moroney wysong tonia marci bowling brandon whiteside georgina aaces alberg bandera jill barnholtz bondy funkhouser moorman ncocs lankevich rex ncocs rex aaces lcp karyopharm kurman giaquinto peres kobel berchuck rossing lheureux gourley oza alsop fereday meldrum defazio chenevix trench sadetzki ramus karlan rothermundt bancroft eeles shanley brcaness vencken kriege hoogwerf beugelink burg mel hooning chemosensitivity pujade lauraine oza scambia oaknin trabert desantis samimi runowicz sharaf saganty ahsan feit sapra micco stopfer putt alberg bandera barnholtz cragun weidner vadaparampil lamacki spychalska maga balay lugo hoskins e082608 deugd villegas viana e151 rebbeck friebel hamann friebel andrulis balmana couch shl donenberg alexis degennaro e210307 burbidge pruss deffenbaugh frye schumm fackenthal sveen ademuyiwa womena kemel gordhandas e2300137 kurian abrahamse bondarenko deapen kurian howlader deapen mariotto caswell petrovchich kingham walens alsten lockhart zingone petrovics schildkraut alberg bandera barnholtz bondy aaces moorman schildkraut calingaert halabi vine berchuck hippen michod nix konstantinopoulos norquist lacchetti grisham goodfellow glodzik morganella staaf hrdetect vangara sigprofilerextractor alvero wallbillich winer hrwild multiomic barinka rahbarinia rnaseqcnv alexandrov haradhvala bristow hammadi bristow ziogas gillen bristow ziogas culver bristow ziogas culver bristow ziogas culver bristow ziogas ziogas bristow bandera kushi bakkum gamez barroilhet behbakht berchuck sugino nakaoka yachida knerr bowles leppig buist wernli nathanson domchek dicks ramus tyrer intermaggio rad51b rad51c rad51d ramus dicks tyrer intermaggio brip1 bard1 palb2 nbn rafnar gudbjartsson sulem jonasdottir sigurdsson jonasdottir brip1 pelttari heikkinen kallioniemi schleutker holli rad51c norquist gulsuner meindl hellebrand wiek erven wappenschmidt niederacher pedigrees rad51c loveday ruark frankum rad51d lilyquist laduca polley shimelis kanchi leiserson wendl schildkraut dempsey akonde aaces nihms2110652 nihms2110652 nihms2110652 28kb nihms2110652 nihms2110652 nihms2110652 dbgap phs002262 bsd clause kmichod hgsc_hrd_characterization','Abstract Background Half of ovarian high-grade serous carcinomas (HGSC) have homologous recombination deficiency (HRD). However, HRD is not well-characterized in Black individuals who experience worse survival after a diagnosis of HGSC. The objective of this study was to characterize ovarian HGSC HRD and examine its association with survival by self-reported race. Methods HRD features were identified using matched tumor-normal whole-exome and RNA sequencing in a HGSC cohort. We calculated age and stage-adjusted hazard ratios (HR) and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for survival, comparing individuals with a feature to those without, separately by self-reported race. Results Any HRD was associated with a 32% reduced risk of death in Black individuals compared with a 62% reduction in White individuals (Black HR=0.68, 95%CI 0.43–1.09; White HR=0.38, 95%CI 0.14–1.04). More of the germline and somatic variants detected among Black individuals were unannotated or variants of uncertain significance (VUS; germline 65% versus 45%; somatic 62% versus 50%, respectively). Black individuals with germline unannotated/VUS were more likely to have tumors with HRD scarring and a first-degree family history of breast or ovarian cancer compared with those without (HRD scar 71.4% versus 49.6%; family history 68.4% versus 34.6%). Conclusions HRD testing informs precision-based medicine approaches that improve outcomes, but a higher proportion of VUS among Black individuals may complicate referral for such care leading to worse outcomes for Black individuals. Impact Our findings emphasize the importance of recruiting diverse individuals in genomics research and better characterizing VUS. INTRODUCTION Ovarian cancer is the sixth leading cause of cancer-related death in women and is comprised of multiple histotypes ( 1 – 3 ). High-grade serous carcinoma (HGSC)—the most common histotype—is usually diagnosed at a late stage. While HGSC initially responds to surgical debulking and chemotherapy, most patients relapse, resulting in poor survival ( 4 ). Approximately 50% of HGSC have genetic or epigenetic alterations in homologous recombination pathway genes, most commonly in BRCA1 and BRCA2 , leading to homologous recombination deficiency (HRD) ( 5 ). HRD is associated with better patient survival and possibly improved response to chemotherapy ( 6 – 9 ). Genetic testing to inform risk-reducing surgery has contributed to prevention of ovarian cancer among BRCA carriers and tumor HRD testing has informed the use of poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitors which have shown effectiveness in improving progression-free survival in patients with HRD-positive ovarian cancer ( 10 , 11 ). The survival benefit from these advances may not be equally distributed across all racial and ethnic groups. While ovarian cancer mortality is declining, the rate of decrease among Black individuals is half that of White individuals ( 12 ). Compared with White individuals, Black individuals are less likely to be referred for genetic testing due to family history, less likely to complete testing following a referral, and less likely to undergo prophylactic salpingo-oophorectomy if they have a pathogenic risk variant ( 13 – 18 ). Further, our understanding of HRD in HGSC is informed by research in predominately White populations and therefore enriched for HRD features specific to White populations ( 19 – 26 ). This may contribute to higher rates of variants of uncertain significance (VUS) among Black individuals, further complicating treatment referral as VUS cannot be used to inform clinical decision-making ( 23 , 24 , 27 – 29 ). Characterizing HRD among Black individuals with HGSC is of particular importance given prior studies that report HRD may be more prevalent in tumors from Black individuals with various cancer types ( 23 , 24 , 30 – 32 ). While prior studies observed variation in germline BRCA1 and BRCA2 variants by geography and ancestry, characterization of this variation is still limited and few studies have evaluated population differences in somatic mutations, non- BRCA genes, or within specific ovarian cancer histotypes ( 19 – 26 ). Here, we characterize HRD in 178 Black and 123 White individuals with HGSC and evaluate the degree to which HRD features are associated with survival in each population. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study Population Our cohort included individuals with pathologically confirmed HGSC, 178 who self-reported Black race (“SchildkrautB”) and 123 who self-reported White race (“SchildkrautW”), enrolled in one of two population-based case-control studies of newly diagnosed epithelial ovarian cancer identified through rapid-case ascertainment, the African American Cancer Epidemiology Study (AACES), and the North Carolina Ovarian Cancer Study (NCOCS) ( 33 – 35 ). AACES included individuals ages 20 to 79 years, self-reporting Black race, and diagnosed between 2010–2015. Individuals eligible for NCOCS were ages 20 to 74 years at diagnosis and diagnosed between 1999–2005. Institutional Review Board (IRB) approval by the Duke University Medical Center was obtained for patient recruitment, sample collection, and research and all research was conducted in accordance with the US Common Rule. Written informed consent was received from all NCOCS participants. All AACES participants provided verbal consent for interview and written consent for blood or saliva sample collection. Both study populations signed release forms for medical records and tissue samples. Data Collection HGSC histotype was confirmed by centralized pathology review. Age at diagnosis was extracted from questionnaires and pathology reports. Stage, debulking status, and receipt of neoadjuvant therapy were obtained from medical records, pathology reports, and cancer registries. Vital status was determined from cancer registries, direct physician contact, and the National Death Index. First-degree family history of breast and ovarian cancer was self-reported in questionnaires. White individuals were matched to Black individuals on 5-year age categories and oversampled for early stage prior to tumor sequencing ( Supplementary Figure 1 ). Homologous Recombination Deficiency Definition Germline and somatic variants were identified through whole-exome sequencing in matched germline and formalin-fixed paraffin embedded tumor samples ( 36 ). We excluded benign and likely benign variants and restricted to germline variants with a quality score of ≥99.9 for SNVs and 98.0 for INDELs, read depth and genotype quality ≥10, alternative read proportion between 20%–80%, and minor allele frequency <0.5% and somatic variants passing Strelka’s empirical variant score model with tumor read depth ≥30, tumor allele fraction ≥15%, normal allele frequency <5%, quality score ≥15, and population frequency <1%. In our primary analyses we included genes involved in the homologous recombination pathway and recommended by the 2020 American Society for Clinical Oncology (ASCO) guidelines to be prioritized in epithelial ovarian cancer germline genetic testing: BRCA1, BRCA2, RAD51C, RAD51D, BRIP1, PALB2 ( 37 ). While 30% of HRD in HGSC is thought to be due to germline or somatic alterations in homologous recombination pathway genes, another ~20% is likely due to epigenetic changes.( 5 ) HRD associated genomic scarring including mutational signatures and loss of heterozygosity (LoH) can be used to approximate the prevalence of HRD due to both genetic and epigenetic changes ( 38 ). In the absence of whole-genome sequencing, we defined HRD scarring as the presence of one of the following: the Catalogue of Somatic Mutations in Cancer (COSMIC; RRID:SCR_002260) single-base pair substitution signature 3 or insertion and deletion signature 6 identified using SigProfilerExtractor for de novo mutational signature analysis; or LoH score ≥16% defined using RNAseqCNV to approximate large-scale copy number alterations ( 39 – 42 ). We calculated the percent of autosomal chromosomal segments with LoH and defined LoH high as greater than or equal to 16% based on previously described methods ( 40 ). We compared the prevalence of HRD features by self-reported race using the following categories: germline variants or somatic mutations in (1) BRCA1 ; (2) BRCA2 ; (3) any other prioritized gene in the ASCO guidelines (labeled as any non-BRCA ASCO prioritized gene in tables and figures) (4) any ASCO prioritized gene ( any ASCO prioritized gene ); (5) HRD scar as defined above; and (6) any HRD which included individuals with one or more of the features described above. Because the ASCO prioritized gene list is based on evidence for susceptibility not outcomes, we performed sensitivity analyses including additional homologous recombination pathway genes: ATM , ATR , ATRX, BARD1, BLM, CHEK1, CHEK2, FANCC, FANCD2, FANCE, FANCF, FANCG, FANCI, FANCL, FANCM, MRE11A, NBN, RAD50, RAD51, RAD51B, RAD52, RAD54L, RPA1 ( 37 ). Statistical Analysis For variants in the homologous recombination genes described above, we compared the distribution of clinical significance based on ClinVar (RRID:SCR_006169) annotations (pathogenic/likely pathogenic versus VUS/unannotated not annotated in ClinVar), as well as variant classification and genomic location, separately by self-reported race. To further characterize VUS/unannotated variants, we examined the distribution of age at diagnosis, stage, first-degree family history of ovarian or breast cancer, and HRD scarring by variant pathogenicity. We compared individuals with a germline VUS/unannotated variant in any ASCO prioritized gene to those with a germline pathogenic/likely pathogenic variant in any ASCO prioritized gene , and those with no germline variant. We used Cox proportional hazards regression to calculate HRs for associations between the presence of the HRD feature and all-cause mortality adjusting for age, stage, year of diagnosis, and debulking status, separately by self-reported race. Left-truncated models were used with survival time defined as the time from study enrollment to all-cause mortality or censoring. Individuals alive at last known follow up were censored. We tested for a multiplicative interaction between each HRD feature observed in 5 or more individuals, and race. All analyses were performed in R version 4.2.2 (Vienna, Austria). Data Availability The deidentified data generated in this study are available through dbGaP application under study ID: phs002262.v3.p1. All software used in this analysis are available under the BSD 3-Clause License and we include scripts for the analyses presented in this manuscript at: github.com/kmichod/hgsc_hrd_characterization . RESULTS Study Population Debulking status was available for 119 Black individuals and 34 White individuals, and among those, optimal debulking was achieved in 61.3% and 88.2%, respectively. A first-degree family history of ovarian cancer was reported by 8.8% of individuals in both groups while a first-degree family history of breast cancer was reported by 33.5% of Black individuals and 20% of White individuals ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Characteristics of high-grade serous ovarian cancer cohort, by self-reported race Self-Reported Race Participant Characteristics Black (n=178) White (n=123) p-value † Year of diagnosis, n (%) <0.01 <2000 5 (2.8) 9 (7.4) 2000–2004 21 (11.8) 65 (53.7) 2005–2009 11 (6.2) 47 (38.8) 2010–2015 141 (79.2) 0 (0.0) Age at Diagnosis, yrs, n (%) * 0.93 Median (Range) 58 (35–78) 58 (35–74) 35–39 5 (2.8) 3 (2.5) 40–44 12 (6.7) 8 (6.6) 45–49 17 (9.6) 16 (13.2) 50–54 28 (15.7) 18 (14.9) 55–59 37 (20.8) 24 (19.8) 60–64 31 (17.4) 24 (19.8) 65–69 20 (11.2) 14 (11.6) ≥ 70 28 (15.7) 14 (11.6) Stage, n (%) * 0.13 FIGO I/II 35 (19.9) 16 (13) FIGO III/IV 141 (80.1) 105 (87) Unknown 2 2 Debulking Status, n (%) <0.01 Optimal 73 (61.3) 30 (88.2) Suboptimal 46 (38.7) 4 (11.8) Unknown 59 89 First-Degree Family History, n (%) Ovarian Cancer 14 (8.8) 10 (8.8) 0.97 Breast Cancer 55 (33.5) 23 (20.0) 0.01 Diagnosis to Enrollment, mos Median (Range) 4.78 (1.24–27.6) 4.15 (1.63–21.3) Open in a new tab Abbreviation: FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics. * Frequency matched on 5-year age category and White cases oversampled for early stage prior to sequencing. † P-value calculated using Fisher’s exact test; Pearson’s Chi-squared test; Wilcoxon rank sum test. Homologous Recombination Deficiency, by Self-Reported Race Germline variants in BRCA1 were identified in 7.3% of Black individuals and 9.8% of White individuals (p=0.45, Supplementary Figure 2 ). The majority of germline BRCA1 variants in Black individuals and White individuals were nonsense (53.8% versus 91.7%); however, more missense variants were identified in Black individuals compared with White individuals (38.5% versus 8.3%) and a single splice site variant was identified in Black individuals but not White individuals ( Supplementary Figure 2 ). Almost all germline BRCA1 variants in White individuals occurred in the large exon 11 (chr17:43094860–43091435, GRCh38) ( Supplementary Figure 3A ). In Black individuals, germline BRCA1 variants were more dispersed and only 4 of 13 variants occurred in exon 11. The prevalence of germline BRCA2 variants was higher in Black individuals compared with White individuals (8% versus 2%, p=0.01, Supplementary Table 1 , Supplementary Figure 2 ). Germline BRCA2 variants in both groups were missense or nonsense. Over half of the 15 BRCA2 variants in Black individuals were unannotated, including 6 nonsense and 2 missense variants ( Supplementary Figure 2 ). Other germline BRCA2 variants in Black individuals included 5 pathogenic or likely pathogenic and 2 VUS both of which were missense. Almost half of the germline BRCA2 variants in Black individuals occurred in the large exon 11 (chr13:32336265–32341196, GRCh38) ( Supplementary 3B ). Even after manual review, only two germline variants were identified in White individuals including a missense VUS and an unannotated nonsense variant ( Supplementary Figure 2 and 3B ). Somatic BRCA1 mutations were identified in 5.1% of Black individuals and 8.9% of White individuals (p=0.18). We observed more frame shift deletions in BRCA1 in White individuals compared with Black individuals ( Supplementary Figure 2 and 3 ). Somatic BRCA2 mutations were identified in 4.5% of Black individuals and 2.4% of White individuals (p=0.53). The majority of somatic BRCA2 mutations in Black individuals were unannotated. Unannotated variants included frame shift deletions, missense, and changes in the 3’UTR. A single pathogenic splice site mutation was identified in Black individuals but not White individuals ( Supplementary Figure 2 and 3B ). Most somatic BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations occurred in exon 11 of the respective gene in both groups ( Supplementary Figure 3 ). HRD associated genomic scarring was observed in 53.8% of Black individuals and 52.1% of White individuals (p=0.77). When examining the components of the HRD scar, the base-pair substitution or insertion and deletion signatures previously characterized in COSMIC were observed in 40.4% of Black individuals compared with 29.3% of White individuals (p=0.05). In contrast, high LoH approximated using CNVs was seen in 21.7% of Black and 29.8% of White individuals (p=0.12). When considering HRD scarring and somatic or germline alteration in any ASCO prioritized gene , any HRD was identified in 62.1% of Black individuals and 66.9% of White individuals (p=0.39). Homologous Recombination Deficiency and Ovarian HGSC Survival, by Self-Reported Race Among Black individuals in our study population, the median time from diagnosis to enrollment was 4.78 months (range: 1.24–27.6 months), and the median time from enrollment to end of follow-up was 46.8 months (range: 4.37–208 months). These intervals were similar among White individuals, with a median of 4.15 months (range: 1.63–21.3 months) from diagnosis to enrollment and 41.8 months (range: 2.07–228 months) from enrollment to end of follow-up. The presence of any HRD was associated with improved survival from study enrollment to end of follow-up in both groups after adjusting for age, stage, year of diagnosis, and debulking status (Black HR=0.68, 95%CI 0.43–1.09; White HR=0.38, 95%CI 0.14–1.04). Among the 73 Black and 30 White individuals with known optimal debulking status, the presence of any HRD was associated with a ~50% reduction in the risk of all-cause mortality in both groups after adjusting for age and stage at diagnosis (Black HR=0.49, 95%CI 0.26–0.94; White HR=0.43, 95%CI 0.16–1.13). HRD scarring defined by mutational signature analysis and LoH was also associated with better survival in both groups (Black HR=0.60, 95%CI 0.38–0.97); White HR=0.38, 95%CI 0.15–1.00). Germline variants in any ASCO prioritized gene were associated with improved survival in White individuals (HR=0.34, 95%CI 0.12–0.97) but not Black individuals (HR=1.01, 95%CI 0.56–1.80). We observed a statistically significant interaction between germline BRCA1 variants and race. Germline BRCA1 variants were associated with worse survival in Black individuals (HR=2.48, 95%CI 1.10–5.58) but not in White individuals (HR=0.46, 95%CI 0.13–1.62). In both groups, the direction and magnitude of the association of germline and somatic BRCA2 alterations was suggestive of improved survival but all models were statistically imprecise. Apart from germline BRCA1 variants, we did not observe any other statistically significant interaction between any HRD feature and race ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Hazard ratios (HR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) associating presence of homologous recombination deficiency feature with all-cause mortality, by self-reported race Self-Reported Race HRD Feature Black (n=176) White (n=121) Interaction § No deaths/ No at risk Adjusted HR (95% CI) † No deaths/ No at risk Adjusted HR (95% CI) † Any HRD 0.48 No 46/65 Ref 33/38 Ref Yes 72/107 0.68 (0.43–1.09) 67/79 0.38 (0.14–1.04) HRD Scar 0.61 No 55/78 Ref 48/55 Ref Yes 62/93 0.60 (0.38–0.97) 52/62 0.38 (0.15–1.00) Germline Variants * BRCA1 0.02 No 108/163 Ref 91/106 Ref Yes 13/13 2.48 (1.10–5.58) 11/13 0.46 (0.13–1.62) BRCA2 - No 114/161 Ref 100/116 Ref Yes 7/15 0.58 (0.23–