s no germline variant) were also more likely to report a first-degree family history of ovarian or breast cancer (68.4% and 58.4% versus 34.6%, p=0.01). HRD scarring was present in 71.4% of tumors with VUS/unannotated variants in any ASCO prioritized gene and 72.7% with a pathogenic variant, compared to 49.6% with no germline variant (p=0.08, Table 3 ). Table 3. Clinical features and HRD scarring among individuals with and without a germline variant in any gene prioritized in the American Society of Clinical Oncology recommendations for genetic testing, by pathogenicity and self-reported race No Germline Variant Germline Pathogenic/Likely Pathogenic Variant Germline VUS/Unannotated Variant p-value Self-Reported Black Race (n=145) (n=12) (n=21) Age at Diagnosis, yrs, median (range) 59 (35–78) 55 (37–63) 56 (40–73) 0.05 Stage, n (%) 0.32 FIGO I and II 32 (22.2) 1 (8.3) 2 (10.0) FIGO III and IV 112 (77.8) 11 (91.7) 18 (90.0) First-Degree Family History Breast or Ovarian Cancer, n (%) 0.01 No 87 (65.4) 5 (41.7) 6 (31.6) Yes 46 (34.6) 7 (58.3) 13 (68.4) HRD Scar, n (%) 0.08 No 71 (50.4) 3 (27.3) 6 (28.6) Yes 70 (49.6) 8 (72.7) 15 (71.4) Self-Reported White Race (n=100) (n=11) (n=12) p-value Age at Diagnosis, yrs, median (range) 58.5 (35–74) 55 (44–69) 55 (35–63) 0.12 Stage, n (%) 0.16 FIGO I and II 13 (13.5) 3 (27.3) 0 (0.0) FIGO III and IV 83 (86.5) 8 (72.7) 12 (100) First-Degree Family History Breast or Ovarian Cancer, n (%) 0.08 No 72 (77.4) 6 (60.0) 6 (50.0) Yes 21 (22.6) 4 (40.0) 6 (50.0) HRD Scar, n (%) 0.10 No 49 (50.0) 2 (18.2) 7 (58.3) Yes 49 (50.0) 9 (81.8) 5 (41.7) Open in a new tab Abbreviation: FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; HRD, homologous recombination deficiency; ASCO, American Society of Clinical Oncology. * P-value calculated using Kruskal-Wallis rank sum test; Fisher’s exact test; Pearson’s Chi-squared test comparing the distribution across the pathogenicity categories. Other Homologous Recombination Pathway Genes The prevalence of germline variants in the HRD genes not prioritized in the ASCO recommendations for genetic testing was 10.7% in Black individuals and 7.3% in White individuals (p=0.20, Supplementary Table 2 ), and the prevalence of somatic mutations was 11.2% in Black individuals and 13% in White individuals (p=0.60). Somatic mutations in these genes were associated with better survival (Black HR=0.48, 95%CI 0.23–1.00; White HR=0.68, 95%CI 0.27–1.72; Supplementary Table 3 ). We did not observe any statistically significant associations between germline variants in these genes and survival. DISCUSSION In this study of HRD in HGSC by self-reported race, unselected for family history or mutation status, HRD was associated with a 32% reduced risk of death in Black individuals compared with a 62% reduced risk of death in White individuals. Additionally, a greater proportion of variants in homologous recombination genes prioritized in ASCO’s recommendations for genetic testing were VUS/unannotated variants among Black individuals compared with White. Black individuals with germline VUS/unannotated variants were more likely to have a first-degree family history of breast or ovarian cancer and HRD scarring compared to those without germline variants—suggesting that some of these VUS/unannotated variants may be pathogenic. While germline and tumor testing for HRD informs precision-based medicine approaches that improve outcomes, a higher proportion of VUS among Black individuals may complicate referral for such care leading to worse outcomes among Black individuals. These findings emphasize the need for continued efforts to characterize variants among racially and ethnically diverse cohorts and to leverage in silico and functional assays to better characterize VUS. Racial disparities in survival are well documented and are in part due to inequities in receipt of guideline-concordant care ( 43 – 50 ). For example, Black individuals have less optimal surgical outcomes and are less likely to receive targeted treatments and be referred for genetic testing due to a family history ( 13 , 14 , 51 ). We observed that the HRD-associated reduction in all-cause mortality was about twice as great in White individuals compared to Black individuals, after adjusting for age, stage, and debulking status. Most of our study population was diagnosed before the FDA approval of PARP inhibitors for ovarian cancer treatment in 2014. Nonetheless, prior studies suggest that HRD is associated with improved survival even in the absence of systemic therapy and may enhance response to chemotherapy, although findings on the latter remain inconsistent ( 6 – 9 ). While we did not have detailed data on adjuvant chemotherapy, we restricted our main analysis to a non-neoadjuvant treated population and performed sensitivity analyses restricted to individuals with optimal tumor debulking. In the sensitivity analysis we observed a 50% reduction in the risk of all-cause mortality for both Black and White individuals with HRD compared to those without, suggesting that, as expected, suboptimal tumor debulking is a strong predictor of ovarian cancer mortality, and that treatment remains a key contributor to racial disparities in ovarian cancer survival. Additionally, a greater proportion of variants detected in Black individuals were classified as VUS or unannotated. This higher burden has been reported previously and is attributed to differences in genetic variation across populations as well as the underrepresentation of diverse groups in genomics research ( 23 , 24 , 27 – 29 ). As VUS are not used for clinical decision-making there is an urgent need to characterize these variants ( 37 ). In our study, Black individuals with germline VUS or unannotated variants in genes prioritized for genetic testing had a higher prevalence of HRD scarring and family history of ovarian and breast cancer compared to those with no germline variant. Additionally, their age at diagnosis and stage were more similar to individuals with pathogenic variants than to those without any germline variant, suggesting that some of these VUS or unannotated variants may be pathogenic. Prior studies have estimated that ~30% of HGSC have a genomic alteration in homologous recombination pathway genes, most commonly BRCA1 (germline 9–12% and somatic 3–8%) and BRCA2 (germline 8% and somatic 3–4%), and another ~20% have an epigenetic alteration in BRCA1 or BRCA2 ( 5 , 52 ). In our cohort, the prevalences of any genomic alteration, germline and somatic BRCA1 variants, and somatic BRCA2 mutations were similar to prior reports. However, the prevalence of germline BRCA2 variants was lower in White individuals compared to Black individuals in our study population and compared to prior studies ( 5 , 52 ). While the combined prevalence of the COSMIC HRD-associated base-pair substitution and insertion and deletion signatures were higher in Black individuals compared with White (40.4% versus 29.3%), LoH was less common in Black individuals compared with White (21.7% versus 29.8%), resulting in a similar prevalence of any HRD scarring across populations (52.1% versus 53.8%). It has been reported that there is a higher prevalence of certain HRD features in ovarian, breast, lung, and prostate cancers in Black individuals compared to White individuals ( 23 , 24 , 30 – 32 ). However, after taking into consideration somatic and germline variants, and HRD associated genomic scarring, the overall prevalence of any HRD was similar in Black and White individuals (62.1% versus 66.9%). Inequities in genetic testing and risk-reducing surgery could have influenced the observed distribution of variants in HRD genes. However, the uptake of genetic testing for BRCA among all populations remains relatively low and was higher during the enrollment period for AACES (2010–2015) compared to NCOCS (1999–2005) ( 53 , 54 ). Additionally, if a higher prevalence of hereditary HGSC was due to lower rates of risk-reducing surgery, we would anticipate observing a higher prevalence of germline variants in all genes used in genetic testing. However, germline BRCA1 variants were more common among White individuals compared with Black individuals. A prior study of families with BRCA alterations reported that a higher proportion of BRCA2 variants among Black individuals localized to genomic regions associated with an elevated ovarian cancer risk compared with variants in White individuals ( 20 ). Our study observed the opposite pattern for BRCA1 variants with a higher proportion localizing to the elevated risk region in White individuals compared with Black individuals. These findings along with observed variation in BRCA variants by geographic region suggest that differences may relate to differences in genetic ancestry ( 19 – 26 ). In 2020, ASCO published its guidelines for ovarian cancer germline and tumor genetic testing which included recommending germline testing for BRCA1, BRCA2 , and other susceptibility genes at the time of diagnosis, and somatic testing for BRCA1 / BRCA2 in women without a germline pathogenic or likely pathogenic BRCA variant ( 37 ). Other susceptibility genes prioritized in ASCO recommendations included RAD51C, RAD51D, BRIP1, and PALB2 ( 28 , 55 – 63 ). Other homologous recombination pathway genes not prioritized for genetic testing were mentioned but evidence supporting associations with ovarian cancer risk were inconclusive. Somatic mutations in these genes were associated with better survival among Black individuals highlighting the need to understand these genes in the context of ovarian cancer outcomes. Limitations Individuals in our study were recruited as part of interview-based studies, which likely introduced survivorship bias. However, prior work suggests that survival for Black individuals in AACES is similar to Black individuals in SEER after conditioning on surviving 10-months ( 64 ). Additionally, methods for recruitment, data collection, and genomic assays were nearly identical in Black and White individuals in our study population which likely mitigated differential bias. Further, time from diagnosis to enrollment was similar in Black and White individuals, and left-truncated models were used to adjust for residual differences. Given that copy number alterations were approximated from whole-exome sequencing, rather than the gold standard of whole-genome sequencing, it is likely that some individuals were misclassified with regard to HRD status. However, we would not expect misclassification to be differential, and while differences in sequencing methods, target coverage, and variant filtering may explain differences across studies, these methods were nearly identical for Black and White individuals in our study population. Sample size limited statistical precision in our analyses and led to imprecise point estimates for some HRD features and survival particularly among White individuals. In our study, individuals were grouped based on self-reported race to understand racial disparities in ovarian cancer survival for Black individuals. This approach was essential for assessing differences in the association HRD and survival outcomes. However, to better understand the extent to which variation in the frequency of specific homologous recombination gene alterations—and particularly VUS—is influenced by founder effects, future research should incorporate genetic ancestry data. Additionally, high rates of VUS have been reported in other racial and ethnic groups, and addressing disparities in understanding of HRD requires further research characterizing HRD across all racial and ethnic groups. Conclusions The HRD associated survival benefit was about half that for Black compared with White individuals with HGSC and more of the germline variants and somatic mutations detected among Black individuals compared with White individuals were VUS/unannotated. These findings suggest that Black individuals may not benefit equally from precision medicine strategies in part due to a higher proportion of VUS which may complicate referral for such care. Our findings emphasize the need to recruit diverse individuals in genomics research. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS2110652-supplement-1.pptx (121.8KB, pptx) 2 NIHMS2110652-supplement-2.docx (27.6KB, docx) 3 NIHMS2110652-supplement-3.docx (28KB, docx) 4 NIHMS2110652-supplement-4.pptx (97.9MB, pptx) 5 NIHMS2110652-supplement-5.docx (25.1KB, docx) 6 NIHMS2110652-supplement-6.docx (102.1KB, docx) Acknowledgments: This work was supported by the National Cancer Institute (NCI) of the National Institutes of Health (R01 CA200854 to J.A. Doherty and J.M. Schildkraut, R01 CA188943–01A1 to M. Hildebrandt, C. Huff, and J.M. Schildkraut, R01 CA142081 to J.M. Schildkraut, R01 CA076016 to J.M. Schildkraut, R01 CA237170 to C.S. Greene and J.A. Doherty). We would like to acknowledge the AACES interviewers, Christine Bard, LaTonda Briggs, Whitney Franz (North Carolina) and Robin Gold (Detroit). We also acknowledge the individuals responsible for facilitating case ascertainment across the ten sites including: Jennifer Burczyk-Brown (Alabama); Rana Bayakly and Vicki Bennett (Georgia); the Louisiana Tumor Registry; Lisa Paddock and Manisha Narang (New Jersey); Diana Slone, Yingli Wolinsky, Steven Waggoner, Anne Heugel, Nancy Fusco, Kelly Ferguson, Peter Rose, Deb Strater, Taryn Ferber, Donna White, Lynn Borzi, Eric Jenison, Nairmeen Haller, Debbie Thomas, Vivian von Gruenigen, Michele McCarroll, Joyce Neading, John Geisler, Stephanie Smiddy, David Cohn, Michele Vaughan, Luis Vaccarello, Elayna Freese, James Pavelka, Pam Plummer, William Nahhas, Ellen Cato, John Moroney, Mark Wysong, Tonia Combs, Marci Bowling, Brandon Fletcher (Ohio); Martin Whiteside (Tennessee) and Georgina Armstrong and the Texas Registry, Cancer Epidemiology and Surveillance Branch, Department of State Health Services. We would also like to acknowledge the AACES investigators, Anthon