INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1557,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12465044','Human epidermal growth factor receptor-2, nectin-4, and trophoblast cell surface antigen-2 expression in endometrial carcinosarcoma.','Lin SY, Chang CT, Wu RC, Lai CR, Chen YJ.','2025','nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 cantrell mcconechy transdifferentiation gotoh cherniack metaplastic mccluggage cantrell amant vaidya paralleling huvila designates upshifting berek erbb escs buza slomovitz montes fader vaidya rottmann ecss rottmann raspollini mizoguchi rottmann mauricio nectin trop2 challita nectin enfortumab trop2 sacituzumab govitecan bardia raji trop2 ecss bignotti raji nectin trop2 ecss taipei vghirb 037cc micrometer 4b5 prediluted ventana tucson nectin epr15613 biosb goleta trop2 bsb nectin trop2 prediluted ventana a16 prediluted ventana g219 prediluted ventana sp93 prediluted ventana do7 glostrup ventana ventana destiny pantumor02 meric bernstam faint basolateral basolateral buza nectin trop2 histochemical challita challita subclonal mmrp subclonal liposarcoma liposarcoma nectin trop2 nectin trop2 verus nectin trop2 mmrp nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 ecss rottmann raspollini evading dumontet statice nectin compartmental trop2 raji trop2 raji nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 ecss cherniack mccluggage nectin trop2 carcinosarcomas nectin trop2 nectin trop2 nectin trop2 chiung jen tgog5001 nstc a49 my3 jen taipei v112c v112ep v113ep v113c jen amant carcinosarcomas bardia sacituzumab govitecan hziy nejmoa1814213 berek bignotti buza nature12113 cantrell duska challita enfortumab nectin nectin cancers15102865 cherniack ccell carcinosarcomas montes dumontet s41573 fader overexpress nct01367002 gotoh carcinosarcomas modpat huvila carcinosarcomas carcinosarcomas mauricio 8201a mccluggage carcinosarcomas metaplastic mcconechy carcinosarcomas cjp2 meric bernstam destiny pantumor02 mizoguchi statfacts ncch1615 statice raji carcinosarcomas hrs7 raspollini carcinosarcomas enfortumab rottmann carcinosarcomas s41379 slomovitz vaidya tjog','Highlights • HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 were preferentially expressed in the carcinomatous component of ECS. • HER2 overexpression (3+) was identified in 7.1 % of ECS. • HER2 2+/3+ expression was associated with serous carcinoma differentiation in ECS. • No strong expression of nectin-4 or TROP2 was observed in ECS. Keywords: Carcinosarcoma, Antibody-drug conjugate, Human epidermal growth factor receptor-2, Nectin-4, Trophoblast cell surface antigen-2 Abstract Objective To characterize the immunohistochemical expression of human epidermal growth factor receptor-2 (HER2), nectin-4, and trophoblast cell surface antigen-2 (TROP2) in endometrial carcinosarcoma (ECS), with particular attention to compartment-specific expression patterns and potential therapeutic implications. Methods We retrospectively analyzed 56 ECS cases and performed immunohistochemistry for HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 on representative tumor sections. Expression levels were semi-quantitatively scored and compared between the carcinomatous and sarcomatous components. Associations with clinicopathological features were also assessed. Results HER2 overexpression (3+) was identified in 4 cases (7.1 %), with an additional 6 cases (10.7 %) showing equivocal (2+) staining. HER2 2+/3+ expression was significantly associated with serous carcinoma differentiation (31.0 % vs. 3.7 %, P = 0.012) and was largely confined to the carcinomatous component ( P < 0.001). Nectin-4 was expressed in 39.3 % of cases, predominantly weak in intensity, with significantly higher expression in the carcinomatous than in the sarcomatous component ( P < 0.001). TROP2 expression was observed in 62.5 % of cases, also confined to the carcinomatous component, with no strong expression detected. No significant correlations were found between marker expression and other clinicopathological variables beyond serous differentiation. Conclusions HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 are preferentially expressed in the carcinomatous component of ECS. HER2-targeted antibody–drug conjugates may be a promising therapeutic strategy for ECS with serous carcinoma differentiation. Further investigation is warranted to determine the clinical relevance of targeting nectin-4 and TROP2 in ECS. 1. Introduction Endometrial carcinosarcoma (ECS), formerly known as malignant mixed Müllerian tumor, is a rare but highly aggressive neoplasm that accounts for less than 5 % of endometrial malignancies ( Cantrell et al., 2015 , National Cancer Institute, 2025 ). Histologically, ECS is defined by a biphasic morphology, comprising both carcinomatous (epithelial) and sarcomatous (mesenchymal) components ( WHO Classification of Tumors Editorial Board, 2020 ). Molecular studies have shown that the carcinomatous and sarcomatous components harbor similar genomic alterations, which overlap with those observed in other high-grade endometrial carcinomas ( McConechy et al., 2015 ). In addition, ECS frequently exhibits features of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition, supporting the hypothesis of mesenchymal transdifferentiation from an epithelial precursor ( Gotoh et al., 2019 , Cherniack et al., 2017 ). Some researchers have propose that ECS may be more accurately classified as a form of metaplastic carcinoma ( McCluggage, 2002 , de Jong et al., 2011 ). Like high-grade endometrial carcinoma, ECS is associated with poor responsiveness to standard therapies and unfavorable prognosis, highlighting the urgent need to identify novel therapeutic targets ( Cantrell et al., 2015 , Powell et al., 2010 , Amant et al., 2005 , Vaidya et al., 2006 , Pham et al., 2025 ). There is increasing interest in applying immunohistochemical and molecular profiling to ECS, particularly in the search for actionable therapeutic targets. According to The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) classification, most ECS fall into the copy-number high (serous-like)/p53-abnormal molecular subtypes, paralleling the classification of endometrial serous carcinoma (ESC), the prototypical representative of this category ( Cancer Genome Atlas Research et al., 2013 , Huvila et al., 2024 ). Similarly, the 2023 FIGO staging system designates ECS as an aggressive histological type, alongside ESC, which may lead to stage upshifting and influence clinical decision-making ( Berek et al., 2023 ). Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2), a member of the ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases, is overexpressed or amplified in approximately 15–35 % of ESCs and is associated with poor outcome ( Buza et al., 2013 , Slomovitz, 2004 , Diaz-Montes et al., 2006 ). Trastuzumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting HER2, has demonstrated clinical benefit in HER2-positive (immunohistochemistry score of 3+ or 2+ with gene amplification confirmed by fluorescence in situ hybridization) ESC ( Fader et al., 2020 , Wang et al., 2024 ). Notably, serous carcinoma differentiation is present in approximately 25–70 % of the epithelial components in ECS cases, and HER2 overexpression or amplification is more frequently observed in these cases ( Vaidya et al., 2006 , Rottmann et al., 2020 , Jenkins et al., 2020 , Jenkins et al., 2022 ). HER2 overexpression or amplification has been reported in approximately 5–15 % of ECSs, predominantly restricted to the carcinomatous component ( Rottmann et al., 2020 , Jenkins et al., 2022 , Raspollini et al., 2005 , Mizoguchi et al., 2025 ). Moreover, intratumoral heterogeneity of HER2 expression or amplification has been documented in ECS ( Rottmann et al., 2020 , Jenkins et al., 2022 ). Preclinical and clinical studies have suggested potential benefit of HER2-targeted therapies in HER2-positive ECS, providing a rationale for its clinical application ( Lu et al., 2025 , Mauricio et al., 2023 , Nishikawa et al., 2023 ). Nectin-4 and trophoblast cell surface antigen 2 (TROP2) are membrane glycoproteins involved in cell adhesion and proliferation, and both have emerged as targets for antibody–drug conjugates (ADCs) ( Challita-Eid et al., 2016 , Rosenberg et al., 2019 ). Nectin-4 is targeted by enfortumab vedotin, which is approved for advanced urothelial carcinoma, while TROP2 is targeted by sacituzumab govitecan, approved for metastatic triple-negative breast cancer ( Rosenberg et al., 2019 , Bardia et al., 2019 ). Although the expression of these markers has been explored in various epithelial malignancies, including endometrial carcinomas, their prevalence and clinicopathological significance in ECS remain poorly characterized. Only Raji et al. have reported TROP2 expression in approximately 35 % of ECSs ( Chang et al., 2023 , Bignotti et al., 2010 , Raji et al., 2011 ). In this study, we aimed to characterize the immunohistochemical expression of HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 in a cohort of ECSs. We evaluated the expression in both carcinomatous and sarcomatous components and analyzed associations with clinicopathological features, with the goal of identifying potential therapeutic targets for this aggressive tumor type. 2. Methods 2.1. Case selection and pathological review This study was approved by the Institutional Review Boards of Taipei Veterans General Hospital (VGHIRB No. 2022-07-037CC). Patients who underwent staging surgery and were diagnosed with ECS between 2015 and 2021 were included. All available pathology slides were reviewed to confirm the diagnosis based on the essential diagnostic criteria outlined in the World Health Organization (WHO) classification of female genital tumors ( WHO Classification of Tumors Editorial Board, 2020 ). 2.2. Immunohistochemistry (IHC) Four-micrometer whole tissue sections containing both carcinomatous and sarcomatous components were used for IHC analysis. IHC for HER2 was performed using the 4B5 antibody (prediluted; Roche Ventana, Tucson, AZ, USA), with routine external positive and negative controls according to laboratory standards. IHC for nectin-4 IHC was performed using the EPR15613-68 antibody (dilution 1:100; BioSB, Goleta, CA, USA). IHC for TROP2 was performed using the BSB-148 antibody (dilution 1:50; Abcam, Cambridge, UK). Normal skin tissue was used as positive control for nectin-4 and TROP2 IHC. IHC for mismatch repair (MMR) proteins was performed using the M1 antibody (prediluted; Roche Ventana) for MLH1; A16-4 (prediluted; Roche Ventana) for PMS2; G219-1129 (prediluted; Roche Ventana) for MSH2; and SP93 (prediluted; Roche Ventana) for MSH6. p53 was performed using the DO7 antibody (dilution 1:50; Dako, Glostrup, Denmark). The IHC stains were performed using the Roche Ventana Benchmark ULTRA System (Roche Ventana). Immunostaining of the whole tumor, the carcinomatous component, and the sarcomatous component were evaluated. HER2 expression in tumor cells was evaluated using the current American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO)/College of American Pathologists (CAP) scoring criteria for gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma in concordance with the DESTINY-PanTumor02 phase II trial ( Meric-Bernstam et al., 2024 ). The staining result was scored as 0 (negative) if no staining was observed or membranous training in less than 10 % of tumor cells, 1+ (negative) if faint membrane staining was observed in 10 % or more tumor cells, 2+ (equivocal) if weak to moderate complete, basolateral or lateral membranous staining was observed in 10 % or more of tumor cells, and 3+ (positive) if intense complete, basolateral or lateral membrane staining was observed in 10 % or more of tumor cells. Intratumoral heterogeneity was defined as at least 5 % of the tumor areas showing a 2-degree or greater difference in staining intensity (none versus moderate, none versus strong, or weak versus strong) according to the criteria proposed by Buza et al. (2013 ). For nectin-4 and TROP2 expression, membranous staining in tumor cells was considered positive and evaluated using the histochemical scoring system (H-score) as in previous studies ( Challita-Eid et al., 2016 ). H-score is the product of the percentage of tumor cells showing positive staining and staining intensity (0, no staining; 1, weak staining; 2, moderate staining; and 3, strong staining), ranging from 0 to 300. The results were further classified as negative (0–14), weak (15–99), moderate (100–199), or strong (200–300) for further analysis as previously described ( Challita-Eid et al., 2016 ). MMR protein expression was interpreted as deficient if there was complete loss of nuclear staining in all tumor cells for any of the four MMR proteins, or if subclonal loss (involving at least 10 % of tumor cells) was observed, provided that internal controls showed intact staining. Expression was considered proficient (MMRp) if all four MMR proteins demonstrated intact nuclear staining in all tumor cells. p53 expression was interpreted as abnormal (overexpression: strong diffuse nuclear staining in at least 80 % of cells; null: no expression with an intact internal control; cytoplasmic staining with or without nuclear staining, or subclonal: at least 10 % tumor cells in discrete areas showing abnormal expression adjacent to areas showing normal expression) or normal (variable nuclear staining). 2.3. Statistical analysis All statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 26.0 (IBM Corp., Armonk, NY, USA). Correlations between categorical variables were assessed using Fisher’s exact test, and comparisons of continuous variables were analyzed using the Mann–Whitney U test. Statistical significance was defined as a two-tailed P < 0.05. 3. Results 3.1. Patient characteristics Clinicopathological features of the patients are summarized in Table 1 . Fifty-six patients with ECS with a median age of 61 years (range, 37–86 years) were included. Based on the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stages at presentation (FIGO 2009), 16 (28.6 %) patients had stage I, five (8.9 %) had stage II, 19 (33.9 %) had stage III, and 16 (28.6 %) had stage IV disease. For the carcinomatous components, 26 (46.4 %) cases were endometrioid, 18 (32.1 %) were serous, 11 (19.6 %) were mixed endometrioid and serous, and one (1.8 %) was clear cell. For the sarcomatous components, 42 (75.0 %) cases were entirely homologous and 14 (25.0 %) contained heterologous elements (eight rhabdomyosarcoma, four chondrosarcoma, one rhabdomyosarcoma and liposarcoma, and one liposarcoma). Table 1. Correlation of HER2, Nectin-4, and TROP2 Expression with Clinicopathological Features in Endometrial Carcinosarcoma. Characteristic HER2 Nectin-4 TROP2 N Negative (0) Negative (1+) Equivocal (2+) Positive (3+) p Negative Weak Moderate Strong p Negative Weak Moderate Strong p All 56 40 (71.4 %) 6 (10.7 %) 6 (10.7 %) 4 (7.1 %) 34 (60.7 %) 22 (39.3 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 21 (37.5 %) 25 (44.6 %) 10 (17.9 %) 0 (0.0 %) FIGO stage 0.262 0.232 0.124 Stage I 16 13 (81.3 %) 1 (6.3 %) 1 (6.3 %) 1 (6.3 %) 9 (56.3 %) 7 (43.8 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 8 (50.0 %) 4 (25.0 %) 4 (25.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) Stage II 5 2 (40.0 %) 2 (40.0 %) 1 (20.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 3 (60.0 %) 2 (40.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 3 (60.0 %) 1 (20.0 %) 1 (20.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) Stage III 19 12 (63.2 %) 1 (5.3 %) 3 (15.8 %) 3 (15.8 %) 9 (47.4 %) 10 (52.6 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 3 (15.8 %) 13 (68.4 %) 3 (15.8 %) 0 (0.0 %) Stage IV 16 13 (81.3 %) 2 (12.5 %) 1 (6.3 %) 0 (0.0 %) 13 (81.3 %) 3 (18.8 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 7 (43.8 %) 7 (43.8 %) 2 (12.5 %) 0 (0.0 %) Carcinomatous component histology 0.269 0.238 0.174 Serous 18 9 (50.0 %) 3 (16.7 %) 3 (16.7 %) 3 (16.7 %) 11 (61.1 %) 7 (38.9 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 10 (55.6 %) 7 (38.9 %) 1 (5.6 %) 0 (0.0 %) Mixed endometrioid and serous 11 6 (54.5 %) 2 (18.2 %) 2 (18.2 %) 1 (9.1 %) 4 (36.4 %) 7 (63.6 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 2 (18.2 %) 7 (63.6 %) 2 (18.2 %) 0 (0.0 %) Endometrioid 26 24 (92.3 %) 1 (3.8 %) 1 (3.8 %) 0 (0.0 %) 18 (69.2 %) 8 (30.8 %) 0 (0.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) 8 