1 %) sarcomatous components showed 1+. ( Table 1 and Fig. 1 ) Intratumoral heterogeneity was observed in 12 (21.4 %) cases, including all cases with 2+ or 3+ staining. For nectin-4, 22 (39.3 %) cases showed weak expression, with a median H-score of 10 (range, 0–60); 30 (53.6 %) and one (1.8 %) carcinomatous components showed weak and moderate expression, respectively, with a median H-score of 15.5 (range, 0–100); nine (16.1 %) sarcomatous components showed weak expression with a median H-score of 0 (range, 0–35). For TROP2, 25 (44.6 %) and 10 (17.9 %) cases showed weak and moderate expression, respectively, with a median H-score of 20.5 (range, 0–120); 22 (39.3 %) and 18 (32.1 %) carcinomatous components showed weak and moderate expression, respectively, with a median H-score of 41 (range, 0–170); none of the sarcomatous components showed any expression. All cases in our cohort were MMRp. Fifty-two (92.9 %) cases showed abnormal expression of p53, with 39 (69.6 %) showed nuclear overexpression and 13 (23.2 %) showed null pattern, and all carcinomatous components and sarcomatous components showed same expression pattern. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Immunohistochemical expression of HER2, Nectin-4, and TROP2 in endometrial carcinosarcoma. (A) HER2 shows a score of 3+ in the carcinomatous component (mixed endometrioid and serous), with a score of 0 in the adjacent sarcomatous component. (B) Nectin-4 shows weak membranous expression in the carcinomatous component, with no expression in the adjacent sarcomatous component. (C) TROP2 shows moderate membranous expression in the carcinomatous component, with no expression in the sarcomatous component. HER2 expression of scores 2+ or 3+ was significantly more frequent in cases with any serous carcinoma differentiation than in those without (31.0 % versus 3.7 %, P = 0.012) and in carcinomatous components than in sarcomatous components (21.4 % versus 0.0 %, P < 0.001) ( Fig. 1 and Table 2 ). Nectin-4 and TROP2 expression was significantly higher in carcinomatous components than in sarcomatous components (nectin-4, median H-score: 15.5 versus 0, P < 0.001; TROP2, median H-score: 41 versus 0, P < 0.001) ( Table 1 ). HER2 overexpression (3+) did not differ significantly between cases with or without any serous carcinoma differentiation (13.8 % versus 0.0 %, P = 0.112), nor between carcinomatous and sarcomatous components (1.8 % versus 0.0 %, P = 0.118) ( Table 2 ). HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 expression was not associated with FIGO stage (HER2, P = 0.262; nectin-4, P = 0.232; TROP2, P = 0.124), sarcomatous component histology (HER2, P = 0.731; nectin-4, P = 1.000; TROP2, P = 0.558), or p53 expression (HER2, P = 0.598; nectin-4, P = 1.000; TROP2, P = 0.108) ( Table 1 ). Table 2. HER2 expression concordance between carcinomatous and sarcomatous components in endometrial carcinosarcoma. Characteristic HER2 N Negative/Equivocal (0–2+) Positive (3+) p Carcinomatous component histology, any serous versus non-serous 0.112 Any serous 29 25 (86.2 %) 4 (13.8 %) Non-serous 27 27 (100.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) Expression of each component 0.118 Carcinomatous component 56 52 (92.9 %) 4 (1.8 %) Sarcomatous component 56 56 (100.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) Characteristic HER2 N Negative (0/1+) Equivocal/Positive (2+/3+) p Carcinomatous component histology, any serous versus non-serous 0.012* Any serous 29 20 (69.0 %) 9 (31.0 %) Non-serous 27 26 (96.3 %) 1 (3.7 %) Expression of each component < 0.001* Carcinomatous component 56 44 (78.6 %) 12 (21.4 %) Sarcomatous component 56 56 (100.0 %) 0 (0.0 %) Open in a new tab *, statistically significant. 4. Discussion We systematically evaluated the immunohistochemical expression of HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 in ECS, focusing on differences between the carcinomatous and sarcomatous components. HER2 overexpression (3+) was identified in four (7.1 %) cases, while an additional six (10.7 %) cases demonstrated equivocal (2+) staining. HER2 expression with scores of 2+ or 3+ was significantly more frequent in tumors with serous carcinoma differentiation compared to those without (31.0 % vs. 3.7 %, P = 0.012). This expression was predominantly localized to the carcinomatous component (25.0 % showed 1+, 14.3 % 2+, and 7.1 % 3+), while the sarcomatous component exhibited minimal staining (1+ in 7.1 %, P < 0.001). All tumors with HER2 scores of 2+ or 3+ demonstrated intratumoral heterogeneity of expression. These findings align with prior reports and support the potential utility of HER2-targeted therapies in ECSs exhibiting serous differentiation ( Rottmann et al., 2020 , Jenkins et al., 2022 , Raspollini et al., 2005 ). The lack of HER2 expression in the sarcomatous component and the presence of intratumoral heterogeneity may limit the efficacy of monoclonal antibody therapies such as trastuzumab, as heterogeneous tumors may harbor HER2-negative clones capable of evading treatment. However, these tumors may still respond to HER2-targeted ADCs like trastuzumab deruxtecan, which can exert a bystander effect on adjacent tumor cells regardless of HER2 expression levels ( Dumontet et al., 2023 ). Notably, trastuzumab deruxtecan has shown clinical efficacy in both HER2-high (2+/3+) and HER2-low (1+) tumors, as demonstrated in the STATICE trial ( Nishikawa et al., 2023 ). In our cohort, 28.5 % of cases demonstrated HER2 expression of 1+ or greater, further supporting the rationale for considering HER2-targeted ADCs in ECS. Nectin-4 expression was observed in 22 cases (39.3 %), all of which exhibited weak staining. Compartmental analysis revealed significantly higher expression in the carcinomatous component (53.6 % weak, 1.8 % moderate; median H-score 15.5, range 0–100) compared to the sarcomatous component (16.1 % weak; median H-score 0, range 0–35; P < 0.001). TROP2 expression was detected in 35 cases (62.5 %), including 44.6 % with weak and 17.9 % with moderate staining. Unlike the findings reported by Raji et al., no cases in our cohort exhibited strong TROP2 expression, which may reflect methodological differences ( Raji et al., 2011 ). As observed with HER2 and nectin-4, TROP2 expression was restricted to the carcinomatous component (median H-score 41, range 0–170), with no detectable staining in the sarcomatous areas ( P < 0.001). Although expression levels of nectin-4 and TROP2 were generally low to moderate, their consistent confinement to the carcinomatous component supports the concept of epithelial-restricted antigen expression in ECS. Given the overall low expression levels, patients with ECS may not be optimal candidates for anti–nectin-4 or anti–TROP2 therapies; however, the therapeutic potential of these agents in tumors with low-level expression remains uncertain and warrants further study. We observed abnormal p53 expression, either overexpression or null, in 92.9 % of cases and showed complete concordance between the epithelial and mesenchymal components. These findings are consistent with previous studies and align with the TCGA molecular classification (despite the lack of POLE mutation testing in our cohort), which classifies 60–78 % of ECSs as copy-number high/p53-abnormal tumors, further supporting a monoclonal origin ( Cherniack et al., 2017 , McCluggage, 2002 , Cancer Genome Atlas Research et al., 2013 , Jenkins et al., 2020 ). Beyond serous carcinoma differentiation, no other clinicopathological parameters in our cohort showed significant correlation with HER2, nectin-4, or TROP2 expression. These findings highlight the potential value of universal immunohistochemical screening and the need for novel predictive biomarkers to optimize patient selection. Our study has several limitations. First, it was a retrospective analysis with a relatively small cohort and limited follow-up duration. Second, POLE mutation testing was not performed, which limited comprehensive classification under the TCGA framework. Recent studies have identified POLE-mutated uterine carcinosarcomas as a distinct molecular subset with favorable prognosis and unique pathologic features ( Hammer et al., 2025 ). Third, we did not perform HER2 in situ hybridization to clarify equivocal (2+) IHC results. Fourth, nectin-4 and TROP2 expression were evaluated using H-scores and arbitrary cutoffs, which may affect reproducibility and clinical interpretation. Finally, the absence of survival and treatment response data precluded assessment of the prognostic or predictive significance of the studied markers. In summary, our study demonstrates that HER2, nectin-4, and TROP2 are predominantly expressed in the carcinomatous component of ECS, with HER2 scores 2+ or 3+ significantly associated with serous carcinoma differentiation. The epithelial-restricted and heterogeneous expression patterns of these markers underscore the potential of targeted therapies, particularly HER2-directed ADCs, in a subset of ECS patients. While the low expression levels of nectin-4 and TROP2 may limit the immediate applicability of ADCs targeting these antigens, their consistent localization to the epithelial component warrants further exploration. These findings provide a foundation for future therapeutic strategies and highlight the need for expanded molecular profiling in ECS to guide precision oncology approaches. CRediT authorship contribution statement Shih-Yao Lin: Writing – original draft, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Chung-Ting Chang: Writing – original draft, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Ren-Chin Wu: Validation, Resources, Methodology, Data curation, Conceptualization. Chiung-Ru Lai: Supervision, Resources, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization. Yi-Jen Chen: Writing – review & editing, Validation, Supervision, Software, Resources, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization. Funding This study was supported by grants from the Taiwanese Gynecologic Oncology Group (TGOG5001 to Shih-Yao Lin), the National Science and Technology Council (NSTC 112-2314-B-A49-038-