INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1560,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12465649','PARPis after PARPis in patients with recurrent epithelial ovarian cancer: a single institutional experience.','Yuan H, Wang T, Yao H, Wu L, Li N.','2025','rechallenge ttnt parpi1 parpi2 ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt1 ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt2 ttnt ttnt1 ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 retreatment retreatment retreatment s13048 repairing oreo rechallenge rechallenge ttnt apatinib ttnt ttnt1 parpi1 ttnt2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi1 fluzoparib parpi1 parpi1 parpi1 fluzoparib olaprib parpi2 parpi2 fluzoparib parpi2 parpi2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi1 parpi2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 ttnt ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt1 ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt2 ttnt ttnt1 ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt2 ttnt1 ttnt2 ttnt1 ttnt2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi1 parpi2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi2 parpi1 parpi2 parpi2 oreo rechallenge retreatment retreatment ttnt retreatment ttnt2 retreatment retreatment retreatment retreatment sensitize ceralasertib macrocytic orthochromatic retreatment retreatment rechallenge retreatment retreatment retreatment indebted cfa202202018 diligently tonghui laversanne tattersall wiggans rogozinska cd007929 pothuri depont graybill coquard pautier pignata perol swisher steffensen veliparib matulonis malander provencher mahner follana ov16 secord cloven quadra swisher oza rucaparib ariel2 oza aghajanian oaknin rucaparib ariel3 scambia oaknin pujade lauraine ledermann selle gebski penson oza solo2 ov21 penson cibula leath bidzinski nonplatinum solo3 ganesan jonkers moubarak ataseven traut welz baert essel behbakht dvorak pujade lauraine selle scambia asselain marme lindemann rechallenge oreo ov38 selle asselain montestruc bazan salutari oreo rechallenge jou kurzrock zajchowski eskander clearity s97 xiu e51 espirito frytak pujade lauraine rechallenge oreo s1308 jou kurzrock zajchowski eskander clearity s97 lheureux oaknin madariaga dhani cediranib kristeleit lisyanskaya fedenko dvorkin shparyk rucaparib ariel4 mcmullen karakasis loembe parr oza duette kgog nirvana','Abstract Objective To explore the clinical efficacy of PARPis maintenance treatment rechallenge in patients with recurrent epithelial ovarian cancer (EOC) in China. Methods We included patients diagnosed with primary EOC who received 2 lines of PARPis maintenance treatment after achieving complete response (CR) or partial response (PR) with the previous chemotherapies. The patients’ full medical records were included in this study. Clinical and pathologic variables, treatment modalities, and outcomes were assessed. We investigated the treatment patterns and time to next treatment (TTNT). Results A total of 31 ovarian cancer patients in our center were included. Among these patients, 20 of them (64.5%) had BRCA1/2 gene mutations. The median duration of PARPi1 and PARPi2 in the entire cohort was 11.2 months (range: 2.0–30.4 months) and 4.8 months (range: 1.0–16.7 months), respectively. Median TTNT1 and TTNT2 for the entire cohort was 12.4 and 7.7 months, respectively. Patients with BRCA1/2 mutation had a significantly better TTNT1 (median TTNT1: 17.3 vs 10.4 months, P = 0.005) than those without. A non- significantly better TTNT2 was observed in patients with BRCA1/2 mutation than those without (median TTNT2: 8.2 vs 5.0 months, P = 0.890). The association between previous chemotherapy response and TTNT was also analyzed. Patients who had a CR to previous chemotherapy had a significantly better TTNT1 (median TTNT1: 16.4 vs 7.6 months, P = 0.001) and TTNT2 (median TTNT2: 11.1 vs 4.9 months, P = 0.003) than those who had a PR. No grade Ⅲ-IV anemia occurred. Grade III PARPis-related thrombocytopenia was found in only 1 patient (3.2%, 1/31) who received PARPi2 treatment. For patients who developed PARPis-related anemia ( n = 9) or thrombocytopenia ( n = 7) during PARPi1 treatment, 7 patients (77.8%, 7/9) and 6 patients (85.7%, 6/7) developed anemia or thrombocytopenia again during PARPi2 treatment, respectively. Conclusions Patients with PARPis resistant recurrent EOC may derive benefit from PARPis re-treatment, especially for those with complete response to the last chemotherapy. Patients with BRCA1/2 mutation were more likely to benefit from PARPis retreatment than those with wild-type. Anemia and thrombocytopenia were more common in PARPis retreatment patients. A small proportion of patients had a longer benefit from PARPis retreatment than from previous PARPis treatment. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s13048-025-01786-0. Keyword: PARPis after PARPis, Recurrent epithelial ovarian cancer Introduction Epithelial ovarian cancer (EOC) is a major cause of death in women worldwide [ 1 ]. There were approximately 314,000 new cases of ovarian cancer diagnosed and 208,000 deaths worldwide [ 2 ]. In 2022, there were approximately 61,100 new cases of ovarian cancer diagnosed and 32,600 deaths in China [ 3 ]. About 10%−15% ovarian cancer patients carry breast cancer susceptibility genes 1/2 (BRCA1/2) germline mutations [ 4 ]. PARP (poly (ADP‐ribose) polymerase) inhibitors (PARPis), are a type of anticancer treatment that works by preventing cancer cells from repairing DNA damage, especially in those with breast cancer susceptibility gene 1/2 (BRCA1/2) variants [ 5 ]. Clinical trials have demonstrated promising response rates among ovarian cancer patients receiving PARPis.[ 6 – 15 ] However, as also experienced with many other anticancer therapeutics, despite initial and often dramatic responses, patients receiving PARPis often ultimately develop treatment resistance [ 16 , 17 ]. The treatment consensus for patients with PARPis resistance has not been established. Retrospective study support that patients with recurrent EOC may derive benefit of the re-treatment with PARPis in case of clear response to the last platinum-based therapy [ 18 – 20 ]. The OReO/ENGOT Ov-38 trial is the first to provide data on PARPis rechallenge in platinum-sensitive recurrent ovarian cancer patients. Olaparib maintenance rechallenge provide a significant survival improvement, irrespective of BRCA1/2 mutation status [ 20 , 21 ]. PARPis after PARPis is still an important challenge in patients with PARPis resistant EOC [ 22 ]. Currently, the benefit of PARPis after PARPis has not been fully elucidated in Chinese EOC patients. In the current study, we investigated the treatment patterns and time to next treatment (TTNT) in patients diagnosed with primary EOC who received 2 lines of PARPis maintenance treatment after achieving complete response (CR) or partial response (PR) with the previous chemotherapies. Patients and methods Patients Following institutional review board approval, we performed a retrospective analysis of all patients diagnosed with primary EOC who received 2 lines of PARPis maintenance treatment after achieving CR or PR with the previous chemotherapies from January 1, 2018 to March 1, 2022 in the department of gynecological oncology of cancer hospital, Chinese academy of medical sciences, national cancer center. Only patients with a diagnosis of EOC confirmed by an experienced gynecologic pathologist in our hospital were included. Angiogenesis inhibitors such as bevacizumab or apatinib were not permitted as part of PARPis maintenance regimen or prior to the application of PARPis. Methods Next-generation sequencing (NGS) for the entire coding regions and exon/intron boundaries of the BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes were conducted in all patients included in our study. The applied method of mutation screening was described in detail in our previous report [ 23 ]. Only those pathogenic variants that lead to a truncated protein or have been previously reported to be associated with disease were considered to be deleterious. The patients’ full medical records were included in this study. Clinical and pathologic variables, treatment modalities, and outcomes were assessed. Stage was retrospectively assigned using the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) 2014 staging system for ovarian cancer. Statistical analyses Time to next treatment (TTNT) is a useful real-world surrogate for progression free survival (PFS) in certain settings [ 24 ]. For the survival analyses, TTNT1 was defined as the time from initiating PARPi1 treatment to initiating the next line of therapy. TTNT2 was defined as the time from initiating PARPi2 treatment to initiating the next line of therapy. Overall survival (OS) was defined as the time from the date of diagnosis to death for which ovarian cancer was the primary or underlying cause. Survival was estimated using the Kaplan–Meier product-limit method, and differences were tested for statistical significance using the log-rank test. Two-sided P values less than 0.05 were considered to be statistically significant. All analyses were performed using the SPSS Statistics 20.0 software. Results Clinicopathological characteristics A total of 31 ovarian cancer patients who received 2 lines of PARPis maintenance treatment after achieving complete response (CR) or partial response (PR) with the previous chemotherapy in our center were included. Among these patients, 20 of them (64.5%) had BRCA1/2 gene mutations (Supplementary Table 1). Patients median age at diagnosis was 54 years (range: 35–76 years) (Table 1 ). 67.7% (21/31) of them were diagnosed after 50 years. The FIGO 2014 distribution by stage was: stage I in 1 patient (3.2%), stage II in 5 patient (16.1%), stage III in 19 patients (61.3%) and stage IV in 6 patient (19.4%) (Table 1 ). All patients had high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma. 27 patients (27/31, 87.1%) who had received cytoreduction surgery achieved R0/R1 resection (Table 1 ). For the first recurrence, 96.8% patients (30/31) were classified as platinum-sensitive recurrence, and only 1 patient (3.2%) was classified as platinum-resistant recurrence (Table 1 ). Table 1. Clinicopathological characteristics of patients in the entire cohort Clinical Characteristics n % N 31 Median age (Range), years 54 (35–76) Age at diagnosis (years) < 50 10 32.3 ≥ 50 21 67.7 BMI (kg/m 2 ) < 24 17 54.8 ≥ 24 14 45.2 Tumor size (cm) < 5 7 22.6 ≥ 5 24 77.4 BRCA1/2 status Mutation 20 64.5 Wildtype 11 35.5 Histology Serous ovarian cancer 29 93.5 Other 2 6.5 FIGO Stage I 1 3.2 II 5 16.1 III 19 61.3 IV 6 19.4 Neoadjuvant chemotherapy Yes 17 54.8 No 14 45.2 Postoperative residual disease status Optimal (R0/R1) 27 87.1 Suboptimal (≥ R2) 4 12.9 First recurrence Platinum-sensitive recurrence 30 96.8 Platinum-resistant recurrence 1 3.2 Response to previous chemotherapy of PARPi1 CR 17 54.8 PR 14 45.2 Response to previous chemotherapy of PARPi2 CR 7 22.6 PR 24 77.4 Open in a new tab Abbreviations : BMI Body mass index, FIGO International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics, CR Complete response, PR Partial response Response to previous chemotherapy of PARPi1 and PARPi2 was analyzed. For PARPi1, 17 (54.8%) and 14 (45.2%) patients had CR or PR for previous chemotherapy, respectively (Table 1 ). For PARPi2, 7 (22.6%) and 24 (77.4%) patients had CR or PR for previous chemotherapy, respectively (Table 1 ). PARPi1 treatment For PARPi1, 64.5% (20/31) patients received olaparib, 32.3% (10/31) patients received niraparib, and 3.2% (1/31) patients received fluzoparib (Table 2 ). 54.8% (17/31) patients received PARPi1 treatment after ≥ 2 lines of chemotherapy (range, 1–6). 45.2% (14/31), 54.8% (17/31) patients received PARPi1 for first line maintenance, or relapsed maintenance, respectively (Table 2 ). All patients discontinued PARPi1 treatment because of disease progression. Table 2. PARPis treatment among the whole cohort PARPi characteristics PARPi 1 PARPi 2 n % n % PARPi received Fluzoparib 1 3.2 3 9.7 Olaprib 20 64.5 16 51.6 Niraparib 10 32.3 12 38.7 Number of previous chemotherapies 1 14 45.2 0 0.0 2 14 45.2 14 45.2 3 1 3.2 14 45.2 ≥ 4 2 6.5 3 9.7 PARPi setting First line maintenance 14 45.2 0 0.0 Relapsed maintenance 17 54.8 31 100.0 Reason for discontinuation of PARPi Ongoing therapy 0 0.0 8 25.8 Progression 31 100.0 23 74.2 Open in a new tab PARPi2 treatment For PARPi2, 51.6% (16/31) patients received olaparib, 38.7% (12/31) patients received niraparib, and 9.7% (3/31) patients received fluzoparib (Table 2 ). 54.8% (17/31) patients received PARPi2 treatment after ≥ 3 lines of chemotherapy (range, 2–7). All patients received PARPi2 for relapsed maintenance treatment (Table 2 ). At the date of data cutoff, 8 patients (25.8%) continued to receive PARPis treatment. 23 patients (74.2%) patients discontinued PARPi2 treatment because of disease progression. Duration of PARPi1 and PARPi2 treatment The median duration of PARPi1 in the entire cohort was 11.2 months (range: 2.0–30.4 months). The median duration of PARPi1 for patients received first line maintenance, relapsed maintenance was 11.0 months (range: 2.0–19.3 months), 11.2 months (range: 2.1–30.4 months), respectively (Table 3 ). The median duration of PARPi2 in the entire cohort was 4.8 months (range: 1.0–16.7 months) (Table 3 ). Among patients who discontinued PARPi2 treatment ( n = 23), 91.3% (21/23) patients had a longer duration of PARPi1 than PARPi2. Table 3. Treatment duration of PARPi and PARPi2 PARPi characteristics Median duration (range) (months) PARPi1 duration All 11.2 (2.0–30.4) First line maintenance 11.0 (2.0–19.3) Relapsed maintenance 11.2 (2.1–30.4) PARPi2 duration Relapsed maintenance 4.8 (1.0–16.7) Open in a new tab Survival analysis The median follow-up was 46.5 months (range: 28.6–122.1 months). 5 patients (5/31, 16.1%) died during follow up. 5-year OS rate was 76.0% (Supplementary Fig. 1). We further analyzed TTNT in this cohort. Median TTNT1 and TTNT2 for the entire cohort was 12.4 and 7.7 months, respectively. Patients with BRCA1/2 mutation had a significantly better TTNT1 (median TTNT1: 17.3 vs 10.4 months, P = 0.005, Fig. 1 A). A better TTNT2 was observed in patients with BRCA1/2 mutation (median TTNT2: 8.2 vs 5.0 months); however, the difference was not statistically significant ( P = 0.890, Fig. 1 B). The association between previous chemotherapy response and TTNT was also analyzed. Patients who had a CR to previous chemotherapy had a significantly better TTNT1 (median TTNT1: 16.4 vs 7.6 months, P = 0.001, Fig. 2 A) and TTNT2 (median TTNT2: 11.1 vs 4.9 months, P = 0.003, Fig. 2 B) than those who had a PR. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Survival analyses by the Kaplan–Meier method according to BRCA1/2 status in the entire cohort ( n = 31). A Time to next treatment 1 (TTNT1) and ( B ) Time to next treatment 2 (TTNT2) Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Survival analyses by the Kaplan–Meier method according to response to previous chemotherapy in the entire cohort ( n = 31). A Time to next treatment 1 (TTNT1) and ( B ) Time to next treatment 2 (TTNT2) Hematological toxicities of PARPi1 and PARPi2 During PARPi1 treatment, 9 patients (29.0%, 9/31) had PARPis-related anemia, including 16.1% (5/31) of grade Ⅰ and 12.9% (4/31) of grade Ⅱ, and no grade Ⅲ-IV anemia occurred. Among them, 4 patients (44.4%, 4/9) developed anemia within 4 weeks after PARPi1 treatment. During PARPi2 treatment, the incidence of PARPis-related anemia was 51.6% (16/31), all of which were grade I or II, and the incidence was 45.2% (14/31) and 6.5% (2/31), respectively. Ten patients (62.5%, 10/16) developed anemia within 4 weeks after PARPi2 treatment. For patients who developed PARPis-related anemia during PARPi1 treatment ( n = 9), 7 patients (77.8%, 7/9) developed anemia again during PARPi2 treatment. During PARPi1 treatment, 7 patients (22.6%, 7/31) had PARPis-related thrombocytopenia, including 16.1% (5/31) of grade Ⅰ and 6.5% (2/31) of grade Ⅱ, and no grade Ⅲ-IV thrombocytopenia occurred. During PARPi2 treatment, the incidence of PARPis-related thrombocytopenia was 38.7% (12/31), and the incidence was 19.4% (6/31), 16.1% (5/31) and 3.2% (1/31) for grade I-III, respectively. For patients who developed PARPis-related thrombocytopenia during PARPi1 treatment ( n = 7), 6 patients (85.7%, 6/7) developed thrombocytopenia again during PARPi2 treatment. Thrombocytopenia of 12 patients (60.0%, 12/20) occurred within 8 weeks after PARPis treatment. Discussion To our knowledge, the current study is one of the largest studies to report the use of PARPis as maintenance treatment among patients with recurrent EOC who have prior PARPis exposure in Chinese population. We found that patients with PARPis resistant recurrent EOC may derive benefit from PARPis maintenance treatment, especially for those with complete response to the last chemotherapies. PARPis provide a promising treatment strategy that is potentially applicable to several stages of cancer progression [ 16 ]. However, durable responses of PARPis are uncommon and the development of acquired PARPis resistance often occurs during disease recurrence. The median duration of PARPi1 and PARPi2 in the entire cohort was 11.2 and 4.8 months, respectively. Most patients had a shorter duration of PARPi2 than PARPi1. It was worth noting that, 25.8% patients continued to receive PARPi2 treatment and the duration of PARPi2 would be longer. Emerging evidence suggested that the opportunity for PARPis use after prior PARPis exposure exists [ 18 , 19 , 25 ]. The OReO/ENGOT Ov-38 trial was the first to provide data on PARPis rechallenge in platinum sensitive recurrent ovarian cancer patients [ 25 ]. Real-world data supported prospective evidence that patients with recurrent EOC may derive benefit of the re-treatment with PARPis in case of clear response to the last platinum-based therapy [ 18 ]. In the present study, 31 patients received PARPis retreatment which was the largest study in Chinese population. Homologous recombination deficiency (HRD) and platinum sensitivity are prospective biomarkers for predicting the response to PARPis in ovarian cancers. Patients with BRCA1/2 mutant ovarian tumors benefit most from PARPis treatment. BRCA1/2 mutation is an important biomarker to select appropriate patients for PARPis treatment. We found BRCA1/2 mutations associated with a better response to PARPis in the entire cohort or the maintenance treatment subgroup. But the benefit of PARPis retreatment was limited for BRCA1/2 mutation carriers, as one recent study indicated that no difference of TTNT for PARPis retreatment between patients with BRCA1/2 mutation or wild-type [ 26 ]. The underlying reason may be the gradually developed resistance to PARPis for patients with BRCA1/2 mutation. Response to platinum chemotherapy is also a strongly predictive of response to PARPis treatment. The present observation is consistent with these findings. We found patients who achieved a CR to the last chemotherapy appeared to receive longer TTNT2 benefit from PARPis retreatment than those with a PR. Other studies found CA-125 level and presence of visceral disease at baseline were the best predictors of patient outcome with PARPis retreatment [ 21 ]. Given the gradual increase of patients with PARPis resistant, there is a significant need to identify the biomarkers to select appropriate patients for PARPis retreatment. Understanding and overcoming resistance to PARPis is important to PARPis retreatment choice. Acquired resistance to PARPis can develop via multiple mechanisms [ 27 , 28 ]. Strategies designed to specifically target tumor cells with resistance to PARPis are still lacking [ 16 ]. Many combinatorial strategies aim to re-sensitize resistant cells to PARPis, such as ceralasertib + olaparib, niraparib + bevacizumab [ 27 , 29 , 30 ]. The exact role of PARPis after PARPis in EOC should be verified in more trials. PARPis-related hematologic adverse effects including anemia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia are common with all the PARPis, but there are some notable differences between them [ 6 – 15 ]. Many patients have discontinued PARPis treatment because of anemia and thrombocytopenia. Thrombocytopenia is more frequent with niraparib than with other PARPis in the relapse setting. PARPis-related anemia mainly manifests as macrocytic orthochromatic anemia, and most patients with normal serum iron and transferrin. In the present study, anemia and thrombocytopenia were more common in PARPis retreatment patients. Blood routine should be closely tested during PARPis retreatment. There are two limitations to our study. The current study was retrospective, and the primary treatment was not assigned at randomized. All patients in this study came from our single center. Therefore, caution is required when interpreting our results. PARPis rechallenge should be studied in prospective manner. Conclusion Patients with PARPis resistant recurrent EOC may derive benefit from PARPis re-treatment, especially for those with complete response to the last chemotherapy. Patients with BRCA1/2 mutation were more likely to benefit from PARPis retreatment than those with wild-type. Anemia and thrombocytopenia were more common in PARPis retreatment patients. A small proportion of patients had a longer benefit from PARPis retreatment than from previous PARPis treatment. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (16.8KB, docx) Supplementary Material 2. (30.3KB, pdf) Acknowledgements We are indebted to all gynaecologists and others of Department of Gynecologic Oncology in our center for their support and their help in data collection. Authors’ contributions Conceptualization: L.N., W.T.H., Y.H.W., W.L.Y., Y.H.; Methodology: Y.H., W.T.H., L.N.; Validation: Y.H., W.T.H., L.N., W.L.Y.; Formal analysis and investigation: L.N., W.L.Y., Y.H.; Resources and data curation: Y.H., W.T.H.; Writing—original draft preparation: L.N., Y.H.; Writing—review and editing: L.N., Y.H., Y.H.W.; Supervision: Y.H.W., W.L.Y., L.N.; Project administration: Y.H., W.T.H.; Funding acquisition: Y.H., W.L.Y., L.N.. All authors have read and approved the manuscript. Funding This work was supported by Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences Cancer Hospital—Shenzhen Hospital Cooperation Fund (No. CFA202202018). Data availability The data that support the findings of this study are not openly available due to reasons of sensitivity and are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate This study was approved by the Research and Ethics Committee of Cancer Hospital, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences, National Cancer Center. Investigation was conducted in accordance with ethical standards, the Declaration of Helsinki and Chinese and international guidelines. Informed consent was diligently obtained from all enrolled patients. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Hua Yuan and Tonghui Wang contributed equally to this work. References 1. Siegel RL, Miller KD, Fuchs HE, Jemal A. Cancer statistics, 2021. CA Cancer J Clin. 2021;71:7–33. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Sung H, Ferlay J, Siegel RL, Laversanne M, Soerjomataram I, Jemal A, et al. Global cancer statistics 2020: GLOBOCAN estimates of incidence and mortality worldwide for 36 cancers in 185 countries. CA Cancer J Clin. 2021;71:209–49. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Han B, Zheng R, Zeng H, Wang S, Sun K, Chen R, et al. Cancer incidence and mortality in China, 2022. J Natl Cancer Cent. 2024;4:47–53. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 4. Cancer Genome Atlas Research Network. Integrated genomic analyses of ovarian carcinoma. Nature. 2011;474:609–15. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Tattersall A, Ryan N, Wiggans AJ, Rogozińska E, Morrison J. Poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitors for the treatment of ovarian cancer. Cochr