mutation: efficacy and safety from ARIEL4, a randomized phase III study. Gynecol Oncol. 2021;162:S3-4. [ Google Scholar ] 29. McMullen M, Karakasis K, Loembe B, Dean E, Parr G, Oza AM. Duette: a phase II randomized, multicenter study to investigate the efficacy and tolerability of a second maintenance treatment in patients with platinum-sensitive relapsed epithelial ovarian cancer, who have previously received poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitor maintenance treatment. Int J Gynecol Cancer. 2020;30:1824–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Park J, Lim MC, Lee J-K, Jeong DH, Kim SI, Choi MC, et al. A single-arm, phase II study of niraparib and bevacizumab maintenance therapy in platinum-sensitive, recurrent ovarian cancer patients previously treated with a PARP inhibitor: Korean Gynecologic Oncology Group (KGOG 3056)/nirvana-r trial. J Gynecol Oncol. 2022;33:e12. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material 1. (16.8KB, docx) Supplementary Material 2. (30.3KB, pdf) Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are not openly available due to reasons of sensitivity and are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',9547,'2025-10-13 06:21:02','2025-10-13 06:21:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1561,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12465266','Global and regional burden of polycystic ovary syndrome, 1990-2021: a systematic analysis of the global burden of disease study 2021.','Chai O, Feng X, Tang N, Cui D, Li H, Tian W, Fan J, Wang Y, Hou Z.','2025','anovulation endocrinopathies asr prognosticated asyr asyr forecasted s12905 gauges e28 ghdx vizhub deferral asr asr asyr uis asrs regressed asr asyr pkg robjhyndman tseries tseries asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr severally asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr asyr peaking alerted eapcs asyr carbs fermented jsog jsog ultraprocessed schoolchildren ghcb urging resmena underdiagnosis arimax 12905_2025_3976_moesm1_esm asyr asyr asyr countriesin asr ghdx asyr jsog xinyu dongqing jinyue huiyang wenyan yingmei zhimin xinyu 23jcybjc00720 2023jsz905 tjyxzdxk ghdx vizhub xinyu yingmei wangyingmei tmu zhimin houzhimin3048 teede laven dokras piltonen pennant cacciapuoti lonardo nasti gautiero belfiore s13668 eurjpc zwae066 hardiman baio dey093 delau markovic ottey 7e0306991 vanhise azziz pisarska s10194 gustavsson ryten ggtranscript bioinf btac409 kinship 16e36262 e36262 safiri noori nejadghaderi karamzad chahhoud sullman mjm deac091 deh098 salari nankali ghanbari jafarpour ghasemi dokaneheifard piltonen ruokojarvi karro kujanpaa papunen tapanainen 12e0226074 sansone irfan copp beidas dokras xfre zaitoun kubaisi alqattan alassouli alameeri s12905 aftab naqvi jahangir ishaqui 5e0285284 goodarzi dgab757 tig rajska buszewska forajta rachon markuszewski ijms21144853 gersh gerhart cavalli rde bettiol r111 cipolla poletini jne huddleston ryswyk varcoe s127475 dead241 giampaolino foreste mercorio serafino ijms22042048 salehi allahverdy pourjafar sarabandi s12602 hawrelak ogs pell allotey hardon imelda soyiri iwase iwasa kanasaki kugu jsog2024 1e16152 jog giulio belvis meregaglia morsella adduci perilli cascini obokata sorimachi kagami cardfail jamrasi jesf asbu masri kaissi s41256 mate deen mccall cantero guijarro garvi riera healthplace naal koussa hamouch hneiny s12992 alur chemerinski lipson xfre livadas anagnostis bosdou bantouna paparodis wjd v13 riestenberg jagasia markovic buyalos azziz dgab613 hawrelak gersh ijerph19031336 bozbulut doger camurdan bideci resmena kolhe chhipa butani chavda s43032 sacca lobaina knopf burgoa okwaraji clinpract14050131 witchel oberfield jakubowska kowal skrzynska gawlik starzyk scannell mantzioris villani metabo12040311 s13048 pirotta joham skouteris 12905_2025_3976_moesm1_esm asyr asyr asyr countriesin ghdx vizhub','Abstract Background Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), characterized by chronic anovulation, hyperandrogenism, and insulin resistance, is one of the most common reproductive endocrinopathies. This study uses the updated Global Burden of Disease (GBD) database (1990–2021), which has a larger number of cases, to explore the global burden of PCOS in depth. Methods This research leveraged the GBD 2021 dataset to examine the incidence, prevalence, years lived with disability (YLDs), and the corresponding age-standardized rate (ASR) of PCOS. The study used the estimated annual percentage change (EAPC) to assess the trends in the burden of PCOS over the period from 1990 to 2021. The association between the burden of PCOS and socio-demographic index (SDI) was then analysed, as well as the changing trends in the incidence and prevalence of PCOS in different age groups. Ultimately, the research prognosticated the disease burden linked to PCOS during the span from 2022 to 2031. Results During the period spanning from 1990 to 2021, the quantity of incident cases, prevalent cases, and YLDs of PCOS gradually grew. The ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR increased most rapidly in Southeast Asia from 1990 to 2021. In addition, there were significant positive correlations between ASIR, ASPR and ASYR of PCOS and the SDI of 21 GBD regions ( P < 0.05, |correlation (cor)| >0.3). Notably, the prevalence of PCOS among women in most age groups showed an upward trend over time. Finally, it was forecasted that between 2022 and 2031, there would be a rising trend in both the incidence and prevalence of PCOS. Conclusion This study showed that the ASIR and ASPR of PCOS were increasing year by year, which suggested that we needed to strengthen the related research as well as the prevention and treatment work on PCOS. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12905-025-03976-w. Keywords: PCOS statistics, Incidence and prevalence, Global burden of disease, Years lived with disability, Infertility threat Background Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is the most common endocrine disorder in reproductive-aged women, affecting them from adolescence to post-menopause. The guideline process indicates a prevalence of 10–13%. PCOS has complex causes, and its clinical manifestations are diverse [ 1 ]. It contributes to infertility and metabolic disorders such as metabolic syndrome, cardiovascular disease and endometrial cancer [ 2 – 4 ]. Globally, 69 million people were affected by PCOS in 2021, with a significant economic burden: more than £237 million in the UK Healthcare costs in 2014 [ 5 ] and more than $15 billion annually in the US in 2019 [ 6 ]. Given the global variation in PCOS burden, it is crucial to identify contributing factors and guide policy decisions on resource allocation for screening. The Global Burden of Disease (GBD) study is a global collaborative effort to assess disease prevalence and trends, providing valuable insights into global health and economic impact [ 7 ]. Age, ethnicity, economic status, and regional differences significantly influence the global burden of PCOS [ 8 , 9 ]. Analysis of GBD data analysis can reveal associations between demographic-geographic-societal factors and PCOS, potentially leading to the discovery of new etiologies and risk factors. Recent assessments have been based on GBD 2019 data. Thus, this was a cross-sectional descriptive study, using the GBD 2021 database and medical analyses. This study uses more comprehensive case data to assess the global and regional disease burden of PCOS via prevalence, incidence, and YLDs. It analyzed age-specific trends and projected future burden (2022–2031) to guide PCOS prevention/management and improve women’s health. Materials and methods Data origins The Global Burden of Disease (GBD) study supplies contemporary epidemiological estimates that detail the effects of 371 different diseases and injuries, such as Ischemic Heart Disease, Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, Alzheimer’s Disease, etc. These data cover the period from 1990 to 2021 and include 21 distinct GBD regions and 204 countries or territories [ 7 ]. In addition, it precisely gauges and exhibits crucial parameters like the incidence, prevalence, years lived with disability (YLDs) of diverse diseases among distinct age, gender and geographical groups [ 10 ]. According to the 10th Revision of International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10), Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) was assigned the classification code E28.200 through established coding procedures. Using the Global Burden of Disease Results tool, accessible via the Global Health Data Exchange (GHDx) online platform ( https://vizhub.healthdata.org/gbd-results/ ) (retrieved on November 21, 2024), data pertaining to PCOS for the period spanning from 1990 to 2021 were successfully retrieved and downloaded. Demographic attributes In the 2021 GBD, PCOS is diagnosed using one of the NIH criteria, Rotterdam criteria or AE-PCOS criteria, and its definition comes from the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG). Female participants were divided into groups according to five-year age ranges for analytical purposes. Specifically, all female participants were categorized into the following groups: 10–14, 15–19, 20–24, 25–29 … 70–74, 75–79, and 80 + years old. The Socio-demographic Index (SDI) is a composite metric that includes several key factors. These measures include: the fertility rate for people under 25 Years old, the average education level of those aged 15 years and older, and the per capita income adjusted for deferral [ 11 ]. It could reflect the social background and economic circumstances influencing the Health status of different regions. All 204 countries (regions) were classified into five distinct development strata according to their SDI scores [ 12 ]. Regions with an SDI score exceeding 0.81 are categorized as high SDI zones; those with an SDI score between 0.70 and 0.81 are categorized as upper-middle SDI regions; the regions with an SDI score in the range of 0.61 to 0.69 are regarded as middle SDI regions; for those with an SDI score between 0.46 and 0.60, they belong to lower-middle SDI regions; and the regions with an SDI score below 0.46 are defined as low SDI regions. In addition, considering similarity in epidemiology and geographical proximity [ 11 ], 204 countries (regions) were categorized into 21 GBD regions. Burden indicator of PCOS The burden of PCOS was quantified using metrics such as prevalence, incidence, YLDs, and age-standardized rates (ASR). The ASR encompassed specific measures like the age-standardized incidence rate (ASIR), age-standardized prevalence rate (ASPR), and age-standardized YLD rate (ASYR). The analysis also included 95% uncertainty intervals (UIs) for the ASRs. YLDs measure the number of Years of Healthy life lost due to illnesses or disabilities. The UI was calculated based on the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles from 1000 simulation estimates. This approach aimed to evaluate the uncertainty resulting from insufficient location data, temporal variations, and data heterogeneity [ 13 ]. The estimated annual percentage change (EAPC) served as a reliable and commonly utilized metric for assessing the temporal trends of the disease burden [ 14 ]. The calculation of the EAPC was founded on the natural logarithm rates of the fitted regression model, with time serving as a variable. Each observed value’s natural logarithm was regressed to form a linear model, and the calculations were based on the slope of this regression line: y = α + βx + ϵ, EAPC = 100 × (exp (β) − 1). Here, x represented calendar year, y denoted natural logarithmic transformation of the rate (such as prevalence and incidence), α was intercept, β was slope, and ε represented random error. The EAPC’s 95% CI was calculated using the same regression model that had been fitted during the analysis [ 15 ]. Statistic analysis For the purpose of understanding the burden both globally and regionally and the temporal trends of PCOS, we analyzed the number of cases based on various metrics, including prevalence, incidence, and YLDs. We also calculated the ASR as percentages per 100,000 population and the EAPC at both the global level and across 21 GBD regions for the Years 1990 and 2021. Meanwhile, “ggplot2” package (v 3.5.1) [ 16 ] was utilized to visualize the burden and temporal change trends of PCOS at the global level from 1990 to 2021. And “ggplot2” package (v 3.5.1) was also utilized to create world maps of 204 countries (regions) and 21 GBD regions in 2021 separately, so as to illustrate the burden of PCOS in 2021. Subsequently, to comprehend the influence of the SDI classification on the global and 21 region burden of PCOS from 1990 to 2021, the Spearman correlation analysis approach within “stats” package (v 4.2.2) [ 17 ] was employed to assess the relationships within SDI and ASPR, ASIR, ASYR at the global level and in 21 GBD regions (|correlation (cor)| >0.3, P < 0.05). And “ggplot2” package (v 3.5.1) was utilized to present the results. In addition, with the aim of investigating the prevalence and incidence of PCOS across different ages from 1990 to 2021, “ggplot2” package (v 3.5.1) was applied to examine the changes in the time trend of the incidence and prevalence of PCOS at different ages, categorized into groups at every 5 years. Finally, an autoregressive integrated moving average (ARIMA) model was built using “forecast” (v 8.23.0) ( https://pkg.robjhyndman.com/forecast/ ) and “tseries” (v 0.10–58) ( https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=tseries ), and the model was assessed by white noise test. The model with the smallest Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) was selected for forecasting the next decade (2022–2032). The 32-year data cycle (1990–2021) showed a continuous upward trend in the PCOS disease burden index, which was consistent with the best applicable range of the ARIMA model during the ten-year forecast period. Given the model’s adaptability to trend data, parameter transparency, computational efficiency, and compatibility with the Global Burden of Disease (GBD) study, ARIMA was selected as the optimal modeling scheme. The model was utilized to forecast ASIR and ASPR of PCOS over the next ten years (2022–2031) and to perform a 95% CI statistical analysis. All statistical evaluations were carried out via R software (v 4.2.2), applying the standard significance threshold of P < 0.05 for result interpretation. Results Global PCOS burden and time trends Globally, the prevalence, incidence, and YLDs due to PCOS were higher in 2021 than they were in 1990. The ASPR, ASIR and ASYR for PCOS in 1990 were 1372.77 (95% UI 984.64-1891.60), 49.45 (95% UI 35.57–68.45) and 12.08 (95% UI 5.38–25.21) cases per 100,000 population, respectively. In 2021, the ASPR, ASIR, and ASYR for PCOS were 1757.83 (95% UI 1253.36-2421.26), 63.26 (95% UI 45.41–87.28), and 15.4 (95% UI 6.91–32.13) cases per 100,000 people, respectively (Table S1 ). In addition, from 1990 to 2021, the count of incidence cases, prevalence cases and YLDs of PCOS was all increasing gradually (Fig. 1 a-c). These data trends demonstrate that PCOS is exerting an increasingly significant impact on global women’s health, with its effects becoming more pronounced over time. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab ( a - c ) The count of incidence cases, prevalence cases and YLDs of PCOS from 1990 to 2021. ( a ) Incidence cases. ( b ) Prevalence cases. ( c ) YLDs PCOS burden at the global and regional levels and its association with the SDI from 1990 to 2021 The ASPR, ASIR and ASYR of PCOS in 21 GBD regions were different in 1990 and 2021. In 1990 and 2021, High-income Asia Pacific had the highest ASPR, ASIR, and ASYR, while Central Europe had the lowest. From 1990 to 2021, Southeast Asia experienced the fastest growth in these rates, increasing annually by 2.31% (95% CI 2.21–2.41), 2.32% (95% CI 2.22–2.42) and 2.28% (95% CI 2.18–2.37) individually. Meanwhile, the growth rates of ASPR, ASIR, and ASYR in South and East Asia were also relatively fast. While the ASPR, ASIR and ASYR of High-income North America decreased the fastest throughout the time frame of 1990–2021, with the annual decreasing rates being 0.52% (95% CI −1.00 to −0.04), 0.56% (95% CI −1.04 to −0.08) and 0.53% (95% CI −1.01 to −0.05) severally (Table S2). Furthermore, across the 21 GBD regions, ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR were relatively high in 2021 in Australasia and high-income North America. The ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR were relatively low in Eastern Europe ( Fig. 2 a-c, Table S2 ) . From the perspective of 204 countries (regions) around the world, in 2021, the ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR were relatively high in the United States and Australia. The ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR were relatively low in Russia ( Fig. 2 d-f, Table S3 ) . These data show regional differences and trends in PCOS-related indicators, suggesting formulating prevention, control strategies, and medical resource allocation plans based on regional characteristics. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab ( a - c ) The ASPR, ASIR and ASYR of PCOS in 21 GBD regions in 2021. ( a ) ASIR in 21 GBD regions. ( b ) ASPR in 21 GBD regions. ( c ) ASYR in 21 GBD regions. ( d - f ) The ASPR, ASIR and ASYR of PCOS in 204 countries (regions) in 2021. ( d ) ASIR in 204 countries (regions). ( e ) ASPR in 204 countries (regions). ( f ) ASYR in 204 countries (regions). ( g - i ) Correlation analysis of ASIR, ASPR, ASYR with SDI globally and across 21 GBD regions, 1990–2021. ( g ) ASIR with SDI. ( h ) ASPR with SDI. ( i ) ASYR with SDI From 1990 to 2021, there was a notable positive correlation between SDI and ASIR (cor = 0.573, P < 0.001). It was worth noting that there were also significant positive correlations within SDI and ASPR (cor = 0.571, P < 0.001) and within SDI and ASYR (cor = 0.574, P < 0.001) ( Fig. 2 g-i ) . The global burden of disease study indicates that economic and social development levels may significantly influence PCOS, providing insights for prevention and control strategies. Trends in the incidence, prevalence, and YLDs of PCOS in different age groups from 1990 to 2021 The incidence rate in each age group generally increased over time, with the 10–14 Years age group reaching the peak incidence of PCOS in 2021 ( Fig. 3 a and d ) . In 2021, the 40–44 age group had the highest global PCOS prevalence, while most other age groups showed increasing prevalence trends over time, based on case number analysis ( Fig. 3 b and e ) . Globally, YLDs were relatively low among individuals aged 10–14, but increased significantly in the 15–49 age group, peaking among those aged 25–29 ( Fig. 3 c and f ) . These changing trends indicate the necessity to develop specific prevention and control strategies for PCOS across different stages and age groups. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab a The incidence trend of PCOS by age group from 1990 to 2021. d The incidence of PCOS by age group in 2021. b The prevalence trend of PCOS by age group from 1990 to 2021. e The prevalence of PCOS by age group in 2021. c The YLDs trend of PCOS by age group from 1990 to 2021. f The YLDs of PCOS by age group in 2021 Global burden of disease for PCOS, 2022–2031 This study utilized the ARIMA model to analyze the trends of the ASIR and ASPR of PCOS over the next ten Years. During the period from 1990 to 2021, the ASIR and ASPR of PCOS generally manifested an upward tendency, and it was predicted that they would continue to show an upward trend from 2022 to 2031. Specifically, it was predicted that by 2031, the ASIR would increase to 66.86 cases per 100,000 people (Fig. 4 a), and the ASPR would increase to 1,874.61 cases per 100,000 people (Fig. 4 b). This prediction alerted us that the threat of PCOS to public health would continue to intensify. It was urgent to pre-plan a prevention and control system to deal with the increasingly severe disease challenges in the future. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab ( a - b ) Forecast Trends of PCOS Incidence and Prevalence from 2022 to 2032 (Red represents the 95% lower interval, and blue represents the 95% upper interval). ( a ) ASIR. ( b ) ASPR Discussion Global PCOS burden: doubled prevalence and increasing incidence Spanning three decades of global Health data, this study establishes a robust epidemiological framework for understanding PCOS distribution, delivering standardized incidence rates and YLD measurements across multiple geographic hierarchies in the female reproductive population. PCOS statistics for 2021 demonstrate a significant Health impact, including 69.4 million existing cases, 2.3 million new diagnoses, and 610,000 disability-adjusted Years, emphasizing its substantial global Health burden. From 1990 to 2021, global age-standardized rates showed slight increases, with EAPCs of 0.74% for prevalence, 0.77% for incidence, and 0.72% for YLDs, indicating gradual upward trends across all indicators. Notably, this increase was slower than that reported in the GBD 2019 study [ 8 ]. In analysing the 2021 data, we found a continuing upward trend in the prevalence, incidence and YLDs associated with PCOS. Notably, the global population affected by PCOS has almost doubled, increasing from approximately 37 million in 1990 to 69 million in 2021, more than in the 2019 GBD statistics [ 18 ]. From 1990 to 2021, the ASIR of PCOS will increase by 13.8% (95% Uncertainty Interval: 9.5–18.8%). The increase in the prevalence and incidence of PCOS can be partly attributed to demographic growth and the trend towards an ageing population. Advances in PCOS diagnostic standards have dramatically enhanced detection capabilities, with the Rotterdam criteria now serving as the international benchmark for clinical diagnosis [ 19 , 20 ]. The adoption of the Rotterdam Criteria, coupled with the provision of evidence-based guidelines for PCOS screening and management, improves the detection rate of PCOS. Moreover, inadequate training of medical students and healthcare professionals worldwide in the recognition of PCOS [ 21 – 24 ] has resulted in ineffective prevention and management of PCOS-related risk factors or complications, leading to delayed diagnoses and an increase in PCOS incidence. National and SDI variations in PCOS burden: highest prevalence in High-Income asia, economic drivers revealed In 2019, the high-income Asia Pacific region had the highest ASPR, ASIR, and ASYR for PCOS among 21 GBD regions, followed by Australasia, while Central Europe had the lowest. From 1990 to 2021, Southeast Asia saw the fastest increase, while East Asia and South Asia also experienced rapid increases. In contrast, the high-income North American region experienced the largest decline. Breakthroughs in genomic sciences, epigenetic regulation, metabolic profiling, and AI-driven computational models have revealed concrete links between lifestyle choices and PCOS development pathways [ 25 – 27 ]. Industrialized regions (e.g., North America/Australasia) show higher EDCs (bisphenol A, phthalates) from plastics/pesticides, disrupting ovarian steroidogenesis and insulin signaling [ 25 , 28 ]. Eastern Europe’s lower industrialization may reduce EDC-mediated PCOS risk. Western diets (refined carbs/ultra-processed foods) in high-SDI regions exacerbate insulin resistance and obesity-core PCOS drivers [ 3 , 29 ]. Italy’s high PCOS burden parallels its 30% childhood overweight/obesity rate [ 30 ]. Eastern European diets (whole grains/fermented foods) may confer metabolic protection [ 3 , 31 ]. PCOS manifestation is influenced by genetics and environmental stimuli, including diet, physical activity, stress, sleep disorders, circadian disruption, and xenobiotic exposure [ 28 , 31 ]. Alterations in all of these factors are associated with significant increases in obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and cancer risk [ 29 ]. The evolutionary adaptive survival advantage conferred by synchronized circadian systems in ancestral populations stands in marked contrast to the multiple circadian disruptions linked to modern lifestyles. Current evidence indicates that circadian disruption adversely affects utero development [ 32 ], induces alterations in metabolism and insulin resistance [ 33 ], contributes to body weight dysregulation and obesity [ 34 ], and impairs fertility [ 35 ]. The gut microbiota varies among females from different regions. PCOS patients show reduced microbial diversity and altered bacterial taxa, which may lead to metabolic issues [ 36 – 38 ]. These factors may indirectly increase the risk of PCOS onset and contribute to disease variability, warranting further investigation. PCOS has a high prevalence in countries such as Italy, Japan, New Zealand, Australia, and Malaysia, ranking them within the top five globally, the ASPR values were 8113.16 (5757.74 to 11265.85), 6334.11 (4579.73 to 8798.66), 5689.13 (4094.5 to 7762.63), 4606.14 (3265.61 to 6417.26), and 4125.99 (2878.52 to 5885.36), respectively. All but Malaysia are high SDI countries, with a trend towards increasing disease burden. One study found high rates of overweight and obesity among Malaysian adolescents and young adults 16–35 Years, with rates of 12.8% and 7.9% for overweight and obesity, respectively, in those aged 16–20 Years, and 7.9% and 20.9% in those aged 31–35 years [ 39 ] Japan Society of Obstetrics and Gynecology (JSOG) has revised the diagnostic criteria for PCOS, establishing the JSOG 2024 criteria. The new criteria may increase the detection of PCOS due to an LH cut-off value for abnormal secretion [ 40 ]. Western Europe reported the highest number of PCOS cases in Europe from 1990 to 2021, with Italy leading globally in PCOS ASPR and ASIR and contributing the most YLDs. Italy’s obesity rate rose by 36% in the last 20 Years. An epidemiological survey of about 30,000 people in Europe showed that ultraprocessed foods make up about 14% of adults’ daily caloric intake and one-quarter of children’s and adolescents’, especially among those aged 5 to 30 [ 30 ]. Additionally, the overweight and incidence population of childhood obesity in Italy is significantly high: nearly 19% of 15-year-olds were overweight or obese in 2018, and 30% of schoolchildren aged 8–9 were overweight or obese in 2019 [ 30 ]. Furthermore, the prevalence of cigarette smoking and alcohol consumption was significantly high among young Italian women [ 41 ]. Asia faces the challenges of an ageing population and metabolic obesity places a high healthcare burden on the healthcare systems in Japan [ 42 , 43 ]. The high ranking of Australia and New Zealand in Australasia is due to economic development and accessibility of medical resource [ 44 ]. The Middle East and North Africa (MENA) region is the only GBD region where PCOS ASPR, ASIR, and YLDs have decreased since 2019. Challenges like insufficient policies, international conflicts, and health worker emigration have led to low PCOS detection and declining health indicators [ 45 , 46 ]. Current evidence shows significant health improvements in the MENA region, including longer life expectancy, lower infant and maternal mortality, and broader health coverage, with variations by country [ 45 – 47 ]. Currently, the Global Health Capacity Building (GHCB) program is crucial for enhancing health workforce capabilities in low-resource settings and addressing regional health challenges [ 48 ]. The largest increases in PCOS incidence and in YLDs were observed in the middle and high-middle SDI quintiles, respectively. PCOS significantly increases the predisposition to a variety of metabolic disturbances disorders, in particular type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease, and the Metabolic Syndrome. It is also also associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, a wide range of oncological disorders and a number of obstetric and perinatal complications. PCOS also correlates with higher rates of mental health problems, particularly anxiety and depressive disorders [ 2 , 4 , 49 – 52 ]. The increase in the SDI suggests more YLDs from PCOS, increasing the health burden and affecting women’s reproductive health. Rising PCOS prevalence in adolescents calls for integrated lifestyle, diagnostic and mental health interventions Age-related PCOS prevalence rose from 1990 to 2021, with the highest incidence in females 10–14, urging early health management. Implementing measures such as the Resmena diet (a modified Mediterranean dietary pattern targeting metabolic syndrome), balanced nutrition, adequate sleep, physical exercise, vitamin and micronutrient supplementation, and mental health care can improve lifestyle and provide psychological support, benefiting school-aged individuals [ 1 , 53 – 55 ]. Given that the clinical manifestations of PCOS in adolescent patients frequently overlap with the physiological changes of puberty, this overlap presents challenges and delays in accurate diagnosis [ 56 ]. Consequently, there is a pressing need for ongoing and intensified training for primary care physicians and pediatricians who cater to minor patients, to ensure swift and precise identification of risk factors and effective diagnosis of PCOS. In multidisciplinary collaboration, what is particularly concerning is the mental health of adolescent patients with PCOS, which requires immediate and professional intervention from psychiatrists and psychologists [ 57 ]. Global projections of PCOS burden (2022–2032) Projecting of the global burden of PCOS from 2022 to 2032 indicate it is anticipated that the incidence and prevalence of this condition will continue to increase over the next decade. Given socio-economic development, increasing population aging, changes in ethnic composition due to immigration, and the strengthening of national health policies, personalized treatment and lifestyle changes are crucial for the management of PCOS. Regardless of the dietary pattern, weight loss has been shown to significantly improve PCOS symptoms. Adolescents and women of childbearing age with PCOS in particular need to be registered with gynecological services, receive appropriate treatment, and undergo regular follow-up [ 58 – 60 ]. Strengths and limitations This study identifies peak PCOS incidence at 10–14 years, underscoring early screening needs. Regional disparities (Southeast Asia fastest-growing) and socioeconomic links (SDI correlation) guide precision care. Projected rising burden informs public health strategies, with global data supporting optimized clinical interventions. However this study has limitations. The GBD might lack data from certain regions, and selected cases may not have captured patients primarily diagnosed with “premature ovarian failure,” “other ovarian disorders,” or “unspecified ovarian disorders” who also had PCOS. Variations in data sources and classification criteria could compromise PCOS burden estimates, including false positives or underdiagnosis due to specialty-specific diagnostic practices or cross-country healthcare disparities, limiting the use of objective diagnostic standards based on lab testing. Diagnostic criteria for PCOS vary between medical specialties, with endocrinologists focusing on androgen levels and gynecologists on ovarian morphology [ 44 ]. The aetiology of PCOS includes genetics, environment, and lifestyle, and this study may not fully capture all risk factors, limiting effective prevention and control strategies [ 8 ]. Based on the above situation, the diagnostic criteria of joint genetic testing may be more objective in the future, reducing missed diagnosis and false positive. The ARIMA model in this study has the following limitations: Predictions may fail if the driving factors of PCOS (e.g., updated diagnostic criteria, emergent public health events) undergo drastic changes. The prediction period spans a long duration, leading to higher uncertainty in long-term forecasts. If multifactorial analysis is integrated in the future, the model could be upgraded to ARIMAX or a state-space model. Conclusion This study reveals a significant global rise in PCOS incidence, prevalence, and YLDs, with a projected increase over the next decade. Socioeconomic development is positively correlated with the burden of PCOS. Peak incidence occurs at 10–19 years, emphasizing the need for early adolescent screening. Strengthening health monitoring and infrastructure for adolescent and reproductive-aged women, while addressing complications like infertility and metabolic syndrome, is crucial to reducing PCOS’s overall burden. Supplementary Information 12905_2025_3976_MOESM1_ESM.zip (59.2KB, zip) Supplementary Material 1: Table S1. The ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR of PCOS global in 1990 and 2021. Table S2. The ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR of PCOS in 21 GBD regions in 1990 and 2021. Table S3. The ASIR, ASPR, and ASYR of PCOS in 204 countriesin 1990 and 2021 Acknowledgements The research presented in this paper is that of the authors and does not reflect the official policy of the National Institutes of Health or other funders. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Abbreviations PCOS polycystic ovary syndrome GBD Global Burden of Disease YLDs years lived with disability ASR age-standardized rate EAPC estimated annual percentage change SDI socio-demographic index GHDx Global Health Data Exchange ASIR age-standardized incidence rate AS