65 downer capper lieff e208 msosa msiska mapulanga mtambo mwalabu heswbl internationalmidwives 2019_final_18 5db05248843e8 kubota khambounheuang theppanya nanthavong tengbriacheu lordfred nzee kabeya mukumpuri eke shikuku allott zeev ameh shikuku jebet nandikove tallam ogoti nyaga sodha grønbæk sudke attendants e001928 barrie amf boas oljira wollega bolan sthreshley ngoy ledy ntayingi makasy mlearning sda magwenya ngatiane mlambo silen metasynthesis merry castiglione rouleau letourneau larue deschenes shikuku zeev ladur allott mwaura nandikove mildenberger bisht kaushal attendants neonatol ameh mdegela madaj broek e0203606 bardosono hildayani basrowi wibowo 128a kjaer vedsted høpner shikuku mwanzia ladur nandikove uyara','Abstract Background Continuous professional development (CPD) for midwifery educators is essential. It strengthens their competencies, improves the quality of midwifery care, and ultimately helps reduce maternal and newborn mortality. Lack of midwifery education CPD standards and limited opportunities for educators to maintain/update their competencies are barriers to midwifery educator development. Limited information exists regarding CPD programs specifically designed for midwifery educators in LMICs, where maternal and newborn mortality and morbidity rates are highest. Consequently, their availability, content, and effectiveness largely remain unknown. This review appraises the current approaches, content and effectiveness of midwifery educator CPD programmes in LMICs. Methods Researchers undertook an in-depth literature search across five electronic databases covering 2000–2021. A quality appraisal, informed by internal and external validity, included a combined numerical summary and categorical description. A narrative synthesis and the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) was used for reporting. The protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD42021283273). Results Twenty-two papers met inclusion criteria although many did not report on CPD directly but on midwifery educator development initiatives. All studies included were of good quality. The main CPD approach applied was twinning (international partnerships) with minimal involvement from national midwifery regulatory bodies. Content primarily covered curriculum design; teaching methodologies; clinical life-saving skills; research; leadership and management in health. However, there was less focus on student assessment, effective feedback, supervision/mentoring in clinical practice, and digital technology integration. Few studies measured effectiveness of the CPD programmes and relied on qualitative and self-reported measures. Programmes contributed to educators’ personal/professional development (advanced academic qualifications, research scholarship, and career progression) and institutional improvements (curriculum reforms and new teaching approaches applied in midwifery education). Evidence on the direct impact of these programs on student learning and MNH outcomes was limited. Conclusion There is low evidence of midwifery educator specific CPD programmes that have been designed/evaluated in LMICs. National midwifery regulatory bodies need to provide clearer guidance and have greater involvement in the design of accredited CPD programmes for midwifery educators. Evaluation of existing CPD programmes using robust experimental designs to measure effect on MNH outcomes is required. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-025-07877-2. Keywords: Midwifery educator, Continuous professional development, Faculty development, Low- and middle-income countries, Midwifery education Introduction Maternal and newborn mortality is highest in low- and middle income countries (LMICs), with sub-Saharan Africa alone accounting for approximately 70% of the global maternal deaths [ 1 , 2 ]. The Global Strategy for Human Resources for Health: Workforce 2030 emphasizes that maternal and newborn health (MNH) priorities in sustainable development goal (SDG) 3 remain aspirational unless accompanied by strategies involving transformational efforts on health workforce – including professional midwives – capability. Competently trained midwives working in a well-supported environment can effectively deliver about 90% of essential MNH care [ 3 ] and avert two-thirds of the global maternal and neonatal deaths and stillbirths by 2030 [ 4 ]. However, the quality of midwifery education in LMICs often falls short of international standards, highlighting both within- and between-country inequality in midwifery workforce education and skills development [ 3 ]. These inequalities (SDG 10) in education and skills development directly impact the effectiveness of the midwifery workforce in these regions. Strengthening the quality and capacity of midwifery education is essential for improving the workforce and quality of midwifery care provision that is required for maternal and newborn mortality reduction [ 5 – 8 ]. Global health strategies [ 9 , 10 ] stress the importance of investing in skilled health personnel educators, noting significant societal benefits. Despite this recognition, developing high-quality education programs and preparing competent faculty in nursing and midwifery continue to be major challenges. To improve the quality and quantity of midwifery care, it is necessary to increase both the number and competence of midwifery educators. This has the potential to ensure high-quality training that produces skilled and capable midwives, ultimately improving care for mothers and newborns. Midwifery educators should be competent in the World Health Organisation (WHO) Midwifery Educator Core Competencies [ 11 ] and International Confederation of Midwives (ICM) Essential Competencies for Midwifery Practice [ 12 ]. One of the ICM standards is that over 50% of midwifery training should be practical-based [ 13 ]. Midwife educators are expected to be competent in maternal and newborn health lifesaving skills to provide effective teaching and training of fully qualified midwives required to provide quality MNH care [ 14 ]. However, a recent global survey by the World Health Organization uncovered several challenges in midwifery education. These include inadequate regulation of training programs, resulting in inconsistent standards and quality. Many educators lack proper training or accreditation, and few feel fully confident in all core competencies [ 3 ]. Additionally, midwifery educators often face resource constraints, limited professional development opportunities, and a disconnect between theoretical and practical teaching. These issues collectively impact the quality of midwifery graduates [ 15 – 17 ]. To address these challenges and strengthen midwifery education, continuous professional development for educators is essential. Continuous professional development (CPD) A critical and yet often overlooked component of strengthening education is ongoing professional development opportunities for midwifery educators throughout their career [ 18 ]. Continuous professional development is defined as the ‘means by which members of the profession maintain, improve and broaden their knowledge, expertise, and competence, and develop the personal and professional qualities required throughout their professional lives [ 19 ]. The Global Strategic Directions for Nursing and Midwifery 2021–2025 [ 10 ], ICM Global Standards for Midwifery Education [ 13 ] and WHO’s framework of action to strengthen quality midwifery education [ 16 ] emphasize that educators must maintain clinical competence, as well as developing and strengthening clinical and didactic teaching and research skills. This is essential for safe, competent and effective practice to achieve universal health coverage (UHC) and maternal and newborn health SDGs [ 13 , 16 ]. In formal CPD activities, there is active and structured learning that often follows set curricula, usually approved by independent accreditation institutions such as the nursing/midwifery council while informal CPD is usually self-directed learning where individuals carry out development activities according to their own choice and without a structured syllabus. Different approaches including skill and knowledge updates for professional development include formal programs of study; peer coaching or mentoring; work-based observation; communities of practice; online learning; research and peer review activities and, the more traditional workshops and courses [ 20 , 21 ]. Midwives and midwifery educators participating in accredited CPD programmes is beneficial to the profession, families and health systems in two folds. First, countries can align CPD to their specific healthcare needs and translate into practice to improve practice and health outcomes [ 22 ]. Secondly, as a mandatory requirement for renewal of practice licensure ensures an up-to-date, relevant nursing and midwifery workforce [ 23 ] and increases the legitimacy of CPD [ 24 , 25 ]. Worldwide, midwives and midwifery educators are required to participate in CPD either by their employers or regulatory bodies to keep updated in recent developments and practices in the profession [ 26 ]. However, many countries do not have organised systems for CPD, whilst others have vast experience in this area [ 27 ]. In most LMICs, midwifery licensure is permanent and demonstrating ongoing education or competence is not a requirement for renewal of practicing license. As a result, CPD processes and systems when available are not fully utilised. A systematic review on CPD status in WHO Afro-region member states reported that nurses and midwives are required to attend formal programmes delivered face-to-face or online. However, only 16 (34.7%) WHO Afro-region countries have established mandatory CPD to ensure the public has access to up-to-date skilled, relevant practitioners [ 23 ]. For instance, CPD is mandatory for nurse midwives including the educators in Kenya and policy guidelines to implement this have been formulated [ 28 ]. Understanding the capacity strengthening initiatives for midwifery educators towards a competent midwifery workforce in LMICs is critical to improving the global MNH targets. Evidence on the available CPD programmes specifically for midwifery educators, their content and impact on the educators, students and health systems in LMICs is limited [ 16 , 29 , 30 ] and most of the programmes available have largely been implemented in high-income countries [ 31 – 39 ]. Smith and colleagues offer valuable evidence on CPD programs for nursing and midwifery faculty, detailing content, delivery methods, and evaluation processes. Their review provides a global perspective but presents opportunities for more targeted research. Future studies are required to focus specifically on LMICs with high mortality rates, concentrate solely on midwifery faculty, and assess the impact of these programs on educators, students, and health systems [ 40 ]. Systematic reviews are robust methodologically, rigorous, appraise the quality of evidence to inform policy development, guide decision-making in practice and provide further directions for practice [ 41 ]. There is need for clear guidance on the type of CPD systems/programmes for midwifery educators, modes of delivery, duration of CPD programmes, sustainability of the CPD systems and their effectiveness for implementation in LMICs. The objective of this systematic review is to identify and critically appraise the current approaches, systems or processes, content and evaluate the effectiveness of the midwifery educator specific CPD programmes on the educators, students and health systems in LMICs for policy guidance and practice. Methods The protocol for this systematic review was prospectively registered on PROSPERO (CRD42021283273) and is reported based on Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) 2020 guideline which included searches of databases, registers and other sources performed for the period between January 1, 2000 and December 31, 2021 [ 42 ]. The research team conducted the searches in December 2021. Search strategy The SPIDER (Sample, Phenomenon of Interest, Design, Evaluation, Research type) and PICO (Population, Intervention, Comparison and Outcomes) frameworks were used to focus search terms and inform the inclusion and exclusion criteria for the review [ 43 ]. The search strategy included both free-text and Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) terms including terms in five categories related to [ 1 ] “Continuous professional development” OR “professional development” OR “faculty development” OR “capacity building” [ 2 ] “Midwife faculty” OR “midwifery faculty” OR “midwife teacher” OR ‘midwifery teacher’ or “midwife trainer” OR “midwifery trainer” OR “midwifery educator” OR “midwifery lecturer” [ 3 ]. “processes” OR “models” OR “programmes” [ 4 ] “Systems” and [ 5 ] LMIC (“Africa” OR “SSA” OR “sub Saharan Africa* “Developing countr*” OR “developing countr*” OR “LMIC” OR “low income countr*” OR “low middle income countr*” OR “threshold countr*” OR “global south” OR “low resource setting*” OR “third world countr*”. Also, a list of low-and-middle-income countries was used according to the World Bank 2019 classification of countries to ensure literature from individual countries was located [ 44 ]. The search was restricted to publications in English. Multiple models of CPD and a framework analysis for understanding and categorizing the nature and purpose of different CPD activities have been described [ 45 ]. Different approaches including skill and knowledge updates for professional development include formal programs of study; peer coaching or mentoring; work-based observation; communities of practice; online learning; research and peer review activities and, the more traditional workshops and courses [ 20 , 21 ]. This review focused on how CPD can be delivered in midwifery education in LMICs. Heather Mack’s approach that primarily identifies and discusses specific practical models for implementing CPD programs, especially in resource-constrained environments was applied [ 24 ]. Due to the limited number of midwifery-specific research papers, a decision was made to include research papers/reports with nurse-midwife or obstetric nurses or nurse educators who teach maternal, child or reproductive health. Besides, studies on capacity building, an essential component of CPD were included. Capacity building is described by United Nations as the process of developing and strengthening the skills, abilities, instincts or processes that individuals, organizations and communities need to survive, adapt and thrive in a fast-changing world through training, development and cultural change [ 46 , 47 ]. Importantly, capacity building activities, like CPD, are essential for long-term success and adaptability as they enhance the ability to perform functions, solve problems, set and achieve objectives over time [ 47 ]. A search of electronic bibliographic databases: MEDLINE, CINAHL, Global Health, PubMed and Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine library database – DISCOVER – that offers database combinations with access to 11 other major databases (including EBSCO host), was performed [ 48 ]. A search using Google Advanced for websites of key international organisations known to be involved in midwifery education and training (International Confederation of Midwives [ICM], United Nations Population Fund [UNFPA], World Health Organisation [WHO], John Hopkins Program for International Education in Gynecology and Obstetrics [JHPIEGO], World Continuing Education Alliance [WCEA], government Ministry of Health websites (countries where CPD initiatives have been reported) was performed for relevant reports and web-based publications. A stopping rule was prospectively applied to the Google Advanced search results after screening of titles and abstracts in five consecutive pages where no new records were taken forward to full text screening. Due to the small number of eligible studies identified, the reference lists of all relevant publications were reviewed using the forward and backward citation searches for relevant articles not identified in the initial search [ 49 ]. Inclusion and exclusion of research papers This review focused on research papers/reports on CPD systems/processes/programmes or approaches for midwifery educators or faculty development interventions in LMICs. Records using recognised quantitative or qualitative methods for data collection and analysis, or mixed methods and available in English were included. Personal accounts, case studies, opinion pieces, editorials, letters to the editor, abstracts with no full texts, study protocols, guidelines, policy documents or related to doctors, nurses only in settings where midwifery was a well-established cadre or traditional birth attendants were excluded. Study selection All references were stored in Endnote X9 software. A title screen was carried out by the first author (DNS) to remove any duplicates from the searches. The retrieved papers were equally divided into two for title and abstract screening by the first author with support from a midwife research associate/academic technical expert in systematic reviews. Firstly, title and then abstract screening was conducted. Any studies identified as either providing likely or unclear evidence for inclusion were retrieved for full text review. The full-text articles were then independently reviewed against the inclusion/exclusion criteria by the first author and academic technical expert followed by data extraction. Discrepancies were reviewed and resolved through discussion and revisiting the record with a third author (CA). Twenty-two [ 22 ] records – 14 research studies and eight reports – met the inclusion criteria and were appraised. Records identification, screening and inclusion are summarised in the PRISMA diagram (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Included papers: PRISMA 2020 flow diagram for systematic reviews which included searches of databases, registers, and other sources Data extraction and synthesis The full texts of the included records were reviewed, and data was extracted into a pre-developed summary table (Table 1 ). A narrative synthesis of research literature approach was used due to the considerable heterogeneity of the included studies in terms of methodology, type of CPD/faculty development and evaluation performed to measure the effect of the CPD [ 50 ]. Table 1. Summary of included papers Authors and Year Published Research methods Objective Countries Content of CPD Approach/system of CPD Key Findings West et al. (2016) [ 5 ] Systematic review – 18 papers were included: Three papers focused on nursing only, five papers midwifery only and ten papers a combination of nurse-midwifery To explore approaches used to build midwifery educator capacity in LMIC and identify evidence to inform improved outcomes for midwifery education. Most papers are from Africa with others included from South America South East Asia, Central Asia, Balkans South Pacific and South Asia Contemporary adult education teaching methodologies; leadership and management; research; computer and internet literacy Engagement between international consultants (capacity builders) and national participants (capacity buildees) using the master-trainer or train-the-trainer approaches Skill and knowledge updates associated with curriculum review, involvement in leadership, management and research training applied. Engaging multilevel approaches and an enabling environment are needed in building capacity of midwifery educators. More research specific to midwifery is required. Erlandsson et al. (2018) [ 48 ] A process evaluation using mixed research methods in a sample of 19 midwifery faculty staff members To examine feasibility and adherence to a mentorship programme among at ten institutes/colleges in Bangladesh. Bangladesh Pedagogical methods and assessment tools – implementing classroom teaching, skills lab sessions and in clinical setting (clinical supervision and communication and assessment of midwifery students) A blended mentorship programme delivered by Swedish midwifery faculty staff members to Bangladesh midwifery faculty members Faculty’s post-test mean scores were above 4.2 out of 5 in applying flipped classroom approach and teaching and evaluating theory and clinical practice. The blended mentorship program was feasible and it strengthened the faculty to effectively deliver theory and supportive supervision to enhance clinical learning for diploma midwifery students Erlandsson et al. (2019) [ 49 ] A pre-post design using mixed research methods at baseline and 12 months later (endline) among 30 midwifery educators To describe the expectations of midwifery educators in Bangladesh who took part in a blended web-based master’s programme in sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR) and the extent to which these were realized after 12 months of part-time study. Bangladesh Content focused on teaching and learning styles (including critical and reflective thinking and decision-making skills), leadership and management strategy, use of recent scientific evidence and the research methodologies. A blended web-based master’s programme in SRHR designed, taught and monitored by foreign expertise from Dalarna University (Sweden) Program improved educators’ confidence from 1 to 4 out of 5 in applying computer technologies and Microsoft office applications applications (MS Powerpoint, MS Excel, MS Word) to teach and research and enhance students’ learning, foster reflective and critical thinking Bogren et al. (2019) [ 50 ] A qualitative research design in a sample of 17 midwifery educators Investigated the usefulness of a simulation-based learning course for midwifery educators in Bangladesh. Ban