a new tab Quality appraisal Study quality appraisal was performed independently by the first author. Two quality appraisal tools – Critical Appraisal Skills Program (CASP) tools and the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT) were used to appraise the white literature due to the single or mixed nature of the research methods utilised by different studies. The CASP tools specific for either qualitative [ 51 ], quantitative (adapted the CASP tool for cohort studies by omitting the questions 3,4,6 and 12 that are relevant to intervention studies) [ 52 ] or systematic review [ 53 ] were used. These checklists are robust, focus on validity of the results, the methodological soundness of the study, the results and clinical relevance of the findings [ 54 , 55 ]. Program evaluation research papers with mixed research methods (quantitative and qualitative) were appraised using the MMAT – version 2018 [ 56 ]. In general, study appraisal against the CASP questions for qualitative, quantitative and systematic review met most criterion for quality. Of the 117 judgements, 106 (90.6%) met the quality criterion (‘yes’), three (2.6%) did not meet the criterion (‘no’), and in eight (6.8%), a judgement could not be conclusively made for that criterion/study (‘CT’). For the two programme evaluation studies using mixed methods research designs [ 57 , 58 ], the papers were judged to be of good quality as all criteria items were met. In all the research studies, the findings were relevant and useful in improving the quality of midwifery education and training in the local settings. Critical appraisal of the eight reports (grey literature) were assessed using the Authority, Accuracy, Coverage, Objectivity, Date, Significance (AACODS) checklist [ 59 ]. The quality criterion was met in 40 (83.3%) of the 48 judgments. All the reports were judged to have been by reputable organisations and authorities in midwifery (authority) and their work was of high significance to improving the quality of midwifery education (significance). Although the reports cover interventions for both midwives in practice and midwifery educators, the components for the midwifery educators are clearly highlighted and therefore judged to have referred to a particular population group. These reports were deemed representative of relevant projects implemented, but no clear methodology was described. The scoring of the quality appraisal tools for the white and grey literature are summarized in the supplementary file. The 14 studies and eight reports were all determined to be of high quality and were included in the analysis. Results A total of 22 records met the inclusion criteria and were included in the review: 10 qualitative studies, one quantitative study, two mixed methods research studies, one systematic review and eight reports. Many of the included records did not report on CPD directly but focused on capacity building initiatives in each setting, some of which were international collaborations which reflect the low availability of CPD programmes for midwifery educators in LMICs. Eleven studies reported programmes specifically targeting nursing and midwifery educators [ 5 , 57 , 58 , 60 – 67 ] and all the seven reports by UNFPA and ICM mention teaching and clinical skills trainings specific for midwifery educators [ 68 – 74 ]. The systematic review included by West et al. explored the approaches in building capacity in leadership, management and research skills for nursing and midwifery educators in LMICs – one of our objectives on faculty development in this review. The eight reports highlighted the strategically designed training programmes with multi-stakeholder engagement, and numerous global and national partners in competency-based education (CBE) methodologies – teaching and clinical skills – that midwifery educators benefited from and one case report that focused on building a new generation of midwifery faculty members in Bangladesh. Most papers included in this review report on faculty development initiatives from Asia ( n = 7) and Africa ( n = 5) with others included from South America ( n = 1), East Central Europe, Central America and the Caribbean ( n = 1). Three research papers and one report are specifically from Bangladesh, two from Somalia and one each from Cambodia, India, Jordan, Nigeria and Brazil with the other three research papers reporting on multiple countries. Six UNFPA annual reports described interventions in 28–42 high priority countries with the highest maternal mortality ratios globally while the ICM annual report covers three African countries - Madagascar, Cote d’Ivoire and Comoros. This reflects the notable low coverage of midwifery educator CPD programmes across all regions. Results from the included records in this review are summarised under the following key themes: CPD approaches; content; delivery and duration of CPD programmes; and effectiveness of the CPD/faculty development initiatives. Midwifery educator CPD approaches The CPD initiatives in this review were implemented using multiple approaches as described by Mack and colleagues. These are: (i) twinning (ii) greenfield (iii) formal program guided by an international medical body (iv) directed by regulators and (v) informal program guided by an international medical/nursing/midwifery body [ 24 ]. This review summarizes these approaches highlighting key advantages and disadvantages for each as appropriate. Twinning This is a specific partnership which directly pairs organizations in LMICs and high-income countries (HICs), that designs and implements projects based on needs identified by the LMIC partner. This approach is typically funded by the HIC country, directly by the high resource partner medical institution, or indirectly by not-for-profit medical or educational agencies or government bodies [ 24 ]. This approach also involves engagement between international consultants who are the capacity ‘builders’ and national participants who are the capacity ‘buildees’ as well as train-the-trainer approaches involving key national educators [ 5 ]. A key advantage of this approach is that it benefits from the educational strength of the HIC partner, while being specific for the needs of the LMIC in which it is being implemented. Most of the papers and reports in this review (20 out of the 22) report this approach designed by an international organization/capacity builder and localised to the needs of the home country beneficiaries [ 57 , 58 , 60 – 77 ]. International institutions funded by HIC partner organisations supported the capacity building of national colleagues in educational and curricular development, leadership and professional development [ 60 , 61 , 63 , 64 ]. In Nigeria, a train-the-trainer model using a combination of theory and clinical practice was used to develop the knowledge, skills and attitudes of 169 midwifery educators from all 70 training institutions on post-abortion care [ 62 ]. In Bangladesh and Somaliland, blended learning faculty development programmes were developed by the Swedish university (Dalarna University) in partnership with local midwifery training institutions. In both countries, Dalarna University staff were responsible for designing, setting up, teaching, and monitoring the programme. They also supported students with supervision and mentoring using both face-to-face and online delivery approaches and occasionally visited to give verbal introductions to new courses and examinations [ 57 , 58 , 65 – 67 , 77 ]. In India, through the Pradesh State government intervention, a nursing and midwifery faculty development programme (Nursing Faculty Development Programme) was initiated by an international non-governmental organization (with a history of innovation in nursing education in South/Central Asia and East Africa). The programme’s main objective was to strengthen the capability and capacity of nursing and midwifery faculty on educational approaches, research, leadership and management and innovations in clinical education [ 60 ]. However, the paper emphasizes the need for more intensive individual and institutional mentorship and involvement of national institutions to provide local and international expertise. Fellowship programmes have also been developed by international organisations as part of health professions education investments. These have been highlighted by WHO for strategic partnerships in education and health in Africa [ 76 ]. They include the Foundation for Advancement of International Medical Education and Research (FAIMER), a USA-based non-profit organisation’s education and leadership faculty development programme which strengthened medical education by developing health professions educators and building a sustainable discipline of medical education in developing countries annually since 2001. The sub-Saharan Africa-FAIMER Regional Institute also offered faculty and research capacity building including scholarship skills to 26 nursing and midwifery educators from sub-Saharan Africa [ 78 ]. The UNFPA and ICM reports also highlight twinning through the UNFPA-funded international organizations and institutions as a common approach to deliver CPD for midwifery educators. These collaborations focused on teaching and clinical skills and emergency obstetric and newborn care (EmONC), defined as the collective minimum set of medical interventions (or bundle of care) required to prevent or manage the main obstetric complications (haemorrhage, pre-eclampsia or eclampsia, sepsis, complications of obstructed labour or abortion) and newborn complications (birth asphyxia) [ 79 ]. Examples of the international organizations’ programmes include (i) Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine’s programme building the capacity of midwifery educators in EmONC; (ii) Laerdal Health and JHPIEGO midwifery faculty trainings in Helping Babies Breathe and Helping Mothers Survive; and ICM-Sanofi Espoir Foundation partnership in competency-based education methodologies training for midwifery educators from Madagascar, Cote d’Ivoire and Comoros [ 69 – 74 ]. The success of the collaborations was largely due to the respect and adaptation of the content and curriculum to the local/cultural context of the beneficiaries by the international capacity builders [ 57 , 58 , 60 , 61 , 64 – 67 , 77 ]. The success of this approach requires that both builders and beneficiaries participate in the design, delivery and coordination of the capacity strengthening programmes [ 57 , 58 , 63 , 65 – 67 , 77 ]. Successful twinning projects also require buy-in by the LMIC partner, and adequate time for capacity building so that the programme may continue independently after withdrawal of the international partner [ 24 , 57 , 58 , 65 – 67 , 77 ]. Greenfield In this approach, the programme is led by a medical/midwifery professional body. Collaborating with stakeholders, the concept of CPD, the competencies required by professionals in the field, curriculum, plan for required activities and implementation plan are developed. Importantly, a greenfield CPD program is specific to the needs of the country in which it is developed and working closely with stakeholders enables all regulatory challenges to be met. However, the need for high level educational expertise (which the professional body may not have), and relatively high cost to the low-income country are key disadvantages of this approach. One study on post-abortion care by Akiode in Nigeria provides an example of this approach [ 62 ]. In this programme, content including the curriculum, assessment and training for national midwifery education was developed through a multistakeholder approach and collaboration between IPAS – an international non-governmental organization working in the field of reproductive health and rights – and the Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria. This was in response to abortion as one of the most frequent obstetric emergencies in the country with many healthcare workers lacking the competencies or limited by the scope of practice to provide post-abortion care services. These challenges had resulted in treatment delays with increased risk of morbidity and mortality from post-abortion complications. The programme increased the number of midwives with skills to provide post-abortion care through improvements in midwifery education including training of 169 midwifery instructors from 70 midwifery training schools in the country through short 24-hour classroom teaching and 16 h of clinical practice [ 62 ]. Directed by regulators In this model, a CPD program directed by regulators is designed and implemented by regulatory bodies, such as the Nursing Councils, Nursing and Midwifery Councils or Midwifery Councils. Stakeholders including practicing health professionals, professional societies and university nursing/midwifery schools/educators may not be consulted. Its main advantage is that the CPD program meets regulatory requirements by definition as some also argue that a regulatory framework increases legitimacy of CPD [ 24 ]. In this review, only one study by Akiode (which overlaps with the greenfield approach) highlights the participation of the nursing and midwifery regulator in the CPD programme. In this study, IPAS initiated the development of the CPD programme which was developed and evaluated in collaboration with the Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN). This improved the capacity of midwifery schools regulated by NMCN to train midwives in post abortion care. However, it may suggest a lack of capacity or resources by the regulatory council to proactively and independently develop and implement CPD programmes [ 62 ]. Formal program guided by an international medical/midwifery body In this model, CPD programmes are designed by international medical/midwifery bodies and typically based on specialist medical/nursing/midwifery disciplines. This could involve collaborations with other leading organizations in the specialist discipline, for example WHO, or ICM. Programme curricula are intended to be adapted and contextualized for each country in which the CPD program is implemented. A key advantage of this model is that it can be customized to the needs of the country, based on approved competencies within the scope of practice as approved by the regulator. Once the adaptation is complete, the cost to the low-income country is minimized as all CPD material is expected to be available for free to member societies, with the intention of minimizing cost to low-income country societies using the material [ 24 ]. Though not a typical example, the FAIMER, a USA-based non-profit organisation affiliated with the Educational Commission for Foreign Medical Graduates provided 16–18 fellowships annually to sub-Saharan Africa health professions educators to attend the sub-Saharan African FAIMER Regional Institute faculty development programme in Cape Town [ 76 ]. This programme is part of WHO’s investments for strategic partnerships in education and health in Africa. Also, the ICM in partnership with Sanofi Espoir Foundation supported the review of midwifery curricula in Madagascar, Cote d’Ivoire and Comoros aligned to ICM’s competency-based education methodologies (CBE). Midwifery educators were trained in the ICM’s CBE methodologies as part of CPD. They were also supported by mentors to apply the new teaching methods in delivery of the updated curriculum. However, there is limited information about the effectiveness of the ICM-Sanofi programme curriculum and training implemented [ 68 ]. The paucity of reports/studies in this category may suggest low uptake of ICM/WHO guidelines for midwifery faculty CPD programmes. In summary, most papers included in this theme commonly describe twinning as an approach used for the design, adaptation and implementation of the midwifery faculty development. The success of the approach relied on (i) adaptation of teaching curriculum to local needs and (ii) involving national institutions and faculty members as part of the coordination, implementation and sustainability of the CPD approaches. The study papers emphasized the importance of addressing cultural differences between the international capacity builders and the national buildees. This was to ensure that approaches to building capacity are effective and culturally relevant. Besides, supportive follow up mentoring was highlighted as a key strategy to ensure that there is continuity of the capacity building interventions for educators even when the main capacity builder or funder withdraws or comes to an end. Only one study by Akiode in Nigeria highlighted the involvement of the midwifery regulator in CPD activities. This highlights the limited role played by regulators in accrediting the programmes to increase the legitimacy of the programmes to its members. Midwifery educator CPD content Curriculum and education approaches focusing on teaching and learning; and maternal and newborn health/sexual reproductive health and rights (SRHR) were the primary covered content areas in the faculty development programmes. Others were evidence-based practice, research methodologies training; leadership and management in health [ 5 , 57 , 58 , 60 – 63 , 65 – 78 ]. All research studies and reports included in this review reported that content were based on the ICM Global Standards for Midwifery Education (2013) and the WHO Midwifery Educators’ Core Competencies (2014) [ 11 , 80 ]. The key content covered by the research studies/reports included in this review is summarised below (Fig. 2 ). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab CPD content covered in the review Eight research studies reported on curricula design and education approaches. They emphasized the integration of both theoretical classroom teaching and clinical practical teaching approaches for a standard competency-based curriculum in midwifery education and training [ 58 , 60 – 63 , 65 , 76 , 78 ]. Educators felt well prepared for theory teaching but lacked training and experience in clinical practice and clinical skills teaching [ 58 , 65 , 75 ]. Only one study reported on course syllabi and lesson planning as important for structured teaching and learning [ 63 ]. Interactive teaching methodologies covered include structured lectures, simulations, clinical skills practice in hospital setting and role modelling [ 61 – 63 , 75 ]. Emergency obstetric and newborn care skills were the most described clinical skills in midwifery educator CPD programmes reported in the six UNFPA reports [ 69 – 74 ]. Other clinical skills described are Helping Babies Breathe (HBB) and Helping Mothers Survive (HMS) in the UNFPA reports [ 69 , 74 ] and manual vacuum aspiration as part of the post-abortion care in the Akiode study [ 62 ]. Although the ICM report showed that the midwifery educators were trained in competency-based education methodologies, the clinical skills content covered were not specified in the report [ 68 ]. Assessments are essential for effective midwifery education and training for competent and quality midwifery graduates. Only five studies reported on student assessment skills such as writing multiple choice questions [ 57 , 60 , 63 , 76 , 78 ]. One study contained a component on supervision of students clinical practice [ 63 ], effective feedback in teaching and learning [ 76 ] and innovations in clinical education and teaching to enhance student learning [ 60 ]. However, it was not specified whether the education and teaching innovations included digital or online teaching strategies although the authors recommended developing/upgrading computer laboratories to match current standards and practice as a key investment [ 63 ]. Literacy on use of information communication and technology skills (use of computers and applications) among faculty for education was cited as the most common expectation by midwifery educators when they enrolled for their master’s programmes in Bangladesh and Somaliland. Educators reported low confidence in use of computers and applications but improved after participating in the master’s programmes as part of their faculty development [ 57 , 66 , 67 ]. These skills were identified to be relevant in their teaching profession as they are an essential part of modern education. Specific improvements after training were in the use of the internet to search for peer-reviewed research articles and the latest evidence-based practice. Improvements were also reported in communication with students using computers and applications and preparing for classes (use of pre-recorded lectures and online presentations, use of links to video clips and books on the internet in teaching). One study highlighted the importance of information technology in enhancing active learning through computerised assessments [ 67 ]. Conducting research was identified as an important role for the midwifery educators to remain relevant and current in their teaching and clinical skills practice. Additionally, research was critical to contribute to the scholarship and profession’s body of knowledge. Ten studies included an education research component, covering research methodology and scholarship training for midwifery educators. This training aimed to equip them as leaders, teachers, scholars and advocates for change at individual, school, university and the health professions levels [ 5 , 57 , 58 , 60 , 61 , 65 – 67 , 76 , 77 ]. Two papers had content on research literature review, writing a research question, article writing clinic and conference presentation [ 76 , 78 ]. Eight studies reported on leadership in health as a key component of the faculty development programmes as they recognised that implementation of new educational innovations would require support from senior leadership [ 5 , 57 , 60 , 64 , 66 , 76 – 78 ]. Project management content was reported in two papers as educators were expected to lead on project innovation in their practice as part of scholarship. Example of project management topics reported were design, implementation, evaluation and change management [ 76 , 78 ]. In summary, the included papers/reports emphasized curriculum and educational approaches including teaching methods that focus on integrating the teaching of both theoretical content and clinical skills to enhance learning and practice. Other content included research, leadership in health and project management as midwifery educators were considered agents of change in the institutions and health systems for change in practice. Information, communication and technology skills were also highlighted as essential in modern midwifery education and for evidence-based practice. However, there was limited information on midwifery faculty development content on students’ assessment (formative and summative), giving effective feedback for learning, mentoring/supervision in clinical practice and digital/electronic innovations in teaching and learning in the studies/reports reviewed. Delivery and duration of CPD programmes The programmes reported in this review were either delivered as short (face-to-face sessions lasting between 1 and 10 days) or long programmes delivered via modular formats integrating face-to-face and distance learning components with occasional out-of-country visits (blended format). Six studies reported on long-term programmes lasting over two years – diploma, masters or fellowship programmes – integrating face-to-face, online/web-based and modular components. Three studies in Bangladesh [ 57 ] and Somaliland [ 66 , 67 ] described a collaborative 3-year master’s programme in sexual reproductive health and rights. This programme was developed and implemented by faculty members from Sweden university in partnership with local in-country midwifery training institutions using an innovative blended learning approach. This program was not medical but education/teaching focused. The web-based approach was the predominant mode of delivery relying on internet infrastructure for accessing online lectures and seminars. Pre-recorded lectures were provided as a back-up option in case the internet connection was weak or for educators to repeat if needed throughout the university’s online learning platforms. A similar approach was described for a 3-year midwifery diploma programme in Bangladesh [ 58 ]. In both studies, information technology infrastructure (internet, equipment, support staff) was strengthened by the Sweden faculty. Both studies in Bangladesh and Somaliland underscored the value of the face-to-face contact sessions as opportunities for introductions to new courses, clinical and field visits, support supervision, mentorship and examinations [ 57 , 58 , 66 , 67 ]. Bogren’s study documented a short four-day simulation-based learning course which added to a one-year international master’s degree programme in SRHR for midwifery educators in Bangladesh. This short course, aimed to build the professional competencies and confidence of educators, used multiple reinforcing modalities (case scenarios, mannequins, and other equipment). The course focused on critical decision-making points related to ICM’s competencies in antenatal, intrapartum, newborn care and family planning [ 65 ]. Three studies reported on programmes with short contact hours of delivery within the 24-months faculty development programmes [ 63 , 76 , 78 ]. Two papers by van Wyk et al. and Frantz et al. reported on a 2-year fellowship programme for building the capacity of midwifery and other healthcare provider educators in educational approaches (curriculum design, assessment, teaching and learning), leadership, project management and programme evaluation. This programme was delivered via an integrated residential and distance-learning format. The residential format had three onsite sessions of 4–7 days each focusing on interactive small group activities in leadership and change management skills, project management tools and health professions education. The distance learning component (all three sessions offered in year 1) were 10 weeks each. The papers highlighted the in-person faculty support provided during the onsite sessions on writing education innovation study proposal, research and project management strategies and other scholarly activities for the educators [ 76 , 78 ]. The Koto et al. study highlighted a 24-month programme where participants travelled to attend face-to-face sessions for one week in a central training centre and remained full-time employees the other weeks of the month. Although the programme was organised by the Cambodian Ministry of Health who allowed individual educators to participate, information about funding for participation in the programme was not provided [ 63 ]. Two studies reported a module format programmes for educators [ 60 , 61 ]. The Nursing Faculty Development Programme in Pradesh, India had four modules, all delivered in face-to-face sessions. Each module lasted between 7 and 10 days and participants were expected to develop a detailed realistic action plan for taking forward relevant learning, on completion. Certificate of attendance on completing each module was awarded. However, excessive workloads and staff shortage were reported as obstacles to participating/completing the time/labour intensive programme. Also, the modular programmes lacked mechanisms for institutional peer-to-peer learning and mentoring, during or after the programme [ 60 ]. Girot et al. study reported a 600-hour programme comprising of five modules delivered through face-to-face theory sessions. Two of the modules were delivered as theory and practical with participants traveling to the central centre for a residential component of the programme [ 61 ]. Both studies, however, did not report how travel and accommodation costs for the residential component of the programme were covered. Wright et al. study reported a 3-month full-time programme for midwifery educators from Central Europe, Caribbean and Central America hosted in the USA. Participants who were not fluent in English were first required to complete their 3-months training in English language. This highlighted language barrier as a potential challenge in faculty development programmes designed and implemented through the twinning model. The nurse and midwife scholars included in this programme were from varied practice areas including clinical practice, nursing education, administration, ministries of health, and professional organizations [ 64 ]. Two studies reported short duration programmes lasting between 1 and 10 days: (i) a 40-hour post-abortion care programme for midwifery instructors in Nigeria through residential workshops comprising of 24-hours of classroom teaching and 16 h of clinical practice for delivery. Delivery was through structured lecture presentations, videos, practice on anatomical models and clinical skills practice in a hospital setting. Supervisory and mentoring visits were part of the implementation of the programme to emphasize collective problem-solving, record reviews and interviews with educators. This was to ensure high standard clinical practice in the schools and associated clinical training facilities [ 62 ]; (ii) face-to-face short continuing education courses offered regularly within 1–10 days with participants travelling from the periphery to the central training centre. Non-academic credit bearing certificates were awarded at the end of the programme [ 75 ]. Both studies did not report how participant costs were covered in the training centres. For the UNFPA and ICM reports on the midwifery educators’ professional development, there was limited information on the mode of delivery and duration of the programs. Funding for participation in the programmes was primarily by the leading organisations supporting the capacity building initiatives [ 68 – 74 ]. In summary, six papers highlighted a blended approach to CPD integrating face-to-face delivery for practical aspects lasting between 1 and 10 days depending on the expected learning outcomes and the distance learning for theoretical components. Although the international faculty supported and strengthened the information technology infrastructure in the local countries, it was not clear who covered the participants’ training related costs involved in the off-site face-to-face sessions in the described programmes in LMICs. Certificates of attendance and non-academic credit bearing certificates were highlighted as recognition for the short-term programmes. Long-term programmes led to award of academic certificates – diploma, masters and fellowships. Papers also emphasized the importance of supportive follow-up and mentoring of educators to promote implementation of the new learning acquired. Effectiveness of midwifery educator CPD programmes Assessment of effect/impact of CPD programmes was reported in 12 studies using different approaches for different outcomes [ 5 , 57 , 58 , 60 , 62 – 67 , 76 , 78 ]. No assessment for effectiveness of the programmes was reported in the six UNFPA reports, ICM report, both studies by Shuriquie et al. and Girot et al. Findings are reported using the robust Kirkpatrick’s framework for evaluation of training programmes [ 81 ]. The four levels of the framework are: change in participants’ reaction to training including expectations of participants in the programmes (Level 1), change in learning: any new knowledge and/or skills and abilities acquired by participants during the programme (Level 2), change in behaviour: transfer of knowledge/skills participants learnt during the programme to change in teaching practice (Level 3) and change in outcome/impact of CPD on the professional development including career progression, student learning, quality of care provided by students (Level 4). Kirkpatrick’s evaluation for each study is summarised in Table 2 . Table 2. Table showing levels of assessment of effectiveness of the CPD programmes using the kirkpatrick’s framework Study Kirkpatrick level 1 Kirkpatrick level 2 Kirkpatrick level 3 Kirkpatrick level 4 West et al., 2016 [ 5 ] N/A Educators improved knowledge and skills in curriculum review and educational approaches to deliver updated curriculum Midwifery curricula reviewed and strengthened including teaching strategies e.g. use of contemporary adult education methods – simulations; educators applied new knowledge in theoretical teaching, clinical supervision of students; educators participated more in leadership, management and research activities and contributed to research improvements and scholarships at home institutions Participation in research strengthened the status of midwifery profession; educators advocated for more resources, shared knowledge and strengthened education systems Frantz et al., 2015 [ 73 ] N/A N/A Educators formed a community of practice for experiences and information sharing and networking; educators increased their educational scholarship awards, scholarly publications, conference attendance and presentations, national research ratings, research development and recognised research roles for educators (research and academic promotions). Also, it improved the formative and summative assessment skills; Improved communication skills and engagement with other academics and education interest groups Led to personal growth as researchers and academicians; created new Education Research Interest Group for mentorship and professional development of newly appointed academic staff; introduced and strengthened research, teaching and learning capacity of home institutions van Wyk et al., 2020 [ 75 ] N/A N/A Developed curricula and educational materials for new programmes; improved use of authentic teaching approaches and support for students in clinical learning; educators strengthened their academic assessment strategies for theory and practical by integration of objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) and simulation-based education; led to career progression including heading of departments, leading education-related research projects and driving faculty development initiatives; improved participation in scholarly activities, such as conferences, and advanced their academic qualifications (Master’s or doctoral degrees); applied the knowledge and skills related to teaching, learning, and assessments in the faculty development activities for newly recruited faculty in home institutions Equipped training institutions with appropriate resources for learning improving teaching and student learning; improved interpersonal relationships and collaborations with home or international colleagues in academic and research support Akiode et al., 2010 [ 60 ] N/A Improved knowledge, confidence and skills in post-abortal care and performing uterine evacuation procedures using manual vacuum aspiration for educators and midwifery students Developed pre-service midwifery curriculum on post-abortion care; educators taught students about post-abortal family planning information and methods; increased exposure for students to theoretical teaching and clinical practice in post-abortal care Training institutions equipped with post-abortion care teaching aids, educational materials and manual vacuum aspiration equipment. Impact on maternal outcomes not evaluated/reported Evans et al., 2013 [ 59 ] Positive evaluation of content – teaching, research, leadership/management and clinical education Improved knowledge and experience in using reflection, problem identification, goal setting and action planning for their needs and personal development; improved confidence in applying new teaching skills and evaluating student learning Reviewed training curriculum; educators were confident, motivated and applied new teaching skills by structuring lectures and group work more effectively, evaluating student learning, using new tools, and made learning more enjoyable (e.g. by using humour or interactive techniques Improved educational experience for the students Koto et al., 2016 [ 61 ] Educators mastered new content areas that included newborn care, statistics and nursing informatics and nursing education Educators expanded their expertise and self-confidence in using informatics in searching for information and transfer to students Improved supervision of students’ clinical practice; applied new teaching methods and styles; improved clinical practicum style; improved student evaluation; improved teaching preparation through core syllabi and lesson plans Prepared legislative framework under the Cambodian Nursing Council which led to strategic improvements in nursing education in Cambodia; upgraded teaching-learning environments (infrastructure and equipment) Wright et al., 2005 [ 62 ] N/A N/A Improved effective working collaborations with international colleagues; educators published first nursing textbook widely used by schools of nursing in Albania; led to curricular revisions in schools of nursing integrating nursing leadership and research; increased clinical learning hours to strengthen the schools’ nursing and midwifery programmes; introduced nursing and midwifery education into some university settings; educators promoted to leadership roles in their organizations while others assumed new roles in other organisations; Developed standards of practice in clinical settings; first private childbirth centre established in Romania; Romanian Midwifery Association published a Code of Ethics; developed new continuing education programmes responsive to key clinical issues and leadership development; enhanced capacity in working across cultures in international and health issues; expanded expertise and abilities as practitioners and teachers Erlandsson et al., 2017 [ 64 ] Increased confidence in use of internet and literature searching for teaching, learning, clinical and personal situations Improved critical thinking skills as educators Improved learning experience of educators; enhanced and applied new teaching and learning styles, applied more student-centered learning with focus on putting theory into practice; improved clinical teaching experience for patients and understanding of women rights; increased career paths of educators in management and leadership; educators gained more respect in society from students and colleagues N/A Erlandsson et al., 2018 [ 57 ] An online and face-to-face blended online was feasible and educators reacted positively to the mentorship programme content N/A Faculty motivated to deliver national midwifery diploma curriculum and carry out supportive supervision of midwifery students in clinical placement; faculty integrated online mentorship in addition to onsite coaching to deliver updated curriculum; improved teaching skills (lecture preparation and presentation using power point and correspondence with students) N/A Erlandsson et al., 2019 [ 56 ] N/A Increased knowledge and skills in using technologies (computers, smartphone, internet) and applications for selection of effective teaching and learning resources Educators improved their application of computer/smartphone technology for teaching and preparing assessments; improved learning and communication with students Enhanced and promoted active learning of midwifery students; raised awareness of educators and students on state of midwifery care and women’s rights including social stigma and discrimination on proper care for every woman and child during pregnancy and childbirth Bogren et al., 2019 [ 63 ] Enhanced confidence in communication and practical skills teaching Increased theoretical knowledge and practical skills and confidence on essential lifesaving skills for a woman in pregnancy, labor and childbirth; Educators applied new pedagogical approaches in teaching & learning including practical skills teaching; transformed teaching experience with research-based knowledge; improved desire for continuing learning for midwifery educators Increased learning experience among students in practical lifesaving skills Hatakka et al., 2019 [ 65 ] Educators met their expectations in teaching and learning, and economic and information technology and reproductive health care Educators improved their skills in midwifery, evidence-based practices, research, and educational organisation Personal and professional development through increased academic and teaching capabilities – improved classroom preparation and organisation, updated course content and evidence-based midwifery practices, increased interactivity in classes; improved learning capabilities and more independent in their learning; understanding of gender (in)equality; promotions at the workplace Promoted women’s rights and health on breastfeeding, how to care for babies, signs and symptoms of anaemia, danger signs of pregnancy and gender equality Open in a new tab Overall, few studies were evaluated, showing positive effect at Kirkpatrick levels 1–4 but there was limited evidence on the impact of midwifery CPD programmes on student and MNH health outcomes. Of note, sustainable improvements in the midwifery education were recorded through curriculum review that included new subject specific content and application of new teaching and assessment approaches such as web-based platforms. Educators achieved both personal and professional growth and development in their careers, improved their research, leadership and management skills and led innovations in teaching to enhance learning in their institutions. Knowledge and skills in project management contributed to advanced qualifications and expanded the options for career progression for educators. Educators who completed leadership, research and project management programmes advanced in their careers as heads of departments, researchers and institutional/departmental managers in international organizations, government and education sector. Also, leadership and management training improved midwifery educators’ communication skills for effective advocacy for resources for midwifery education. This led to the positive student perception of improved learning and teaching support. Although the studies report personal, professional and institutional improvements from the programmes, little has been documented on the impact of programmes on maternal and newborn health outcomes. Discussion Key findings This review focuses on approaches used in midwifery educator CPD, the content covered in the CPD programmes and the effectiveness of such programmes. Primary evidence of CPD in LMICs from research studies was limited, highlighting the little attention CPD in midwifery education has received as an important professional agenda. Midwifery faculty development programs commonly used the twinning model. There was limited regulator involvement in regulation and accreditation of CPD programs that is crucial to ensure midwifery education and practice standards are maintained. Content primarily focused on curriculum, teaching, and clinical skills, but lacked focus on students’ assessment, mentoring, and use of digital technology in supporting teaching and learning. Programs combined in-person and virtual delivery. Long-term programs offered certificates, while short-term ones offered attendance proof. Post-program mentoring aided implementation. Programs improved educators’ skills, transformations in training curricula and teaching, but impact on maternal-newborn health outcomes was rarely evaluated. Interpretation of findings Evidence of implementation and monitoring of CPD interventions in LMICs is primarily from seven countries, suggesting low investment globally in strengthening the capacity of midwifery faculty. Strengthening faculty through CPD is a key pillar to achieve quality midwifery education as recommended by the WHO Global Strategic Directions for Nursing and Midwifery 2021–2025 , the 7-step action plan for strengthening quality midwifery education and the State of the World Midwifery Report (2021) [ 3 , 10 , 16 ]. This review identified multiple approaches used in provision of CPD with some overlap of approaches described within the papers. The twinning approach was the commonest model in this review. This is consistent with findings from a scoping review by Smith and colleagues where three-quarters of programs on faculty development programmes in nursing and/or midwifery professions involved international partnerships [ 82 ]. Although the twinning approach is popular, evidence from the review underscores the need to involve local stakeholders in the design, building the capacity of local trainers and implementation of these programmes. This is critical for a seamless roll-out, effective implementation and sustainability even after withdrawal of the international partners. Mentorship of local faculty after completing the CPD programmes was well documented in the review papers as a powerful tool for enhancing the effectiveness and sustainability of the interventions. Evidence suggests that mentorship programmes facilitate pedagogic and assessment processes effective for critical thinking, reflection and decision-making in learning [ 83 ]. Countries should design CPD programmes adapted to their local needs and embed sustainable mentoring approaches for scale-up. There was limited involvement of midwifery regulators in the regulation and accreditation of the CPD programmes reported in this review. Involving the local nursing and midwifery councils (regulators) in the accreditation of the CPD programmes provides an opportunity to increase the legitimacy of the CPD programmes. This has the potential to improve uptake and sustainability of the programmes in the respective LMICs [ 24 ]. As per the ICM Global Standards for Midwifery Regulation , regulation of midwifery is critical to ensure that safe and competent midwives provide high standards of midwifery care to every woman and baby. National midwifery regulators are mandated to give guidance and direction to underpin and enable autonomous midwifery practice within the scope of practice and enhance high quality midwifery care [ 84 ]. Nursing and midwifery councils have a responsibility to ensure that the standards, guidelines and tools for midwifery education, practice and regulation are maintained. This is by implementing mandatory midwifery educators and preceptor CPD policy as recommended by ICM [ 13 ]. This provides the best platform as regulators of the CPD programmes to ensure that the right programmes are designed and implemented in the training of nursing and midwives to international standards. Although midwifery CPD programmes are increasing in Africa and linked to re-licensure [ 23 ], midwifery educator and preceptor specific midwifery programmes are uncommon. These programmes need to be developed and capacity to monitor implementation by nursing/midwifery regulators strengthened. Limited involvement of national regulators could potentially highlight inadequate regulator capacity and thus the need for strengthening to optimize their functions. The described content of the programmes covered aligns with the WHO midwifery educator core competencies and ICM essential competencies for midwifery practice [ 11 , 12 ]. Most papers in this review reported on midwifery curriculum design and updated educational approaches to promote effective teaching and learning for students. However, few programmes particularly the long duration fellowship had research, leadership and management in health content and limited on students’ assessment (formative and summative), giving effective feedback for learning, mentoring/supervision in clinical practice and digital/electronic innovations in teaching and learning. Our findings are similar to the learning needs assessment survey by Smith and colleagues for midwifery faculty from LMICs in the Asia Pacific Region [ 18 ] and the global cross-sectional exploratory survey on current challenges to midwifery education based on ICM standards [ 85 ]. However, gaps in research and publication, leadership and management development, and the more traditional activities of teaching and curriculum development are identified as priority needs for midwifery faculty. This underscores the value and recognition educators expect in their professional development and progression. As recommended by WHO, participating in research, program management and evaluation and leadership are core competencies for midwifery educators to participate in policy formulation and improve the quality of midwifery education [ 11 ]. Midwifery educators with enhanced leadership and management skills can potentially influence policy and resource allocation for midwifery education at educational and clinical institutions particularly in resource constrained countries. Participating in research helps educators to remain current in their teaching and clinical practice. This clearly demonstrates that midwifery educators demand for professional support to strengthen their expected expanded competencies for effective teaching and learning of future midwifery graduates. Evidence shows enhanced student learning and improved performance in theory and clinical skills when taught by trained and regularly mentored educators in teaching pedagogy and clinical lifesaving skills [ 86 – 88 ]. For quality midwifery education, CPD programmes should consider the expanded roles of midwifery educators in research, institutional management and project design and management in addition to their traditional teaching roles as change agents in academics and clinical practice. Blended learning approach was common in the CPD programmes reviewed. This approach is becoming increasingly popular in midwifery education [ 40 ]. Besides, lessons from the COVID-19 pandemic have provided opportunities for online and virtual teaching practices to be continued post-pandemic. This approach is flexible, cost-effective and improves the education quality and accessibility of learning programmes compared to the traditional face-to-face models [ 89 – 93 ]. CPD programmes should enhance clinical skills development. Blended learning has also been proven effective in achieving clinical competencies (knowledge, skills, attitude, and practice) compared to the conventional face-to-face and online-only instructional approaches [ 94 – 96 ]. However, evidence suggests that educators are poorly prepared to deliver online/virtual teaching [ 97 , 98 ]. Therefore, strengthening the capacity of educators to use technology in delivering instructional teaching and learning in midwifery education is essential. Besides, training institutions are poorly equipped with skills lab maternal and newborn skills equipment for competency-based education to bridge the gap between theory and practice [ 29 , 30 ]. For effective uptake of blended learning, countries should invest in relevant infrastructure – technological, training facilities and equipment to promote self-directed learning technologies as well as resources to participate in the residential programmes for practical components [ 13 , 16 ]. Participation in CPD programmes led to award of certificates of attendance, academic credits, advanced qualifications and professional promotion in academic ranks for educators. For effective implementation of faculty development programmes, participation should be supported and sustained as a continuous pathway for educators to improve their teaching capacity and to further develop themselves [ 99 ]. Importantly, participation in CPD programmes for educators should bring a sense of accomplishment, recognition and self-improvement [ 99 , 100 ]. Faculty development programmes should increase faculty motivation, satisfaction, and productivity [ 100 ]. Therefore, for effective implementation of CPD programmes, designers of such programmes should consider recognition as a sustainable approach to promote uptake by midwifery educators. Duration of the capacity strengthening programmes varied between short and long approaches. Due to the staffing challenges commonly experienced in LMICs, findings in this review underscore the need for effective regular shorter duration programmes that are relevant to midwifery educators and follow-up supportive mechanisms to promote implementation of new learning. However, adequate time during the practical sessions should be considered during the design of such programmes to allow participants complete clinical practice/simulation [ 62 ]. This has the potential to allow participants demonstrate the learning for proficiency [ 101 ]. Long duration programmes leading to higher qualifications and academic credits are also critical to strengthening the capacity of the educators in the professional competencies. This is essential for creating and implementing an enhanced community of practice for faculty development in midwifery profession [ 99 ]. The UNFPA reports highlight important maternal and newborn health lifesaving skills for educators – emergency obstetrics and newborn care. These lifesaving skills are essential and part of the competencies required by skilled health personnel – including midwives and midwifery educators – who support and provide pregnancy, childbirth and postnatal and newborn care [ 102 , 103 ]. However, evidence shows that educators are often lacking these skills and consequently these are not fully covered in the pre-service midwifery curricula [ 104 – 107 ]. Future midwifery educator CPD programs should prioritize clinical skills training, teaching capacity, and hands-on practice opportunities, including simulations. Research is needed to better understand how educators maintain clinical competencies across regions and their preferred learning methods. Impact of CPD programs on MNH outcomes is limited in this review. The effects of capacity strengthening on individual performance are often not immediate as learners must first apply the new knowledge and skills learned (Kirkpatrick Level 3 Behaviour). This necessitates long-term quantitative and qualitative data collection and monitoring for the desired outcomes to manifest and stabilize [ 108 ]. Recognizing the lag time challenges of evaluating impact/outcomes at Kirkpatrick Level 4, use of randomized control trials, quasi-experimental designs, stepped-wedge trials, or long-term/longitudinal observational studies that link CPD interventions to health facility data on quality of care and health outcomes are needed. These designs are recommended for evaluating higher-level impacts more effectively [ 109 ]. This review demonstrates that wider investments in health systems can complement investments in midwifery pre-service education, ultimately reinforcing professional development of midwifery educators. This will better prepare graduates for midwifery practice and contribute to attainment of MNH SDG targets and universal health coverage particularly in countries with insufficient progress towards these targets [ 13 , 16 , 17 ]. Implications for practice and research Future midwifery educators’ CPD programmes should focus on the educators’ (i) conventional roles (theoretical and clinical skills teaching and learning – student assessments, giving effective feedback for learning, mentoring support and use of technology/digital innovations) and (ii) expanded roles (research, leadership and management in health) competencies as a pathway for good returns on maternal and newborn health investments. Adequate policy and regulatory environment are required to ensure legitimacy and sustainability of midwifery faculty development programmes. Blended learning approaches in midwifery faculty development programmes have been proven effective and are a cornerstone for sustainability of the programmes. mHealth learning tools including the Maternity Foundation Safe Delivery App are increasingly being accessed and effective in improving clinical competencies of midwives [ 110 – 112 ]. To increase access to CPD in remote and resource-constrained settings, CPD programs developers should integrate self-directed learning modules available and accessible on smartphones in offline modes as viable sustainability options to CPD programs [ 113 ]. Resource constraints including financial and infrastructural (e.g. computers and internet access) are key barriers to participation in CPD activities especially the online self-directed learning approach [ 24 ]. Thus, stakeholder engagement on cost and sustainability are required as key policy components for effective CPD programmes. Other reviews suggest that the process for developing and implementing a successful CPD program should include a financing plan and advocate for low-cost activities so that educators do not bear unreasonable burden in order to participate in CPD [ 114 – 116 ]. The financing plan should be long term with little dependent on grants or international aid [ 114 , 116 ]. As evidenced in nurses CPD, midwifery institutions should adequately fund and make CPD accessible. This is motivation for continued active engagement in CPD activities towards maintaining high standards of education and competent clinical practice [ 115 ]. From the limited evidence on the available educators’ capacity strengthening/faculty development programmes available, there is need to build the capacity of LMICs to report/publish CPD interventions within their settings and provide opportunities for a comprehensive evaluation of such programmes. Although there was evaluation of the effectiveness of the interventions, almost all studies (11 out of 14) relied on qualitative participant self-reported measures which are largely subjective and similar to the scoping review by Smith and colleagues on nursing and/or midwifery faculty development interventions [ 40 ]. Our review provides self-reported evidence on the impact of the faculty development programmes specific for midwifery educators at personal, professional and institutional level. Reliance on qualitative and participant self-reported measures for assessing effectiveness limits the objective measurement of impact, particularly at higher Kirkpatrick levels such as student learning outcomes and MNH outcomes. In addition, the impact of such programmes on participants’ reactions, student learning and MNH outcomes in this review is limited. While recognizing the challenges of evaluating impact at Kirkpatrick Level 4 (outcomes), use of randomized control trials, quasi-experimental designs, stepped-wedge trials, or long-term/longitudinal observational studies that link CPD interventions to health facility data on quality of care and health outcomes would be beneficial. Direct effectiveness of CPD programs on student learning and, more significantly, on maternal and newborn health outcomes, are not fully supported by strong experimental studies within the included literature. Future studies employing more robust quantitative or mixed methods designs (e.g., randomized controlled trials, quasi-experimental studies with control groups, interrupted time series analyses) that directly measure the impact of specific CPD interventions on objective outcomes such as student competencies, changes in student clinical practice, and ultimately, MNH outcomes (e.g., reduction in maternal/neonatal morbidity/mortality, improved quality of care metrics) are needed. Experimental trials using objective measures and follow-up nested qualitative study within the cluster randomized control trial are effective in demonstrating the impact of training and regular structured mentoring and capacity strengthening interventions on educator teaching competencies, and students learning in maternal and newborn health clinical competencies including EmONC [ 88 , 106 , 117 – 119 ]. To strengthen the approaches to evaluate effectiveness of CPD programs, longitudinal studies that track educators and their students over extended periods to assess the sustained impact of CPD programs on knowledge/skill retention, skill application, and patient outcomes are essential [ 118 , 120 , 121 ]. Therefore, studies with greater integration of quantitative objective measures for evaluating effectiveness, such as observed clinical skill assessments, analysis of curriculum changes implemented, clinical practice and review of facility-level MNH data are critical. To create impact, CPD programmes should be mandatory, accredited, meaningful, and relevant focusing on strengthening the individual’s professional capacity, augment skills and competencies and allow for the expansion of their scope of practice when warranted [ 33 , 34 , 36 – 38 , 122 ]. This review reported on broader capacity building initiatives focusing on educator competencies. Although ICM emphasizes that educator competencies should be reviewed on a regular basis following an established process, clear standards for midwifery faculty development are needed [ 13 ]. Strengths and limitations Our systematic review evaluated the impact/effectiveness of the faculty development programmes specific for midwifery educators at personal, professional, institutional and health systems level in LMICs. Importantly, our systematic review provides useful guidance on the CPD approach, duration, sustainability, and effectiveness of the programmes for CPD programme designers and policy-makers in LMICs [ 41 ]. This review provides targeted evidence specific from LMICs and focused on midwifery faculty. Additionally, this review assessed the effectiveness/impact of the CPD programs, methods used to measure the impact of the programmes and suggested practical approaches for designing, funding, and implementing the programs. Importantly, this review also recommends robust designs for evaluating the effectiveness of CPD programs at higher impact levels in LMICs effectively. Findings from this review were catalytic to the multistakeholder co-design of the EmONC and the blended midwifery educator CPD programme that were effective in improving the competencies of educators in theory and practical skills teaching including digital innovations in teaching and learning [ 107 , 123 ]. The online component of the blended programme is publicly available on the World Continuing Education Alliance (WCEA) platform with global access by learners [ 123 ]. The quality appraisal of the included papers used multiple recommended tools for each specific study design. This was critical to improve the rigor and credibility of the review and strengthens the trustworthiness of the findings reported [ 41 , 54 ]. This review was restricted to English-language publications only, thus potential for language bias. Limited funding and time necessitated this decision. However, one paper extracted in the Arabic language was available from the searches on the topic. Relevant literature from non-English speaking LMICs which may offer differing findings, or important contextual information would be informative. However, there is little documented evidence on the topic in LMICs. Thus, the review was judged robust as little evidence on the topic in LMICs was available. Conclusion This review finds limited evidence of effective midwifery educator CPD programs in LMICs, with minimal national regulator involvement. Programs, mostly implemented by international partners, focused on curriculum and teaching but lacked emphasis on student assessment, feedback, and digital technology use. Research, leadership and management in health programmes were essential for educators for resource mobilisation and as advocates of change in teaching and clinical practice. CPD participation improved personal and institutional growth, review and improvements in training curricula and teaching practices, but program evaluation was limited. Direct effectiveness of CPD programs on student learning and, more significantly, on maternal and newborn health outcomes was limited. Sustainable, cost-effective programs with local stakeholder involvement and regulatory guidance are needed to enhance midwifery education quality. Future research employing robust experimental designs integrating objective measures for evaluating effectiveness should assess CPD programs’ direct impact on student learning, application and maternal and newborn health outcomes. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (23.2KB, docx) Acknowledgements Alison Derbyshire, LSTM librarian for expert support with data search extraction and Hannah McCauley for her role in protocol design, screening of records, data extraction and synthesis. Thank you also to Geeta Lal at UNFPA Headquarters. Author contributions Duncan N Shikuku and Charles Ameh conceived the idea and designed the study protocol. Duncan N Shikuku registered the protocol in PROSPERO, designed and performed the data searches, extraction, synthesis and critical appraisal and drafted the primary manuscript. Sarah Bar-Zeev and Charles Ameh critically reviewed the protocol, data searches, synthesis and quality appraisal of papers and reviewed the draft manuscripts. All the authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding The study was not funded but was part of the larger “Strengthening capacity of midwifery education and training: faculty and institutions project” funded by UNFPA Headquarters. Data availability Data is provided within the manuscript or supplementary information files. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Not applicable. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. UNICEF, World Health Organization. Levels and Trends Child Mortality-Report 2023: Estimates Developed by the United Nations Inter-agency Group for Child Mo