hort study. Eur J Surg Oncol. 2025;51(5):109671. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Uwins C, Bhandoria G. COVID-19 pandemic impact on gynaecological cancers: a perspective. J Br Surg. 2020;107(8):e265-e. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The data are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',15,'2025-10-13 06:27:22','2025-10-13 06:27:22');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1590,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12466053','Clinicopathological characteristics and survival outcomes in early-stage endometrial clear cell carcinoma: a dual-center retrospective study.','Chen Y, Ma Y, Yuan H, Wu H, Li J, Li L, Li N.','2025','pwc pwc pwc bso overtreated bso omental omentectomy extrauterine erbt vbt multiplicative dmi pwc pwc pwc lymphadenectomies dmi pwc extrafascial modifed omentectomy vbt vbt vbt vbt supraclavicular pwc pwc pwc pwc pwc pwc multiparity pwc dmi pwc dmi pwc dmi pwc pwc pwc dmi mitogenic multiparity pwc pwc dmi pwc reaffirmed pwc pwc bso pwc extrauterine dmi pwc dmi pwc vbt vbt vbt ncdb orellana pwc dmi pwc bso vbt dmi pwc yiran yiran huatuo jiangtao yiran yiran yiran donghui555 concerto2000 laversanne jeans mullikin looker keeney cirisano fdj robboy krigman synan surgicopathologic cloutier pina orellana soong rives filiaci mcmeekin gruenigen yashar matei filiaci mutch steinhoff disilvestro mileshkin katsaros bessette haie meder bogani coquard concin ngoi nyl morice enomoto doria nobre sassine ataseven olawaiye boruta dmn sgo pecorelli mcvicker cardwell mccluggage wylie nicolucci czyzyk szczepanik setiawan franasiak bolac armbruster previs westin fellman jhingran halpenny graziano wahab mutch dowdy hensley solmaz ekin gezer biler peker abdulfatah sakr wahab mutch dowdy doria momeni boroujeni selenica delair selenica middha zannoni angelico spadola arciuolo baniak fadare kobel decoteau parkash mcconechy ewanowich gilks rudd urick lozy fadare crispens khabele gwin rustum yashar chino chon foote broadwater havrilesky chino ayeni bakkum gamez mcgree haddock mahdi moulton nutter cherian gockley rauh clemmer','Abstract Objectives To investigate clinicopathological predictors and evaluate the impact of adjuvant therapy on survival outcomes in patients with early-stage (FIGO 2023 Stage I-II) endometrial clear cell carcinoma (ECCC). Methods This dual-center retrospective study enrolled patients newly diagnosed with stage I-II ECCC between January 1, 2010 and December 31, 2023 at two tertiary hospitals in China. Clinicopathological parameters and survival outcomes were systematically analyzed. Kaplan-Meier curves and Cox regression analyses were used to assess the associations between variables and overall survival (OS) or progression-free survival (PFS). Results Among 80 eligible patients (median age: 59 years), 30.0% ( n = 24) were stage I and 70.0% ( n = 56) stage II. After a median follow-up of 76 months, 11 patients experienced disease progression and 6 died, with 5-year PFS and OS rates of 87.0% and 91.6%, respectively. Kaplan-Meier survival analysis demonstrated no significant differences in PFS or OS between patients undergoing surgery alone versus surgery with adjuvant therapy. Multivariate Cox analysis identified positive peritoneal washing cytology (PWC) (HR = 8.062, 95% CI: 1.208–53.814, P = 0.031) and deep myometrial invasion (≥ 1/2) (HR = 7.455, 95% CI: 1.187–46.803, P = 0.032) as independent adverse prognostic factors for OS. Diabetes mellitus emerged as the only significant predictor for reduced PFS (HR = 3.831, 95% CI: 1.053–13.935, P = 0.042). Conclusions Positive PWC and deep myometrial invasion (≥ 1/2) were independent adverse prognostic factors for OS in stage I-II ECCC, while diabetes mellitus emerged as the sole predictor of poorer PFS. Notably, adjuvant therapy failed to demonstrate any significant survival benefit compared to surgery alone in this cohort. These findings challenge the routine use of adjuvant therapy for stage I-II ECCC and underscore the importance of individualized risk stratification incorporating PWC, myometrial invasion depth and diabetic status. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12885-025-14965-5. Keywords: Endometrial clear cell carcinoma, Early stage, Prognosis, Adjuvant treatment Introduction Endometrial carcinoma (EC) is one of the most common gynecologic cancers worldwide, second only to cervical cancer [ 1 ]. Among its histological variants, endometrial clear cell carcinoma (ECCC) represents a rare yet aggressive subtype, comprising < 5% of all EC cases and demonstrating a higher prevalence in East Asian populations [ 2 ]. Clinically, ECCC is associated with advanced-stage disease at diagnosis, relative chemoresistance, elevated recurrence rates, and poorer survival outcomes compared to endometrioid adenocarcinoma [ 3 , 4 ]. Due to the aggressiveness and rarity of this histologic subtype, clinical data on ECCC remain limited, especially for patients with early-stage disease (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics [FIGO] 2023 stage I-II). For these cases, comprehensive surgical staging (including hysterectomy, bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy [BSO], and lymph node assessment) remains the foundation of primary treatment. Current therapeutic decision-making largely relies on subgroup analyses of prospective clinical trials with a small proportion of ECCC patients and retrospective studies [ 5 – 8 ]. This evidence gap is most pronounced in the realm of adjuvant therapy, where the survival benefits of chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy (RT) remain controversial [ 5 , 9 , 10 ]. The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) guidelines classify ECCC as a high-risk histologic subtype and recommend adjuvant therapy for most cases. Considering the financial burden and treatment-related toxicities of adjuvant therapies (particularly for early-stage patients who may be overtreated), there exists an urgent need to optimize risk-adapted adjuvant strategies in this patient population. Furthermore, the clinicopathological characteristics and independent prognostic factors specific to early-stage ECCC remain inadequately characterized. To address this knowledge gap, we conducted a dual-center retrospective study analyzing survival outcomes in early-stage ECCC patients from two experienced tertiary hospitals in China. This study aims to elucidate the impact of clinicopathological factors and adjuvant treatment modalities on disease prognosis. Patients and methods Patients This dual-center retrospective study enrolled patients with newly diagnosed FIGO 2023 Stage I-II ECCC treated between January 1, 2010 and December 31, 2023. Patients received primary surgery and adjuvant treatment at either Department of Gynecologic Oncology, National Cancer Center/Cancer Hospital, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences, or Department of Gynecology, Affiliated Tumor Hospital of Xinjiang Medical University. The study was approved by the Institutional Review Boards (IRB) of both participating centers (Approval Nos. NCC-017345 and K-2025022) with waiver of informed consent due to the retrospective nature. All procedures complied with the Declaration of Helsinki and relevant regulations. The inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) patients who underwent surgical staging (minimal requirements: hysterectomy and BSO); complete staging additionally included peritoneal cytology, systematic pelvic/para-aortic lymph node evaluation (dissection or sampling), omental biopsy or omentectomy, and biopsy of suspicious extrauterine lesions [ 11 ]; (2) pathologically proven pure or mixed ECCC. The exclusion criteria were as follows: (1) secondary or metastatic ECCC, (2) non-surgical management, (3) incomplete medical records, and (4) follow-up less than 12 months. Data collection This study retrospectively analyzed comprehensive medical records, including clinicopathological variables, treatment modalities, and oncologic outcomes. Prognostic data were collected through medical records supplemented by telephone follow-up. Disease staging was adjudicated according to the FIGO 2023 uterine cancer classification system [ 12 ]. Postoperative management included either surveillance or adjuvant treatment, with treatment options consisting of radiotherapy (external-beam radiotherapy [ERBT], vaginal brachytherapy [VBT], or combined modalities), chemotherapy, or a combination thereof. Follow-up and outcomes Patients were followed every 3 months for the first 2 years, every 6 months for the subsequent 3 years, and annually thereafter. Clinicopathological data were retrospectively analyzed from diagnosis to death or the last follow-up date (December 31, 2024). Progression-free survival (PFS) was defined as the interval (in months) from primary surgery to first recurrence/progression, death, or the last follow-up. Overall survival (OS) was calculated from surgery to death or the final follow-up. Statistical analysis Continuous variables were presented as mean ± standard deviation (SD), and categorical variables as frequencies (percentages). Intergroup comparisons of categorical variables were performed using Pearson’s chi-square test or Fisher’s exact test, as appropriate. For survival analysis, the Kaplan-Meier method was employed to estimate survival distributions, with between-group comparisons assessed by the log-rank test. Prognostic factors were evaluated using Cox proportional hazards regression models, with both univariate and multivariate analyses. Interaction effects between treatment modalities and pathological factors were assessed by incorporating multiplicative interaction terms in Cox proportional hazards regression models. A two-sided P-value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. All analyses were conducted using SPSS (version 26.0) and R software (version 4.2.2). Results Patients clinical characteristics A total of 80 patients with FIGO 2023 Stage I-II ECCC were identified in our cohort. As shown in Table 1 , the median age at diagnosis was 59 years (range: 36–79) with a median BMI of 24.8 kg/m 2 (range: 17.3–38.4). The most common presenting symptom was abnormal vaginal bleeding (87.5%), followed by vaginal discharge (11.3%). The comorbidities included hypertension ( n = 28), diabetes ( n = 11), and both conditions ( n = 7). Preoperative CA125 levels were available for 76 patients, with elevation (> 35 U/ml) observed in 18.4% (14/76) of evaluable cases. Table 1. Patient clinical characteristics Total patients N = 80 Dates of cases, years 2010–2023 Median age (range), years 59 (36–79) Median BMI (range), kg/m 2 24.8 (17.3–38.4) Institution, n (%) National Cancer Center 55 (68.8) Affiliated Tumor Hospital of Xinjiang Medical University 25 (31.3) Menopause, n (%) Yes 70 (87.5) No 10 (12.5) Parity, n (%) <2 35 (43.8) ≥2 45 (56.3) Symptoms, n (%) Abnormal vaginal bleeding 70 (87.5) Vaginal discharge 9 (11.3) Abdominal distension and pain 3 (3.8) Others 4 (5.0) Medical comorbidities, n (%) Hypertension 28 (35.0) Diabetes 11 (13.8) History of cancer 9 (11.3) CA125, median(range), n (%) >35 U/ml 14 (17.5) ≤35 U/ml 62 (77.5) Unknown 4 (5.0) Open in a new tab BMI Body mass index; CA125 Cancer antigen 125 Pathologic characteristics The pathologic characteristics of the patients are summarized in Table 2 . Among the cohort, 51 patients (63.8%) exhibited pure clear cell histology, while 29 (36.3%) demonstrated mixed histology. Of those with mixed histology, the predominant subtype was a combination of clear cell and endometrioid carcinoma, accounting for 72.4% (21/29) of cases. According to FIGO 2023 staging system, 24 patients (30.0%) were classified as stage I and 56 (70.0%) as stage II. Additional high-risk pathologic features included: deep myometrial invasion (DMI; ≥50%) in 17 cases (21.3%), lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) in 9 cases (11.3%), and positive peritoneal washing cytology (PWC) in 7 of 70 evaluable cases (10.0%). Immunohistochemical analysis demonstrated the following molecular characteristics: p53 abnormalities (p53-abn) in 27 of 60 tested cases (45.0%), mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) in 11 of 60 cases (18.3%), estrogen receptor (ER)-negative in 38 of 69 cases (55.1%), and progesterone receptor (PR)-negative in 45 of 67 cases (67.2%). Notably, we observed 29 cases demonstrating dual ER/PR negativity, while 15 cases demonstrated dual ER/PR positive. No cases exhibited co-occurrence of p53-abn and dMMR. Table 2. Pathologic characteristics Characteristic N (%) Histology Pure 51 (63.8) Mixed CCC CCC + EmC 21 (26.3) CCC + SC 4 (5.0) CCC + sarcoma 1 (1.3) CCC + EmC + SC 3 (3.8) FIGO 2023 stage I 24 (30.0) II 56 (70.0) Myometrial invasion depth None 24 (30.0) <50% 39 (48.8) ≥50% 17 (21.3) LVSI Negative 71 (88.8) Positive 9 (11.3) PWC Negative 63 (78.8) Positive 7 (8.8) Unknown 10 (12.5) p53 abnormality Yes 27 (33.8) No 33 (41.3) Unknown 20 (25.0) dMMR Yes 11 (13.8) No 49 (61.3) Unknown 20 (25.0) ER Positive 31 (38.8) Negative 38 (47.5) Unknown 11 (13.8) PR Positive 22 (27.5) Negative 45 (56.3) Unknown 13 (16.3) Open in a new tab CCC Clear cell carcinoma; EmC Endometrioid carcinoma; SC Serous carcinoma; LVSI Lymphovascular space invasion; PWC Peritoneal washing cytology; dMMR Deficient mismatch repair; ER Estrogen receptor; PR Progesterone receptor Surgical treatment and adjuvant therapy Laparoscopic staging was performed in 38 patients (47.5%). Overall, 72 patients (90.0%) received lymph node assessment, with 56 (77.8%) undergoing both pelvic and para-aortic lymphadenectomies. Eight patients (10%) did not undergo a lymph node assessment, including 1 patient with stage I disease and 7 patients with stage II disease. A total of 18 patients received surgery alone, including 8 with stage I and 10 with stage II disease. Among these cases, 11 patients (61.1%) were aged 60 years or older, 4 (22.2%) had DMI, and none exhibited LVSI. PWC was negative in all 15 cases with available cytology data. While 7 patients declined adjuvant therapy, one patient with mixed histology and superficial myometrial invasion underwent postoperative endocrine therapy. Of the entire cohort, 62 patients (77.5%) received postoperative adjuvant therapy, with 45 receiving chemotherapy, 3 receiving radiotherapy, and 14 receiving both. Among patients who underwent chemotherapy with or without RT (59/80, 73.8%), 98.3% received platinum-based regimens, with paclitaxel-carboplatin being the most common protocol (50/59, 84.7%). The median number of adjuvant CT cycles was 4 (range: 1–6). Additionally, one stage II dMMR patient received a combination of chemotherapy and immunotherapy. Detailed treatment data are summarized in Table 3 . Table 3. Treatment characteristics Characteristic N (%) Surgical routes Laparotomy 42 (52.5) Laparoscopy 38 (47.5) Hysterectomy Extrafascial hysterectomy 64 (80.0) Modifed radical hysterectomy 7 (8.8) Radical hysterectomy 9 (11.3) Lymph node assessment Pelvic alone 16 (20.0) Pelvic + para-aortic 56 (70.0) No 8 (10.0) Median Lymph Node Resection Count (range) 31.5 (2–53) Omentectomy Yes 51 (63.8) No 29 (36.3) Postoperative treatment Surgery alone * 18 (22.5) Chemotherapy alone 45 (56.3) Radiotherapy alone VBT 1 (1.3) EBRT 2 (2.5) Chemotherapy + Radiotherapy Chemotherapy + VBT 5 (6.3) Chemotherapy + EBRT 4 (5.0) Chemotherapy + VBT + EBRT 5 (6.3) Postoperative chemotherapy cycles, median(range) 4 (1–6) Open in a new tab VBT Vaginal brachytherapy; EBRT External beam radiation therapy * Includes one patient receiving postoperative endocrine therapy (no chemotherapy/radiotherapy) Factors associated with survival outcomes The median follow-up time was 76 months (range: 12–178). During the follow-up period, disease recurrence occurred in 11 patients (13.8%), and cancer specific mortality was observed in 6 patients (7.5%). Among the 11 recurrent cases, metastatic sites were identified in 9 patients, including the vaginal stump ( n = 3), abdominal cavity ( n = 2), lung ( n = 2), pelvic cavity ( n = 1), liver ( n = 1), retroperitoneal lymph nodes ( n = 1), and supraclavicular lymph node ( n = 1). Notably, two patients (18.2%) presented with multiple metastatic lesions. Among 70 patients with evaluable PWC status, all 7 PWC-positive cases (100%) received adjuvant therapy, while 48 of 63 PWC-negative patients (76.2%) underwent adjuvant treatment. In the MI-stratified cohort ( n = 80), adjuvant therapy was administered to 76.5% (13/17) of patients with MI ≥ 50% and 77.8% (49/63) of those with MI < 50%. No significant interaction was observed between adjuvant therapy and either pathological factor (PWC status or MI depth) for survival outcomes (all P -interaction > 0.05), regardless of treatment modality (any adjuvant therapy, chemotherapy, or radiotherapy). Notably, due to universal adjuvant treatment administration in PWC-positive cases, analyses had to be restricted to the PWC-negative subgroup (adjuvant treatment vs. no treatment: OS P = 0.774; PFS P = 0.495). Full statistical details were provided in Supplementary Table S1 . In our study, immunohistochemical analysis was performed on 60 out of 80 ECCC cases. Among these, 11 exhibited dMMR, including two cases with a personal history of colorectal cancer and one with a family history of both colorectal and ovarian cancer, suggesting possible Lynch syndrome-associated tumors. Kaplan-Meier survival analysis demonstrated no statistically significant association between dMMR status and survival outcomes ( P = 0.643 for PFS, P = 0.867 for OS), nor between p53-abn status and survival ( P = 0.705 for PFS, P = 0.312 for OS). The 5-year PFS and OS rates for the entire cohort were 87.0% and 91.6%, respectively. Kaplan-Meier analysis revealed no significant differences in survival outcomes among different treatment modalities (surgery alone, postoperative chemotherapy, postoperative RT, and combined RT/chemotherapy; P = 0.297 for PFS, P = 0.215 for OS; Fig. 1 A-B). Similarly, comparison between surgery-only ( n = 18) and any adjuvant therapy ( n = 62) showed comparable 5-year survival rates (PFS: 88.9% vs. 86.3%, P = 0.666; OS: 88.9% vs. 92.7%, P = 0.613; Fig. 1 C-D). Furthermore, subgroup analyses demonstrated no statistically significant association between survival outcomes and specific adjuvant therapies, including chemotherapy ( P = 0.993 for PFS, P = 0.222 for OS), RT ( P = 0.266 for PFS, P = 0.821 for OS), and their combination ( P = 0.126 for PFS, P = 0.295 for OS). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Kaplan-Meier survival analysis in 80 patients with early-stage ECCC. A PFS stratified by adjuvant treatment modality; B OS stratified by adjuvant treatment modality; C PFS according to receipt of adjuvant treatment; D OS according to receipt of adjuvant treatment Table 4 presents prognostic factors associated with survival outcomes in patients with early-stage ECCC. Univariate analysis identified that multiparity (≥ 2) and diabetes mellitus were significantly associated with increased relapse risk. Multivariate analysis confirmed diabetes as an independent predictor of PFS (HR: 3.8, 95% CI: 1.1–13.9, P = 0.042), with significantly lower 5-year PFS rates (63.6% vs. 90.9% in non-diabetic patients). For OS, univariate analysis revealed that positive PWC and DMI were correlated with adverse outcomes. These associations remained significant in multivariate analysis. Positive PWC (HR: 8.1, 95% CI: 1.2–53.8, P = 0.031) and DMI (HR:7.5, 95% CI: 1.2–46.8, P = 0.032) were independently associated with poorer OS, corresponding to 5-year OS rates of 71.4% versus 94.3% (PWC-negative), and 74.8% versus 96.6% (DMI-negative), respectively. Table 4. Factors associated with overall survival and progression-free survival Variables OS PFS Univariate analysis Multivariate analysis Univariate analysis Multivariate analysis HR, 95%CI P -value HR, 95%CI P -value HR, 95%CI P -value HR, 95%CI P -value Age of diagnosis ≤ 60, > 60 2.428 (0.445–13.262) 0.306 -- -- 3.566 (0.946–13.448) 0.060 1.781 (0.445–7.123) 0.414 Parity < 2, ≥2 3.437 (0.401–29.448) 0.260 -- -- 8.044 (1.029–62.864) 0.047 6.151 (0.751–50.344) 0.090 Hypertension No, Yes 0.341 (0.040–2.917) 0.326 -- -- 1.032 (0.302–3.530) 0.959 -- -- Diabetes mellitus No, Yes 1.269 (0.148–10.879) 0.828 -- -- 4.780 (1.383–16.520) 0.013 3.831 (1.053–13.935) 0.042 Histology Pure mixed 0.920 (0.169–5.026) 0.924 -- -- 0.621 (0.1