s been particularly understudied, with limited data available to guide clinical decision-making. To address these gaps, we conducted a dual-center retrospective study to systematically evaluate the clinicopathological characteristics, identify prognostic factors, and assess the efficacy of adjuvant therapies in FIGO Stage I-II ECCC. Our findings indicate that diabetes mellitus is independently associated with poorer PFS, while positive peritoneal washing cytology (PWC) and deep myometrial invasion (DMI, ≥50%) independently correlate with worse OS. Notably, adjuvant therapy did not significantly improve survival outcomes, a finding that may support treatment de-escalation strategies to preserve patients’ quality of life. Diabetes mellitus has long been recognized as a prognostic risk factor in EC [ 13 ]. The proposed mechanisms include mitogenic effects of insulin growth factor-1 (IGF-1), dysregulated insulin/glucose signaling, oxidative stress, chronic inflammation, and other metabolic pathways [ 14 , 15 ]. ECCC, as a major subtype of Type II EC (which predominantly comprises uterine serous carcinoma [USC] and ECCC), has traditionally been considered unrelated to diabetes and obesity [ 16 ]. However, emerging evidence suggests that diabetes affects a comparable proportion of Type I and Type II EC patients, indicating that its oncogenic role extends beyond hyper-estrogenic obesity-driven carcinogenesis [ 16 , 17 ]. These findings underscore the need to investigate metabolic dysregulation in both EC subtypes rather than focusing exclusively on Type I. Although prior studies reported no significant association between diabetes and worse outcomes in Type II or high-grade EC [ 17 ], such conclusions may reflect limited statistical power due to the rarity of ECCC subsets. In our dual-center cohort of stage I-II ECCC patients, univariate analysis identified diabetes and multiparity as prognostic factors for PFS, with diabetes retaining independent predictive value in multivariate analysis ( P = 0.042). Notably, this study provides the first evidence that diabetes may serve as an independent prognostic marker for PFS in early-stage ECCC. Future research should prioritize elucidating the role of metabolic dysregulation in ECCC carcinogenesis to identify potential therapeutic targets. A review of literature reveals several prognostic factors for early-stage ECCC, including LVSI [ 18 , 19 ], positive PWC [ 10 ], advanced age [ 10 , 18 ], disease stage [ 19 ], racial disparities [ 20 ], omission of lymph node dissection [ 19 ], and chemotherapy cycles [ 5 ]. In our multicenter cohort, multivariate analysis adjusted for clinicopathological characteristics identified positive PWC ( P = 0.031) and DMI ( P = 0.032) as independent predictors of poorer OS. The prognostic significance of PWC has remained controversial for decades, resulting in its exclusion from the both FIGO 2009 and 2023 staging systems. However, recent evidence from a Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) database analysis (2010–2019) reaffirmed its clinical relevance, demonstrating that malignant PWC significantly correlated with reduced OS and cancer-specific survival (both P < 0.001) in ECCC [ 21 ]. Our findings align with these observations, supporting positive PWC as an independent adverse prognostic factor and underscoring the importance of routine peritoneal cytology sampling during total hysterectomy with BSO. These findings have prompted a reevaluation of adjuvant therapy indications for PWC-positive ECCC patients lacking extrauterine risk factors. Although myometrial invasion has been consistently associated with ECCC prognosis in previous studies, most analyses included mixed stage populations or combined cases with uterine serous carcinoma [ 22 , 23 ]. In contrast, our study specifically focused on early-stage ECCC cases, where DMI emerged as an independent prognostic factor for OS in both univariate ( P = 0.020) and multivariate analyses ( P = 0.032). Based on the multivariate analysis results, we propose incorporating positive PWC and DMI into risk stratification models for early-stage ECCC. Notably, our findings suggest that in early-stage ECCC, adjuvant therapy failed to show significant survival improvement regardless of PWC status or MI depth. Given the limited sample size, these negative results require cautious interpretation. Larger studies are needed to clarify their clinical utility in therapeutic decision-making. The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) has extensively characterized molecular heterogeneity in endometrioid and serous endometrial carcinomas [ 24 ], yet the applicability of the TCGA classification to ECCC remains poorly defined. Recent studies have proposed integrating molecular classification into ECCC risk assessment [ 25 – 28 ]. In our study, the evaluation of p53 and mismatch repair (MMR) status was performed solely by immunohistochemistry. Consistent with prior reports [ 29 – 31 ], the p53-abn subtype was the most prevalent in our cohort (45.0%). While previous studies have identified aberrant p53 expression as being associated with reduced PFS in univariate analysis and higher rates of peritoneal metastases compared to other subtypes [ 26 , 32 ], our study failed to demonstrate a statistically significant association between p53-abn and poorer survival outcomes. This discrepancy may originate from our study’s exclusive focus on early-stage disease, the intrinsic aggressiveness of ECCC, molecular heterogeneity, and limited sample size. Furthermore, no significant PFS benefit from adjuvant therapy was observed in the p53-abn subgroup ( P = 0.282). These findings should be interpreted with considerable caution given the constrained cohort size. Collectively, our findings suggest that ECCC may not entirely align with the TCGA molecular model, implying unique biological underpinnings. Future investigations should focus on delineating ECCC subgroups with distinct clinical behaviors to optimize risk stratification and facilitate precision therapeutic approaches. The availability of diverse guideline-recommended adjuvant therapies frequently results in inconsistent treatment selection for ECCC cases. According to NCCN guidelines, early-stage non-invasive ECCC may be managed with observation or VBT, with chemotherapy administration primarily guided by peritoneal cytology status. For invasive ECCC confined to the uterus, the recommended options include: (1) systemic therapy ± EBRT or VBT, or (2) EBRT ± VBT [ 33 ]. This therapeutic variability underscores the need for more standardized treatment protocols. In our survival analyses, we evaluated the effects of chemotherapy alone, RT alone, and combination of RT and chemotherapy, but found no statistically significant association with any survival outcome. This observation aligns with existing literature: Eric et al. conducted a multi-institutional review of 125 patients with uterine-confined ECCC and reported no significant survival benefit from adjuvant therapy [ 10 ]. Similarly, a large-scale National Cancer Database (NCDB) analysis of 2,516 stage I ECCC patients showed no OS advantage with adjuvant chemotherapy or RT [ 34 ]. Other studies have corroborated these findings, concluding that adjuvant therapy does not significantly influence survival in early-stage ECCC [ 18 , 35 – 37 ]. Collectively, these studies underscore the lack of an optimal therapeutic approach that demonstrates survival benefits in this patient population. Furthermore, high-quality evidence supporting adjuvant therapy for early-stage ECCC remains limited. Orellana et al. reported a statistically significant difference in OS between patients receiving 0–5 versus 6 cycles of chemotherapy ( P = 0.004) [ 5 ], our study did not replicate this finding ( P = 0.501). In our cohort, chemotherapy-only regimens was the most common adjuvant approach (45/80, 56.3%), consistent with the prior Chinese study [ 38 ]. Intriguingly, none of the 14 patients treated with adjuvant chemoradiotherapy experienced recurrence or death, suggesting a potential subset of patients who may derive benefit from combined modality therapy. Future studies incorporating molecular profiling may help identify such beneficiaries. This study represents one of the largest multicenter Chinese cohorts investigating FIGO Stage I-II ECCC, providing novel insights into its clinicopathological characteristics and survival determinants. However, several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the retrospective design inherently carries risks of selection bias, missing data, and potential confounding due to evolving clinical practices and inter-institutional treatment variations. Second, the rarity of ECCC restricted our sample size, particularly limiting subgroup analyses. Notably, the predominance of adjuvant chemotherapy ( n = 45) over radiotherapy ( n = 3) precluded robust comparisons between treatment modalities. Finally, our historical cohort design could not incorporate contemporary diagnostic and therapeutic advances, including molecular testing and immunotherapy regimens that have gained clinical relevance in recent years. Conclusion In this dual-center study of FIGO 2023 Stage I-II ECCC patients, both positive PWC and DMI emerged as independent adverse prognostic factors for OS, while diabetes mellitus was the sole predictor of poorer PFS. Notably, adjuvant therapy showed no significant survival benefit compared to surgery alone in this cohort. Our findings provide population-specific evidence to address the knowledge gap in Chinese patients with early-stage ECCC. These results challenge the universal application of adjuvant therapy for stage I-II ECCC. Future research should focus on individualized risk assessment, incorporating PWC, myometrial invasion depth and diabetic status to optimize both survival outcomes and quality of life. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1 (14.9KB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors would lik