INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1591,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12465174','Phylogenetics and selective pressure analysis of human papillomavirus types 31 and 33 variants in Eastern China.','Wang Y, Qu W, Zhou Q, Gong Y, Wang Y, Chen F, Mo J, Lin L, Bi T, Shi W, Zhang F, Wu Z, Sun Y, Liu X, He N, Li Y, Xu C.','2025','sublineages datamonkey nonsynonymous a862t q97l g7064a s491s a862t q97l g7064a s491s papillomaviruses hpvs hpvs nonenveloped orfs oncoproteins proteasomal prb sublineages hsils quadrivalent nonavalent nonavalent sublineages sublineages predominates sublineage sublineage sublineage nonsynonymous nonsynonymous sublineages nonsynonymous lithotomy speculum cytobrush scrape ectocervix preservative thinprep surepath scripting colposcopists exfoliated tianamp tiangen nuclease takara taq takara taq mg2 dntp yeared transillumination 3730xl gtgaaccgaaaacggttggtatataaagc ttgcctcaggttgcaaatctaacac atttggataaaaagaaacgattccacaacatagg atgttgtcccctgtctgtctgt gcaccctagttattatatgttaaaac catacatataccatatataacacacaa tgtgtatactgttgtttgtattggta cgtgataatatatgttggttcgtgtt tagggtgtaaccgaaagcgg cctttaacgttggcttgtgtcc tttcataatatttcgggtcgttggg ttcttctctctatgactgcttctac acaccattgttgtagacggtgc caacacaaagtacatagacagaacaa tgcacatggtggtgttttaacatt ggtacaggaggcaggtacac j04353 m12732 neighbour replications j04353 hq537675 hq537676 hq537680 hq537682 hq537684 hq537685 m12732 hq537698 eu918766 hq537705 kf436865 neighbour clustalw sublineages oncoprotein lxxll e6ap ligase 6slm hpv58 pentamer 6igd slac fel fubar nonsynonymous slac fel fubar phylogenies slac fel fubar datamonkey tmrca uncorrelated yule tempest treeannotator discarding figtree diverged kya kya coinfected birthplace jiangxi sublineage neighbour neighbour neighbour replications nonsynonymous nonsynonymous nonsynonymous nonsynonymous nonsynonymous nonsynonymous nonsynonymous nonsynonymous c286t h60y c520t a138v g695a e46k a743g k62e c6367t v272v c6817a v422v t4948a s260t t4950g s260s c4999t l277l a213c k35n a273g r55r a364c n86h a387c k93n a446g q113r a862t q97l g7064a s491s c5222t v338v numbering j04353 numbering m12732 threading tasser unmutated sublineage sublineage psipred bioinf sublineage sublineage threading tasser unmutated nonsynonymous a862t q97l g7064a s491s a862t q97l g7064a s491s nonsynonymous slac fel fubar fel fubar slac fel fubar hpv31e6 hpv31e7 hpv31l1 hpv31l2 hpv33e6 hpv33e7 hpv33l1 hpv33l2 slac fel fubar ancestors mrcas diverged ancestors mya mya diverged nonsynonymous q97l c286t c520t g695a a743g c6367t c6817a t4948a t4950g c4999t g695a a743g a743g nonsynonymous a213c a273g a364c a387c a446g a862t g7064a c5222t a213c a273g a364c a387c a446g a862t nonsynonymous preliminarily q97l nonsynonymous codons nonsynonymous nonsynonymous q97l fel fubar q97l ancestors mya hpv58 diverged mya diverged mya sublineage predates q97l q97l 283kb gwvi xd13 qingpu gf2023 lx2023 my2023 my2023 2022yfc2407406 op900638 op900915 wenjie nhe yanyun liyanyun congjian xucongjian georges tsakogiannis nikolaidis zagouri sanjose e217 hpv35 taizhou hausen papillomaviruses sharifian shoja jalilvand sublineage gco cytopathol desalle schiffman rector lemey tachezy speciation felidae r57 burk papillomaviruses tasser tasser w174 e0171140 kogure intrapatient precancer bissett beddows oumeslakht ababou badaoui papillomaviruses sublineage salavatiha shoja heydari sublineage e29262 shank kosakovsky datamonkey doorslaer peretti polyomaviruses e1005574 bedoya pilozo magues argent deluca mbayed corrientes phylodynamics argent 283kb op900638 op900915','Abstract Objectives The aim of this study is to identify the distribution of HPV31 and HPV33 lineages and sublineages, characterize their genetic variability, explore associations between cervical lesions, persistent/multiple infections, and HPV31/33 genetic variations, and estimate selective pressures and divergence times for both genotypes. Methods In this study, a total of 94 samples were collected, with 276 full-length gene sequences obtained for analysis. Phylogenetic analysis evaluating genetic variant diversity was performed using MEGA software. Correlation analyses were conducted using SPSS 20.0. Selective pressure and divergence time estimations were performed using the Datamonkey web server and BEAST v1.8.3, respectively. Results Lineage A, B, and C variants were identified in 26.1%, 4.3%, and 69.6% of the HPV31 isolates, respectively, whereas all the HPV33 variants belonged to lineage A. We detected 108 nucleotide variations in HPV31 and 126 in HPV33. For HPV33, the nonsynonymous mutation A862T (Q97L) in the E7 gene was significantly correlated with cervical lesions (χ 2 = 4.441, p < 0.05). Similarly, the synonymous mutation G7064A (S491S) was significantly associated with cervical lesions (χ 2 = 5.021, p < 0.05). Notably, eleven sites were under positive selection in this study. Conclusion Our study revealed two substitutions—A862T (Q97L) and G7064A (S491S)—associated with cervical lesions, along with eleven sites under positive selection. This work provides new insights into the clinical characteristics of HPV31/33 genetic variations and offers novel perspectives for developing next-generation vaccines in Eastern China. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12879-025-11489-y. Keywords: Human papillomavirus 31 and 33, Phylogenetics analysis, Selective pressure, Eastern China Introduction Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are a family of small DNA viruses that are globally prevalent sexually transmitted pathogens. Epidemiological studies document over 200 phylogenetically distinct genotypes [ 1 ]. Among them, approximately 15 high-risk (HR-HPV) types are oncogenic and are strongly associated with the development of cervical cancer, anogenital cancers, and an increasing fraction of head and neck squamous cell carcinomas [ 2 ]. HPVs are nonenveloped, double-stranded circular DNA viruses of approximately 8 kb in size that infect basal keratinocytes of the mucosal and cutaneous epithelia [ 3 ]. Within the human papillomavirus (HPV) genome, eight open reading frames (ORFs) encode key viral proteins: six early regulatory genes (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two late structural genes (L1 and L2). Among these, E6/E7 (responsible for oncogenic transformation) and L1/L2 (mediating viral entry and capsid assembly) have been the focus of extensive research because of their central roles in carcinogenesis and vaccine development [ 4 , 5 ]. As key viral oncoproteins, the early genes E6 and E7 drive oncogenesis by specifically binding to and inducing proteasomal degradation of the tumour suppressor proteins p53 and retinoblastoma (pRb). These mechanisms are fundamentally required for both tumour initiation and progression [ 6 , 7 ]. The L1 and L2 genes encode viral capsid proteins. The L1 gene sequence is relatively conserved [ 8 ] and is commonly used for HPV classification. An HPV genome is designated a distinct type if the DNA sequence of its L1 gene differs by more than 10% from that of any other HPV genome. Within a given type, lineages and sublineages are defined by genomic differences of approximately 1–10% and 0.5-1%, respectively [ 9 ]. According to World Health Organization reports, the ten most prevalent oncogenic HPV types in Chinese women with cervical cancer are HPV16, 18, 58, 52, 33, 31, 59, 45, 39, and 56 [ 10 ], which are ranked by descending frequency. In China, HPV31 and HPV33 are recognized as high-risk types that significantly contribute to cervical carcinogenesis. Collectively, these two types account for 16.8% of high-grade cervical lesions (HSILs) [ 11 ]. While current research in China has focused predominantly on HPV16, 18, 52, and 58, scientific attention to HPV31 and HPV33 remains disproportionately limited despite their significant clinical implications. Currently, there are three types of HPV vaccines: bivalent vaccines (targeting HPV-16 and − 18), quadrivalent vaccines (covering HPV-6, −11, −16, and − 18), and nonavalent vaccines (protecting against HPV-6, −11, −18, −31, −33, −45, −52, and − 58), with HPV-31 and HPV-33 exclusively covered by the nonavalent formulation. Among eligible Chinese females (9–45 years old), first-dose HPV vaccination coverage reached 10.15% nationwide, increasing to 20.99% in economically developed cities such as Shanghai. Although annual HPV vaccination doses and coverage rates continue to increase steadily, they remain below the global average and are insufficient to establish herd immunity [ 12 ]. HPV31 can be classified into seven sublineages (A1, A2, B1, B2, C1, C2, and C3), with previous studies indicating geographical predilection among lineages A, B, and C [ 6 ]. Reports from the United States and Brazil demonstrate that the distribution of HPV31 is dominated by lineage A, followed by lineages B and C; however, studies from China remain limited [ 13 ]. HPV33 can be further classified into five sublineages (A1, A2, A3, B, and C). In China, lineage A predominates, with the A1 sublineage distributed globally at regionally variable frequencies [ 6 ]. The A2 sublineage is rarely detected in Africa and South America, whereas lineage B is Africa-specific [ 2 ]. Lineage C has been identified exclusively in African samples, whereas the A3 sublineage appears to be specific to Asia/Oceania, as evidenced by studies from China and Japan [ 8 ]. The detection and quantification of evolutionary pressures driving genetic variation have emerged as active research areas [ 9 ], now constituting an established component of the statistical framework for sequence analysis. Multiple methods exist to identify rapidly evolving or unusually conserved sites in protein-coding regions. These approaches estimate site-specific synonymous (dS) and nonsynonymous (dN) substitution rates and then perform statistical tests to determine whether dS = dN. When one nonsynonymous mutation confers an advantage in fitness, it increases to a higher rate than does a synonymous mutation, resulting in dN/dS > 1. Although evolutionary pressures critically influence viral distribution and spread, studies focusing on HPV31 and HPV33 remain scarce, and discussions on their genetic variation and selection pressures are still limited. Mutations in E6/E7 may influence oncogenic potential, whereas L1/L2 variations could affect vaccine efficacy and molecular diagnostics. Although HPV16/18 are well characterized, HPV31/33 represent high-risk types with increasing clinical prevalence; nevertheless, their genetic variability remains understudied. The aim of this study is to characterize the phylogenetic distribution of HPV31/33 lineages and sublineages in Eastern China while investigating the selective pressures driving their genetic evolution. By analysing genomic variations across viral sequences, we aim to elucidate evolutionary trajectories, evaluate the functional consequences of nonsynonymous mutations, and identify clinically relevant mutation profiles associated with persistent infection, coinfection patterns, and disease progression severity. Furthermore, we systematically examined the correlations between specific genetic variations and clinical outcomes to inform lineage-targeted therapeutic interventions for HPV-associated malignancies. Materials and methods Study population and sample collection For this cross-sectional observational study, we selected HPV-positive patients who underwent colposcopy at the Cervical Disease Diagnosis and Treatment Center of the Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital affiliated with Fudan University between January 2021 and December 2022. The inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) age ≥ 18 years; (2) infection with HPV31 or HPV33; (3) pathological diagnosis results (including normal, inflammation, LSIL, HSIL, and cancer); and (4) volunteering for the program. Participants were excluded if they (1) were pregnant, (2) had undergone hysterectomy, or (3) declined informed consent. Specimen Collection: (1) The patient was positioned in lithotomy position. (2) A sterile speculum was inserted to visualize the cervix.(3) A cytobrush was inserted into the endocervical canal and rotate 360°to scrape the cells of transformation zone, ectocervix and endocervical canal. The device was then rinsed into a preservative liquid vial (e.g., ThinPrep or SurePath) and agitated to release cells.All samples were collected by gynecologist with more than 10 years of experience and cytological diagnoses were made by two consultant pathologists in our hospital. Before colposcopy, the patients were asked to finish a self-designed questionnaire face-to-face by the investigator, who was in charge of this program. The interviewers were trained to use neutral scripting and avoid leading questions. The questionnaires were anonymized before analysis to eliminate response bias. There were 13 questions included in the questionnaire: (1) age; (2) native place; (3) marital status; (4) fertility history; (5) history of smoking; (6) HPV vaccination status; (7) Age of first sexual contact; (8) number of sexual partners; (9) method of contraception; (10) time of first diagnosis of HPV infection; (11) result of cytology; (12) genotype of HPV infection (HPV Typing Test Report of Medical Institution); and (13) colposcopy diagnosis and histopathological result of biopsy directed by colposcopy (supplemented by colposcopists). Multiple HPV infections were identified as coinfections with more than one HPV genotype. Persistent infection was defined as a confirmed HPV infection detected ≥ 12 months after the initial diagnosis date. This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital of Fudan University. Viral DNA amplification and sequencing Genomic DNA was extracted from the exfoliated cervical cells using a TIANamp Genomic DNA Kit (TIANGEN) and collected in 50 µL nuclease-free water. The sequences of HPV31 and 33 type-specific primers are shown in Table 1 . PCRs were performed in a 50 µL reaction volume containing 1 unit of TaKaRa Ex Taq (Takara, Japan), 1×Ex Taq Buffer (Mg2 + plus), 200 µM dNTP mixture, and 20 pmol of each primer. The thermal cycling parameters were 98 °C for 10 s, 55 °C for 30 s, and 72 °C for 40 s (E6)/90 sec (L1), with a final extension at 72 °C for 7 min. PCR amplicons were separated on 1.2% agarose gels and visualized by YeaRed nucleic acid gel stain under UV transillumination. A reaction mixture without template DNA was included as a negative control in each set of PCRs. After purification, the DNA amplicons were sequenced using an ABI 3730xl DNA Analyzer. All the samples were subjected to repeated PCR and sequencing from both directions to exclude PCR artefacts. Table 1. E6, E7, L1, and L2 gene sequence primers for HPV31, 33 HPV type Gene Sequence primers Primer positions * PCR product size HPV31 E6 5’→3’ GTGAACCGAAAACGGTTGGTATATAAAGC 3’ →5’ TTGCCTCAGGTTGCAAATCTAACAC F: nt 48–77 R: nt 617 − 593 ~ 570 bp E7 5’→3’ ATTTGGATAAAAAGAAACGATTCCACAACATAGG 3’ →5’ ATGTTGTCCCCTGTCTGTCTGT F: nt 460–493 R: nt 956 − 935 ~ 500 bp L1 5’→3’ GCACCCTAGTTATTATATGTTAAAAC 3’ →5’ CATACATATACCATATATAACACACAA F: nt 5493–5518 R: nt 7159 − 7133 ~ 1660 bp L2 5’→3’ TGTGTATACTGTTGTTTGTATTGGTA 3’ →5’ CGTGATAATATATGTTGGTTCGTGTT F: nt 4081–4106 R: nt 5660 − 5635 ~ 1580 bp HPV33 E6 5’→3’ TAGGGTGTAACCGAAAGCGG 3’ →5’CCTTTAACGTTGGCTTGTGTCC F: nt 30–49 R: nt 600 − 579 ~ 570 bp E7 5’→3’ TTTCATAATATTTCGGGTCGTTGGG 3’ →5’ TTCTTCTCTCTATGACTGCTTCTAC F: nt 481–505 R: nt 960 − 936 ~ 480 bp L1 5’→3’ ACACCATTGTTGTAGACGGTGC 3’ →5’ CAACACAAAGTACATAGACAGAACAA F: nt 5501–5522 R: nt 7149 − 7124 ~ 1650 bp L2 5’→3’ TGCACATGGTGGTGTTTTAACATT 3’ →5’ GGTACAGGAGGCAGGTACAC F: nt 4115–4138 R: nt 5643 − 5624 ~ 1520 bp Open in a new tab *All positions are referenced to the standard genomic sequences (HPV31: GenBank J04353 ; HPV33: GenBank M12732 ) Phylogenetic analysis A neighbour-joining phylogenetic tree based on the HPV31/33 E6 and L1 genes was constructed using MEGA software (version 11.0) with the Kimura two-parameter model. The number of bootstrap replications was set to 1000. For phylogenetic branch construction, the following reference sequences from the NCBI GenBank database were included: HPV31 ( J04353 -A1, HQ537675 -A2, HQ537676 -B1, HQ537680 -B2, HQ537682 -C1, HQ537684 -C2, HQ537685 -C3) and HPV33 ( M12732 -A1, HQ537698 -A2, EU918766 -A3, HQ537705 -B, KF436865 -C). Tree topology and evolutionary distances were calculated using the neighbour‒joining method with 1000× bootstrap replicates. Variant identification and homology modelling All the obtained sequences were aligned with the reference sequences by the ClustalW and MUSCLE methods, and pairwise distances were calculated via the bootstrap method to consolidate the items with the same sequence and estimate the p-distance (bootstrap 1000). The pairwise comparisons were visualized by Microsoft Excel. The nucleotide sequences were translated into amino acid sequences using MEGA software to determine amino acid changes caused by nucleotide changes. We carried out homologous modelling of the protein structures of the HPV31, 33 E6 and HPV33 L1 proteins and compared the protein structures of the mutant and A1 sublineages. HPV31 and HPV33 E6 variant residues were mapped to the surface of the crystal structure of the full-length HPV31 E6 oncoprotein in complex with the LXXLL peptide of the E6AP ubiquitin ligase (PDB accession number: 6SLM). HPV33 L1 variant residues were mapped to the surface of the crystal structure of the HPV58/33 chimeric L1 pentamer (PDB accession number: 6IGD). Structural analysis was performed by the Swiss PDB viewer (v4.1.0; Deep View). Selective pressure analysis Natural selection manifests through distinct modes—diversifying, directional, or purifying. To enhance the detection of genomic sites under diversifying (positive) and purifying (negative) selection in HPV31 and HPV33, we applied an integrated evolutionary analysis framework comprising single likelihood ancestor counting (SLAC), fixed effects likelihood (FEL), a mixed effects model of evolution (MEME), and fast unbiased Bayesian approximation (FUBAR). These methods infer site-specific synonymous (dS) and nonsynonymous (dN) substitution rates using maximum likelihood (SLAC, FEL, MEME) or Bayesian approaches (FUBAR) on the basis of coding sequence alignments and phylogenies. SLAC, FEL, and FUBAR identified sites under pervasive diversifying or purifying selection, whereas MEME detected sites experiencing both pervasive and episodic diversifying selection. All analyses were conducted via the Datamonkey 2.0 adaptive evolution server. Divergence time estimates We estimated the divergence times of major lineages using BEAST v2.5.0. To determine the time to the most recent common ancestor (TMRCA) of prevalent variants in eastern China, Bayesian Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) phylogenetic analysis was performed on the collected sequences. The BEAST analysis employed an uncorrelated log-normal relaxed clock model with a Yule tree prior, running for 100 million (1 × 10 8 ) generations and sampling trees every 1,000 generations. Initial substitution rates were estimated with TempEst v1.5.3 to inform tree priors. Each MCMC chain was executed for 30 million iterations. Convergence was assessed through Tracer v1.7.2, ensuring effective sample size (ESS) values > 200. A maximum clade credibility (MCC) tree was generated with TreeAnnotator v2.6.2 after discarding the first 10% of the samples as burn-in. The time-scaled phylogenetic data were visualized in FigTree v1.4.4. According to other studies, HPV16 diverged approximately 618.5 thousand years ago (kya) (95% HPD: 331.5-996.1 kya), which we used as a calibration point for estimating the divergence times of HPV31 and HPV33 [ 14 ]. Using the feline papillomavirus as an external calibration point, the substitution rate was estimated at 1.84 × 10 -8 substitutions per site per year [ 15 ]. We also implemented models incorporating time-dependent substitution rates for each open reading frame (ORF): L1 ORF at 1.84 × 10 -8 (95% CI: 1.27 × 10 -8 to 2.35 × 10 -8 ), L2 ORF at 2.13 × 10 -8 (95% CI: 1.46 × 10 -8 to 2.76 × 10 -8 ), E6 ORF at 2.39 × 10 -8 (95% CI: 1.70 × 10 -8 to 3.26 × 10 -8 ), and E7 ORF at 1.44 × 10 -8 (95% CI: 0.97 × 10 -8 to 2.00 × 10 -8 ). The MCMC analysis was run for 10,000,000 steps. On the basis of previous research, we set the evolutionary rate for the human papillomavirus coding genome at 1.95 × 10 -8 (95% CI: 1.32 × 10 -8 to 2.47 × 10 -8 ) nucleotide substitutions per site per year. Statistical analysis The demographic characteristics are presented as frequencies and percentages. Relationships between proportions were assessed using Pearson’s chi-square test or Fisher’s exact test, as appropriate. All analyses were performed using SPSS (version 20.0), with statistical significance defined as p < 0.05. Results Characteristics of the population in Eastern China In our study, a total of 94 samples were collected: 36 were HPV31-positive (either single infections or coinfections with other types), 58 were HPV33-positive (also either single infections or coinfections with other types), and 2 samples were coinfected with both HPV31 and HPV33. The demographic characteristics and clinical features of the patients are summarized in Table 2 . As shown in Table 2 , there was no significant difference ( p > 0.05) in the distribution of these two HPV types with respect to demographic characteristics. Table 2. Patient demographic characteristics and clinical features ( n = 94) Characteristics Frequency (percent) HPV31 infection HPV33 infection χ 2 value OR (95% CI) P value Age (years) 18–30 25(26.6) 8 17 1.621 Ref. 0.445 31–60 47(50) 21 26 0.99(0.29, 3.39) > 60 22(23.4) 7 15 0.58(0.20, 1.68) Birthplace Shanghai 37(39.3) 14 23 2.118 Ref. 0.101 Jiangsu province 15(16.0) 5 10 1.41(0.51, 3.40) Anhui province 9(9.6) 3 6 1.71(0.46, 6.43) Jiangxi province 6(6.4) 1 5 1.71(0.35,