topology and evolutionary distances were calculated using the neighbour‒joining method. The number of bootstrap replications was set at 1000(c) The reference sequences of HPV31 and 33 were used to construct phylogenetic branches Analysis of genetic variability and mutation sites All sequences were submitted to the GenBank database, and accession numbers were obtained. Compared with reference sequences, 108 nucleotide variations were identified in HPV31, including 14 in the E6 gene (6 nonsynonymous substitutions), 6 in the E7 gene (4 nonsynonymous substitutions), 42 in the L1 gene (13 nonsynonymous substitutions), and 46 in the L2 gene (25 nonsynonymous substitutions). Additionally, 126 nucleotide variations were detected in HPV33, comprising 16 in the E6 gene (10 nonsynonymous substitutions), 40 in the E7 gene (28 nonsynonymous substitutions), 34 in the L1 gene (13 nonsynonymous substitutions), and 36 in the L2 gene (17 nonsynonymous substitutions). The most frequent substitutions in these genes included HPV31 E6: C286T (H60Y), C520T (A138V); HPV31 E7: G695A (E46K), A743G (K62E); HPV31 L1: C6367T (V272V), C6817A (V422V); HPV31 L2: T4948A (S260T), T4950G (S260S), C4999T (L277L); HPV33 E6: A213C (K35N), A273G (R55R), A364C (N86H), A387C (K93N), A446G (Q113R); HPV33 E7: A862T (Q97L); HPV33 L1: G7064A (S491S); and HPV33 L2: C5222T (V338V). Summaries of nucleotide and amino acid variations across the E6, E7, L1, and L2 gene fragments of HPV31 and HPV33 are shown in Figs. 2 and 3 , respectively. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Genetic variability of the nucleotide sequences of the HPV31 E6 ( A ), E7 ( B ), L1 ( C ), and L2 ( D ) genes(a) Numbering refers to the first nucleotide of the HPV31 prototype reference sequence (GenBank: J04353 )(b) Each row indicates the isolate identification and the nucleotide sequence alignment compared to the reference(c) Novel nucleotide substitutions are highlighted in grey Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Genetic variability of the nucleotide sequences of the HPV33 E6 ( A ), E7 ( B ), L1 ( C ), and L2 ( D ) genes(a)Numbering refers to the first nucleotide of the HPV33 prototype reference sequence (GenBank: M12732 ) (b)Each row indicates the isolate identification and the nucleotide sequence alignment compared to the reference (c)Novel nucleotide substitutions are highlighted in grey According to the homology modelling in Fig. 4 , iterative threading assembly optimization (I-TASSER) was used to determine the most likely three-dimensional model of protein structure; PDB viewer software was used to thread the protein primary sequence onto the 3D template, and the mutant and unmutated protein structures were aligned in combination with the B-factor [ 16 , 17 ]. The amino acid substitution in our clinical variants did not change the structure of the HPV L1 or E6 protein. According to the B factor staining results, the mutant protein and A1 sublineage protein maintained strong consistency. This finding suggests that the increase in pathogenicity or infectivity of the mutant is not mediated by a change in protein structure. Because the structures of the E7 and L2 proteins are not clear, we cannot use homologous modelling to simulate the structures of these two proteins. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Protein structure of HPV33 variants ( a )Secondary structure prediction of the E7 protein of the HPV33 A1 sublineage and clinical variant. (PSIPRED, http://bioinf.cs.ucl.ac.uk/ ) ( b )Homology modelling of the E7 protein tertiary structure of the HPV33 A1 sublineage and clinical variant. The white line refers to the A1 sublineage, but the mutant is coloured according to the B factor (c)Iterative threading assembly optimization (I-TASSER) was used to determine the most likely three-dimensional model of protein structure; PDB viewer software was used to thread the protein primary sequence onto the 3D template, and the mutant and unmutated protein structures were aligned in combination with the B-factor. If the position of the atom in the measured model changes little, it is displayed in dark blue, which represents the strong consistency between the two structures. Red indicates amino acid substitution Analysis of genetic variability and correlation with cervical lesions We further analysed the correlations between genetic variation and pathological results, persistent infection, and multiple infections in the four genes of HPV31, 33. The results of our study revealed no correlation between genetic variation and persistent/multiple infections in the four genes of HPV31 and HPV33 ( p > 0.05). For HPV33, the nonsynonymous A862T (Q97L) mutation in the E7 gene was correlated with cervical lesions (χ 2 = 4.441, p < 0.05). The presence of synonymous G7064A (S491S) was significantly correlated with cervical lesions (χ 2 = 5.021, p < 0.05) (Table 3 ). To further explore the correlation between virus mutations and patients’ clinical characteristics, we performed a χ 2 test. We found that there were no associations between mutation and clinical characteristics, including age; native place; marital status; fertility history; history of smoking; HPV vaccination status; Age of first sexual contact; number of sexual partners; or method of contraception. Table 3. Correlation between genetic variability and cervical lesions of HPV33 Variability < LSIL a ≥LSIL b χ 2 value P OR (95% CI) E7 Gene A862T (Q97L) Yes 1 9 4.441 0.035 * 4.50(0.52, 38.69) No 22 26 Ref. L1 Gene G7064A (S491S) Yes 11 7 5.021 0.025 * 0.17(0.05, 0.58) No 12 28 Ref. Open in a new tab * p < 0.05. a Inflammation; b LSIL, HSIL, and cancer. Variations with more than ten incidences were included. Analysis of positive selection sites in critical mutations To determine whether positive selection has been a force in the evolution of HPV31 and HPV33 variants, nonsynonymous/synonymous rate ratios (dN/dS) were estimated. We used a combination of different evolutionary analysis methods, including: SLAC, FEL, FUBAR, and MEME. As shown in Table 4 , eleven sites were under positive selection, including HPV31 E7-23, HPV31 L1-212, HPV31 L2-14 and 269, HPV33 E6-93, HPV33 E7-45, 93 and 97, and HPV33 L2-131 and 450. One positive (diversifying) selection at position 97 in the HPV33 E7 gene was identified via both the “FEL” and “FUBAR” methods. This suggests that there is extremely strong evidence for positive selection. In addition, four codon positions and six codon positions were found to be positively selected in HPV31 and HPV33, respectively, using at least one method. Table 4. Positively selected sites in HPV31, 33 identified by four different methods Gene Codon Models SLAC FEL MEME FUBAR dN-dS P dN-dS P ω P dN-dS Posterior HPV31E6 - - - - - - - - HPV31E7 23 - - - - - - 30.575 0.963 * HPV31L1 212 - - - - - - 19.423 0.901 * HPV31L2 14 - - - - - - 24.420 0.903 * 269 - - - - - - 26.061 0.918 * HPV33E6 93 - - - - - - 17.961 0.910 * HPV33E7 45 - - - - 58.39 0.04 * - - 93 - - - - 30.47 0.04 * - - 97 - - 13.525 0.0798 * - - 25.708 0.979 * HPV33L1 - - - - - - - - HPV33L2 77 - - - - - - 28.750 0.949 * 131 - - - - - - 23.300 0.916 * 450 - - - - 8314.31 0.04 * - - Open in a new tab *SLAC: P ≤ 0.1; FEL: P ≤ 0.1; MEME: P ≤ 0.1; FUBAR: Posterior value > 0.9. The first number for every method is an appropriately scaled dN-dS to enable direct comparison. When a test is significant, the corresponding cell entry is presented in bold Estimation of the divergence time of common ancestors of HPV31, 33 To calculate the approximate divergence times of the HPV31 and HPV33 variant lineages from their MRCAs, a Bayesian MCMC method was employed. Variants representing the main lineage were selected for analyses based on nucleotide sequence alignments of L1 ORF. As shown in Figs. 5 and 6 ., HPV31 and HPV33 lineages diverged from their common ancestors within the last million years (0.85 Mya and 0.95 Mya), which corresponds to a period of time when several Homo species, including H. sapiens , diverged and migrated across the globe. Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Phylogenetic analysis and molecular dating of HPV31 variants(a)The evolutionary history of the HPV31 variants was inferred using the Bayesian method Timeline: the X axis indicates one million years ago (b)A lineages are highlighted in purple, B lineages are highlighted in pink, and C lineages are highlighted in green Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Phylogenetic analysis and molecular dating of HPV33 variants(a)The evolutionary history of the HPV33 variants was inferred using the Bayesian method Timeline: the X axis indicates one million years ago (b)A lineages are highlighted in purple, B lineages are marked in pink, and C lineages are marked in green Discussion Analysis of HPV DNA sequences provides important information for elucidating the implications of sequence variation on biological and pathological properties, as well as understanding viral evolution and taxonomy [ 18 ]. The relatively limited data available make variant analysis of HPV31 and HPV33 particularly valuable in China. In this study, we identified three lineages of HPV31 and one lineage of HPV33 in Eastern China. Significantly, a nonsynonymous substitution (Q97L) in the HPV33 E7 gene was found to be associated with cervical lesion grade and under positive selection pressure. This evidence suggests that the mutant variant gains adaptive advantages and may evolve into a prevalent pathogenic variant in the future [ 19 ]. Our study revealed that lineage C (69.6%) of HPV31 was the most common variant in Eastern China, followed by lineage A (26.1%) and lineage B (4.3%). The distribution of these three lineages in our study was similar to that reported in a survey from North America [ 20 ]. In contrast, a study conducted in northern China reported a different distribution, identifying lineage A as the most common, followed by lineages C and B [ 21 ]. Additionally, all the HPV33 variants we collected belonged to lineage A, with no lineage B or C detected. This finding is consistent with a study in Serbia, where all HPV33 isolates clustered within lineage A. However, another study identified lineages B and C in Southwest China. Collectively, this evidence suggests geographic variation and potential ethnic differences. More importantly, given that the distribution in Eastern China resembles that in North America but differs from that in other regions within China, differences in the distribution of HPV variants should not be attributed solely to national or racial boundaries. We further explored genetic variation sites in HPV31 and HPV33 in Eastern China by analysing the E6, E7, L1, and L2 gene sequences. Compared with the reference sequences, a total of 108 nucleotide variations were identified in HPV31, and 126 were identified in HPV33. The nine most prevalent nucleotide substitutions in HPV31 were C286T, C520T, G695A, A743G, C6367T, C6817A, T4948A, T4950G, and C4999T. G695A and A743G were also identified in other studies in China: notably, A743G in the E7 gene of HPV31 was the most common nonsynonymous mutation across all the samples. Additionally, the eight most prevalent nucleotide substitutions in HPV33 were A213C, A273G, A364C, A387C, A446G, A862T, G7064A, and C5222T. Our findings for HPV33 coincide with those of a previous study by Chen et al. [ 21 ], which detected A213C, A273G, A364C, A387C, A446G, and A862T. Among all nucleotide variations, forty-eight nonsynonymous sites were detected in HPV31, and sixty-eight were detected in HPV33. Many strategies involving prophylactic vaccination against HPV have been implemented to decrease the prevalence of cancer and cervical intraepithelial neoplasia. Currently, the majority of available vaccines are designed on the basis of the major HPV capsid protein L1, which contains specific epitopes recognized by the immune system [ 22 ]. Genetic variations within the HPV L1 gene can modify epitopes that are implicated in HPV recognition by L1 antibodies, which may lead to inefficiency in prophylactic vaccination [ 23 ]. It is important to continue investigating vaccine safety and effectiveness to address this issue and reduce the incidence of HPV-related cancers. Furthermore, the study of genetic variations within the HPV L1 gene and its evolution within populations has a very important impact on long-term vaccination efficacy [ 24 ]. This research can provide more perspectives for the development of second-generation vaccines. On the basis of these results, we further analysed the relationships between genetic variation sites and the clinical characteristics of patients (including cervical lesions, persistent infection, and multiple infections). Our analysis revealed specific mutations in the E7 and L1 genes that were significantly associated with cervical lesions ( p < 0.05). However, no statistical evidence has supported a direct link between these mutations and lesion severity grading, suggesting that their role may be confined to early oncogenic processes. Although several previous studies have recognized the relationship between HPV genetic variability and increased pathogenicity or carcinogenicity [ 25 – 27 ], the specific mutation sites and mechanisms underlying this enhanced pathogenicity remain unclear. To address this gap, we aimed to preliminarily explore the potential link between protein structural alterations caused by mutations and increased cervical lesion severity through protein secondary structure prediction and homology modelling. However, no significant secondary or tertiary structural changes were observed in the Q97L mutant HPV33 E7 protein. Given the correlation identified between synonymous mutations in HPV33 L1 and cervical lesions, the observed association might not be mediated by structural changes in viral proteins. Instead, transcriptomic alterations induced by nucleotide mutations, particularly those involving regulatory noncoding RNAs, could play a role in this process. Nevertheless, further investigations are needed to validate this hypothesis. Additionally, no significant associations were found between HPV31/33 genetic variability and persistent infection or coinfection in this study. To determine whether positive selection has shaped the evolution of the HPV31 and HPV33 variants, we estimated nonsynonymous/synonymous rate ratios (dN/dS). Eleven codons under positive selection were identified using four different methods. All positively selected sites corresponded to common nonsynonymous mutations, and an observational association was noted where nearly all (89.4%) of these sites were found in samples with persistent infection lasting over one year. However, due to the small number of positively selected sites ( n = 11) and limited sample size of persistent infection cases, this association has not been statistically validated. These findings suggest that positively selected HPV31 and HPV33 variants may be widespread and potentially exhibit characteristics of long-term persistence, though further large-scale cohort studies are needed to confirm these traits and their link to transmission efficiency. Interestingly, the previously mentioned nonsynonymous mutation, Q97L, was identified by both the FEL and FUBAR methods as being under positive selection pressure. The proportion of the Q97L mutation in the “LSIL and above” group was significantly greater than that in the normal group (25.7% vs. 4.3%; p < 0.05). Although positive selection pressure at this site has been briefly reported previously [ 28 , 29 ], our study provides more detailed information on its biological characteristics. The combination of adaptive advantages and high pathogenicity suggests that this mutant variant will play an important role in future research. Divergence time estimation of the most recent common ancestors suggested that HPV31 and HPV33 expanded relatively recently, with their estimated timelines (0.85–0.95 Mya) showing temporal overlap with the period of H. sapiens divergence and subsequent population growth—though this alignment does not imply direct causal linkage. While surveys from Ecuador indicate that HPV58 diverged approximately 0.84 million years ago (Mya) and that HPV16 diverged approximately 0.99 Mya [ 30 ], placing HPV31/33 divergence intermediate between these two. Additionally, sublineage divergence of HPV31 and HPV33 predates the expansion of modern humans out of Africa, a pattern consistent with estimates from Marin et al. [ 31 ]. To the best of our knowledge, this study provides the first time frame for this observation. The relatively slow population mobility in western China may preserve ancient viral strains with slower evolutionary rates, potentially offering insights relevant to human migration or viral evolution. Crucially, our research highlights the importance of the HPV33 E7 Q97L mutation and suggests that other HPV genotypes may harbour highly pathogenic, positively selected mutation sites warranting further investigation. Our study has several strengths. First, the 276 full-length gene sequences obtained significantly expand the publicly available HPV31 and HPV33 E6, E7, L1, and L2 sequences in GenBank, providing valuable genomic insights. Second, eleven sites were under positive selection in this study, which may provide new insights into next-generation vaccine research in eastern China. However, there are also several limitations. (1) The sample size is relatively small, which may limit the detection of rare variants and reduce the statistical power of association analyses, especially for less prevalent lineages. (2) All samples were collected from Eastern China, so the findings may not be representative of HPV31/33 variants in other regions of China or globally.(3) Although we identified genetic variations associated with cervical lesions and positively selected sites, we did not perform functional assays to validate their biological roles, which hinders a deeper understanding of their mechanistic significance. Conclusion In our study, the distribution of HPV31/33 variants in Eastern China was investigated. We identified a clinically significant mutation (Q97L) in the HPV33 E7 gene that is under positive selection pressure and strongly associated with cervical lesions. Preliminary evidence further suggests that the increased oncogenic potential of this mutant may not involve structural alterations in the encoded protein. Phylogenetic analysis indicated that the diversification of HPV31 and HPV33 lineages showed a temporal alignment with the emergence of H. sapiens, with accelerated expansion coinciding with subsequent human population growth—though this temporal correlation does not imply a direct causal relationship. This work provides valuable insights into the clinical implications of genetic variations in HPV31 and HPV33. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (283KB, docx) Supplementary Material 2. (16.9KB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors thank the Shanghai Key Laboratory of Female Reproductive Endocrine Related Diseases. Authors’ contributions N.H, C.X. and Y.L. conceived the study, critically revised the manuscript and checked all data and analyses. Y.W, W.Q. and Q.Z participated in study design, execution, analysis planning, manuscript drafting and editing. Y. G, Y.W, F.C, J.M, L.L, T.B, Z.W collected data and performed statistical analysis. All authors reviewed the manuscript. Funding This work was supported by the following funding: (1) National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grants 82272970 and 82472628); (2) Shanghai Public Health System Construction Three-year Action Plan (2023–2025)-Academic Leader Program (GWVI-11.2-XD13); (3) Fifth Round of Disciplinary Development Program from QingPu District Health Commission: Key Discipline Construction (GF2023-3, LX2023-2); Talent Doctor Cultivation (MY2023-3, MY2023-5); and (4) National Key Research and Development Program of China (2022YFC2407406). Data availability The datasets generated and analysed during the current study are available in the GenBank, NCBI repository, [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/genbank/,accession number: OP900638 ~ OP900915 ]. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate All procedures were performed in compliance with relevant laws and institutional guidelines and were approved by the Ethical Committee of Gynecology and Obstetrics Hospital of Fudan University (ID: 2022 − 132). Written informed consent was obtained from all individuals. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. The authors declare that there are no conflicts of interest. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Yan Wang, Wenjie Qu and Qi Zhou contributed equally to this work. Contributor Information Na He, Email: nhe@fudan.edu.cn. Yanyun Li, Email: liyanyun@fudan.edu.cn. Congjian Xu, Email: xucongjian@fudan.edu.cn. References 1. Wei F, Georges D, Man I, et al. Causal attribution of human papillomavirus genotypes to invasive cervical cancer worldwide: a systematic analysis of the global literature. Lancet. 2024;404(4355):435–44. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. World Health Organization. Human papillomavirus (HPV) and cervical cancer. 2020. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/human-papillomavirus-(hpv)-and-cervical-cancer . Accessed 20 May 2024. 3. Tsakogiannis D, Nikolaidis M, Zagouri F, et al. Mutation profile of HPV16 L1 and L2 genes in different geographic areas. Viruses. 2023;15(1):141. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Muñoz N, Bosch FX, de Sanjosé S, et al. Epidemiological classification of human papillomavirus types associated with cervical cancer. N Engl J Med. 2003;348:518–27. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Schiller JT, Müller M. Next generation prophylactic human papillomavirus vaccines. Lancet Oncol. 2015;16(5):e217-25. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Yuan 