INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1594,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12465011','Alcohol Craving and Cue Exposure in Real Time: A Pilot EMA-Based Personalized Feedback Intervention for Young Adults.','Fairlie AM, Lee CM, Delawalla MLM, Ramirez JJ.','2025','situationally momentary capitalizing emas pfis momentary blackouts hingson hingson momentary shiffman tiffany berridge pfis cronce larimer cronce riper lipsey pfis pfis misperceptions overestimating pfis pfis pueyo garrigues pfis emas ecologically kiluk moos resonant colby dager 2015a 2015b ecologically curtin escalations dager pfis pfis fluctuate bolger laurenceau shiffman validly pfis drank drank psychoeducational mug nct05373706 cellphone aoc 10am 11am 9am 12pm customizable 3pm 6pm 9pm hyperlink buttons hyperlink downloadable hyperlink graupensperger drank yesterday woke fairlie fairlie preoccupied capitalizing psychoeducation companions moods surfing clickable surfing moods lonely iccs 3pm 6pm 9pm drank preoccupied colby dager 2015a 2015b surfing populate pfis r34aa027302 fairlie niaaa delawalla niaaa t32aa007455 larimer niaaa bolger laurenceau laj demartini addbeh kiluk adb0000311 tiffany colby rohsenow gwaltney gulliver sirota addbeh cronce larimer cronce bittinger kilmer curtin colby rohsenow jsa dager khadka jiantonio dager pulido jiantonio pearlson e161 e171 fairlie graupensperger acer fairlie graupensperger duckworth adb0000738 graupensperger fairlie addbeh hingson zha weitzman jsads hingson zha larimer rhew acer aghamoosa meyerhoff tomko squeglia neurometabolite kohen cofresi bartholow piasecki pha0000540 rhew fairlie cronce larimer cadigan expectancies jsad leeman corbin fromme litt cooney alcoholics mackillop lisman helmuth abraha hitchcock bidwell multisubstance followback e01486 brb3 leffingwell claborn a0031174 seavey mcnulty momentary a0034127 moos oslin slaymaker colleran blow miech monitoringthefuture mcelrath polce schulenberg addbeh kornreich maurage letesson verbanck campanella pueyo garrigues parr lavilla gracia alfaro esandi larramendi canga armayor colwill 2015a pha0000018 colwill 2015b underage adb0000028 s00213 mackillop ch14 riper straten keuken schippers cuijpers curbing berridge schacht myrick shiffman momentary a0017074 squeglia tapert jacobus meloy rohlfing pfefferbaum momentary clinpsy lipsey jsat tapert sher piasecki adb0000696 tellegen panas hingson witteman tarvainen s00213','Abstract Objective: Alcohol cues vary situationally across contexts and have been recognized as a factor that contributes to craving and alcohol use. We describe a brief web-based personalized feedback intervention (PFI) that summarizes ecological momentary assessment (EMA) data tested in a pilot study. The PFI highlighted young adults’ desire to drink and the presence/absence of alcohol cues, capitalizing on variability found in the natural environment across 17 days. We examined the extent to which the EMA data collected in the pilot study were optimal for use in the PFI and participants’ evaluations of the PFI. Method: Eligibility criteria included being age 18-24 years and report 1+ heavy episodic drinking occasion in last month. Data were collected from 51 participants (mean age = 21.69 years (SD=1.86); 54.9% female, 56.9% non-Hispanic white). Data were from a baseline survey, 17 days of EMAs (4x/day), and evaluation survey. Results: High EMA survey retention was obtained (85.8% completion). The intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) for desire to drink showed that 22% of the variability was between-persons and 78% was within-person. Cues of interest were adequately reported (e.g., physical cues reported on 23.2% of PM surveys, being around people with whom typically drink on 31.9% of PM surveys). Participants rated the intervention favorably (mean on 8 of 14 items greater than 4.0 on scale 1-5). Conclusions: EMA data offers rich opportunities for PFIs aimed to reduce alcohol use and consequences. The Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI described here can inform future intervention research. Keywords: Personalized feedback intervention, alcohol cues, ecological momentary assessment, young adults Alcohol use and misuse peak in young adulthood. Although the prevalence of heavy drinking has declined in the last decade, 2023 estimates show that 27.2% of young adults (ages 19-30) in the United States reported heavy drinking (5+ drinks in a row) in the past two weeks ( Patrick et al., 2024 ). Alcohol use among young adults is associated with negative consequences and societal costs, including blackouts, injuries, and fatalities ( Hingson et al., 2009 , 2017 ; Patrick et al. 2020 ; White & Hingson, 2013 ). A critical area of research is the development and refinement of effective interventions to increase the magnitude of intervention effects ( Huh et al., 2015 ). This paper describes the development of a web-based personalized feedback intervention (PFI) for young adults that incorporates ecological momentary assessment (EMA), which entails surveying individuals multiple times a day to assess current states and recent behaviors ( Shiffman, 2009 ). The Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI described here uses EMA data to highlight craving (i.e., desire to drink) in relation to alcohol cues in the natural environment. This paper is intended to inform other researchers interested in using EMA data in personalized feedback. Alcohol Cue Reactivity and Craving The phenomenon of alcohol cue reactivity stems from classical conditioning theories of alcohol approach behavior (e.g., Carter & Tiffany, 1999 ; Robinson & Berridge, 1993 ) that characterize craving as a conditioned response to the presence of alcohol and associated cues acting as conditioned stimuli. Alcohol reactivity has historically been studied in lab settings by measuring subjective craving, neural and physiological activity, cognitive processes, and behavioral economic demand in response to the presence of alcohol cues (for review, see Reynolds & Monti, 2013 ). The emergence of EMA has allowed for a richer characterization of how craving is experienced in real-world conditions in response to cues and other situational factors. For example, EMA can capture moments during which young adults encounter alcohol cues in their natural environment, such as being around people with whom one regularly drinks, being in places associated with drinking, time of day, day of week, or internal cues like affective states. Highlighting how craving varies as a function of these cues may be beneficial whereby young adults can reflect on how their drinking behavior may vary based on situational factors. Personalized Feedback Interventions for Alcohol Use Traditionally PFIs are used to highlight patterns of behavior such as alcohol use and consequences and prompt an individual to reflect on their behavior ( Carey et al., 2007 ; Cronce & Larimer, 2011 ; Cronce et al., 2014 ; Miller et al., 2013 ; Riper et al., 2009 ; Tanner-Smith & Lipsey, 2015 ). Many PFIs with young adults incorporate summaries of general or typical behavior across a given timeframe (e.g., past month) and/or provide skills or protective behavioral strategies that may help reduce use or consequences. PFIs may present normative feedback to leverage misperceptions of peers’ use (i.e., overestimating peers’ alcohol use) and highlight discrepancies between one’s personal alcohol consumption in relation to peers’ alcohol use ( Miller et al., 2013 ). Web-based PFIs reduce weekly alcohol consumption among university students, presenting accessible and cost-saving alternatives to PFIs requiring face-to-face contact ( Pueyo-Garrigues et al., 2024 ). However, despite the importance of craving as a driver of young adult alcohol misuse, existing PFIs rarely incorporate craving and we know of none that utilize EMAs to measure craving as actually experienced in real-time to provide rich, ecologically valid intervention feedback. Existing forms of intervention that do target craving include cognitive behavioral therapies that include training to increase recognition of cues/triggers likely to evoke craving and to learn how to effectively cope with craving ( Carroll & Kiluk, 2017 ; Moos, 2007 ). The intervention described here is housed within a PFI framework to provide feedback to individuals to understand which real-world factors are most tied to their own craving. The incorporation of situational factors associated with craving may especially resonant with young adults and prompt reflection. Alcohol cue reactivity (and craving more generally) represents a strong candidate for inclusion in a young adult intervention in the form of EMA-based personalized feedback. Craving responses to alcohol cues have been reliably observed across studies with young adults in carefully controlled lab conditions ( Colby et al., 2004 ; Dager et al., 2013 ; Petit et al., 2012 ; Ramirez et al., 2015a , 2015b ) as well as in ecologically valid EMA studies ( Ramirez & Miranda, 2014 ). Further, studies have shown positive associations between craving and subsequent drinking, again both in lab and real-world environments ( Ramirez & Miranda, 2014 ). Additionally, the strength of cue-elicited craving responses among young adults is positively associated with drinking levels and alcohol-related problems ( Curtin et al., 2005 ; Ramirez & Miranda, 2014 ) and predicts prospective escalations in drinking and alcohol-related problems one year later ( Dager et al., 2014 ). Conceptual Framework of the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI The guiding framework of the brief, web-based Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI highlights the cue-craving-alcohol link with two primary mechanisms ( Figure 1 ). First, if individuals can identify their personalized alcohol cues in their environment, then they may later choose to avoid being in the presence of cues that elicit a strong response, thus reducing desire to drink and subsequent alcohol-related outcomes. Second, if individuals are also able to recognize their response to cues (i.e., increased desire to drink) and are provided with strategies to cope with their desire to drink, they may be less likely to experience an elevated craving response and/or act upon a craving response, potentially reducing alcohol use and consequences. The purpose of the feedback was described to participants as an opportunity to learn about cues that increase their desire to drink and become more aware of these cues in their environments. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Guiding Framework of Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI and Associated Intervention Content (Orange Boxes). The Current Paper This paper is intended to inform the efforts of other researchers seeking to incorporate rich, EMA data into PFIs. The Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI was used in a pilot study designed to assess initial feasibility and acceptability. This paper has four objectives: (1) Describe how traditional PFI content differs from the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI incorporating EMA data and articulate the advantages of using EMA data; (2) Describe decision points pertaining to key constructs underlying the intervention; (3) Examine the extent to which the EMA data collected in the pilot study met conditions to optimize the use of EMA data in personalized feedback (e.g., high survey compliance, sufficient within-person variability on key constructs); and (4) Examine the extent to which the participants in the pilot study evaluated the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI in a favorable manner. Advantages of Using EMA in Personalized Feedback Relative to Traditional PFI PFIs for young adults have not traditionally focused on cues and desire to drink as key aspects of personalization (even with data from a single survey rather than EMA). Notably, young adults may be around alcohol cues during the course of their daily lives and their desire to drink may fluctuate in response to these different cues and contexts. A key advantage of the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI is its incorporation of EMA data (collected 4x/day over 17 days) to increase the salience of the intervention content and enable young adults to see how their own experiences of desire to drink may vary in relation to context as reported at multiple times during the day. A 17-day reporting period was selected in order to capture behavior on three weekends, when drinking is likely to occur. Individuals reported on their psychological states, characteristics of current surroundings, and behaviors in or near real-time, thus increasing ecological validity ( Bolger & Laurenceau, 2013 ; Shiffman, 2009 ). Although lab-based cue reactivity protocols can personalize alcohol cues to varying degrees, EMA-based methods allow for feedback regarding self-reported desire to drink as actually experienced in response to real-world alcohol cues. Reporting of current desire to drink captures natural variability in the presence/absence of cues without being subject to recall bias or requiring that participants evaluate their “average” or “typical” desire in some fashion. Although young adults may be able to report alcohol use retrospectively, as in the case of a timeline follow-back (e.g., Martin-Willett et al., 2020 ) where individuals report behaviors for a certain time period (e.g., past month), it would not be possible to reliably and validly assess desire to drink retrospectively for the previous 17 days (and at different times during the day). The personalized information may enhance understanding/insight into motivational states and how these states vary in relation to real-world factors, increase recognition of how cues can influence desire to drink and subsequent behavior, and build skills to manage being around alcohol cues and experiencing an increased desire to drink. The content on alcohol use and consequences in the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI is more similar to what would appear in a traditional PFI. Traditionally, PFIs begin with a presentation of the participant’s recent alcohol use and consequences, as done here in the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI. However, usually alcohol use and consequences are reported in a baseline survey whereas here we presented summaries of the alcohol-related information from the daily surveys, which are less influenced by recall bias and potential inaccuracies pertaining to characterizing “typical” use over a period of time. In order to frame the feedback in the context of the participant’s recent alcohol use, we first presented summary information on alcohol use and consequences (17 assessed) reported during the 17-day EMA period ( Figure 2 ). To provide situational context and increase salience, personalized information was provided on who the participant drank with and where they drank. Psychoeducational content prompted participants to consider (a) what they could do when drinking to maximize the good feelings while also reducing the likelihood of experiencing consequences and (b) when they are more likely to experience consequences. Participants could click on a pop-up box to access a list of a protective behavioral strategies that could be used to minimize consequences. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Personalized Feedback on Alcohol Behaviors. Decision Points for Key Intervention Constructs A central aspect of the feedback was the concept of “craving” or “desire to drink.” The term “craving” is used throughout the scientific literature. We intentionally avoided the word “craving” in the feedback, because this can elicit images of severe or problematic alcohol use and be perceived as more indicative of an alcohol use disorder. In contrast to the term “craving,” “desire to drink” (at some level of intensity) is less associated with problematic use and tied more to the real-world experience of the desire to consume alcohol. A second decision point pertained to defining and assessing “cues.” When cue reactivity is examined in the laboratory, alcohol cues are typically operationalized as an alcohol beverage or another physical object like a beer mug. We defined and assessed cues more broadly (physical alcohol cues, people typically drink with, and places typically drink). We also considered time of day and anticipation of drinking later that day to be cues. It was important to present personalized feedback on various types of cues that may be experienced in the natural environment and may be salient to young adults. Methods Participants and Procedures Young adults from Washington state were recruited for the pilot randomized clinical trial ( NCT05373706 ). Eligibility criteria were: (1) be ages 18-24 years, (2) live in Washington state, (3) report drinking 2+ days/week in the last six months, (4) report 1+ heavy episodic drinking occasion (i.e., 4+/5+ drinks in a day for females/males) in the last month, (5) be open to or currently thinking about drinking less or reducing alcohol’s negative effects (but not actively seeking treatment), (6) have a schedule allowing for daily survey participation, (7) be willing to attend a zoom training session, and (8) have a cellphone and be willing to receive texts for daily surveys. A total of 215 individuals met initial eligibility and were advanced to the next phase (i.e., phone screen for identity verification), and 106 participants completed the baseline survey and advanced to the online training session. A total of 99 participants completed the training and were enrolled in the pilot trial. Data analyzed here come from the 51 participants randomized to the intervention condition (mean age at baseline = 21.69 years (SD=1.86); 54.9% female). Regarding race and ethnicity of the analytic sample, 56.9% identified as non-Hispanic white, 19.6% non-Hispanic Asian, 9.8% Hispanic white, 5.9% more than one race, and the remaining 7.8% another race. Data reported here were collected in baseline, the EMA period, and the evaluation survey. Participants were recruited using various methods (predominantly social media/Instagram). After being identified as eligible in the online screening survey, individuals underwent phone verification (e.g., confirm name) with study staff. Upon verification, participants completed the online baseline survey and scheduled an online training session conducted via Zoom for ID verification and review of study procedures. Participants whose IDs were verified during the training session were considered fully enrolled and randomized to one of two conditions using stratified randomization: EMA-based Cue Reactivity PFI or EMA-based Assessment Only Control (AOC). All participants completed a 17-day EMA period with surveys 4x/day. The EMA period always began on the first Friday after the training session so that the 17 consecutive days included three weekends. Participants received surveys 4x/day; the AM survey was available in a 3-hour window that participants could choose to best fit their schedule (7-10am, 8-11am, or 9am-12pm), and the three PM surveys were available in set 2-hour windows that were not customizable (1-3pm, 4-6pm, and 7-9pm). Email and text invitations and reminders were sent for every EMA survey. All participants completed online follow-up assessments at 2-weeks and 3-months post-intervention (or matched to this time for the control condition). Participants earned up to $180 in the form of electronic gift cards. On the last day of the EMA period (Day 17), participants randomized to receive the PFI received an email with a hyperlink to their online personalized feedback and a text notification to check their email. The personalized feedback had 13 pages of varying lengths, designed to be easily viewed in a web browser and readable on a smartphone or another internet-enabled device. Navigation buttons allowed participants to go forward and backward from page-to-page. At the end of the feedback, participants were provided with the hyperlink to a downloadable pdf of the “tip sheet” containing protective behavioral strategies and project contact information. Participants could revisit the feedback by returning to the hyperlink in the email. Selection of Key EMA Measures Providing Data for Personalized Feedback Desire to drink. In all EMA surveys, desire to drink was assessed for “right now.” Participants were asked: “How strong is your desire to drink alcohol?” on a scale from “0 = No desire at all” to “10 = Strong desire” (adapted from Graupensperger et al., 2022 ). Presence/absence of alcohol cues. Alcohol cues were assessed in the three PM surveys. Participants were asked “Are you currently in the presence of any alcohol cues? Alcohol cues are things you can see, touch, hear, or smell and that you associate with alcohol (e.g., bottle or can of beer, social media post with alcohol).” Responses were coded “0 = No” and “1 = Yes.” Two separate items (each coded “0 = No” and “1 = Yes”) asked “Are you with people with whom you typically drink alcohol?” and “Are you in a place where you typically drink alcohol?” Alcohol use. AM surveys asked whether or not participants drank alcohol “yesterday (i.e., from the time you woke up until the time you went to sleep)” ( Fairlie et al., 2023 ). A standard drink chart was provided. Participants reported how many total drinks they had (“1 = 1 drink” to “25 = 25 or more drinks”). Drinking intentions. PM surveys assessed drinking intentions, “Thinking about the rest of today, do you think you will drink alcohol?” with responses “0 = No” and “1 = Yes” (adapted from Fairlie et al., 2021 ). Evaluation Survey At the end of the intervention feedback, participants received the online evaluation survey. Fourteen items assessed participants’ satisfaction with the intervention (e.g., “I liked the personalized information,” “I would recommend this experience to a friend”). All items appear in Table 1 . Response options ranged from “1 = Strongly Disagree” to “5 = Strongly Agree.” Table 1. Results of the evaluation survey for the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI. Evaluation Item M (SD) Range I found the personalized information to be clear and easy to follow. 4.37 (0.67) 2-5 Overall, I found the experience to be favorable. 4.29 (0.69) 2-5 I value my experience as a Project ACE participant. 4.26 (0.69) 3-5 I liked the personalized information. 4.18 (0.61) 3-5 I would recommend this experience to a friend. 4.16 (0.89) 2-5 I found the personalized information to be relevant. 4.11 (0.83) 1-5 I was attentive when viewing the personalized information. 4.03 (0.91) 2-5 I liked the message presented in the personalized information. 4.00 (0.77) 2-5 I found the personalized information to be engaging. 3.95 (0.93) 1-5 The personalized information made me think about my drinking behavior. 3.89 (0.76) 2-5 I found the personalized information accurate. 3.74 (0.76) 1-5 I found the personalized information to be helpful for my drinking behavior. 3.68 (1.07) 1-5 I found the personalized information surprising. 2.63 (1.10) 1-5 I was preoccupied when viewing the personalized information. 2.39 (1.15) 1-5 Open in a new tab Note . 38 of 51 participants completed the evaluation survey that appeared at the end of the PFI. Response options ranged from “1 = Strongly Disagree” to “5 = Strongly Agree.” A Closer Look at the Personalized Content on Desire to Drink Throughout the feedback development, points of decision-making involved determining how to summarize the large quantity of data (surveys up to 4x per day for 17 days), capitalizing on the natural variability with EMA data while also highlighting the key intervention messages based on the guiding framework. A substantial component of the feedback was related to the single item assessing desire to drink. As part of the psychoeducation content, the feedback described that higher desire to drink can increase alcohol use, which can lead to consequences, and therefore, reducing desire to drink may ultimately reduce consequences. Personalized information summarized multiple aspects of the participant’s desire to drink (e.g., average, highest, lowest) ( Figure 3 ). For example, participants were presented with the number of days they reported any desire to drink (i.e., non-zero responses from AM and PM surveys). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Personalized Feedback Summarizing Desire to Drink. EMA offered the unique ability to present the following situational information that corresponded with when participant’s reported their highest desire to drink across the EMA period: (1) alcohol cues present (e.g., bottle or can of alcohol), (2) locations (any endorsed), (3) companions/alone (any endorsed), and (4) moods experienced. This contextual information has the potential to help young adults better understand their personal cues/factors that may be related to their own drinking desire. Participants were prompted to consider how contexts and situations relate to their desire to drink. Urge surfing was described as a potentially helpful strategy to use when experiencing a strong desire to drink, with a clickable pop-up box containing Figure 4 . Figure 4. Open in a new tab Urge Surfing Graphic in Personalized Feedback. Given our focus on how desire to drink may vary across situations, we included a series of graphs that allowed for the participant to observe potential patterns in their desire to drink as it related to (1) being around alcohol cues (or not), (2) being in places where they typically drink alcohol (or not), (3) being with people with whom they typically drink alcohol (or not), and (4) anticipating drinking later that day compared to when they did not. For example, Figure 5 shows average desire to drink for the 17 days (averaged across the three PM surveys). For feedback content on mood, the five moods (e.g., social, lonely) associated with a higher desire to drink were shown. This information was intended to increase young adults’ awareness of personally relevant affective cues. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Personalized Feedback on Average Desire to Drink for Each of the 17 Days. An Examination of Whether EMA Data from the Pilot Study Were Optimal for Use in the Personalized Feedback The Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI was centered around young adults’ reports of their desire to drink and cues assessed 4x/day over 17 days, which necessitates the following conditions: (1) adequate survey completion during the EMA period, (2) sufficient within-person variability on key constructs (e.g., desire to drink cues), and (3) sufficient reports of the behaviors of interest (any desire to drink, alcohol use, and consequences). We examined the extent to which these conditions were met in this pilot trial using EMA data from the 51 intervention participants. Overall, the retention rate was high with 85.8% of the surveys completed during the EMA period, suggesting sufficient EMA data to provide content in the feedback. The intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) for desire to drink (ICC = .228 for AM surveys and .222 for PM surveys) showed that 22% of the variability was between-persons and 78% was within-person, thus high variability in desire to drink suggesting situational influences. ICCs for being around physical cues (.301), drinking with people typically drink with (.369), locations tend to drink (.348), and anticipating drinking or not (.426) all exhibited within-person variability (calculated as ICC-1.0), with more than half of the variability within-persons. Across the EMA period, participants reported any desire to drink on 57.0% of EMA surveys (21.1% of the morning surveys and 50.3% of the three PM surveys), suggesting the EMA design captured desire to drink in the three PM survey windows occurring 1-3pm, 4-6pm, and 7-9pm. Physical cues were reported on 23.2% of PM surveys, being in a place that reminded the participant of drinking on 41.3% of PM surveys, being around people with whom typically drink on 31.9% of PM surveys, and anticipating drinking later that day on 34.7% of PM surveys, together suggesting that the survey windows adequately captured occurrences of the factors of interest. The ICC was .365 for whether or not participants drank the prior day. The 17-day EMA period always started on the first Friday after the training session, so that three weekends were always assessed, and 42.9% of days were drinking days, suggesting we adequately captured drinking days. Participants’ Evaluation of the Intervention Overall, participants (n = 38) rated the PFI favorably ( Table 1 ). Eight of the 14 items had means of 4.0 or greater showing favorable evaluations on average (where “4 = Agree” and “5 = Strongly Agree”). Also in a favorable direction, participants had low agreement with the statement that they were preoccupied when viewing the feedback. Notably, the mean rating for “I found the personalized information accurate” was 3.74 (SD = 0.76) on a scale from 1-5, even though the personalized information in the feedback came from the participants own reports. Although evaluations were largely favorable, some results suggest improvements can be made so that the intervention is more engaging and further encourages young adults to reflect on their drinking behavior. Discussion By querying young adults about their experiences in their natural environment and leveraging the substantial amount of EMA data collected, this Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI sought to engage known mechanisms linking cues, desire to drink, and alcohol use ( Colby et al., 2004 ; Dager et al., 2013 ; Petit et al., 2012 ; Ramirez et al., 2015a , 2015b ; Ramirez & Miranda, 2014 ). Through identifying individuals’ in-the-moment salient alcohol cues, we aimed to provide participants with content that is most relevant for them (e.g., identifying circumstances of high desire to drink and pairing with information on urge surfing). Findings support the EMA design used in the pilot trial and the inclusion of the EMA data in the PFI, including a high survey completion rate, adequate within-person variability of key constructs, and adequate assessment of drinking over the 17-day period. Overall, participants evaluated the intervention favorably. This Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI was grounded in known mechanisms of action to decrease alcohol use and consequences via the guiding framework. Individualized feedback that participants received on alcohol cues and use behaviors was based on large amounts of data collected via EMA that reflected experiences in their daily lives. Further, although data collection was extensive, the PFI itself was brief, potentially increasing accessibility and uptake. A major limitation of PFI in general and especially when using EMA data to populate feedback is survey nonresponse, which results in missing data and could result in less informative or meaningful PFI content for some individuals ( Stone et al., 2023 ). Thus, attention needs to be paid to understanding how the displayed PFI content will be impacted by missing data and decision rules/algorithms must be in place to address this. For example, though not personalized, certain PFI content that is consistent with the theoretical framework of the intervention can be displayed when data for a prespecified number of surveys (or items) are missing. The information presented here may serve to inform the planning, design, and implementation of future brief, EMA-based PFIs. Readers interested in developing a PFI based on EMA data are encouraged to (1) develop a concise conceptual framework that delineates the purported mechanisms of action for producing the desired intervention effect and use that framework to guide intervention content; (2) consider decision points pertaining to key terminology underlying the intervention; and (3) consider whether pilot data are available to determine whether the EMA design and resulting data are optimal for use (e.g., sufficient variability in key constructs given timing of measurement). The intervention described here was tested in a small pilot trial. The EMA data collected during this pilot trial will serve a critical purpose of informing a revised version of the Alcohol Cue Reactivity PFI, and participants’ evaluations will guide intervention revisions for future testing in a large-scale randomized clinical trial. Acknowledgments Intervention development, data collection, and analyses were supported by a grant (R34AA027302; Multiple PIs: AM Fairlie and JJ Ramirez) from the National Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism (NIAAA). Dr. Delawalla’s contributions were supported by a training grant from NIAAA (T32AA007455, PI: ME Larimer). Manuscript content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of NIAAA or the National Institutes of Health. Footnotes We have no known conflict of interest to disclose. References Bolger N, & Laurenceau J-P. (2013). Intensive longitudinal methods: An introduction to diary and experience sampling research. New York, NY: Guilford. [ Google Scholar ] Carey KB, Scott-Sheldon LAJ, Carey MP, & DeMartini KS (2007). Individual-level interventions to reduce college student drinking: A meta-analytic review. Addictive Behaviors, 32(11), 2469–2494. 10.1016/j.addbeh.2007.05.004 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Carroll KM, & Kiluk BD (2017). Cognitive behavioral interventions for alcohol and drug use disorders: Through the stage model and back again. Psychology of Addictive Behaviors, 31(8), 847–861. 10.1037/adb0000311 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Carter BL, & Tiffany ST (1999). Meta-analysis of cue reactivity in addiction research. Addiction, 94, 327–340. 10.1046/j.1360-0443.1999.9433273.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Colby SM, Rohsenow DJ, Monti PM, Gwaltney CJ, Gulliver SB, Abrams DB, … Sirota AD (2004). Effects of tobacco deprivation on alcohol cue reactivity and drinking among young adults. Addictive Behaviors, 29(5), 879–892. 10.1016/j.addbeh.2004.03.002 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cronce JM, & Larimer ME (2011). Individual-focused approaches to the prevention of college student drinking. Alcohol 