Sears CS, Robinson JW, Walker LM. A comprehensive review of sexual health concerns after cancer treatment and the biopsychosocial treatment options available to female patients. Eur J Cancer Care 2018; 27: e12738. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Eldridge SM, Lancaster GA, Campbell MJ, et al. Defining feasibility and pilot studies in preparation for randomised controlled trials: development of a conceptual framework. PLoS one 2016; 11: e0150205. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Elliott S. Proof of concept research. Philos Sci 2021; 88: 258–280. [ Google Scholar ] 35. Julious SA. Sample size of 12 per group rule of thumb for a pilot study. Pharm Stat 2005; 4: 287–291. [ Google Scholar ] 36. Browne RH. On the use of a pilot sample for sample size determination. Stat Med 1995; 14: 1933–1940. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Canadian Institutes of Health Research . Strategy for patient oriented research. http://www.cihr-irsc.gc.ca/e/41204.html (accessed 20 April 2022). 38. Dolgoy N, Bernard S, Huang F, et al. Determining the destination: a co-designed chronic advanced cancer rehabilitation conceptual framework for engagement of individuals with lived experience in rehabilitation research. Res Involv Engage 2024; 10: 35. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Harris PA, Taylor R, Thielke R, et al. Research electronic data capture (REDCap)—a metadata-driven methodology and workflow process for providing translational research informatics support. J Biomed Inform 2009; 42: 377–381. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Dumoulin C, Booth J, Cacciari L, et al. Conservative management of UI and POP in adults including neurological patients. In: Cardoza ESR L, Wagg AS, Wein AJ. (eds) Incontinence. 7th ed. Bristol, UK: International Continence Society, 2023, pp.795–1038. [ Google Scholar ] 41. Frawley H, Shelly B, Morin M, et al. An international continence society (ICS) report on t