INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1602,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12460047','Clinical outcomes and prognostic factors of adjuvant radiotherapy for vulvar cancer: a Japanese Gynecologic Oncology Group nationwide survey study.','Tsuchida K, Ando K, Okonogi N, Mori M, Nishimura M, Yoshida K, Sugino N, Takekuma M, Okamoto A, Ohno T, Nishio S.','2025','adenosquamous sdjuvant gog037 disks adenosquamous adsc adenosquamous lcr lcr lcr chained vulvectomy vulvectomy vulvectomy myocutaneous vulvectomy lcr lcr lcr vulvectomy lcr lcr wpi dmf wpi vulvectomy vulvectomy wpi wpi kunos parthasarathy fons swanick mahner ncdb ignatov 1cm ncdb swanick stuckey swanick fons intracapsular woebler groinss oonk macrometastases foul saitama keio aichi kindai kitasato tsukuba niigata kurume iwate jikei tohoku juntendo marianna tokai kansai rosai shikoku hokkaido yamagata kyushu ryukyus shizuoka kure tottori saitama nagoya niigata jichi oita toho ohashi hokkaido kyushu misawa hirosaki kyushu nagasaki rissho koseikai oji gifu nagasaki nagasaki okinawa chubu sagamino kumamoto komagome aichi dokkyo saitama hiroshima yamanashi prefectural nara prefectural ibaraki prefectural tachikawa nara hyogo saga ibaraki kagoshima kobe sapporo kagoshima iizuka miyagi himeji rosai ise akita nagoya saiseikai nagasaki omihachiman kagawa kokura yokohama kyorin tottori seirei hamamatsu tomishiro kkr sapporo saiseikai hita prefectural tama ntt kanto ehime kanagawa jikei kashiwa saiseikai fukuoka tokushima gunma shinshu hyogo prefectural kenta murotani kurume editage editage keisuke tsuchida kanagawa nakao asahi yokohama kanagawa gunma showa machi maebashi gunma noriyuki okonogi juntendo hongo bunkyo mayuyo hongo bunkyo masato tokushima kuramoto tokushima kenta edobashi tsu norihiro sugino minami kogushi ube munetaka takekuma shizuoka shimonagakubo nagaizumi sunto shizuoka aikou jikei nishi shimbashi minato tatsuya gunma showa machi maebashi gunma nishio kurume asahi machi kurume fukuoka jsps kakenhi 23k14855 umin nishio shibata jcm8122081 physician_gls faul mirmow s0360 heaps montz ignatov eggemann kunos simpkins 0b013e3181b12f99 parthasarathy osann mahner jueckstock hilpert dju426 dju426 ncdb s12905 fons groenen oonk nishio murotani nakao jgog pecorelli swanick eifel stuckey schutzer rizack dizon 0b013e3182467e9f klopp moughan portelance klopp kannan parcer rishi rollins mell roeske mundt tsujii woelber eulenburg choschzick 0b013e31823eed4c oonk mhm slomovitz pjw groinss contouring oonk mhm planchamp','Abstract This study aimed to analyze the clinical outcomes and prognostic factors of postoperative adjuvant radiotherapy (RT) for vulvar cancer based on a retrospective Japanese nationwide survey. Data were collected from 108 institutions for patients diagnosed with vulvar cancer between January 2001 and December 2010. Patients with histologically confirmed squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, or adenosquamous carcinoma who underwent curative surgery and adjuvant radiotherapy were included in this study. Survival outcomes were estimated using the Kaplan–Meier method, and prognostic factors were analyzed via univariate and multivariate models. A total of 139 patients were included, with a median follow-up of 44 months (range: 3–169). The 5-year overall survival (OS) rates (95% confidence interval [CI]) for stages I, II, III, and IV were 71.8% (50.8–92.8%), 61.3% (40.1–82.5%), 58.0% (45.8–70.2%), and 47.3% (27.5–67.1%), respectively. The corresponding 5-year cause-specific survival (CSS) rates (95% CI) for stages I, II, III, and IV were 71.8% (50.8–92.8%), 73.4% (53.0–93.8%), 62.2% (50.0–74.4%), and 47.3% (27.5–67.1%). Multivariate analysis identified age ≥70 years as an independent adverse prognostic factor for OS (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.848; 95% CI: 1.039–3.281; P = 0.042), while the presence of ≥2 inguinofemoral lymph node metastases was significantly associated with poorer CSS (HR: 2.179; 95% CI: 1.109–4.280; P = 0.030). Our analysis identified advanced age and a higher nodal burden as significant predictors of poorer survival outcomes in patients with vulvar cancer receiving postoperative adjuvant RT. Keywords: vulvar cancer, surgery, sdjuvant radiotherapy, lymph node metastasis, nationwide study INTRODUCTION Vulvar cancer is rare, with a global age-standardized incidence and mortality rates of 0.9 per 100,000 inhabitants and 0.3 per 100,000, respectively [ 1 ]. Smoking and human papillomavirus infection are considered as risk factors for vulvar cancer development. While smoking cessation and vaccination are expected to reduce morbidity, the vulvar cancer incidence is increasing in both developed and developing regions [ 2 ]. In Japan, the age at onset is increasing, with more cases being diagnosed at advanced stages. Thus, developing improved preventive and therapeutic strategies is crucial [ 3 ]. Surgery is the standard treatment of choice for resectable vulvar cancer, whereas adjuvant radiotherapy (RT) combined with concurrent chemotherapy is recommended for patients with postoperative risk factors [ 4 ]. Indications for adjuvant RT depend on the primary tumor margin and the status of inguinofemoral lymph node (IFLN) metastases in vulvar cancer. Several retrospective studies have demonstrated the efficacy of adjuvant RT for primary lesions in patients with positive or close margins [ 5–8 ]. In the GOG037 trial, compared with the pelvic lymphadenectomy group, the adjuvant RT group showed significant improvement in overall survival, especially in patients with clinical, more than one, or fixed ulcerative IFLN metastases [ 9 ]. Several retrospective studies and reports using the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results database have demonstrated the efficacy of adjuvant RT in patients with IFLN metastases [ 10–13 ]. However, some studies have reported a lack of efficacy in patients with a single IFLN metastasis and no extracapsular spread [ 11 , 14 ]. Notably, limited number of randomized controlled trials and retrospective studies have demonstrated the efficacy of adjuvant RT. Furthermore, while described in the guidelines [ 4 ], detailed data are limited. In particular, the stage-specific outcomes and prognostic factors of adjuvant RT have not been fully explored. This study aimed to evaluate the outcomes of adjuvant RT for vulvar cancer using data from a nationwide Japanese survey, and to report the survival outcomes based on the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage, prognostic factors, and real-world treatment characteristics. MATERIALS AND METHODS 1.1. Study design This study was a sub-analysis of a multicenter, retrospective, observational study involving 108 centers belonging to the Japanese Gynecologic Oncology Group. Data were collected from consecutive patients diagnosed with vulvar cancer between January 2001 and December 2010. The study protocol was approved by the ethics committee of each participating institution. The requirement for obtaining informed consent was waived owing to the retrospective study design. In accordance with the journal’s guidelines, we will provide our data for independent analysis by a selected team by the Editorial Team for the purposes of additional data analysis or for the reproducibility of this study in other centers if such is requested. 1.2. Study eligibility and data collection In the original study, the eligibility criteria were as follows: (i) patients treated for vulvar cancer during the 10-year period from January 2001 through December 2010, including those who initially received palliative therapy, (ii) primary vulvar cancer, excluding metastatic vulvar cancer, and (iii) all histologic types other than malignant melanoma. The survey was conducted using questionnaires in de-identified Excel files that were mailed on disks to the participating institutes; these included patient characteristics, surgical treatment, and RT. The detailed criteria and survey components are summarized in our previous study [ 15 ]. Our study analyzed patients from the original cohort who received postoperative adjuvant RT. To select patients, following exclusion criteria were applied: (i) not received surgical treatment, (ii) histology other than squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and adenosquamous carcinoma, (iii) not received RT, (iv) received non-adjuvant RT, (v) received RT with palliative intent, and (vi) unknown RT status. Stages were described according to FIGO 2008 [ 16 ]. Figure 1 shows a patient selection flowchart. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flowchart of patient selection. SCC, squamous cell carcinoma; AD, adenocarcinoma; ADSC, adenosquamous carcinoma; RT, radiotherapy. 1.3. Statistical analysis Collected data were analyzed for overall survival (OS), cause-specific survival (CSS), local control rates (LCR), and distant metastasis-free rates (DMFR) using the Kaplan–Meier method. OS and CSS were defined as the time from the start of initial treatment to death from any cause and vulvar cancer, respectively. In this study, recurrence data were divided into three groups: “vulva only,” “distant metastasis,” and “vulva and distant metastasis.” In the analysis of LCR, vulvar recurrence was defined as an event, whereas death was considered a censoring event. For the assessment of DMFR, the occurrence of distant metastasis was defined as an event, with death likewise treated as a censoring event. Univariate analysis was performed using the log-rank test and included age, histology, FIGO stage, tumor diameter, surgical margin, number of IFLN metastases, surgical procedure, chemotherapy, radiation field, and total radiation dose as prognostic factors. Multivariate analysis was performed using a Cox proportional hazards regression model. Covariate selection was informed by prior literature and clinical relevance, and the number of covariates included was determined according to the number of events observed for each endpoint. For OS and CSS, the covariates included age, surgical margin status, number of IFLN metastases, and concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT). For LCR, surgical margin status, total radiation dose, and CCRT were included. For DMFR, covariates included the number of IFLN metastases, radiation field, and CCRT. Data were missing for several covariates: surgical margin status in 21 cases, number of IFLN metastases in 18 cases, CCRT in 6 cases, radiation field in 10 cases, and total radiation dose in 16 cases. Missing data were addressed using multiple imputation by chained equations (MICE) implemented in the R software (version 4.5.0, R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria). A total of 100 imputed datasets were generated using predictive mean matching with a maximum of 10 iterations per imputation. A random seed of 1 was set to ensure reproducibility. Multivariable analyses were performed on each imputed dataset, and results were combined using Rubin’s rules to obtain pooled estimates and standard errors. The magnitude of effect was expressed as a hazard ratio (HR) with a 95% confidence interval (CI). Additionally, patient and treatment characteristics stratified by age and the number of IFLN metastases were compared using the chi-square test. Statistical analyses, excluding multivariate analysis, were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics version 28 (IBM Corp., Armonk, NY, USA), with statistical significance set at P <0.05. 1.4. RESULTS Out of 1093 patients in the original data, 139 were eligible. Table 1 summarizes the patient and treatment characteristics. The median follow-up period was 44 (range: 3–169) months for all patients and 59 (range: 3–155) months for surviving patients. The median patient age was 67 (range: 38–86) years. The FIGO stage was I, II, III, and IV in 18, 22, 74, and 25 patients, respectively. The histological type was squamous cell carcinoma in 128 patients and adenocarcinoma in 11 patients. The median tumor diameter was 35 (range: 10–200) mm. Overall, 41 patients had positive surgical margins, whereas 36 patients had close margins (0–1 cm). Surgical treatment involving the IFLN was performed for dissection in 93 patients and for biopsy in 29 patients. IFLN metastases were observed in ≥2 nodes in 52 patients and in a single node in 31 patients. The median total radiation dose was 50.4 (interquartile range [IQR]: 50–60) Gy in all patients, 50.4 (IQR: 50–55.2) Gy in margin negative patients, and 55 (IQR: 50–60) Gy in margin positive patients. CCRT was administered to 24 patients. Table 1. Patient and treatment characteristics (n = 139) Patient characteristics n (range or %) Age at diagnosis (years), median (range) 67 (38–86) Follow-up period (month), median (range) 44 (3–169) FIGO stage (2008) IA 8 (5.8) IB 10 (7.2) II 22 (15.8) IIIA 33 (23.7) IIIB 34 (24.5) IIIC 7 (5.0) IVA 17 (12.2) IVB 8 (5.8) Histology Squamous cell carcinoma 128 (92.1) Adenocarcinoma 11 (7.9) Tumor diameter (mm), median (range) 35 (10-200) Vulva surgery type Partial vulvectomy 36 (25.9) Simple vulvectomy 36 (25.9) Radical vulvectomy 67 (48.2) Surgical margin status (cm) Positive 41 (29.5) 0–1 36 (25.9) 1–2 16 (11.5) > 2 25 (18.0) N/R 21 (15.1) Vulva reconstruction type None 97 (69.8) Cutaneous flap 6 (4.3) Partial skin graft 29 (20.9) Myocutaneous flap 7 (5.0) Surgery involving the IFLN Not performed 16 (11.5) LN biopsy 29 (20.9) Lymphadenectomy 93 (66.9) N/R 1 (0.7) Sentinel LN evaluation Not performed 134 (96.4) Performed 3 (2.2) N/R 2 (1.4) Positive IFLN number 0 38 (27.3) 1 31 (22.3) ≥ 2 52 (37.4) N/R 18 (12.9) Irradiation field Primary lesion 16 (11.5) Primary lesion and inguinofemoral region 55 (39.6) Whole pelvis (including the inguinofemoral region) 52 (37.4) N/R 13 (9.4) Irradiation field in patients with IFLN metastases Primary lesion 1 (1.2) Primary lesion and inguinofemoral region 37 (44.6) Whole pelvis (Including the inguinofemoral region) 34 (41.0) N/R 10 (12.0) Total radiation dose (Gy), median (IQR) 50.4 (50–60) Margin negative (Gy), median (IQR) 50.4 (50–55.2) Margin positive (Gy), median (IQR) 55 (50–60) Modality of radiotherapy X-ray 99 (71.2) Electron 24 (17.3) X-ray and electron 5 (3.6) N/R 11 (7.9) CCRT No 109 (78.4) Yes 24 (17.3) N/R 6 (4.3) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; IFLN, inguinofemoral lymph node; IQR, interquartile range; CCRT, concurrent chemoradiotherapy; N/R, not reported. Figure 2 shows the Kaplan–Meier curves for OS and CSS stratified by FIGO stage. Among all patients, the 5-year OS and CSS rates (95% CI) were 56.6% (47.6–65.6%) and 62.5% (53.9–71.1%), respectively. The 5-year OS rates (95% CI) for stages I, II, III, and IV were 71.8% (50.8–92.8%), 61.3% (40.1–82.5%), 58.0% (45.8–70.2%), and 47.3% (27.5–67.1%), respectively. The corresponding 5-year CSS rates (95% CI) for stages I, II, III, and IV were 71.8% (50.8–92.8%), 73.4% (53.0–93.8%), 62.2% (50.0–74.4%), and 47.3% (27.5–67.1%), respectively. A total of 60 patients died from any cause, and 53 deaths were attributable to vulvar cancer. Four patients died from other diseases, while the causes of death in the remaining three patients were unknown. Figure 2. Open in a new tab OS and CSS curves stratified by FIGO staging. (A) Kaplan–Meier curves for OS stratified by FIGO staging. (B) Kaplan–Meier curves for CSS according to FIGO staging. OS, overall survival; CSS, cause-specific survival. Table 2 presents the univariate analysis of prognostic factors for OS and CSS. Age ≥70 years was the only significant adverse prognostic factor for OS (P = 0.002). For CSS, age ≥70 years and the presence of ≥2 IFLN metastases were identified as significant adverse prognostic factors (P = 0.008 and P = 0.043, respectively). Table 2. Assessment of prognostic factors with univariate analysis Factor N OS CSS 2-y (%) 5-y (%) P value 2-y (%) 5-y (%) P value Age (years) 0.002 0.008 < 70 81 79.5 67.7 80.8 70.4 ≥ 70 58 54.9 43.2 58.4 50.7 Histology 0.492 0.686 Squamous cell carcinoma 128 67.8 56.0 70.3 61.1 Others 11 90.0 80