urvival; HR, hazard ratio; CI, confidence interval; IFLN, inguinofemoral lymph node; CCRT, concurrent chemoradiotherapy Local and distant failures occurred in 44 and 32 patients, respectively. Table 4 presents the univariate analysis of factors associated with LCR and DMFR. For local control, age <70 years and the use of a whole-pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region were identified as significant favorable prognostic factors. No significant prognostic factors were identified for distant metastasis. In the multivariate analysis, no significant prognostic factors were found for either LCR or DMFR ( Table 5 ). Table 4. Assessment of prognostic factors with univariate analysis Factor N LCR N DMFR 2-y (%) 5-y (%) P value 2-y (%) 5-y (%) P value Age (years) 0.012 0.145 <70 79 82.4 73.9 79 86.7 76.3 ≥70 56 65.9 52.1 56 71.8 67.6 Histology 0.122 0.152 Squamous cell carcinoma 124 74.6 63.1 124 80.1 76.4 Others 11 88.9 88.9 11 88.9 40.0 FIGO stage (2008) 0.286 0.575 I–II 37 81.0 77.9 37 80.4 67.3 III–IV 98 73.8 60.1 98 81.4 75.6 Tumor diameter (mm) 0.824 0.570 <50 90 74.1 65.1 90 78.0 70.0 ≥50 30 83.3 68.8 30 82.7 78.4 Surgical margin status 0.323 0.129 Negative 75 77.5 69.1 75 81.2 75.8 Positive 40 68.2 51.5 40 73.7 58.8 IFLN metastasis 0.992 0.431 Negative 35 79.0 67.1 35 78.5 63.1 Positive 82 73.2 65.0 82 80.5 76.1 Positive IFLN number 0.808 0.544 <2 65 79.0 65.5 65 83.4 72.5 ≥2 52 68.9 65.6 52 75.2 70.5 Vulva surgery type 0.127 0.143 Radical vulvectomy 66 82.2 71.0 66 85.7 78.5 Others 69 69.3 60.2 69 75.9 66.4 Surgery involving the IFLN 0.733 0.798 Bilateral lymphadenectomy 75 75.4 67.4 75 80.7 74.5 Others 60 76.1 63.1 60 80.9 69.7 CCRT 0.106 0.404 No 106 72.9 61.0 106 79.3 69.8 Yes 23 90.7 84.2 23 86.3 80.9 Irradiation field 0.028 0.263 Whole pelvis 50 81.9 78.7 50 86.9 77.4 Others 85 72.1 57.8 85 77.1 69.7 Total radiation dose (Gy) 0.202 0.074 <60 85 80.6 69.8 85 81.2 78.0 ≥60 34 70.8 58.0 34 77.4 53.1 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: N, number of patients, y, years, LCR, local control rate; DMFR, distant metastasis free rate; FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; IFLN, inguinofemoral lymph node; CCRT, concurrent chemoradiotherapy Table 5. Assessment of prognostic factors with multivariate analysis Factor LCR Factor DMFR P value HR (95% CI) P value HR (95% CI) Positive for surgical margin 0.276 1.529 (0.725–3.222) Positive IFLN number ≥ 2 0.728 1.141 (0.548–2.372) Total radiation dose ≥60 Gy 0.771 1.132 (0.497–2.577) WPI including the inguinofemoral region 0.223 0.607 (0.277–1.328) CCRT 0.191 0.371 (0.088–1.571) CCRT 0.248 0.778 (0.263–2.307) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: LC, local control rate; DMF, distant metastasis-free rate; HR, hazard ratio; CI, confidence intervals; IFLN, inguinofemoral lymph node; CCRT, concurrent chemoradiotherapy; WPI, whole pelvis irradiation. Table 6 summarizes patient and treatment characteristics stratified by age and the number of IFLN metastases. Patients aged ≥70 years were significantly less likely to undergo high-intensity treatments, including radical vulvectomy, bilateral lymphadenectomy, and whole-pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region, compared to those aged <70 years. Bilateral lymphadenectomy was significantly more frequent in patients with ≥2 IFLN metastases compared to those with <2. However, there were no significant differences between the two groups in the rates of whole-pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region, irradiation including the inguinofemoral region, or CCRT. Among patients with ≥2 IFLN metastases, 33% received whole-pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region, 83% received irradiation including the inguinofemoral region, and 21% received CCRT. Table 6. Patient and treatment characteristics stratified by age and IFLN metastasis Factors Age (years) Positive IFLN number < 70 (%) ≥ 70 (%) P value < 2 (%) ≥ 2 (%) P value Age ≥ 70 years 39 40 0.889 Positive IFLN number ≥ 2 42 44 0.889 FIGO Stage (