dy design Treatment year No. of patients Age (Median or Mean) Stage Margin positive IFLN positive (≥ 2 LNs) Median follow-up (months) OS CSS Kunos et al. (2009) RCT 1977–1984 59 70 I–IV (FIGO 1969) N/R 100% (68%) 74 51% (6y) 71% (6y) Parthasarathy et al. (2006) SEER database 1988–2001 102 65 III N/R 100% (N/R) N/R N/R 77% (5y) Fons et al. (2009) Retrospective Multi-institution 1984–2005 31 72 N/R N/R 100% (0%) 75 N/R 69% (5y) Swanick et al. (2017) SEER-Medicare 1991–2009 306 78 N/R N/R 100% (63%) 17 32% (3y) 24% (5y) 44% (3y) 41% (5y) Mahner et al. (2015) Retrospective Multi-institution 1998–2008 244 67 N/R 18.9% (R1 resection) 100% (62%) 39 58% (3y) N/R Gill et al. (2015) NCDB 1998–2011 473 (CT [+]) 1324 (CT [−]) 61 (CT [+]) 69 (CT [−]) IIIA–IVA (AJCC) 28.4% (CT [+]) 18.2% (CT [−]) 100% (64.2%) (56.3%) 28 53.9% (CT [+]) 46.9% (CT [−]) (3y) N/R Ignatov et al. (2015) Retrospective Cancer database 2000-2011 34 72 N/R 100% (Including < 1cm) 54.2% (N/R) 40 67.6% (5y) N/R Present study Retrospective Multi-institution 2001–2010 139 67 IA–IVB (FIGO 2008) 29.5% (< 1 cm: 25.9%) 59.7% (37.4%) 44 69.6% (2y) 56.6% (5y) 71.8% (2y) 62.5% (5y) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: IFLN, inguinofemoral lymph node; OS, overall survival; CSS, cause specific survival; RCT, randomized controlled trial; FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; SEER, Surveillance Epidemiology and End Results; NCDB; National Cancer Database; AJCC, American Joint Committee on Cancer; CT, chemotherapy; N/R, not reported. Age was a significant prognostic factor for OS and CSS in the univariate analysis, and remained significant for OS in the multivariate analysis. Swanick et al. investigated the outcomes of adjuvant RT in elderly patients and reported 5-year OS and CSS rates of 24% and 41%, respectively. These outcomes appear worse than those reported in other studies and are comparable to our findings [ 17 ]. Despite no significant differences in FIGO stage or tumor diameter ( Table 4 ), older patients in our cohort received significantly less intensive treatment, both in terms of surgical management of the primary lesion and inguinofemoral lesion, as well as in the extent of the irradiation fields. Although not statistically significant, there was a trend toward reduced use of CCRT in older patients. Gill et al. analyzed data from the National Cancer Database and demonstrated a survival benefit from the addition of chemotherapy to adjuvant RT in patients with node-positive vulvar cancer; however, the rate of chemotherapy administration was significantly lower in patients older than 75 years (39% vs. 18%) [ 12 ]. Stuckey et al. , in a meta-analysis of patients with vulvar cancer treated with definitive concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT), reported a trend toward higher mortality due to intercurrent disease or treatment-related complications in elderly patients [ 18 ]. Swanick et al. in a database study focusing on elderly patients with node-positive vulvar cancer, found that the rate of adjuvant RT decreased with age and that chemotherapy was added in only 16% of cases [ 17 ]. Therefore, the benefit of adding chemotherapy to adjuvant RT in elderly patients has not been sufficiently established, and careful consideration of treatment-related toxicity is warranted. In the future, it will be important to determine the indications and selection criteria for CCRT not solely based on chronological age, but also by considering the patient''s overall physical condition or performance status. Recently, high-precision RT, such as intensity-modulated radiotherapy (IMRT), has been proposed to reduce adverse events. For other gynecological malignancies, such as endometrial and cervical cancers, postoperative adjuvant CCRT using IMRT has been reported to reduce acute genitourinary, gastrointestinal, and hematologic toxicities, as well as late gastrointestinal toxicity [ 19–21 ]. A retrospective study reported favorable outcomes of definitive CCRT using IMRT for advanced vulvar cancer [ 22 ]. Although data on the implementation rate of IMRT are absent in this study, it is presumed that IMRT was rarely utilized during the study period (2001–2010) due to its limited global adoption at that time, including in Japan [ 23 , 24 ]. Therefore, the proactive use of high-precision RT, such as IMRT, combined with concurrent chemotherapy at adequate dose and field coverage, may contribute to improved clinical outcomes in the future. In this study, the mere presence of IFLN metastases was not a significant prognostic factor in the univariate analysis; however, the presence of ≥2 IFLN metastases was significant associated with poorer CSS in both univariate and multivariate analyses. Fons et al. reported that adjuvant RT for patients with a single intracapsular IFLN metastasis did not represent a benefit [ 14 ]. Conversely, Woebler et al. reported that the number of IFLN metastases did not affect prognosis in patients receiving adjuvant RT to the groin and pelvis, which contrasts with our finding that multiple IFLN metastases were associated with poorer prognosis [ 25 ]. Furthermore, there were no significant differences in the irradiation rates to the inguinofemoral and pelvic lymph node regions between patients with <2 and ≥2 IFLN metastases in this study ( Table 6 ). Although adjuvant RT for patients with multiple IFLN metastases should include the inguinofemoral region, its omission in some cases may result in poor outcomes. Furthermore, for patients with multiple IFLN metastases, the recommended RT field includes both inguinofemoral and pelvic lymph node regions [ 4 ]. Therefore, increasing the utilization rate of whole pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region may contribute to improve clinical outcomes. In the GROINSS-V II study, Oonk et al. demonstrated that irradiation of the inguinofemoral and distal part of the external iliac region with a total dose of 50 Gy is a safe alternative to inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy for early-stage vulvar cancer with sentinel lymph node micrometastases. However, among patients with macrometastases, this dose was associated with a higher rate of isolated groin recurrences compared to inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy, possibly due to an inadequate radiation dose [ 26 ]. Although the sentinel lymph node biopsy rate was low in our study, the median total radiation dose was 50 Gy, suggesting that an insufficient dosing to the inguinofemoral region in the presence of lymph node metastasis may contribute to a poor prognosis. Previous reports have suggested that CCRT is superior to RT alone and have provided recommendations for patients who can tolerate it [ 4 , 12 , 27 , 28 ]. In this study, patients with ≥2 IFLN metastases received whole-pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region and CCRT at rates of 33% and 21%, respectively, which were not significantly different from those with <2 IFLN metastases. As mentioned above, prognosis may be improved by increasing the rate of CCRT with a sufficient irradiation field, including the pelvic regions, using IMRT, which has shown favorable outcomes in definitive settings [ 22 ]. In this study, age <70 years and whole pelvis irradiation including the inguinofemoral region were significant favorable prognostic factors for local control, while positive margins and total radiation dose were not significant prognostic factors in the univariate analysis. Foul et al. reported the efficacy of adjuvant RT for vulvar cancer in cases of positive or close-margins [ 5 ]. Viswanathan et al. reported a reduction in local recurrence with local radiation doses of ≥56 Gy [ 8 ]. Although local radiation dose was not explicitly mentioned in this study, the median total dose for margin-positive cases was 55 Gy, suggesting effective local control was likely achieved with adequate irradiation. This study has several limitations. Owing to the retrospective design, clinical endpoints and toxicity had significant missing data and could not be evaluated. Due to the lack of detailed data on recurrence patterns, particularly regarding pelvic and inguinofemoral recurrences, the evaluation of the appropriateness of the RT field remains insufficient. Relationship of IFLN metastasis with survival could not be evaluated as information on extracapsular spread was absent. Regarding the analysis of prognostic factors, detailed data on the treatment, such as the regimens/methods and doses of chemotherapy and RT, were lacking, especially the total dose to each site and the fractionation in RT. Data on co-morbidity, which could be a competing risk for survival, were also missing. Furthermore, because eligibility and treatment regimens were not defined prospectively, potential variations in patient and treatment backgrounds might have affected the OS and CSS. The method used to diagnose IFLN metastasis, a significant prognostic factor, was not explicitly defined in the study protocol. However, the strength of this study is the identification of stage-specific survival outcomes and prognostic factors in postoperative adjuvant RT for rare vulvar cancer using data from a large nationwide study. In conclusion, we reported the survival outcomes of postoperative adjuvant RT for vulvar cancer using data from a nationwide survey. Age ≥70 years and the presence of ≥2 IFLN metastases were identified as significant adverse prognostic factors for OS and CSS, respectively. Further prospective studies are warranted to evaluate the efficacy of adjuvant CCRT using high-precision RT techniques in patients with vulvar cancer and adverse prognostic features. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS We thank all the Japanese Gynecologic Oncology Group institutions that participated in this study and the Japanese Gynecologic Oncology Group Cervical Cancer Committee members for their administrative work. The following Japanese Gynecologic Oncology Group institutions participated in this study: Saitama Cancer Center, Keio University, Aichi Cancer Center